ups","content":"Consumer groups"},"2858":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#joining-and-leaving","content":"Joining and leaving"},"2859":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#system-operations","content":"System operations"},"2860":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#option-catalog","content":"Option catalog"},"2861":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#raw-binary-requests","content":"Raw binary requests"},"2862":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#snapshots","content":"Snapshots"},"2863":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#segment-management","content":"Segment management"},"2864":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","content":"This guide covers client configuration and the full `IIggyClient` API surface - the low-level, per-call operations. For the ergonomic producer/consumer abstractions built on top of these, see the High-level SDK."},"2865":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","content":"The API fragments below reuse a connected, authenticated `client` (see Creating a client) and the imports in the configuration example. Use existing streams and topics for read, update, delete, publish, and poll operations. The create examples show how to prepare them."},"2866":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#client-configuration","content":"`IggyClientConfigurator` exposes the full set of connection options - buffer sizes, TLS, heartbeat, automatic reconnection with exponential backoff, and auto-login:"},"2867":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#client-configuration","content":"With TCP auto-login configured, the client logs in once connected. Successful manual username/password or PAT login is also remembered for reconnects. HTTP `ConnectAsync` does no network work and does not auto-login. Call `LoginUserAsync` or `LoginWithPersonalAccessTokenAsync` explicitly."},"2868":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property      | Type     | Default    | Description                                                                                                         |\n| ------------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `BaseAddress` | `string` | *required* | Server address. TCP takes `host:port` (e.g. `127.0.0.1:8090`). HTTP needs a full URI (e.g. `http://127.0.0.1:3000`) |","table":"table-0"},"2869":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property   | Type       | Default    | Description                                  |\n| ---------- | ---------- | ---------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `Protocol` | `Protocol` | *required* | Transport: `Protocol.Tcp` or `Protocol.Http` |","table":"table-0"},"2870":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-42","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property               | Type  | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------- | ----- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `MaxResponseFrameSize` | `int` | 64 MiB  | Largest reply frame accepted over TCP. A reply above the bound is refused and the connection dropped. Raise it if a single response legitimately exceeds it (a large snapshot is the usual case) |","table":"table-0"},"2871":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-43","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property            | Type   | Default | Description                                                         |\n| ------------------- | ------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ReceiveBufferSize` | `int?` | `null`  | TCP receive buffer override in bytes. Null preserves the OS default |","table":"table-0"},"2872":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-44","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property         | Type   | Default | Description                                                      |\n| ---------------- | ------ | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SendBufferSize` | `int?` | `null`  | TCP send buffer override in bytes. Null preserves the OS default |","table":"table-0"},"2873":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-45","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property            | Type       | Default | Description                                                                                                                                           |\n| ------------------- | ---------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HeartbeatInterval` | `TimeSpan` | `5s`    | Interval between the automatic pings the TCP client sends while connected (see Heartbeat). Init-only. Must be between 1 millisecond and about 49 days |","table":"table-0"},"2874":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-46","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property      | Type          | Default  | Description                                                                                    |\n| ------------- | ------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `TlsSettings` | `TlsSettings` | disabled | TCP TLS configuration (see below). HTTP uses its HTTPS URI and standard certificate validation |","table":"table-0"},"2875":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-47","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property               | Type                   | Default | Description                           |\n| ---------------------- | ---------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `ReconnectionSettings` | `ReconnectionSettings` | enabled | TCP reconnection behavior (see below) |","table":"table-0"},"2876":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-48","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property            | Type                | Default  | Description                    |\n| ------------------- | ------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `AutoLoginSettings` | `AutoLoginSettings` | disabled | TCP automatic login on connect |","table":"table-0"},"2877":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-49","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property        | Type             | Default                      | Description                                                            |\n| --------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `LoggerFactory` | `ILoggerFactory` | `NullLoggerFactory.Instance` | Logger factory for diagnostics (currently applied to TCP clients only) |","table":"table-0"},"2878":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-50","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property           | Type                 | Default | Description                                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `MessageEncryptor` | `IMessageEncryptor?` | `null`  | Client-side payload encryptor (encrypts on send, decrypts on poll - see Message encryption) |","table":"table-0"},"2879":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-51","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#iggyclientconfigurator","content":"| Property                       | Type   | Default | Description                                        |\n| ------------------------------ | ------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `AllowAutoCommitWithEncryptor` | `bool` | `false` | Allow auto-commit while an encryptor is configured |","table":"table-0"},"2880":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-52","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#heartbeat","content":"The TCP client pings the server on its own every `HeartbeatInterval` while connected, so an idle session survives the server's heartbeat verification and consumer-group memberships and assignments stay fresh. The ping goes through the regular request path: a lost session is repaired by the same reconnect and auto-login any other request would trigger. Heartbeat failures are logged and the loop keeps going until the client is disposed."},"2881":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-53","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#tlssettings","content":"| Property  | Type   | Default | Description            |\n| --------- | ------ | ------- | ---------------------- |\n| `Enabled` | `bool` | `false` | Whether TLS is enabled |","table":"table-1"},"2882":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-54","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#tlssettings","content":"| Property   | Type     | Default | Description                       |\n| ---------- | -------- | ------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `Hostname` | `string` | `\"\"`    | Server name for the TLS handshake |","table":"table-1"},"2883":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-55","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#tlssettings","content":"| Property          | Type     | Default | Description                                     |\n| ----------------- | -------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `CertificatePath` | `string` | `\"\"`    | Path to the certificate (CA / self-signed) file |","table":"table-1"},"2884":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-56","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property  | Type   | Default | Description                                             |\n| --------- | ------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `Enabled` | `bool` | `true`  | Enable automatic reconnection when the connection drops |","table":"table-2"},"2885":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-57","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property     | Type  | Default | Description                                                              |\n| ------------ | ----- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `MaxRetries` | `int` | `0`     | Maximum failed passes through the known address roster (`0` = unlimited) |","table":"table-2"},"2886":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-58","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property       | Type       | Default | Description                                 |\n| -------------- | ---------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `InitialDelay` | `TimeSpan` | `5s`    | Delay before the first reconnection attempt |","table":"table-2"},"2887":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-59","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property   | Type       | Default | Description                    |\n| ---------- | ---------- | ------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `MaxDelay` | `TimeSpan` | `30s`   | Maximum delay between attempts |","table":"table-2"},"2888":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-60","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property             | Type       | Default | Description                                                          |\n| -------------------- | ---------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `WaitAfterReconnect` | `TimeSpan` | `1s`    | Pause after a successful reconnect (e.g. to rejoin a consumer group) |","table":"table-2"},"2889":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-61","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property                | Type   | Default | Description                        |\n| ----------------------- | ------ | ------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `UseExponentialBackoff` | `bool` | `true`  | Use exponential backoff for delays |","table":"table-2"},"2890":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-62","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"| Property            | Type     | Default | Description                        |\n| ------------------- | -------- | ------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `BackoffMultiplier` | `double` | `2.0`   | Multiplier for exponential backoff |","table":"table-2"},"2891":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-63","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#reconnectionsettings","content":"> **Warning:** TCP reconnection is **on by default** with **unlimited retries**. A failed pass tries the known roster addresses before consuming one retry. Connection and TLS handshake failures can be retried. An invalid local CA file stops after the pass, and rejected login credentials are surfaced. Pass a `CancellationToken` or set `MaxRetries` to bound connection attempts, or set `Enabled = false` to disable reconnection. Request replay also has its own deadline and requires configured or remembered login credentials."},"2892":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-64","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#autologinsettings","content":"| Property  | Type   | Default | Description                         |\n| --------- | ------ | ------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `Enabled` | `bool` | `false` | Log in automatically once connected |","table":"table-3"},"2893":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-65","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#autologinsettings","content":"| Property   | Type     | Default | Description             |\n| ---------- | -------- | ------- | ----------------------- |\n| `Username` | `string` | `\"\"`    | Username for auto-login |","table":"table-3"},"2894":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-66","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#autologinsettings","content":"| Property   | Type     | Default | Description             |\n| ---------- | -------- | ------- | ----------------------- |\n| `Password` | `string` | `\"\"`    | Password for auto-login |","table":"table-3"},"2895":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-67","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#autologinsettings","content":"| Property              | Type     | Default | Description                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------- | -------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `PersonalAccessToken` | `string` | `\"\"`    | Personal access token to sign in with instead of a username and password. Takes precedence over them when set |","table":"table-3"},"2896":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-68","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#autologinsettings","content":"All properties are init-only: build the settings with an object initializer or the static factories `AutoLoginSettings.For(username, password)` and `AutoLoginSettings.ForPersonalAccessToken(token)`. TCP reconnects use configured auto-login credentials first, then credentials remembered from a successful manual login. Without either, a reconnect cannot restore the authenticated session."},"2897":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-69","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"Set `IggyClientConfigurator.MessageEncryptor` to encrypt message payloads and user headers client-side. They are encrypted on send and decrypted on poll. Routing information and message metadata remain visible to the server. The built-in `AesMessageEncryptor` uses AES-GCM (confidentiality + authenticity) and takes a 16-, 24-, or 32-byte key for AES-128/192/256:"},"2898":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-70","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"Keep the same key for clients that exchange encrypted messages and for later reads. The generated key above lasts only for this example. For a custom scheme, implement `IMessageEncryptor`."},"2899":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-71","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"Things to know:"},"2900":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-72","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"The encryptor applies to **every message on the connection** - topics mixing encrypted and plaintext messages are not supported."},"2901":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-73","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"You own the encryptor and must dispose it once the client is done with it."},"2902":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-74","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"With an encryptor configured, polling with `autoCommit: true` throws `InvalidOperationException` by default: the server commits the offset **before the client decrypts**, so a decryption failure would silently skip the whole batch. Poll with `autoCommit: false` and store offsets after processing, or opt in with `AllowAutoCommitWithEncryptor = true`. `IggyConsumer` separately rejects wire auto-commit with encryption. Use `AfterReceive` or `Disabled` there."},"2903":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-75","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#message-encryption","content":"On the high-level builders, `WithEncryptor(...)` is only valid when the builder creates its own client. For an external client, set `MessageEncryptor` on the configurator instead."},"2904":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-76","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#connection-events","content":"TCP clients publish connection state changes, useful for reacting to reconnects. HTTP subscription methods are no-ops:"},"2905":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-77","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#identifiers","content":"Streams, topics, users, and consumer groups are referenced by an `Identifier`, which can be either numeric or a name:"},"2906":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-78","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#identifiers","content":"Server-assigned ids are **0-based**: the first stream, topic, partition, or consumer group gets id 0. The root user has id 0 as well."},"2907":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-79","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#user-login","content":"Use the credentials configured when the server was initialized. These examples use the `iggy` / `iggy` setup from the introduction:"},"2908":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-80","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#creating-users","content":"Create new users with customizable permissions:"},"2909":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-81","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#creating-users","content":"Besides `Global`, `Permissions.Streams` scopes permissions to specific streams (keyed by numeric stream id), and each `StreamPermissions` can in turn scope down to specific topics:"},"2910":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-82","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#personal-access-tokens","content":"Create and use Personal Access Tokens (PAT) for programmatic access:"},"2911":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-83","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#topics","content":"Create a topic with typed `TopicOptions`. Fields left null use the server default. Compression, durability, partition resize policy and replication factor are fixed at creation."},"2912":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-84","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#topic-options","content":"`DescribeOptionsAsync(OptionsScope.Topic)` returns the catalog. Set fields in `TopicUpdate` to change them. `Reset` returns a supported key to its server default. Omitted fields keep their stored values. Compression cannot be updated. See Topic options."},"2913":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-85","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#topic-options","content":"Stream and topic purge are removed. `DeleteTopicAsync` deletes the topic and its data. There is no on-demand flush command. Select both durability policies when creating the topic."},"2914":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-86","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#partitions","content":"Add partitions to or remove them from an existing topic:"},"2915":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-87","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#sending-messages","content":"A `Message` takes an id (`Guid` or `UInt128`) and a payload:"},"2916":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-88","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#sending-messages","content":"`SendMessagesAsync` returns a `SendMessagesResponse` whose `Confirmations` carry `StreamId`, `TopicId`, `PartitionId`, and `BaseOffset` (the offset of the first message of the batch) per committed batch. An empty list reports no assigned offsets. A single-message overload is also available: `SendMessagesAsync(streamId, topicId, partitioning, message)`."},"2917":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-89","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#sending-messages","content":"To send many payloads without a `byte[]` allocation per message, build them into a single pooled buffer with `RentedMessageBatchBuilder` - see Publishing with rented batches."},"2918":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-90","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#partitioning-strategies","content":"Control which partition receives each message:"},"2919":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-91","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#partitioning-strategies","content":"Partition selection is resolved **client-side** on both transports. Balanced partitioning round-robins over the topic's partitions, and key-based routing hashes the key with xxHash32 modulo the partition count, consistent with the Rust SDK for the same encoded key bytes and partition count. Strings use UTF-8, integers use little-endian bytes, and GUID keys use `Guid.ToByteArray()`."},"2920":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-92","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#user-defined-headers","content":"Add typed custom headers to messages. Build keys with `HeaderKey.FromString` and values with the `HeaderValue.From*` factories:"},"2921":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-93","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#user-defined-headers","content":"Available value factories: `FromString`, `FromBool`, `FromBytes`, `FromUInt8`, `FromInt32`, `FromInt64`, `FromInt128`, `FromUInt32`, `FromUInt64`, `FromUInt128`, `FromFloat`, `FromDouble`, `FromGuid`."},"2922":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-94","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#fetching-messages","content":"A poll reads one explicit partition. Use a reader for several partitions or a consumer group assignment. `PollOptions` defaults to no wait and manual commits."},"2923":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-95","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#fetching-messages","content":"A checkpoint retains the partition history and owner. A commit after a partition recreation or ownership change is refused. `polled.Continuation` continues the same partition without losing that context."},"2924":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-96","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#fetching-messages","content":"`CommitPolicy.OnRead` submits the offset with the read, before application processing. `CommitPolicy.Auto()` is available to readers and consumers, but not a one-shot poll. The deferred polling guide explains limits and cancellation."},"2925":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-97","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#partition-reader","content":"TCP groups reads by primary. HTTP uses one poll per partition and supports standalone consumers only. A group reader joins and follows its assignment. `ReadException` reports the partition and any continuation that skips an undecodable record. A stopped partition needs an explicit seek before reading resumes."},"2926":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-98","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#polling-with-rented-buffers","content":"The rental API returns payload slices backed by a pooled buffer. Dispose each rental and copy any bytes that must outlive it. Do not retain a payload or raw-header reference after disposal. The high-level consumer exposes `ReceiveRentedAsync` for this path."},"2927":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-99","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#polling-strategies","content":"Control where consumption starts:"},"2928":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-100","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#offset-management","content":"Consumer offsets are keyed by consumer + stream + topic + partition. Note the argument order - the `Consumer` comes first:"},"2929":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-101","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups coordinate message consumption across multiple consumers, load-balancing partitions between members."},"2930":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-102","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#joining-and-leaving","content":"> **Note:** Join/Leave are TCP-only and throw `FeatureUnavailableException` on HTTP."},"2931":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-103","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#system-operations","content":"`PingAsync` is **more than a bare ping** over TCP: it also re-syncs the assignment of every consumer group this client has joined, at the cost of one extra round trip per joined group. The client already pings on its own every `HeartbeatInterval`, so an explicit ping is only needed to force an assignment refresh sooner. `GetCurrentAddress()` is useful under leader redirection, when the client may have followed the cluster roster to a different node than the one it was configured with (see Connection semantics)."},"2932":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-104","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#option-catalog","content":"`DescribeOptionsAsync` enumerates the option keys a server accepts for a resource scope (`OptionsScope.Topic`, `OptionsScope.Stream`, or `OptionsScope.User`), with the kind, default, and description of each. This is the discovery surface for the topic options dictionary:"},"2933":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-105","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#raw-binary-requests","content":"`SendBinaryRequestAsync` is an escape hatch that sends a raw command code with a prebuilt payload and returns the raw response bytes, for commands the typed API does not cover:"},"2934":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-106","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#raw-binary-requests","content":"Session-control codes (login, logout, and the like) are rejected with an invalid-command error, and the operation is TCP-only: HTTP clients throw `FeatureUnavailableException`."},"2935":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-107","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#snapshots","content":"Capture a system snapshot as a compressed archive:"},"2936":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-108","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#snapshots","content":"Compression methods: `Stored`, `Deflated`, `Bzip2`, `Zstd`, `Lzma`, `Xz`. Snapshot types: `FilesystemOverview`, `ProcessList`, `ResourceUsage`, `Test`, `ServerLogs`, `ServerConfig`, `All`."},"2937":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-109","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#snapshots","content":"A full snapshot can exceed the default 64 MiB reply-frame bound, in which case the reply is **refused and the connection dropped**. Raise `MaxResponseFrameSize` before requesting large snapshots."},"2938":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-110","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#segment-management","content":"Delete up to N of the oldest sealed segments from a partition. The active segment is retained:"},"2939":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide-111","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/guide#segment-management","content":"> **Note:** TCP-only - throws `FeatureUnavailableException` on HTTP."},"2940":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"page","content":"High-level SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C#"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk"},"2941":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"IggyPublisher and IggyConsumer for C#, with background batching, retries and automatic offset commits."},"2942":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggypublisher","content":"IggyPublisher"},"2943":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"Publisher builder options"},"2944":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"Publishing with rented batches"},"2945":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"Typed publisher"},"2946":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggyconsumer","content":"IggyConsumer"},"2947":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"2948":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Consumer builder options"},"2949":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"Consuming with rented buffers"},"2950":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"Typed consumer"},"2951":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"The per-call `IIggyClient` API is explicit but verbose - you manage partitioning, batching, retries, and offsets yourself. The high-level `IggyPublisher` and `IggyConsumer` wrap that surface with fluent builders that handle:"},"2952":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"**Background sending** with buffering, batching, and retries"},"2953":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"**Automatic stream/topic/consumer-group creation**"},"2954":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"**Automatic offset commits** (on each poll, after each received message, or manual)"},"2955":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"**Consuming a topic as an async stream** (`IAsyncEnumerable`)"},"2956":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"**Typed message (de)serialization**"},"2957":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"Sending and receiving over **rented, pooled buffers** to avoid separate payload copies on TCP"},"2958":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","content":"The fragments below reuse an authenticated `client` and the imports in the first example. The named streams/topics must already exist unless the snippet creates them. Both abstractions can be built from an existing connected client via `IggyPublisherBuilder.Create(...)` / `IggyConsumerBuilder.Create(...)`, or equivalently via the `client.CreatePublisherBuilder(...)` / `client.CreateConsumerBuilder(...)` extension methods from `Apache.Iggy.Extensions`."},"2959":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggypublisher","content":"Configure a publisher, initialize it, then send. `InitAsync` validates (and optionally creates) the stream and topic:"},"2960":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                                                                                                             | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `WithConnection(protocol, address, login, password, receiveBufferSize = null, sendBufferSize = null, reconnectionSettings = null)` | Connection settings - only used when the builder creates its own client (i.e. the `Create(streamId, topicId)` overload, without an existing client). A builder-created TCP client auto-logs in and reuses the credentials across reconnects. For HTTP, supply an explicitly authenticated client |","table":"table-0"},"2961":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                                                                                                                 | Description                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `WithConnection(protocol, address, personalAccessToken, receiveBufferSize = null, sendBufferSize = null, reconnectionSettings = null)` | Same, authenticating with a personal access token instead of a username and password |","table":"table-0"},"2962":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                           | Description                                                |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `WithPartitioning(partitioning)` | Routing strategy for produced messages (default: balanced) |","table":"table-0"},"2963":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                          | Description                                      |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `CreateStreamIfNotExists(name)` | Auto-create the stream on `InitAsync` if missing |","table":"table-0"},"2964":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                                                                                                                 | Description                                     |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `CreateTopicIfNotExists(name, topicPartitionsCount = 1, compressionAlgorithm = None, messageExpiry = TimeSpan.Zero, maxTopicSize = 0)` | Auto-create the topic on `InitAsync` if missing |","table":"table-0"},"2965":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                                                                                      | Description                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `WithRetry(enabled = true, maxAttempts = 3, initialDelay = 100ms, maxDelay = 10s, backoffMultiplier = 2.0)` | Retry background sends with exponential backoff. Direct sends surface errors |","table":"table-0"},"2966":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                                                                                                       | Description                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `WithBackgroundSending(enabled = true, queueCapacity = 10000, batchSize = 100, flushInterval = 100ms, disposalTimeout = 5s)` | Queue and flush sends in the background for higher throughput |","table":"table-0"},"2967":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                     | Description                                                                                                                                            |\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `WithEncryptor(encryptor)` | Client-side payload encryption. Only valid on a builder-created client - for an external client, set `IggyClientConfigurator.MessageEncryptor` instead |","table":"table-0"},"2968":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                | Description                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `SubscribeOnBackgroundError(handler)` | Observe background-processing errors (only fires when background sending is enabled) |","table":"table-0"},"2969":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                                   | Description                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SubscribeOnMessageBatchFailed(handler)` | Fires when a batch fails after all retries are exhausted (only with background sending - direct sends throw to the caller instead) |","table":"table-0"},"2970":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Method                      | Description                    |\n| --------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `WithLogger(loggerFactory)` | Logger factory for diagnostics |","table":"table-0"},"2971":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"**`WithRetry` parameters**"},"2972":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter | Type   | Default | Description              |\n| --------- | ------ | ------- | ------------------------ |\n| `enabled` | `bool` | `true`  | Whether retry is enabled |","table":"table-1"},"2973":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter     | Type  | Default | Description                                   |\n| ------------- | ----- | ------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `maxAttempts` | `int` | `3`     | Total send attempts, including the first send |","table":"table-1"},"2974":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter      | Type        | Default | Description                  |\n| -------------- | ----------- | ------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `initialDelay` | `TimeSpan?` | `100ms` | Delay before the first retry |","table":"table-1"},"2975":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter  | Type        | Default | Description                   |\n| ---------- | ----------- | ------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `maxDelay` | `TimeSpan?` | `10s`   | Maximum delay between retries |","table":"table-1"},"2976":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter           | Type     | Default | Description                    |\n| ------------------- | -------- | ------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `backoffMultiplier` | `double` | `2.0`   | Exponential backoff multiplier |","table":"table-1"},"2977":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"**`WithBackgroundSending` parameters**"},"2978":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter | Type   | Default | Description                           |\n| --------- | ------ | ------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `bool` | `true`  | Whether background sending is enabled |","table":"table-2"},"2979":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter       | Type  | Default | Description                                                                             |\n| --------------- | ----- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `queueCapacity` | `int` | `10000` | Max queued send calls (one slot per `SendMessagesAsync` call, regardless of batch size) |","table":"table-2"},"2980":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter   | Type  | Default | Description                                                       |\n| ----------- | ----- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `batchSize` | `int` | `100`   | Flush threshold in messages. An individual send call is not split |","table":"table-2"},"2981":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter       | Type        | Default | Description                                    |\n| --------------- | ----------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `flushInterval` | `TimeSpan?` | `100ms` | Interval at which pending messages are flushed |","table":"table-2"},"2982":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"| Parameter         | Type        | Default | Description                                                                             |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `disposalTimeout` | `TimeSpan?` | `5s`    | How long `DisposeAsync` waits for the processor to stop. Pending sends may be discarded |","table":"table-2"},"2983":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-42","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publisher-builder-options","content":"A batch also flushes once its accumulated payload reaches `IggyPublisherConfig.BackgroundMaxBatchBytes` (default 256 KiB, `0` disables the byte gate) - whichever of `batchSize` or the byte limit is hit first. This knob has no builder method. Set it on the config directly if needed. Await `WaitUntilAllSendsAsync` before disposal to finish queued sends. Disposal alone can discard them. Background send calls return an empty confirmation list before delivery, and failures are reported through the subscribed events."},"2984":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-43","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"`SendMessagesAsync` accepts messages with `ReadOnlyMemory<byte>` payloads, which can share backing storage. On hot paths, build a `RentedMessageBatch` instead - every payload is written into a single buffer rented from the shared array pool - and hand it to `SendAsync`:"},"2985":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-44","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"`RentedMessageBatchBuilder`:"},"2986":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-45","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"| Member                                           | Description                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `new RentedMessageBatchBuilder(sizeHint = 1024)` | Initial capacity hint (bytes) for the rented buffer |","table":"table-3"},"2987":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-46","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"| Member                                        | Description                                                  |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `Add(payload, id = null, userHeaders = null)` | Copies a `ReadOnlySpan<byte>` payload into the shared buffer |","table":"table-3"},"2988":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-47","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"| Member                                                    | Description                                                                                       |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `Add(state, writePayload, id = null, userHeaders = null)` | Writes the payload via an `Action<TState, IBufferWriter<byte>>` callback directly into the buffer |","table":"table-3"},"2989":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-48","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"| Member  | Description                     |\n| ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `Count` | Number of payloads added so far |","table":"table-3"},"2990":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-49","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"| Member    | Description                                                                    |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `Build()` | Materializes the `RentedMessageBatch`. The builder must not be used afterwards |","table":"table-3"},"2991":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-50","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"A `null` message id sends the message with id 0, letting the server assign one."},"2992":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-51","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"Ownership rules:"},"2993":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-52","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"`publisher.SendAsync(batch)` **takes ownership** - do not dispose the batch yourself. Its buffer returns to the pool once the batch is sent (immediately, or after the background flush)."},"2994":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-53","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"For a direct low-level send, pass `batch.Messages` to `client.SendMessagesAsync(...)` yourself, `await` it, then dispose the batch (`using var batch = builder.Build()`)."},"2995":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-54","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"After disposal, `batch.Messages` and every payload's `Span` throw `ObjectDisposedException` instead of reading recycled pool memory."},"2996":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-55","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"Dispose the builder only when abandoning it before `Build()`. A `using` on the builder is safe either way (it becomes a no-op after `Build()`)."},"2997":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-56","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"For automatic object serialization, use `IggyPublisherBuilder<T>` with an `ISerializer<T>`:"},"2998":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-57","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"Besides the collection overload, `SendAsync` also accepts a single item (`SendAsync(order, messageId: null, userHeaders: null)`) or a collection of `(data, messageId, userHeaders)` tuples when you need per-message ids or headers."},"2999":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-58","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"For JSON you don't need a custom serializer - the built-in `SystemTextJsonSerializer<T>` (in `Apache.Iggy.Publishers`) writes System.Text.Json output directly into the send buffer, with optional `JsonSerializerOptions`:"},"3000":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-59","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"With background sending enabled, typed values are serialized at flush time. Keep them immutable or pass a snapshot until `WaitUntilAllSendsAsync` completes."},"3001":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-60","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"There is **no built-in deserializer counterpart** - consumers implement `IDeserializer<T>` themselves (see Typed consumer)."},"3002":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-61","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggyconsumer","content":"The consumer reads through a `PartitionReader` and yields one message at a time. A standalone consumer requires `WithPartitions`. A group consumer follows the server assignment."},"3003":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-62","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggyconsumer","content":"The fragment assumes a connected client. Manual commits are the default. Commit the checkpoint after processing. `DisposeAsync` calls `ShutdownAsync` to close the reader and leave the group."},"3004":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-63","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"Use `Consumer.Group(\"my-group\")` and `WithConsumerGroup(\"my-group\", createIfNotExists: true)`. Do not supply standalone partitions. Set `WithPollOptions(new PollOptions { Commit = CommitPolicy.Auto() })` for automatic commits after processing."},"3005":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-64","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"`AutoCommit.Default` stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. `CommitAfter.Processing` counts a message when enumeration advances or a `ConsumeAsync` handler succeeds. A handler exception ends consumption and leaves that message uncounted. `CommitAfter.Marked` supports explicit progress marking after concurrent work."},"3006":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-65","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"`CommitPolicy.OnRead` submits the offset before processing. It cannot be combined with a consumer encryptor. See Deferred polling."},"3007":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-66","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Use `WithPollOptions` for count, wait, minimum count, byte budget and commit policy. Use `WithPartitions` for standalone partition selection, and `WithPollingStrategy` for the initial position. The previous `WithBatchSize`, `WithPollingInterval`, `WithPartitionId` and `WithAutoCommitMode` consumer methods are replaced."},"3008":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-67","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"A read error that stops a partition ends enumeration with `ReadException`. Inspect `StopsPartition`, `IsResumable` and `Skip` before selecting a new start with `SeekAsync`. Automatic retries do not discard an undecodable record without an application decision."},"3009":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-68","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"`ReceiveRentedAsync` exposes payloads from a pooled buffer. Dispose each yielded message before moving on, and copy bytes that must survive disposal. Read limits and commit policies are the same as for `ReceiveAsync`."},"3010":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-69","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"`IggyConsumerBuilder<T>` accepts an `IDeserializer<T>` and exposes `ReceiveDeserializedAsync`. Keep the typed builder in a variable before configuring it because inherited fluent methods return the base builder type. Use `WithPollOptions` to select the commit policy, and inspect each message status before accepting its progress."},"3011":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-70","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"See the C# examples for complete producer and consumer programs."},"3012":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"page","content":"C# SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C#"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro"},"3013":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The async-first C# SDK for .NET applications over TCP and HTTP, from installation to creating a client."},"3014":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"3015":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Supported protocols"},"3016":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Connection semantics"},"3017":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Creating a client"},"3018":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3019":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"3020":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"3021":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"3022":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The Iggy SDK for C# is a modern, async-first client library for interacting with an Iggy message streaming server from your .NET applications. It supports TCP and HTTP transports. The package is available on NuGet and the source code lives on GitHub."},"3023":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The SDK is built around the `IIggyClient` interface, which aggregates every feature (publishing, consuming, stream/topic management, users, offsets, consumer groups, and system operations). For the low-level per-call API and the full configuration reference, see the Guide. For the ergonomic, batteries-included producer/consumer abstractions, see the High-level SDK."},"3024":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"The SDK targets .NET 8 and .NET 10 (`net8.0` and `net10.0`). Version `0.10.0` matches the server 0.10.0 protocol and includes both topic durability options. Version `0.8.0` uses the older TCP framing. When building from source, use the server and SDK from the same checkout."},"3025":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"To use the source SDK with these examples, run from the Iggy checkout root:"},"3026":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Put a sample in `IggySample/Program.cs` and run `dotnet run --project IggySample`. For console logging, add its package to that project:"},"3027":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"The SDK supports two transport protocols:"},"3028":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"**TCP** - binary protocol for optimal performance and lower latency (recommended). Over TCP the SDK speaks the VSR consensus framing, the only wire protocol the server accepts. See Connection semantics"},"3029":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"**HTTP** - RESTful JSON API for stateless operations. `BaseAddress` must then be a full URI such as `http://127.0.0.1:3000` (the server's default HTTP port is 3000, TCP is 8090)"},"3030":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Some operations are **TCP-only** and throw `FeatureUnavailableException` on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, `GetMeAsync`, `DeleteSegmentsAsync`, and `SendBinaryRequestAsync`."},"3031":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"TCP uses the current binary protocol and routes partition requests to their primaries. The client discovers the cluster roster and follows metadata leadership separately. Balanced and message-key partitioning are resolved on the client."},"3032":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"A retained session can survive transport loss. Reconnect binds it before registering a new session. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end the session. An uncertain mutation retains its request identity for recovery. Keep the same client and use `ResolveOperationAsync` or `AbandonOperationAsync` when an exception reports a retained operation. Abandonment does not undo the server result."},"3033":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"A committing deferred poll whose reply is lost can throw `VsrFetchProgressUncertainException`. Later progress calls can throw `VsrPartitionLaneBlockedException` until the operation is abandoned. Offset reads remain available for inspection. Do not convert every exception into a fresh send."},"3034":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"The reader and high-level consumer follow group assignments. A one-shot poll names one partition. They default to manual checkpoint commits. See Deferred polling and the Guide."},"3035":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"The examples use `iggy` / `iggy`. For a new server data directory, start the matching source server from the Iggy repository root:"},"3036":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"For an existing server, use its configured credentials. Create a client with `IggyClientFactory.CreateClient`, then call `ConnectAsync`:"},"3037":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Optionally, provide an `ILoggerFactory` for diagnostics (defaults to `NullLoggerFactory.Instance`). The console logger requires the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console` package:"},"3038":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"`IggyClientConfigurator` also exposes TCP socket buffers, TLS, heartbeat, response-frame limits, reconnection with exponential backoff (on by default), auto-login, and message encryption. HTTP clients require explicit login. The TCP connection settings are not applied to HTTP. See Client configuration for the full reference."},"3039":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the equivalent low-level, per-call flow, see the Guide."},"3040":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"`ReceiveAsync` polls **indefinitely** - pass a `CancellationToken` or `break` out of the loop to stop."},"3041":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Guide - client configuration reference and the full API surface: auth, streams, topics, partitions, publishing, consuming, offsets, consumer groups, system operations"},"3042":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"High-level SDK - `IggyPublisher` / `IggyConsumer` with background sending, retries, auto-commit, typed (de)serialization, and pooled (rented) buffers to avoid separate payload copies on TCP"},"3043":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Examples - producer, consumer-group, and typed-message samples, plus links to runnable projects"},"3044":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"page","content":"Deferred Polling","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling"},"3045":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","content":"Wait for messages, read several partitions and choose when to store consumer offsets."},"3046":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"Choose read limits"},"3047":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#read-several-partitions","content":"Read several partitions"},"3048":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-when-to-commit","content":"Choose when to commit"},"3049":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#cancellation-and-uncertain-results","content":"Cancellation and uncertain results"},"3050":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#http-reads","content":"HTTP reads"},"3051":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#server-capacity","content":"Server capacity"},"3052":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","content":"Deferred polling lets the server wait for messages before answering a read. The read returns when enough messages are available or its wait ends. A quiet partition does not need a repeated request loop."},"3053":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"`PollOptions` separates read limits from the polling strategy and consumer identity. A strategy selects the starting position, such as a specific offset or the next stored offset."},"3054":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"| Field   | Default | Meaning                      |\n| ------- | ------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `count` | 1000    | Maximum messages in one read |","table":"table-0"},"3055":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"| Field      | Default | Meaning                                                       |\n| ---------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_wait` | Zero    | Zero returns immediately. A positive duration enables waiting |","table":"table-0"},"3056":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"| Field       | Default | Meaning                                                          |\n| ----------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `min_count` | 1       | Messages needed to finish the wait early, from 1 through `count` |","table":"table-0"},"3057":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"| Field       | Default | Meaning                                                   |\n| ----------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_bytes` | 1 MiB   | Reply budget, including binary metadata and batch framing |","table":"table-0"},"3058":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"| Field    | Default | Meaning                             |\n| -------- | ------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `commit` | Manual  | When to store the returned position |","table":"table-0"},"3059":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"These are the shared poll defaults. High-level consumers can select a positive wait by default. The SDK derives a request timeout from the wait plus 10 seconds. The wire protocol permits waits up to 600 seconds, but the server caps them at `sharding.deferred_poll_max_wait`, 30 seconds by default."},"3060":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-read-limits","content":"A reply can contain fewer than `min_count` messages when the wait ends. The first message can exceed `max_bytes` and return alone, so a large message does not prevent progress. Treat the byte budget as a batching limit, not an absolute allocation guarantee."},"3061":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#read-several-partitions","content":"A partition reader groups targets by their current primary and uses FetchMessages. Each fetch applies `count`, `min_count` and `max_bytes` across its targets together. The reader can keep several fetches active. It can return a partition with messages without waiting for a quiet partition first."},"3062":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#read-several-partitions","content":"A group consumer uses its assigned partitions. Assignment changes and primary changes require a refresh before further reads. Use the SDK reader or consumer to manage this state. A raw fetch caller must also supply the route and ownership information required by the protocol."},"3063":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-when-to-commit","content":"| Policy | Behavior                                                       |\n| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Manual | The application stores checkpoints after successful processing |","table":"table-1"},"3064":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-when-to-commit","content":"| Policy  | Behavior                                                        |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| On read | The server submits the last returned offset as part of the read |","table":"table-1"},"3065":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-when-to-commit","content":"| Policy    | Behavior                                                                        |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Automatic | A reader or consumer tracks progress and stores it by interval or message count |","table":"table-1"},"3066":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-when-to-commit","content":"A one-shot poll rejects an automatic policy because it has no continuing processing state. On-read commits can precede application processing. A failover before offset replication can also deliver the last batch again. Use manual commits or automatic commits after processing when messages must be processed before their offsets advance."},"3067":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#choose-when-to-commit","content":"The Rust automatic policy defaults to every 10,000 messages or five seconds, whichever happens first, after processing. It also stores progress on close, shutdown and partition revocation. Processing counts when the application requests the next item, a handler succeeds, or the reader closes. Marked progress supports applications that finish work concurrently. Do not close a reader as if processing succeeded after a failed handler."},"3068":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#cancellation-and-uncertain-results","content":"Cancellation removes a parked request when the server receives it. If a committing poll loses its reply, the client cannot assume that its offset stayed unchanged. SDK recovery tracks the uncertain operation and blocks conflicting work until the application resolves or abandons it. Abandoning permits later work but does not undo an offset that already committed."},"3069":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#http-reads","content":"Use `GET /streams/{stream_id}/topics/{topic_id}/messages/deferred` for a deferred read without offset commits. A read that sets `auto_commit=true` must use `POST` on that path. The server rejects a committing `GET`."},"3070":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#http-reads","content":"Raw HTTP defaults differ from `PollOptions`: count is 10, wait is one second, and request timeout is 11 seconds. Minimum count is one and the byte budget is 1 MiB."},"3071":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#http-reads","content":"The query uses `wait_us`, `min_count`, `max_bytes` and `request_timeout_us` alongside the normal consumer, partition, strategy and count fields. A positive wait needs at least one second between the wait and request timeout. Configure a proxy timeout long enough to receive the reply."},"3072":{"id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/deferred-polling#server-capacity","content":"The sharding configuration bounds parked requests, per-session requests, disk reads and completion delivery. HTTP requests without a binary session count against their user. Capacity exhaustion can return a retryable error. Keep retries within a bounded application deadline."},"3073":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples"},"3074":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"Runnable Go examples from the core repository, and how to start a server they can connect to."},"3075":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"3076":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"3077":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"TLS"},"3078":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example lives in the examples/go directory of the core repository. Its `go.mod` carries a `replace` directive pointing at the in-repo SDK, so the example always builds against the SDK source in the same checkout. The examples are exercised in CI via `scripts/run-examples-from-readme.sh --language go`."},"3079":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"The Go SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples require Go 1.25 or newer and log in as `iggy`/`iggy`. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides:"},"3080":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory** (`local_data` by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"3081":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"The producer creates `sample-stream` and `sample-topic` (if missing) and sends 5 batches of 10 messages to partition `0`. The consumer polls them from offset `0` and exits after 5 batches. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are **0-based**. These repository examples use numeric stream and topic IDs `0`, so create no other streams or topics before running the producer on the fresh server. Run the producer first."},"3082":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Run both from the `examples/go` directory:"},"3083":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Each consumer reads partition `0` independently, starting from offset `0` or the earliest retained message. Running several consumers reads the same messages. These examples do not join a consumer group."},"3084":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Both binaries accept the same flags:"},"3085":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"`--tcp-server-address` - server address, default `127.0.0.1:8090`"},"3086":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"`--tls` - enable TLS"},"3087":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"`--tls-ca-file` - path to a CA certificate file"},"3088":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"`--tls-domain` - TLS server domain name for SNI"},"3089":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"From the repository root, start a disposable server with TLS enabled and the development certificates:"},"3090":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Run the producer before the consumer with the TLS flags (paths relative to `examples/go`):"},"3091":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"For the code walkthrough, see the quick start on the intro page, which follows the same example."},"3092":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"page","content":"Go SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro"},"3093":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Go SDK, a blocking client speaking the VSR wire protocol over TCP, and which servers it pairs with."},"3094":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"3095":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"3096":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3097":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"3098":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"3099":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"TLS"},"3100":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Delivery semantics"},"3101":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"3102":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#connection-strings-and-metadata","content":"Connection strings and metadata"},"3103":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"3104":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Iggy Go SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Go applications. It is a blocking client that speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol over TCP, with or without TLS. The package is available on pkg.go.dev and the source code lives in foreign/go."},"3105":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"The SDK and the server must speak the same wire protocol. Tagged releases up to `v0.8.0` predate VSR (and the current API) and pair only with servers of the same era. The current SDK speaks VSR only. This page targets server `0.10.0`. Use a compatible SDK release or edge version. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."},"3106":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"The current source requires Go 1.25 or newer. Run these commands from your application module:"},"3107":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"To pin the release that matches server 0.10.0:"},"3108":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"For unreleased changes, use the local SDK replacement shown in the repository examples. A published edge tag need not contain every change in your checkout."},"3109":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are **0-based**: the first partition of a topic is partition `0`."},"3110":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Run the server from the repository root with disposable development data and no root credential overrides:"},"3111":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory**. `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` take precedence over the flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials, including credentials recovered from cluster peers. The snippets use `iggy`/`iggy`. Run the producer before the consumer."},"3112":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"`CreateTopic` accepts `*iggcon.TopicOptions`. A nil value selects server defaults. `CreateStream` accepts `*iggcon.StreamOptions`. `UpdateTopic`, `UpdateStream` and `UpdateUser` accept typed patches. `DescribeOptions` lists the keys and mutability flags."},"3113":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"The consumer reads partition `0` independently and keeps its offset only in memory. Restarting reads from offset `0` again, or the earliest retained message if older messages were removed."},"3114":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Uncompressed payloads and user headers alias the reply buffer, so retaining their slices pins the whole reply. Copy those bytes to retain only the message you need. S2 decompression allocates a separate payload buffer."},"3115":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"Replace the quick-start client constructor with the following TCP options. Start the TLS server as shown on the examples page. The development CA path below is relative to `examples/go`:"},"3116":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"`tcp.WithTLSValidateCertificate` controls certificate validation. The client also accepts `client.WithLogger` for a custom `*slog.Logger` and runs a built-in heartbeat loop (every 5 seconds by default) once connected."},"3117":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"`SendMessages` returns the placements reported by the server. An uncertain mutation retains its frozen request and original identity. Reconnection binds the existing session before attempting a new registration. `ResolveOperation` retries the retained identity, and `AbandonOperation` releases local state without undoing the server result. Keep the same client for recovery. A fresh send after losing receipt access can duplicate work."},"3118":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Crash durability follows the topic's `durability` policy: `replicated` confirms replication, while `persisted` also waits for the required replicas to persist the message data. See Durability. An empty confirmation list is a valid success but does not by itself prove that new messages were appended."},"3119":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"The client exposes `CreateConsumerGroup`, `JoinConsumerGroup`, `LeaveConsumerGroup`, `GetConsumerGroups` and `SyncConsumerGroup`. A one-shot `PollMessages` reads an explicit partition. Use `reader.NewPartitionReader` or `reader.NewIggyConsumer` with `iggcon.NewGroupConsumer` to read the full assignment."},"3120":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"The reader joins, refreshes assignments and leaves on `Close`. Its default wait is five seconds. `Commit` stores a delivered checkpoint after processing. `iggcon.AutoCommitPolicy(iggcon.DefaultAutoCommit())` selects automatic commits after processing. See Deferred polling."},"3121":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#connection-strings-and-metadata","content":"`client.ParseConnectionString` returns TCP options from a connection string. Pass them to `client.WithTcp` when constructing the client. It accepts credentials, TLS and reconnect options. Use `GetMetadataChanges` to read resource or activity changes without listing the entire catalog. Raw requests can target a replica with `SendBinaryRequestToReplica`. See Metadata changes for restart and retention behavior."},"3122":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example with producer, consumer, and TLS flags lives in examples/go. See the examples page for how to run it."},"3123":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","type":"page","content":"Idempotent Producers","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers"},"3124":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","content":"Prevent duplicate appends of a retried producer batch and understand the limits of that guarantee."},"3125":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#rust-producer","content":"Rust producer"},"3126":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#kafka-gateway","content":"Kafka gateway"},"3127":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#retained-producer-history","content":"Retained producer history"},"3128":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#compatibility-and-recovery","content":"Compatibility and recovery"},"3129":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","content":"An idempotent producer lets a partition recognize a batch it already accepted. Each batch carries a producer ID, epoch and sequence. This identity survives a reconnect under a new client session. It is separate from the ID field of an individual message."},"3130":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#rust-producer","content":"The Rust high-level producer supports opt-in idempotence on binary transports:"},"3131":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#rust-producer","content":"The fragment assumes a connected `IggyClient` in an async function. Initialization obtains the producer ID. The producer selects each chunk's partition and sends chunks for that partition in sequence. The default producer does not enable this mode."},"3132":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#rust-producer","content":"Do not treat a message ID as a deduplication key. Recreating a producer or submitting the same business event as a new batch is not the same as retrying the original producer batch. Consumers still need application-level handling for repeated work."},"3133":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#kafka-gateway","content":"Set `IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS=true` on the Kafka gateway to enable its idempotent producer path. The bridge must also be enabled. The default is false. Without this switch, a Kafka producer can obtain an ID but its retries do not receive the batch deduplication guarantee."},"3134":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#kafka-gateway","content":"The gateway does not support transactions. Idempotent appends do not make application processing and offset commits one atomic operation. See Kafka gateway."},"3135":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#retained-producer-history","content":"Each partition retains the last five batches of each producer and at most 4096 producers. A producer idle for 24 hours can expire. A retry outside this retained history can append a duplicate. These limits also apply to the gateway and source connector paths."},"3136":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#compatibility-and-recovery","content":"Upgrade every reader of the topic to a compatible 0.10 client before writing producer-aware batches. The batch format contains additional producer fields. The older SDKs cannot safely decode it."},"3137":{"id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/idempotent-producers#compatibility-and-recovery","content":"Session retry protection and producer idempotence solve different problems. A client can retain an uncertain request and resolve it using its original identity. Ending that session can remove receipt access. An idempotent producer adds batch identity at the partition level, but does not turn an uncertain application operation into a confirmed success."},"3138":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction"},"3139":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"The official Iggy client SDKs, their packages and registries, and the transports each one supports."},"3140":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"3141":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"3142":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"3143":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"Common operations"},"3144":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"Polling strategies"},"3145":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"Partitioning strategies"},"3146":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#read-and-recovery-apis","content":"Read and recovery APIs"},"3147":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"Iggy provides official client SDKs in multiple languages. The **Rust SDK** is the most feature-complete, offering both a high-level API (with auto-batching, consumer groups, offset management, retry logic) and a low-level API for direct protocol operations."},"3148":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package | Registry  | Transport                  | Connection string |\n| -------- | ------- | --------- | -------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| Rust     | iggy    | crates.io | TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket | Yes               |","table":"table-0"},"3149":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package     | Registry | Transport                  | Connection string  |\n| -------- | ----------- | -------- | -------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Python   | apache-iggy | PyPI     | TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket | Yes (PyO3 wrapper) |","table":"table-0"},"3150":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package | Registry      | Transport | Connection string |\n| -------- | ------- | ------------- | --------- | ----------------- |\n| Java     | iggy    | Maven Central | TCP, HTTP | Yes               |","table":"table-0"},"3151":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package     | Registry | Transport | Connection string |\n| -------- | ----------- | -------- | --------- | ----------------- |\n| Node.js  | apache-iggy | npm      | TCP       | Yes               |","table":"table-0"},"3152":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package | Registry   | Transport | Connection string |\n| -------- | ------- | ---------- | --------- | ----------------- |\n| Go       | iggy-go | pkg.go.dev | TCP       | Yes               |","table":"table-0"},"3153":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package     | Registry | Transport | Connection string |\n| -------- | ----------- | -------- | --------- | ----------------- |\n| C#       | Apache.Iggy | NuGet    | TCP, HTTP | Yes               |","table":"table-0"},"3154":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package  | Registry     | Transport                  | Connection string |\n| -------- | -------- | ------------ | -------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| C++      | iggy-cpp | GitHub (WIP) | TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket | Yes (Rust FFI)    |","table":"table-0"},"3155":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package         | Registry        | Transport                  | Connection string |\n| -------- | --------------- | --------------- | -------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| PHP      | apache/iggy-php | GitHub (source) | TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket | Yes (Rust FFI)    |","table":"table-0"},"3156":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"| Language | Package | Registry              | Transport             | Connection string |\n| -------- | ------- | --------------------- | --------------------- | ----------------- |\n| Swift    | Iggy    | Swift Package Manager | TCP with optional TLS | Yes               |","table":"table-0"},"3157":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"Python, C++, and PHP wrap the Rust SDK, so they inherit all transport protocols when using connection strings. Java and C# implement TCP and HTTP natively. Go and Node.js currently support TCP *only*."},"3158":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction","content":"The Rust SDK is always the first to receive new features."},"3159":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"Server 0.10.0 uses binary protocol 0.12.0. Upgrade clients and server together. There is no fallback to the 0.9 protocol. The table lists the component versions released with each server version."},"3160":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component         | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| ----------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| Rust SDK (`iggy`) | `0.12.0`      | `0.11.0`     |","table":"table-1"},"3161":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component        | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| ---------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| CLI (`iggy-cli`) | `0.15.0`      | `0.14.0`     |","table":"table-1"},"3162":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component                  | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| -------------------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| Python SDK (`apache-iggy`) | `0.10.0`      | `0.9.0`      |","table":"table-1"},"3163":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component                         | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| Java SDK (`org.apache.iggy:iggy`) | `0.10.0`      | `0.9.0`      |","table":"table-1"},"3164":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component                   | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| --------------------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| Node.js SDK (`apache-iggy`) | `0.11.0`      | `0.10.0`     |","table":"table-1"},"3165":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component                                    | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| Go SDK (`github.com/apache/iggy/foreign/go`) | `v0.10.0`     | `v0.9.0`     |","table":"table-1"},"3166":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component              | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| C# SDK (`Apache.Iggy`) | `0.10.0`      | `0.9.0`      |","table":"table-1"},"3167":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component | Server 0.10.0 | Server 0.9.0 |\n| --------- | ------------- | ------------ |\n| Swift SDK | `0.2.0`       | Not included |","table":"table-1"},"3168":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component | Server 0.10.0   | Server 0.9.0 |\n| --------- | --------------- | ------------ |\n| C++ SDK   | `0.2.0`, source | Source       |","table":"table-1"},"3169":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"| Component | Server 0.10.0   | Server 0.9.0 |\n| --------- | --------------- | ------------ |\n| PHP SDK   | `0.2.0`, source | Source       |","table":"table-1"},"3170":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#server-compatibility","content":"C++ and PHP are included in the source archive. Swift uses the root Swift Package Manager manifest. See Upgrading before replacing an existing deployment."},"3171":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#prerequisites","content":"The examples below use the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials. For a new local development instance, with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides, use:"},"3172":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#prerequisites","content":"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory**. In a cluster, it can recover stored credentials from another replica. The environment takes precedence over the default-credentials flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. This setup is for *development only*. Use a disposable data directory. See Connection Strings for the authentication details."},"3173":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"Every SDK has connection-string support. Accepted schemes, credentials and options differ by language. Java currently accepts username/password TCP strings with `tls` and `tls_ca_file` only. They provide a compact way to configure the client, including the transport protocol, credentials, server address, and options."},"3174":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"**Format:**"},"3175":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"**Transport selection** via scheme:"},"3176":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"| Scheme    | Transport     |\n| --------- | ------------- |\n| `iggy://` | TCP (default) |","table":"table-2"},"3177":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"| Scheme        | Transport |\n| ------------- | --------- |\n| `iggy+tcp://` | TCP       |","table":"table-2"},"3178":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"| Scheme         | Transport |\n| -------------- | --------- |\n| `iggy+quic://` | QUIC      |","table":"table-2"},"3179":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"| Scheme         | Transport |\n| -------------- | --------- |\n| `iggy+http://` | HTTP      |","table":"table-2"},"3180":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"| Scheme       | Transport |\n| ------------ | --------- |\n| `iggy+ws://` | WebSocket |","table":"table-2"},"3181":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-42","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"**Personal Access Token** authentication: any credential without a colon is treated as a PAT. Server-minted tokens carry no prefix, so paste the token exactly as returned:"},"3182":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-43","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"**Examples:**"},"3183":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-44","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"The Rust snippets assume an async Tokio context. HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Connection-string credentials are applied automatically only by the binary transports."},"3184":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-45","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#connection-string","content":"The option keys differ per transport and unknown keys are rejected as **hard errors**. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables, defaults, and default ports."},"3185":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-46","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"The Rust SDK exposes these operations through traits on `IggyClient`. Availability and method names vary across foreign SDKs and transports:"},"3186":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-47","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**SystemClient** - ping, stats, snapshot"},"3187":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-48","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**StreamClient** - create, get, list, update, delete streams"},"3188":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-49","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**TopicClient** - create, get, list, update, delete topics"},"3189":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-50","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**PartitionClient** - create, delete partitions"},"3190":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-51","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**SegmentClient** - delete segments"},"3191":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-52","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**MessageClient** - send, poll messages"},"3192":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-53","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**ConsumerOffsetClient** - get, store, delete consumer offsets"},"3193":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-54","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**ConsumerGroupClient** - create, get, list, delete consumer groups"},"3194":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-55","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**UserClient** - create, get, list, update, delete users, change password, update permissions"},"3195":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-56","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**PersonalAccessTokenClient** - create, list, delete PATs"},"3196":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-57","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#common-operations","content":"**ClusterClient** - cluster metadata"},"3197":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-58","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"When polling messages, you can choose from several strategies (Rust method names are shown):"},"3198":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-59","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"| Strategy    | Description                  |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `offset(n)` | Start from a specific offset |","table":"table-3"},"3199":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-60","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"| Strategy       | Description                                         |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `timestamp(t)` | Start at or after the given broker append timestamp |","table":"table-3"},"3200":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-61","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"| Strategy  | Description                               |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `first()` | Start from the earliest available message |","table":"table-3"},"3201":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-62","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"| Strategy | Description                                                             |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `last()` | Read the tail ending at the committed offset, up to the requested count |","table":"table-3"},"3202":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-63","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#polling-strategies","content":"| Strategy | Description                                                                   |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `next()` | Continue after the stored consumer offset, or start at zero if none is stored |","table":"table-3"},"3203":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-64","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"When sending messages, you can control partition routing (Rust method names are shown):"},"3204":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-65","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"| Strategy          | Description                  |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `partition_id(n)` | Send to a specific partition |","table":"table-4"},"3205":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-66","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"| Strategy     | Description                   |\n| ------------ | ----------------------------- |\n| `balanced()` | Round-robin across partitions |","table":"table-4"},"3206":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-67","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"| Strategy            | Description               |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `messages_key(key)` | Hash-based routing by key |","table":"table-4"},"3207":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-68","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"The Rust SDK resolves these strategies before sending a binary request. The HTTP API can resolve the strategy on the server. Binary clients route to the selected partition primary."},"3208":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-69","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#read-and-recovery-apis","content":"Use deferred polling to wait for messages. A partition reader groups partitions by primary, and a consumer hands messages to the application one at a time. `PollOptions` defines the count, wait, byte budget and commit policy. Manual commits are the shared default."},"3209":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-70","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#read-and-recovery-apis","content":"Resource creation accepts typed options. Updates use patches that change only supplied fields and can reset supported defaults. See Topic options and Stream options."},"3210":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-71","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#read-and-recovery-apis","content":"SDKs also expose metadata changes and raw requests to a selected replica. Recovery APIs differ. Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution or pending mutation limits. It has abandonment for uncertain committing reads. Read each language guide before implementing retries after an uncertain write."},"3211":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples"},"3212":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable Java examples from the core repository, built with Gradle, and how to start a server for them."},"3213":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"3214":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"3215":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Blocking vs. async"},"3216":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable examples live in the examples/java directory of the core repository as a standalone Gradle project. The project builds against the in-repo SDK (via an `includeBuild` substitution), so the examples always match the SDK source in the same checkout. Java 17 or newer is required. The included `gradlew` wrapper downloads the pinned Gradle version on first run."},"3217":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"These examples target server **0.10.0** and speak the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples log in as `iggy`/`iggy`. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides:"},"3218":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory** (`local_data` by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"3219":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Run each example from the `examples/java` directory with its Gradle task. Run the producer before its consumer. The ordinary consumers keep their cursor in memory and start from offset `0` or the earliest retained message on each run. The multi-tenant consumer uses consumer groups and server-managed offsets."},"3220":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**gettingstarted** - basic blocking producer and consumer, the best starting point."},"3221":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**messageheaders** - message metadata via custom header keys and values, with a `message_type` header selecting the application handler."},"3222":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**messageenvelope** - JSON envelope pattern for polymorphic message handling (order events wrapped in envelopes for type identification)."},"3223":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**multitenant** - multi-tenant isolation: per-tenant streams, users with stream-specific permissions, and concurrent producers and consumers across tenants."},"3224":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**sinkdataproducer** - high-volume data generation (100 batches of 1000 to 1099 messages) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."},"3225":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**streambuilder** - a combined producer and consumer in a single class. It deletes its `test_stream` stream after the run."},"3226":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**async** - the async client: `CompletableFuture` chaining, submitting multiple sends without blocking, backpressure, error recovery with exponential backoff, and thread pool separation."},"3227":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"**tcptls** - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with CA certificate verification. Requires a TLS-enabled server. Run this server command from the repository root:"},"3228":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Return to `examples/java` for the client commands. Their CA path is relative to that directory."},"3229":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Use the blocking client for scripts, CLI tools, integration tests, and anywhere sequential code is easier to reason about. Use the async client for high throughput, reactive applications, and composing non-blocking requests with `CompletableFuture`. The async client runs I/O on Netty event loop threads: never block them with `.join()`, `.get()`, `Thread.sleep()`, or blocking I/O inside `thenApply`/`thenAccept`. Offload blocking work with `thenApplyAsync(fn, executor)`. The examples/java README covers these async patterns in detail."},"3230":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"page","content":"Java SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro"},"3231":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Java SDK, with blocking and CompletableFuture clients over TCP, and which servers they pair with."},"3232":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"3233":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"3234":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Maven"},"3235":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Gradle"},"3236":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3237":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"3238":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"3239":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Client types and configuration"},"3240":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"Deferred reads and recovery"},"3241":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"3242":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Iggy Java SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Java applications. It provides a blocking client and an async client (based on `CompletableFuture`) over TCP, plus a blocking HTTP client. The artifact is published to Maven Central and the source code lives in foreign/java."},"3243":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"This documentation targets server **0.10.0** and SDK **0.10.0**. The TCP transport speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. The Java artifact `0.8.0` uses the older protocol. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."},"3244":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Use Java 17 or newer. The SDK is published to Maven Central."},"3245":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Add the dependency under the `project` element in `pom.xml`:"},"3246":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Check Maven Central for the latest published version. Snapshot builds are available from the ASF snapshot repository."},"3247":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are **0-based**: the first partition of a topic is partition `0`."},"3248":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Start a development server using the example setup, with `iggy`/`iggy` credentials, then run the producer before the consumer. Bootstrap credentials do not replace recovered credentials."},"3249":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"Creation uses `StreamOptions` and `TopicOptions`. Updates use `StreamUpdate`, `TopicUpdate` and `UserUpdate`. Their builders send only named fields and explicit reset keys. Compression and other creation-only options cannot change later."},"3250":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"The consumer reads partition `0` independently and stops on an empty poll. Its cursor starts at offset `0` or the earliest retained message each time it runs. `autoCommit` is disabled, so this sample does not store its progress on the server."},"3251":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"`org.apache.iggy.Iggy` is the unified entrypoint for building clients. `Iggy.tcpClientBuilder().blocking()` returns the same builder as `IggyTcpClient.builder()` used above."},"3252":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Close clients when finished: `client.close()`, `asyncClient.close().join()`, and `httpClient.close()`. The HTTP close method declares `IOException`."},"3253":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"The TCP builder also accepts TLS and resilience options. Start the TLS development server and run this fragment with `examples/java` as the working directory:"},"3254":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Beyond streams, topics, and messages, the client exposes consumer groups, partitions, users, and personal access tokens through `client.consumerGroups()`, `client.partitions()`, `client.users()`, and `client.personalAccessTokens()`. The SDK's custom exception types inherit from `IggyException`. Failed futures can wrap them in `CompletionException` when joined."},"3255":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"`PollOptions.waitFor(Duration.ofSeconds(5))` enables deferred polling. `CommitPolicy.manual()` is the default. A reader or consumer also accepts `CommitPolicy.auto(...)`. One-shot polls reject automatic policies. The async client exposes a partition reader through `client.reader(...)`. A blocking wrapper is available as `org.apache.iggy.client.blocking.reader.PartitionReader`."},"3256":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"TCP retains uncertain mutation identities. Keep the client and use `resolveOperation` or `abandonOperation` when an exception reports a retained operation. An uncertain committing poll blocks progress for its partition until abandonment. HTTP supports standalone consumers and deferred reads, but not consumer group membership."},"3257":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"The TCP builders accept `fromConnectionString`. Only `iggy://` and `iggy+tcp://`, username/password credentials, `tls` and `tls_ca_file` are supported. PAT connection strings and additional options are rejected. The builder also exposes shutdown quiet-period configuration for applications that need prompt close."},"3258":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"`getMetadataChanges` follows metadata history, and raw requests can target a named replica. See Metadata changes and Client failover."},"3259":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Runnable examples covering the async client, message headers and envelopes, multi-tenant setups, and TLS live in examples/java. See the examples page for how to run them."},"3260":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","type":"page","content":"Metadata Changes","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes"},"3261":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","content":"Follow resource and activity changes, retain a position and rebuild after history expires."},"3262":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"Select a starting point"},"3263":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"Handle each response"},"3264":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"SDK methods"},"3265":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#retention-and-node-freshness","content":"Retention and node freshness"},"3266":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","content":"The metadata change log records public results of committed metadata operations. It includes resource changes, permissions, sessions and consumer group membership. Reading it requires root or the global `manage_servers` permission."},"3267":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"| Start    | Use                              |\n| -------- | -------------------------------- |\n| Earliest | Read the oldest retained changes |","table":"table-0"},"3268":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"| Start  | Use                                     |\n| ------ | --------------------------------------- |\n| Latest | Establish a position at the current end |","table":"table-0"},"3269":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"| Start | Use                           |\n| ----- | ----------------------------- |\n| After | Continue after a saved cursor |","table":"table-0"},"3270":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"| Start   | Use                                                  |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| Current | Read pages of current state before following changes |","table":"table-0"},"3271":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"A cursor identifies a position in this log. Save the returned `next` value after applying the corresponding records. Cursor text starts with `mc1_`. Current-state page tokens start with `cs1_`. Treat both as opaque values and use the SDK parsers."},"3272":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#select-a-starting-point","content":"Queries can select resource or activity groups. Current-state queries must include parent groups, such as streams when topics are selected. Limits range from 1 through 1000. Time bounds are not valid with Latest or Current starts."},"3273":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"| Status | Action                                                        |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Ok     | Apply records or current items and save the returned position |","table":"table-1"},"3274":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"| Status | Action                                        |\n| ------ | --------------------------------------------- |\n| Behind | Wait and retry without advancing the position |","table":"table-1"},"3275":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"| Status  | Action                                                      |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Expired | Rebuild because the requested history is no longer retained |","table":"table-1"},"3276":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"| Status | Action                                                    |\n| ------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Reset  | Rebuild because the cursor belongs to a different history |","table":"table-1"},"3277":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"A rebuild reads Current pages, then follows changes from the returned anchor. Keep the rebuilt state private until a change page reports no more records and reaches at least the last current page's `head_op`. A sequence of current pages alone does not establish that the mirror caught up."},"3278":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#handle-each-response","content":"Unknown record kinds retain their codes and raw bodies. Stop without advancing the cursor when the application cannot apply a record. Skipping it can leave a mirror inconsistent. Key material is never exposed in stream records. Their public options contain only the key source and fingerprints."},"3279":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK  | Method                 |\n| ---- | ---------------------- |\n| Rust | `get_metadata_changes` |","table":"table-2"},"3280":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK    | Method                 |\n| ------ | ---------------------- |\n| Python | `get_metadata_changes` |","table":"table-2"},"3281":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK     | Method                      |\n| ------- | --------------------------- |\n| Node.js | `system.getMetadataChanges` |","table":"table-2"},"3282":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK | Method               |\n| --- | -------------------- |\n| Go  | `GetMetadataChanges` |","table":"table-2"},"3283":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK  | Method                                    |\n| ---- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| Java | `getMetadataChanges` on the system client |","table":"table-2"},"3284":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK | Method                    |\n| --- | ------------------------- |\n| C#  | `GetMetadataChangesAsync` |","table":"table-2"},"3285":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK | Method               |\n| --- | -------------------- |\n| C++ | `GetMetadataChanges` |","table":"table-2"},"3286":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK | Method               |\n| --- | -------------------- |\n| PHP | `getMetadataChanges` |","table":"table-2"},"3287":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"| SDK   | Method               |\n| ----- | -------------------- |\n| Swift | `getMetadataChanges` |","table":"table-2"},"3288":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#sdk-methods","content":"The Python implementation example shows follow and rebuild loops. The other SDK source guides show their equivalent typed queries."},"3289":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#retention-and-node-freshness","content":"`metadata.change_log_resource_capacity` defaults to 65536 records. `metadata.change_log_activity_capacity` defaults to 16384 records. Each budget also limits record bodies to 128 MiB. A busy activity log does not evict resource history. Nodes can retain different depths."},"3290":{"id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/metadata-changes#retention-and-node-freshness","content":"A node can lag behind the metadata primary. A cursor that is valid elsewhere can receive Behind from that node. Wait or use a node that has caught up. Expired and Reset require rebuilding, not a loop over the same stale position."},"3291":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples"},"3292":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Build the local Node.js SDK and run its TypeScript examples."},"3293":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"3294":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. They use the SDK from the same checkout:"},"3295":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**getting-started** - basic producer and consumer"},"3296":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**basic** - producer and consumer with utilities"},"3297":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**message-envelope** - JSON message envelope pattern"},"3298":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**message-headers** - message-type dispatch using a JSON wrapper in the payload"},"3299":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**multi-tenant** - separate streams for tenants, using one client"},"3300":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**tcp-tls** - TLS-encrypted TCP connections"},"3301":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**stream-builder** - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"},"3302":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"**sink-data-producer** - bulk data generation for sink connectors"},"3303":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These examples target server **0.10.0**. See Getting started for prerequisites:"},"3304":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The default-root flag sets the `iggy`/`iggy` credentials used by the examples. `--fresh` deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables override the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials."},"3305":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"From the repository root, build the local SDK before installing the examples. The stream-builder example creates a stream and topic, sends and consumes three messages, and deletes its resources:"},"3306":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Each example has a script in `examples/node/package.json`. Set `DEBUG=iggy:examples*` to see its progress."},"3307":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The separate producer and consumer scripts do not consistently share resources: the envelope and headers consumers create new empty topics, and the tenant producer deletes its streams before exiting. The getting-started and basic producers spread batches over five partitions, while their consumers poll partition 0. Use the self-contained stream-builder command above for a complete send-and-consume walkthrough. The sink-data producer deletes its selected topic and stream when it finishes, including resources that already existed."},"3308":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The TCP/TLS pair needs a TLS-enabled source server. Run this command from the repository root, then the two `test:tcp-tls:*` scripts from `examples/node`, where they load `../../core/certs/iggy_ca_cert.pem`:"},"3309":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These are repository development certificates."},"3310":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Node.js SDK intro."},"3311":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"page","content":"Node.js SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro"},"3312":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Node.js and TypeScript SDK, from installation to keeping client and server versions in step."},"3313":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"3314":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3315":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"3316":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"3317":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"Consumer group"},"3318":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"Running the samples again"},"3319":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"3320":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"3321":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"3322":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Iggy Node.js SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Node.js and TypeScript applications. It communicates with the Iggy server over TCP or TLS using the binary protocol. The package is available on npm and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"3323":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"These docs target server **0.10.0** and Node SDK **0.11.0**. The Node package 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. The current SDK speaks only the VSR wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats."},"3324":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below expect an Iggy server on `127.0.0.1:8090`. See Getting started for the server prerequisites:"},"3325":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and set the root credentials the samples log in with. Without `--with-default-root-credentials` (or the `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` / `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` variables), a fresh server refuses to start. For source runs, `--fresh` deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables take precedence over the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace credentials already stored in recovered data."},"3326":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Save the following snippets as `producer.mjs` and `consumer.mjs` in the project where you installed the SDK. Run `node producer.mjs` before `node consumer.mjs`. A consumer group sample follows them."},"3327":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"The client resolves the target partition and routes requests to its primary. A primary can differ from the metadata leader."},"3328":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"The poll response does not wait for the auto-commit to finish. The sample processes messages after polling, so auto-commit does not confirm that the application handled each message."},"3329":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"A consumer group distributes partitions among its members. The high-level consumer creates and joins the group, then reads its assigned partitions. Save this as `consumer-group.mjs`:"},"3330":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"This loop waits for messages until it is stopped. It stores checkpoints after printing. The group has its own offsets, separate from a standalone consumer."},"3331":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"All three samples can be run again without changes:"},"3332":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"`node producer.mjs` finds the existing stream and topic and appends ten more messages."},"3333":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"`node consumer.mjs` prints only the new messages, because `PollingStrategy.Next` continues after the stored offset. With nothing new to read, it prints nothing."},"3334":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"`node consumer-group.mjs` does the same from the group's stored offset."},"3335":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"To start again with an empty server, stop the container and run the `docker run` command again. `--rm` removes the container and its data."},"3336":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"The client constructor accepts a few options:"},"3337":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"**reconnect**: automatic reconnection with `{ enabled, interval, maxRetries }`."},"3338":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"**heartbeatInterval**: the client pings the server every 5 seconds by default. `0` disables heartbeats. When server heartbeat eviction is enabled, silent consumer-group members can be evicted, so keep it well below the server's heartbeat timeout."},"3339":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"**maxResponseFrameSize**: response frames larger than this limit (default 64 MiB) are rejected and close the connection. Raise it when polling very large batches."},"3340":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"**poolSize**: the client currently supports exactly one pooled connection. `min`/`max` above 1 throw."},"3341":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"**TLS**: set `transport: 'TLS'` and pass Node `tls.ConnectionOptions` in `options`. See the tcp-tls example."},"3342":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Consumer groups**: `client.group` manages groups (including ensure-and-join in one call), and `client.message.reader` or `client.consumerGroup` reads the assigned partitions. A one-shot poll names one partition."},"3343":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Consumer streams**: A high-level consumer supports async iteration, handler-based consumption and `toReadable()`."},"3344":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Administration**: users, personal access tokens, offsets, partitions, segments, and cluster metadata are all available on the client."},"3345":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Errors**: the package exports `ResponseError`, `DeserializeError`, `ProtocolFrameError`, and `VsrEvictionError` for precise error handling."},"3346":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"See the foreign/node README for the full API walkthrough."},"3347":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"3348":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"page","content":"PHP SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","PHP"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro"},"3349":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The experimental PHP SDK, a native extension wrapping the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source."},"3350":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Requirements"},"3351":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Build and install"},"3352":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3353":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"3354":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"API notes"},"3355":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"Resource options and recovery"},"3356":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"3357":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The Iggy PHP SDK is a native PHP extension built in Rust with ext-php-rs. It wraps the Rust SDK and exposes a synchronous `Iggy\\Client`: each call drives the extension's internal Tokio runtime and blocks the calling PHP thread until the operation completes. The source code is available on GitHub."},"3358":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The extension is experimental. It's defined as the `apache/iggy-php` composer package (`php-ext` type). To use it today, build it from the repository source."},"3359":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Rust and Cargo"},"3360":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"PHP 8.3 or newer with `php-config` (non-thread-safe builds only, no ZTS)"},"3361":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"`cargo-php`"},"3362":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Alternatively, skip the install and point PHP at the built library per invocation:"},"3363":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"On macOS the library is `libiggy_php.dylib`."},"3364":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The sample below targets Iggy 0.10.0 on `127.0.0.1:8090`. Start the server:"},"3365":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and bootstrap the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials for a new data directory. Existing stored credentials are not replaced. For the source command, ensure `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` are unset or match the sample. Environment values take precedence over the flag. `--fresh` removes the local replica state, so use it only for disposable development data."},"3366":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"For binary transports, credentials in the connection string take effect on `connect()`. HTTP requires `loginUser()` explicitly. Alternatively, construct the client with a plain address (`new \\Iggy\\Client('127.0.0.1:8090')`) and call `loginUser('iggy', 'iggy')` explicitly."},"3367":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"`consumerGroup()` creates and joins a group by default. It reads assigned partitions and stores no offsets unless you select a policy or commit checkpoints. This example processes five messages:"},"3368":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"`consumeMessages()` requires a finite limit. A callback exception ends the run. With automatic commits after processing, the failed message stays uncounted. `PollOptions` carries the commit policy. Use `CommitPolicy::manual()`, `CommitPolicy::onRead()` or `CommitPolicy::auto(...)`."},"3369":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"`reader()` reads batches from explicit partitions. `groupReader()` reads an existing group's assignment. Close a reader with `close()`. A timeout on `next(timeout_micros)` returns null and keeps a later batch available for the next call."},"3370":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Methods are camelCase and classes live in the `Iggy` namespace, for example `Iggy\\Client` and `Iggy\\SendMessage`."},"3371":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Partition ids are 0-based: for a topic with one partition, use `0`."},"3372":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PHP strings are passed as named identifiers, including strings that contain only digits. PHP integers are passed as numeric identifiers."},"3373":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"`PollingStrategy::timestamp()` and `timestampMicros()` expect microseconds since the Unix epoch. Use `PollingStrategy::timestampSeconds()` for PHP `time()` values."},"3374":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Message IDs and checksums are returned as decimal strings. Offset and timestamp getters return PHP integers and are limited to `PHP_INT_MAX`."},"3375":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Errors are thrown as exceptions under `Iggy\\Exception`: `AuthenticationException`, `ConnectionException`, `NotFoundException`, `TransientException`, and the `IggyException` base."},"3376":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"TLS uses the Rust SDK connection-string format, for example `iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port?tls=true&tls_domain=localhost&tls_ca_file=/path/to/ca.pem`."},"3377":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"The extension owns a lazy global Tokio runtime. Don't call `pcntl_fork()` after the first Iggy call: the child inherits file descriptors but not the runtime's worker threads."},"3378":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"`createStream($name, $encryption_key)` accepts the standard padded base64 of a 32-byte stream key. `createTopic` accepts named creation arguments, including `replication_factor`. Stream and topic updates change only named fields and reset keys. `describeOptions()` lists each catalog. User and PAT administration are not exposed as typed PHP methods in this release. Reads expose encryption fingerprints, never key material."},"3379":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"`getMetadataChanges()` follows the metadata log. `sendBinaryRequestToReplica()` sends supported non-replicated commands to a selected node. See Metadata changes."},"3380":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"Retained mutation errors expose an operation ID. Use `resolveOperation()` on the same client to retry its retained identity, or `abandonOperation()` to accept the unknown result and release local state. A lost committing poll throws `FetchProgressUncertain` and blocks progress for the partition until abandonment."},"3381":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/php directory. They read `IGGY_CONNECTION_STRING` for a binary transport with credentials. Otherwise they connect over TCP using `IGGY_HOST` and `IGGY_PORT`, then log in with the literal `IGGY_USERNAME` and `IGGY_PASSWORD` values:"},"3382":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples"},"3383":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working Python examples from the core repository, covering connection strings, message headers and TLS."},"3384":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"3385":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. CI runs the source examples against a source-built server, including a separate TLS pass:"},"3386":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"**getting-started** - producer and consumer with optional TLS via `TcpConfig`"},"3387":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"**basic** - producer and consumer using connection strings"},"3388":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"**message-headers** - user headers with `str`, `bytes`, `bool`, `int`, and `float` values, in two variants: `plain-headers` (plain `dict`, the SDK infers wire types) and `typed-headers` (explicit `HeaderKey`/`HeaderValue`)"},"3389":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These examples target Iggy 0.10.0. Start the server:"},"3390":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and bootstrap the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced, and environment credentials override the source command's default-credentials flag. Use `--fresh` only with disposable local replica data."},"3391":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"With Python 3.11 or newer and Rust/Cargo available, run from `examples/python`. `uv` uses the local SDK path in `pyproject.toml`. The pip command below names that path explicitly:"},"3392":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Python SDK intro."},"3393":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"page","content":"Python SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro"},"3394":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Python SDK, a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, and how to install it."},"3395":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"3396":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3397":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"3398":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"3399":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"3400":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"3401":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Iggy Python SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Python application. It is built as a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, which means it supports TCP, QUIC, HTTP, and WebSocket transports via connection strings. The package is available on PyPI and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"3402":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The wheel bundles the Rust SDK, whose binary transports speak the current Iggy wire protocol without falling back to older formats. Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build the SDK and server from the same source checkout. The published wheel can lag source API changes."},"3403":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Python 3.11 or newer is required. Install the published package in a virtual environment:"},"3404":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"For the source version described here, install from the repository root with Rust and Cargo available:"},"3405":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below target Iggy 0.10.0 on `127.0.0.1:8090`:"},"3406":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and bootstrap the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced. Environment credentials override the default-credentials flag, so unset them or set them to match the sample. `--fresh` deletes local replica state. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."},"3407":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**TLS**: use `TcpConfig` with `tls_enabled` and `tls_ca_file`, or the TLS options in a connection string. The certificate must match `tls_domain`, which defaults to the dialed host. The getting-started example shows the full setup."},"3408":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Consumer groups**: `await client.consumer_group(...)` returns an `IggyConsumer` that creates and joins the group by default and commits offsets according to the `commit` policy in `poll_options`, which defaults to manual commits. Groups require a binary transport. HTTP supports ordinary consumers only."},"3409":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**User headers**: `SendMessage(data, user_headers=...)` accepts a plain `dict` with `str`, `bytes`, `bool`, `int`, or `float` values. `HeaderKey`/`HeaderValue` give explicit control over the wire type. See the message-headers examples."},"3410":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Topic options**: `create_topic(..., options=TopicOptions(...))` accepts typed options. `TopicOptions.raw` accepts extra keys as `dict[str, str]`, validated by the server. `client.describe_options(\"topic\")` lists the keys a server accepts."},"3411":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"**Administration**: user and permission management. Personal access tokens can be used for login via `AutoLogin.personal_access_token(...)` in the connection config. PAT management isn't exposed yet."},"3412":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"3413":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples"},"3414":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The Rust SDK examples in the core repository, and how to start a server to run them against."},"3415":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"In the core repository, you can find the following examples using the Rust SDK."},"3416":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. Start the server from the repository root in a separate terminal. The examples use `iggy`/`iggy`. For a new local instance with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides:"},"3417":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. `--fresh` deletes this replica's local data. Use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password. A new cluster requires explicit credentials."},"3418":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The examples are cargo example targets of the `iggy_examples` crate. Run them from the **repository root** with `cargo run --example <name>` so relative certificate paths resolve. Run each producer before its consumer. Some examples create fixed names and require fresh data when repeated. The getting-started consumer also expects stream and topic IDs `0`, so run that pair first on a fresh server. Partition IDs are **0-based**, but balanced producers and consumer groups can use multiple partitions."},"3419":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Getting started** - the basic example which is discussed in the getting started guide."},"3420":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Basic** - producer and consumer using the low-level `Client` trait with CLI args for transport selection (TCP/QUIC/HTTP/WebSocket)."},"3421":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**New SDK** - an introduction to the high-level SDK as discussed in the dedicated guide."},"3422":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Stream builder** - examples using the `IggyStream`, `IggyStreamProducer`, and `IggyStreamConsumer` builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."},"3423":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Message envelope** - the example of how to send a message with a custom envelope e.g. to differentiate between different types of messages."},"3424":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Message headers** - the example of how to send a message with custom headers (typed headers, message type discrimination, client-side compression)."},"3425":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Multi-tenant** - the larger example of how to structure your application to support multiple tenants (separated by the unique streams) with the different users, permissions, topics etc."},"3426":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**TCP TLS** - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with custom CA certificates. Stop the plain server and start a TLS-enabled server from the repository root:"},"3427":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"These certificates are for local testing. Then run:"},"3428":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"**Sink data producer** - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."},"3429":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"page","content":"High-level SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk"},"3430":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"IggyProducer and IggyConsumer: batching, consumer groups and offset commits without the boilerplate."},"3431":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"3432":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Producer"},"3433":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Background producer configuration"},"3434":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Send confirmations"},"3435":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"3436":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Consumer configuration"},"3437":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"Automatic commits"},"3438":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#read-errors","content":"Read errors"},"3439":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#idempotent-producer","content":"Idempotent producer"},"3440":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"Raw requests"},"3441":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"`IggyProducer` batches writes and `IggyConsumer` reads messages through a partition reader. Both share the client's routing, session and recovery behavior. The consumer manages group membership and tracks checkpoints. Select when it stores those checkpoints through `PollOptions`."},"3442":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"The snippets assume an async function with a `Result` return type and the `iggy`, Tokio and `futures-util` dependencies. Use the Rust SDK setup and a development server with the sample credentials."},"3443":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Instead of providing the configuration for the client, you can use the connection string. It's a string that contains all the necessary information to connect to the Iggy API, and it works with all four transports:"},"3444":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Which can be used to create the client like this:"},"3445":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Consumer groups require a binary transport. Use an ordinary consumer with an explicit partition over HTTP."},"3446":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Options are appended as query parameters. For TCP:"},"3447":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The option keys differ per transport (for example QUIC uses `reconnection_max_retries` and `reconnection_reestablish_after` where TCP uses `reconnection_retries` and `reestablish_after`). Unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"3448":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Unless you need client-side encryption, a custom `Partitioner`, or other settings outside the connection-string options, you should be good to go with the connection string. On the other hand, you can always make use of `IggyClientBuilder::from_connection_string()` to extend the options on top of the provided connection string."},"3449":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The `IggyClient` internally wraps its transport in an `IggyRwLock`, but the client itself does not implement `Clone`. Share an `Arc<IggyClient>` across tasks. Cloning the `Arc` keeps the same client and connection. Typically, it's a good idea to create a separate connection for producing and consuming, but it's not a requirement."},"3450":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The producer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to send messages to the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke `client.producer()` to get the `IggyProducerBuilder` allowing you to configure the producer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"},"3451":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The direct producer splits each `send()` call into requests of at most 1000 messages. For sequential calls, the 5 ms linger setting delays a call until at least 5 ms after the previous successful send. It does not buffer messages between calls or delay individual chunks within a call. Choose `background` to buffer sends on worker tasks. `balanced` routes requests round-robin across partitions. By default, `init()` creates a missing stream and topic, requiring the corresponding permissions."},"3452":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Finally, you can use the `send()` method to send the messages to the topic. The producer **never needs to be a mutable binding**: both `init()` and `send()` take `&self`, so a plain `let producer` is enough for its whole life. Here's how you can send the messages:"},"3453":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Of course, you can provide the message headers, custom binary serialization etc. as it's the same `IggyMessage` type as the one used in the low-level API (use `IggyMessage::builder()` to set headers or a custom ID). The producer will take care of the rest, including the retries, partitioning, batching etc."},"3454":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Besides `send()`, the producer also offers `send_one(message)`, `send_with_partitioning(messages, partitioning)` to override the partitioning per call, and `send_to(stream, topic, messages, partitioning)` to target a different stream and topic."},"3455":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"The `background` mode buffers messages and sends them from shard workers, trading latency for throughput and backpressure control. `BackgroundConfig::builder()` exposes:"},"3456":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field        | Default | Description                                 |\n| ------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `num_shards` | `1`     | Number of shard workers running in parallel |","table":"table-0"},"3457":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field      | Default           | Description                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sharding` | `OrderedSharding` | Routes each send to a worker. `OrderedSharding` keeps a stream/topic pair on one sequential worker. `BalancedSharding` round-robins across workers for maximum throughput and can reorder sends |","table":"table-0"},"3458":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field         | Default | Description                                                   |\n| ------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `linger_time` | `1ms`   | How long a shard may wait before flushing an incomplete batch |","table":"table-0"},"3459":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field        | Default | Description                                                    |\n| ------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `batch_size` | 1 MiB   | Per-worker flush threshold in bytes. `0` disables this trigger |","table":"table-0"},"3460":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field          | Default | Description                                                                                        |\n| -------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `batch_length` | `1000`  | Per-worker flush threshold in **queued sends**, not individual messages. `0` disables this trigger |","table":"table-0"},"3461":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field          | Default | Description                                                                                             |\n| -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `failure_mode` | `Block` | Behavior when the byte budget is exhausted: `Block`, `BlockWithTimeout(duration)`, or `FailImmediately` |","table":"table-0"},"3462":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field             | Default | Description                                                                                 |\n| ----------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_buffer_size` | 32 MiB  | Budget for message bytes buffered or in flight across all workers. `0` disables this budget |","table":"table-0"},"3463":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field           | Default | Description                                                                                                         |\n| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_in_flight` | `1`     | Maximum concurrent writes across workers. Each worker remains sequential. `0` uses the semaphore's maximum capacity |","table":"table-0"},"3464":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"| Field            | Default            | Description                                                       |\n| ---------------- | ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `error_callback` | `LogErrorCallback` | Async callback invoked on errors the producer cannot recover from |","table":"table-0"},"3465":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"`batch_size` and `batch_length` are flush triggers checked after adding a send. The final send can take the buffer past the byte threshold. The linger deadline starts when an empty buffer receives a send. The first trigger reached causes a flush. Each worker also has a bounded queue, which can make dispatch wait independently of `failure_mode`. A send larger than the entire byte budget fails under every backpressure mode. Nonzero `max_buffer_size` and `max_in_flight` values must not exceed `tokio::sync::Semaphore::MAX_PERMITS`. Constructing the dispatcher with a larger value panics."},"3466":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Stop all senders, then call `producer.shutdown().await` to drain background queues and wait for writes and error callbacks. Dropping the producer without shutdown can lose buffered messages. Shutdown does not turn failed writes into successful ones. The default error callback logs and drops failed sends."},"3467":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Every send method returns a `SendMessagesResponse` carrying the commit confirmations of the chunks the send was split into. The list is **empty** whenever the server sends no confirmation payload, and always for a `background` producer, which hands the messages to a dispatcher and returns before the send happens. Branch on `confirmations.is_empty()` instead of indexing. A retried chunk may already have been committed by an earlier attempt, so a confirmation offset does not imply uniqueness. Direct-send completion follows the topic's durability policy."},"3468":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"A consumer group reads its assigned partitions. A standalone consumer requires an explicit `.partitions(...)` list. The builder uses `PollingStrategy::next()`, a five-second server wait, up to 1000 messages and a 1 MiB reply. It stores no offsets by default."},"3469":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Import `futures_util::StreamExt` to use `next()`. The mutable consumer drives reads and retries when polled. A checkpoint keeps the partition history and ownership that delivered the message. Committing it after recreation or an ownership change is refused. Process messages before committing their checkpoints."},"3470":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method              | Default                         | Behavior                      |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `poll_options(...)` | Five-second wait, manual commit | Read limits and commit policy |","table":"table-1"},"3471":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method            | Default                          | Behavior                                    |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `partitions(...)` | Required for standalone consumer | Explicit partition IDs. Refused for a group |","table":"table-1"},"3472":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-42","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method                | Default | Behavior                                                  |\n| --------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `prefetch_bytes(...)` | 16 MiB  | Encoded bytes reserved for buffered and in-flight replies |","table":"table-1"},"3473":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-43","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method                   | Default                         | Behavior          |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `prefetch_messages(...)` | 16000, or the larger poll count | Reserved messages |","table":"table-1"},"3474":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-44","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method                  | Default  | Behavior                                |\n| ----------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `polling_strategy(...)` | `next()` | Start of a partition without a position |","table":"table-1"},"3475":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-45","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method                                  | Default | Behavior                        |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `create_consumer_group_if_not_exists()` | Enabled | Create the group before joining |","table":"table-1"},"3476":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-46","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method                        | Default    | Behavior                   |\n| ----------------------------- | ---------- | -------------------------- |\n| `polling_retry_interval(...)` | One second | Delay after a refused read |","table":"table-1"},"3477":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-47","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method              | Default  | Behavior                                            |\n| ------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `init_retries(...)` | Disabled | Retry missing stream or topic during initialization |","table":"table-1"},"3478":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-48","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method           | Default  | Behavior                                 |\n| ---------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `allow_replay()` | Disabled | Permit reads at or below stored progress |","table":"table-1"},"3479":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-49","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"| Method                      | Default      | Behavior                             |\n| --------------------------- | ------------ | ------------------------------------ |\n| `offset_drain_timeout(...)` | Five seconds | Shutdown budget for automatic stores |","table":"table-1"},"3480":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-50","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Group membership is managed by the reader. The old `auto_join_consumer_group`, `poll_interval`, `batch_length` and enum-based auto-commit consumer configuration are replaced by the current reader and `PollOptions` API."},"3481":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-51","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"To store progress after successful processing, use:"},"3482":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-52","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"The default automatic policy stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds, after processing. `CommitAfter::Delivery` counts a yielded message immediately. `CommitAfter::Marked` counts checkpoints that the application marks. A handler failure leaves its message uncounted when using processing-based commits."},"3483":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-53","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"`CommitPolicy::OnRead` submits offsets with the read and can advance them before processing. The consumer refuses this policy with a client-side encryptor because decryption can fail after the store. Use manual or automatic commits instead."},"3484":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-54","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"Always await `consumer.shutdown()` before dropping it. Shutdown flushes automatic progress, adjusts on-read progress to delivered messages, and leaves the group. It is not a substitute for successful application processing. See Deferred polling for the shared contract."},"3485":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-55","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#read-errors","content":"`ReadError` identifies the failed partition and contains its underlying error. Recoverable read failures are retried. A history change, uncertain read or undecodable message can stop a partition. Inspect the error before calling `seek(partition_id, strategy)` to select a new start. A `skip` continuation lets an application deliberately move past a record it cannot decode."},"3486":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-56","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#read-errors","content":"An uncertain committing read can require `abandon_operation` before seeking. Abandon does not undo a store. The source consumer example shows explicit recovery choices."},"3487":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-57","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#idempotent-producer","content":"An idempotent producer prevents duplicate appends when the same producer batch is retried. Enable it with `.idempotent()` before `build()` and call `init()` to obtain a producer ID. The default is disabled, and this mode requires a binary transport."},"3488":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-58","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#idempotent-producer","content":"Each chunk carries a producer ID, epoch and per-partition sequence. This is separate from message IDs and session retry protection. Upgrade all topic readers to compatible 0.10 clients before writing these batches. See Idempotent producers for limits."},"3489":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-59","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"`send_binary_request(code, payload)` sends a raw request through a binary transport. `send_binary_request_to_replica(code, payload, replica_id)` targets a replica. Session-management commands remain restricted so a raw call cannot replace the client's session state. HTTP returns `FeatureUnavailable` for binary calls."},"3490":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-60","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"`send_http_request(method, path, body)` sends an HTTP request and returns its raw body. Binary transports return `FeatureUnavailable` for that call. Use typed APIs for commands whose route, ownership or recovery identity the SDK must manage."},"3491":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"page","content":"Intro","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro"},"3492":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"The Rust SDK, the primary and most complete Iggy client, and where its source lives."},"3493":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"3494":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"High-level vs low-level API"},"3495":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"3496":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"Quick example"},"3497":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"The Rust SDK is the primary and most feature-complete client for Iggy. It is available on crates.io and the source code is part of the core repository."},"3498":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"Run from your application crate to add the published SDK:"},"3499":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build both from the same checkout. The Getting Started guide shows the source dependency setup."},"3500":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"The examples below log in with `iggy`/`iggy`. For a new local development instance, with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides, use:"},"3501":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory**. Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. In a cluster, a fresh replica can recover credentials from peers. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."},"3502":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"The SDK provides two layers. The snippets below assume an async Tokio context with a `Result` return type."},"3503":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"**High-level API (recommended)** - the easiest way to get started. It handles connection management, auto-batching, consumer group lifecycle, offset commits, retry logic, and reconnection out of the box. Use `IggyClient`, `IggyProducer`, and `IggyConsumer` for the best developer experience."},"3504":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"**Low-level API** - direct access to individual protocol operations. Useful when you need fine-grained control over every request, or when building custom abstractions on top."},"3505":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"The high-level API is built on top of the low-level one, so you can always drop down when needed."},"3506":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"The connection string is the simplest way to configure the client. It supports all four transport protocols:"},"3507":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials on `connect()`. HTTP requires an explicit login and supports ordinary consumers only."},"3508":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Options can be appended as query parameters:"},"3509":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Personal Access Tokens are also supported. Any credential without a colon is treated as a PAT. Paste the token exactly as the server returned it, with **no prefix**:"},"3510":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"3511":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"Partition IDs are **0-based**: the first partition of a topic is partition `0`. The consumer uses the default consumer identity and requests auto-commit. Its offset can advance before application processing finishes. Use explicit offset storage after successful processing when that distinction matters."},"3512":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"For the full getting started tutorial, see Getting Started. For the high-level producer/consumer builders, see High-level SDK and Stream Builder."},"3513":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"page","content":"Stream Builder","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder"},"3514":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"Configure a Rust producer and consumer by stream and topic names."},"3515":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"Build a producer"},"3516":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"Build a consumer"},"3517":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#share-an-existing-client","content":"Share an existing client"},"3518":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"Defaults and buffering"},"3519":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The stream builder creates an `IggyProducer`, an `IggyConsumer`, or both from configuration objects. It uses the same APIs described in High-level SDK. Stream and topic names identify the resources. The 0.10 API does not take stream or topic IDs in these configuration objects."},"3520":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"This complete example uses a local server with the development credentials. The producer creates missing resources during initialization. Existing topics keep their configuration."},"3521":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"`batch_length` limits messages in a direct request. `linger_time` sets the minimum gap between sequential direct sends. It does not buffer separate calls. Use the high-level producer's background mode when you need a buffer."},"3522":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"`with_topic_durability(Durability::Persisted)` selects the policy when the builder creates a topic. It does not change an existing topic. Client-side `encryptor` configuration is separate from stream encryption."},"3523":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"The consumer uses a group by default. This example creates the group, reads messages and commits each checkpoint after printing its payload. Add `futures-util` to use `StreamExt`."},"3524":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"A standalone consumer uses `ConsumerKind::Consumer`. Select its partitions with `with_partitions`. The default configuration selects partition zero. Group consumers use the server assignment instead. `ExternalGroup` represents externally managed offsets and cannot poll."},"3525":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"`PollOptions` defaults to manual commits. `CommitPolicy::Auto(AutoCommit::default())` stores progress after processing every 10,000 messages or five seconds. `CommitPolicy::OnRead` stores with the read, before application processing. Read the commit contract before selecting a policy."},"3526":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#share-an-existing-client","content":"`IggyStreamProducer::build(&client, &producer_config)` and `IggyStreamConsumer::build(&client, &consumer_config)` reuse a connected client. To build both, combine the configuration:"},"3527":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#share-an-existing-client","content":"`IggyStream::with_client_from_connection_string` also creates the client. Its result contains the client, producer and consumer. Resource creation flags remain explicit on the consumer configuration."},"3528":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"`IggyConsumerConfig::default()` names `test_stream`, `test_topic` and `test_consumer`. It uses a group, `next()`, a five-second deferred wait and manual commits. It retries initialization five times at three-second intervals. It does not create a missing stream or topic by default."},"3529":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"The reader reserves up to 16 MiB and 16000 messages for buffered and in-flight replies. If the poll count is larger, that count is the default message reservation. Configure `prefetch_bytes` and `prefetch_messages` to bound read-ahead independently of each fetch."},"3530":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"The stream-builder examples cover producer, consumer and combined configurations. The source consumer configuration defines each builder field."},"3531":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Swift"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples"},"3532":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"Run the Swift producer and consumer from the Apache Iggy source release."},"3533":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"The Swift examples use the SDK from the same checkout. Start the server from the repository root with development credentials and a separate data directory:"},"3534":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"From `examples/swift`, run the producer and then the consumer:"},"3535":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"The producer creates `sample-stream` and `sample-topic` if needed and sends five batches of ten messages. The consumer reads from the first message, stores no offset and exits after five batches. Running it again replays retained messages."},"3536":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"Both programs accept `--tcp-server-address`, `--tls`, `--tls-ca-file` and `--tls-domain`. The example README provides the development TLS commands."},"3537":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"page","content":"Swift SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Swift"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro"},"3538":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","content":"Use Swift concurrency to produce, read and manage Iggy resources over TCP with optional TLS."},"3539":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"Requirements and installation"},"3540":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Connect and create resources"},"3541":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#readers-and-consumers","content":"Readers and consumers"},"3542":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#options-metadata-and-recovery","content":"Options, metadata and recovery"},"3543":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","content":"The Swift SDK 0.2.0 provides an async TCP client with optional TLS, resource management, producers, partition readers and consumers. It routes partition requests to their primaries and reconnects with heartbeat support."},"3544":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"The SDK requires Swift 6.0 or later. Supported platforms are macOS 13, iOS 16, tvOS 16, watchOS 9, visionOS 1 and Linux. The runnable examples require Swift 6.1 or later."},"3545":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"The repository root contains the Swift Package Manager manifest and exports the `Iggy` product. The package dependency below names the 0.10.0 release tag:"},"3546":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"Add `.product(name: \"Iggy\", package: \"iggy\")` to your target dependencies. Build the server and client from the same release source."},"3547":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Start the development server with `iggy`/`iggy` credentials. Run these calls from an async Swift context:"},"3548":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Use new resource names when running this creation example again. `disconnect()` closes the current connection and permits a later `connect()`. `shutdown()` is final."},"3549":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Connection strings accept `iggy://` and `iggy+tcp://`. TLS uses system roots or the configured CA file. `tls_domain` overrides the name checked in the certificate. The parser accepts pending-operation limits, but does not implement retained mutation limits."},"3550":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#readers-and-consumers","content":"`client.reader` returns batches from explicit partitions or a group assignment. `client.consumerGroup` yields individual messages. A consumer needs `initialize()` before iteration:"},"3551":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#readers-and-consumers","content":"This fragment uses an existing connected client. Commit after processing. Automatic commits use `.auto(.default)`, which counts processing and stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. `.onRead` stores with the read. Close readers and shut down consumers to finish offset work and leave groups."},"3552":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#options-metadata-and-recovery","content":"Creation and updates use `StreamCreateOptions`, `TopicCreateOptions`, `StreamUpdateOptions`, `TopicUpdateOptions` and `UserUpdateOptions`. `EncryptionKey` accepts exactly 32 bytes. The server must have a wrapping key configured. Only fingerprints return in resource details."},"3553":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#options-metadata-and-recovery","content":"`getMetadataChanges` exposes the metadata log. `sendRawRequest(code:payload:replica:)` targets a replica for supported non-replicated commands. See Metadata changes."},"3554":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#options-metadata-and-recovery","content":"Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution or mutation lanes. It does track uncertain committing reads. `fetchProgressUncertain` carries an operation ID, and affected progress calls fail with `partitionLaneBlocked` until `abandonOperation` releases it. Abandonment does not undo a committed offset. A `ReadError` can stop a partition until `seek` selects a new position."},"3555":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"page","content":"Benchmarking","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking"},"3556":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-0","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"How Iggy is benchmarked, the tooling that ships with it, and the public platform for comparing results."},"3557":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-1","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#running-benchmarks","content":"Running benchmarks"},"3558":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-2","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Benchmark kinds"},"3559":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-3","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#transports","content":"Transports"},"3560":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-4","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#storing-and-comparing-results","content":"Storing and comparing results"},"3561":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-5","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#performance","content":"Performance"},"3562":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-6","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#prepare-the-host-and-topic-policies","content":"Prepare the host and topic policies"},"3563":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-7","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#deferred-consumer-workloads","content":"Deferred consumer workloads"},"3564":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-8","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"**Benchmarks should be the first-class citizens**. We believe that performance is crucial for any system, and we strive to provide the best possible performance for our users. Please check, why we believe that the **transparent benchmarking** is so important."},"3565":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-9","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"We've also built the **benchmarking platform** where you can browse published benchmarks and compare results. This is the another open-source project available here."},"3566":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-10","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"*Historical dashboard screenshot showing a 0.5.0 run, not 0.10.0 measurements.*"},"3567":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-11","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Iggy comes with a built-in benchmarking tool, `iggy-bench`. It's written in Rust and uses the `tokio` runtime for asynchronous I/O, mimicking the example client applications, so you can use it to estimate the performance of the server in your environment. It is part of the core repository and lives in the `core/bench` directory."},"3568":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-12","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"*Historical CLI screenshot. Server-start and cleanup flags shown there have been removed. Use the commands below and the current `iggy-bench --help`.*"},"3569":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-13","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#running-benchmarks","content":"First build the project in release mode:"},"3570":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-14","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#running-benchmarks","content":"**`iggy-bench` doesn't start a server.** It only connects, and fails with a connection error when nothing is listening. For a disposable development server with an empty data directory, use the credentials expected by the benchmark defaults:"},"3571":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-15","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#running-benchmarks","content":"This flag affects only the first creation of the root user. Existing credentials remain unchanged, and environment variables take precedence. For another server, pass its credentials with `iggy-bench -u USER -p PASSWORD` before the benchmark kind."},"3572":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-16","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#running-benchmarks","content":"The repo's driver script builds, starts the server, runs a send and a poll benchmark, and shuts the server down. **Use it only in a disposable checkout on an isolated host: it deletes `local_data` and signals matching `iggy-server` and `iggy-bench` processes by name.**"},"3573":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-17","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#running-benchmarks","content":"With a server running, invoke the benchmark kind and transport of your choice. Workloads that produce create their benchmark streams and topics, deleting pre-existing `bench-stream-{n}` streams unless `--reuse-streams` is set. Consumer-only workloads (`pc` and `bcg`) require those streams and enough messages to exist already. Run the corresponding producer workload first, with matching stream, partition, and message counts."},"3574":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-18","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Eight kinds are available, each with a short alias:"},"3575":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-19","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Producer and consumer counts default to six. Pinned workloads also default to six streams. Set explicit counts to match the workload and available CPUs."},"3576":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-20","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Pinned producer (`pp`): N producers, each sending to its own stream-topic with a single partition:"},"3577":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-21","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Pinned consumer (`pc`): N consumers, each polling its own stream-topic with a single partition:"},"3578":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-22","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Pinned producer and consumer (`ppc`), in parallel:"},"3579":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-23","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Balanced producer (`bp`): N producers sending to M partitions in K streams with balanced partitioning:"},"3580":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-24","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Balanced consumer group (`bcg`): N consumers assigned to M consumer groups polling from K partitions:"},"3581":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-25","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"Balanced producer and consumer group (`bpcg`), in parallel:"},"3582":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-26","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"End-to-end producing consumer (`e2e`): each task alternates producing and consuming, measuring latency from the messages' producer timestamps:"},"3583":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-27","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#benchmark-kinds","content":"End-to-end producing consumer group (`e2ecg`): N producing consumers assigned to M consumer groups:"},"3584":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-28","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#transports","content":"The transport subcommands are `tcp`, `quic`, `http`, and `websocket` (alias `ws`). Consumer-group workloads (`bcg`, `bpcg`, and `e2ecg`) require a binary transport: HTTP consumer-group joining returns `FeatureUnavailable`, even though the benchmark CLI accepts that combination. Each transport subcommand takes `--server-address` for a non-default server, for example:"},"3585":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-29","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#transports","content":"Message count, batch size, message size, and both durability flags precede the kind subcommand. Producer, consumer, stream, and partition counts belong to the selected kind. `iggy-bench --help` and `iggy-bench examples` show the full surface."},"3586":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-30","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#storing-and-comparing-results","content":"Each transport subcommand accepts a trailing `output` subcommand that persists the run (raw JSON data and charts):"},"3587":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-31","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#storing-and-comparing-results","content":"When the server address uses `localhost` or `127.0.0.1`, the saved command includes non-secret environment settings from the server configuration catalog. Credentials and unknown `IGGY_` variables are omitted."},"3588":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-32","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#storing-and-comparing-results","content":"`-o/--output-dir` defaults to `performance_results`. Optional flags (`--remark`, `--gitref`, extra info) annotate the run. Generated charts use the in-repo report library (`core/bench/report`). The runner (`core/bench/runner`) executes benchmarks across Git revisions. You can browse or compare results in the dashboard (`core/bench/dashboard`), which also powers the public benchmarking platform. A prebuilt dashboard image is available: `docker pull apache/iggy-bench-dashboard:edge`."},"3589":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-33","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#performance","content":"The server is thread-per-core and shared-nothing, built on `io_uring` on Linux (via `compio`), with shard and CPU pinning configurable under `[sharding]`. Throughput and latency depend heavily on hardware, transport, and payload shape (`messages-per-batch * message-size`). Run the benchmarks on your own hardware, or browse current, dated results on the benchmarking platform."},"3590":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-34","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#prepare-the-host-and-topic-policies","content":"Use the Linux tuning guide to check process limits, swap, huge pages, CPU/NUMA placement, writeback, and network capacity before measuring. Record the exact host settings and change one variable at a time. Use a separate load generator, or disjoint CPU sets when it shares a host with the server."},"3591":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-35","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#prepare-the-host-and-topic-policies","content":"`--durability` and `--consumer-offset-durability` independently default to `replicated`. Place them before the benchmark kind. For example, this keeps consumer offsets replicated while measuring persisted message acknowledgments:"},"3592":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-36","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#prepare-the-host-and-topic-policies","content":"Add `--consumer-offset-durability persisted` to request that policy too. These are create-time options and have no effect on existing topics with `--reuse-streams`. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous, so poll latency is not an acknowledged offset-store measurement. `iggy-bench examples` shows all benchmark kinds, transports, policy combinations, and available topic-option examples."},"3593":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-37","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#prepare-the-host-and-topic-policies","content":"Keep both policies, replication-group size, batch and message sizes, CPU allocation, and storage consistent between runs. Run long enough to include flushes and checkpoints, and report tail latency, errors, memory pressure, and client utilization alongside throughput."},"3594":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-38","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#deferred-consumer-workloads","content":"`--max-wait` defaults to one second, and `--min-count` defaults to one. `--max-bytes` defaults to at least 1 MiB, increased when needed to fit the configured batch. Place these flags before the benchmark kind."},"3595":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-39","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking#deferred-consumer-workloads","content":"Keep `min_count` within the smallest `messages-per-batch` value. After warmup, consumer-only runs restart their reads from offset zero, including group consumers. Record wait and byte limits with results because they change batching and latency."},"3596":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"page","content":"Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration"},"3597":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-0","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"The server's TOML configuration, how a file is merged over the embedded defaults, and what each section controls."},"3598":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-1","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"How configuration loads"},"3599":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-2","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#environment-variables","content":"Environment variables"},"3600":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-3","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#secrets","content":"Secrets"},"3601":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-4","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#validation-at-boot","content":"Validation at boot"},"3602":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-5","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"Command-line flags"},"3603":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-6","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"Relocated configuration keys"},"3604":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-7","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#reference","content":"Reference"},"3605":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-8","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"`[http]`"},"3606":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-9","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"`[http.cors]`"},"3607":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-10","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"`[http.jwt]`"},"3608":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-11","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwttrusted_issuers","content":"`[[http.jwt.trusted_issuers]]`"},"3609":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-12","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpmetrics","content":"`[http.metrics]`"},"3610":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-13","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#httptls","content":"`[http.tls]`"},"3611":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-14","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"`[tcp]`"},"3612":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-15","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcptls","content":"`[tcp.tls]`"},"3613":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-16","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"`[quic]`"},"3614":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-17","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#quiccertificate","content":"`[quic.certificate]`"},"3615":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-18","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"`[websocket]`"},"3616":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-19","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#websockettls","content":"`[websocket.tls]`"},"3617":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-20","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"TLS across transports"},"3618":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-21","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#root-data-path-and-runtime","content":"Root data path and runtime"},"3619":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-22","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"`[logging]`"},"3620":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-23","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#encryption","content":"`[encryption]`"},"3621":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-24","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#external_auth","content":"`[external_auth]`"},"3622":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-25","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory_pool","content":"`[memory_pool]`"},"3623":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-26","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#data_maintenancemessages","content":"`[data_maintenance.messages]`"},"3624":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-27","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#heartbeat","content":"`[heartbeat]`"},"3625":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-28","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"`[telemetry]`"},"3626":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-29","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"`[sharding]`"},"3627":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-30","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"`[metadata]`"},"3628":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-31","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"`[partition]`"},"3629":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-32","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"`[message_bus]`"},"3630":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-33","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#personal_access_token","content":"`[personal_access_token]`"},"3631":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-34","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#consumer_group","content":"`[consumer_group]`"},"3632":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-35","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#node","content":"`[node]`"},"3633":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-36","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"`[cluster]`"},"3634":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-37","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"The server reads a single TOML file. A copy of `core/server/config.toml` is embedded into the binary and serves as the default configuration, so the server boots with working defaults even without any file on disk. The embedded file is commented throughout and is the authoritative reference for the exact version you're running."},"3635":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-38","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"A config file doesn't have to be complete. It is **merged over the embedded defaults**, so a minimal file overrides only what it names:"},"3636":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-39","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"Both listeners here bind a wildcard, which tells the server nothing about where clients reach it, so `[node]` has to name that address explicitly - without it this file is refused at boot."},"3637":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-40","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"Host and kernel settings are configured separately from the server TOML. See Linux tuning for service limits, memory policies, huge pages, CPU placement, and validation before deployment."},"3638":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-41","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"Configuration is resolved in three layers. Later layers win:"},"3639":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-42","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"**Embedded defaults**: the `config.toml` compiled into the binary."},"3640":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-43","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"**Config file**: the path in `IGGY_CONFIG_PATH`, or `core/server/config.toml` resolved against the current working directory. A missing file is only a warning. The server continues on embedded defaults."},"3641":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-44","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"**Environment variables**: any `IGGY_`-prefixed override."},"3642":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-45","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"Before the environment is read, the server loads a `.env` file from the working directory or its parents, or from the path named by `IGGY_ENV_PATH`. Existing environment values take precedence over the `.env` file."},"3643":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-46","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#how-configuration-loads","content":"After boot the server writes the effective configuration, including the addresses it actually bound, to `{path}/runtime/current_config.toml` with the default runtime subdirectory."},"3644":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-47","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#environment-variables","content":"Every configuration key can be overridden with an `IGGY_` variable. The name is the TOML path, uppercased, with dots turned into underscores:"},"3645":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-48","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#environment-variables","content":"Two variables live outside the config schema: `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` set the root credentials, always as a pair. They initialize the root user **only at first creation**. On an existing data directory the stored root user is recovered unchanged, but supplied environment credentials must still pass validation."},"3646":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-49","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#secrets","content":"Secret configuration includes: `http.jwt.encoding_secret`, `http.jwt.decoding_secret`, `encryption.key`, `encryption.previous_keys`, `external_auth.url`, `cluster.auth.shared_secret`, and `cluster.auth.previous_shared_secret`. Prefer setting them through their environment variables (`IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET`, `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET`, and so on) instead of storing them in a file. Secret values are masked in logs and **never serialized** into `runtime/current_config.toml`."},"3647":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-50","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#validation-at-boot","content":"Array-valued environment settings replace the corresponding TOML array. For an indexed array such as `cluster.nodes`, supply every entry and its required fields through environment variables. A single field override does not merge into the existing TOML roster."},"3648":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-51","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#validation-at-boot","content":"Unknown top-level TOML fields and unknown server `IGGY_` environment names **reject startup**. Some nested tables can still ignore unknown fields, so compare the effective configuration with the intended settings."},"3649":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-52","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#validation-at-boot","content":"The old `[system]` table and its environment mappings are rejected. Removed placeholders such as `archive_expired` and `recreate_missing_state` must be removed, not set to `false`."},"3650":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-53","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#validation-at-boot","content":"Several sections validate relationships between keys (QUIC windows, sharding shutdown budgets, metadata journal sizing, partition transfer floors). A violation aborts boot with an error naming the keys. The constraints are listed with their sections below."},"3651":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-54","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#validation-at-boot","content":"The `RUST_LOG` environment variable takes precedence over `logging.level`. An invalid effective filter stops startup."},"3652":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-55","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"`iggy-server` accepts these four startup options, plus `--help` (`-h`) and `--version` (`-V`):"},"3653":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-56","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"| Flag            | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--fresh`, `-f` | Delete the configured data directory (`local_data` by default, see `IGGY_PATH`) before boot and start on empty state. In cluster mode this wipes **this replica only**. It rejoins and refills by state transfer from the others. Wiping a quorum at the same time can destroy committed data. Do not put `--fresh` in a service unit: it would re-transfer the whole dataset on every restart. |","table":"table-0"},"3654":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-57","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"| Flag                              | Description                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--with-default-root-credentials` | Set `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` to `iggy` unless they are already present in the environment. These values initialize the root user only at first creation. Development only. |","table":"table-0"},"3655":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-58","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"| Flag               | Description                                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--replica-id <N>` | Identify this node within `cluster.nodes`. Required when `cluster.enabled = true`. The value must match exactly one `replica_id` in the roster. |","table":"table-0"},"3656":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-59","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"| Flag                     | Description                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--list-config-env-vars` | Print the supported configuration environment-variable names and templates, then exit without starting the server. See Environment variables. |","table":"table-0"},"3657":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-60","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-flags","content":"Run `iggy-server --list-config-env-vars` to print every supported server variable and exit before loading dotenv or configuration files or creating runtime state. The output is sorted, contains names only, and uses `<N>` for indexed vector entries. Most indexed fields accept `0` through `255`. `cluster.nodes.<N>.advertised_addresses.<N>` accepts `0` through `15` for its inner index. Whole-value vector fields are listed once without a placeholder."},"3658":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-61","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"The server configuration no longer has a `[system]` wrapper. Its runtime tables live at the root, and the data directory is the root `path` key. Old `IGGY_SYSTEM_*` overrides are rejected. Retention, durability, and segment layout are topic creation options, not server-wide defaults."},"3659":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-62","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                     | Replacement               |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `system.path` / `IGGY_SYSTEM_PATH` | Root `path` / `IGGY_PATH` |","table":"table-1"},"3660":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-63","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                                                                                   | Replacement                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `system.runtime`, `system.logging`, `system.encryption`, `system.memory_pool`, `system.sharding` | Root tables `runtime`, `logging`, `encryption`, `memory_pool`, `sharding`. Remove `SYSTEM_` from their environment names |","table":"table-1"},"3661":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-64","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                       | Replacement                   |\n| ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n| `system.partition.validate_checksum` | `partition.validate_checksum` |","table":"table-1"},"3662":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-65","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                                                    | Replacement                                        |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `system.partition.path`, `partition.path` / `IGGY_PARTITION_PATH` | Removed. The `partitions` path component is fixed. |","table":"table-1"},"3663":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-66","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key          | Replacement                   |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `system.topic.max_size` | `max_topic_size` topic option |","table":"table-1"},"3664":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-67","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                | Replacement                   |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `system.topic.message_expiry` | `message_expiry` topic option |","table":"table-1"},"3665":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-68","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                   | Replacement                                            |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `system.partition.enforce_fsync` | `durability` topic option: `replicated` or `persisted` |","table":"table-1"},"3666":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-69","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                            | Replacement                                                            |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `partition.consumer_offset_enforce_fsync` | `consumer_offset_durability` topic option: `replicated` or `persisted` |","table":"table-1"},"3667":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-70","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                               | Replacement                              |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `system.partition.messages_required_to_save` | `messages_required_to_save` topic option |","table":"table-1"},"3668":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-71","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                                       | Replacement                                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `system.partition.size_of_messages_required_to_save` | `size_of_messages_required_to_save` topic option |","table":"table-1"},"3669":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-72","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key        | Replacement                 |\n| --------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `system.segment.size` | `segment_size` topic option |","table":"table-1"},"3670":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-73","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key               | Replacement                         |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `system.segment.preallocate` | `preallocate_segments` topic option |","table":"table-1"},"3671":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-74","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                            | Replacement                                                                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `system.stream.path`, `system.topic.path` | Removed. The `streams` and `topics` path components are fixed. Root `[stream]` and `[topic]` tables are rejected. |","table":"table-1"},"3672":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-75","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                                                             | Replacement                                    |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `system.segment.archive_expired`, `system.recovery.recreate_missing_state` | Removed. Delete the keys and their old tables. |","table":"table-1"},"3673":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-76","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key                                 | Replacement                           |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `system.message_deduplication` (whole section) | Removed. No equivalent configuration. |","table":"table-1"},"3674":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-77","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"| Old server key          | Replacement                                                         |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `extra` (whole section) | Removed. Admission caps are compile-time constants. No replacement. |","table":"table-1"},"3675":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-78","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"Remove the old tables and environment variables after migrating their values. Topic policies cannot be configured through TOML or `IGGY_` overrides, and the former `enforce_fsync` topic option is also rejected. Both new durability policies independently default to `replicated`. Explicitly select `persisted` at topic creation where required. Neither can change after topic creation."},"3676":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-79","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#relocated-configuration-keys","content":"Other removed sections, such as `[message_saver]`, `[system.state]`, `[system.backup]`, `[system.compression]`, and `[tcp.socket]`, must also be removed. Consult the embedded configuration for the deployed binary rather than relying on ignored legacy fields."},"3677":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-80","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#reference","content":"The tables below list every section with its shipped defaults. In cluster mode, every enabled transport takes its **port** from this node's `cluster.nodes` `ports` entry. The transport's own `address` picks only the bind interface."},"3678":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-81","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"| Key       | Default | Description              |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------ |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Serve the HTTP REST API. |","table":"table-2"},"3679":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-82","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"| Key       | Default            | Description            |\n| --------- | ------------------ | ---------------------- |\n| `address` | `\"127.0.0.1:3000\"` | Bind address and port. |","table":"table-2"},"3680":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-83","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"| Key                | Default  | Description                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ------------------ | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_request_size` | `\"2 MB\"` | Maximum request body size, at most `\"256 MiB\"`. Keep it at or below `message_bus.max_message_size` for multi-replica topics so an admitted batch fits replica frames. |","table":"table-2"},"3681":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-84","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"| Key      | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| -------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `web_ui` | `false` | Serve the embedded Web UI at `/ui`. Requires the `iggy-web` feature and static assets built with `npm --prefix web ci` and `npm --prefix web run build:static` before compiling the server. Without the feature, `true` logs a warning. Missing assets return 404. |","table":"table-2"},"3682":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-85","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"HTTP sessions hold live server-side session state (they count against `metadata.clients_table_max`). Consumer group management (create, get, delete) is available over HTTP. Group membership (join, leave) is not and needs a stateful transport."},"3683":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-86","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"In cluster mode, HTTP requests use the metadata or partition primary as appropriate. A contacted node can forward in one hop when shared JWT key material enables signed relay proofs. Partition polls also use the partition route. See Client failover."},"3684":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-87","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                                        |\n| --------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Handle cross-origin requests with the rules below. |","table":"table-3"},"3685":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-88","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key               | Default                            | Description           |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `allowed_methods` | `[\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\"]` | Allowed HTTP methods. |","table":"table-3"},"3686":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-89","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key               | Default | Description                                                                                                                             |\n| ----------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `allowed_origins` | `[\"*\"]` | Permitted origins. `\"*\"` is only valid as the first entry (anything after it is ignored). `\"*\"` in any other position fails the config. |","table":"table-3"},"3687":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-90","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key               | Default                             | Description                       |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `allowed_headers` | `[\"content-type\", \"authorization\"]` | Request headers clients may send. |","table":"table-3"},"3688":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-91","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key               | Default         | Description                                                                          |\n| ----------------- | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `exposed_headers` | `[\"iggy-view\"]` | Response headers browsers may read. `iggy-view` carries the current VSR view number. |","table":"table-3"},"3689":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-92","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key                 | Default | Description                                                                                                                  |\n| ------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `allow_credentials` | `false` | Allow cookies and HTTP auth in CORS requests. `true` requires explicit (non-wildcard) origins, headers, and exposed headers. |","table":"table-3"},"3690":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-93","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpcors","content":"| Key                     | Default | Description                           |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `allow_private_network` | `false` | Allow requests from private networks. |","table":"table-3"},"3691":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-94","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key         | Default   | Description                               |\n| ----------- | --------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `algorithm` | `\"HS256\"` | Signing algorithm for self-issued tokens. |","table":"table-4"},"3692":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-95","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key      | Default             | Description                    |\n| -------- | ------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `issuer` | `\"iggy.apache.org\"` | Issuer claim of minted tokens. |","table":"table-4"},"3693":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-96","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key        | Default             | Description                      |\n| ---------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `audience` | `\"iggy.apache.org\"` | Audience claim of minted tokens. |","table":"table-4"},"3694":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-97","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key             | Default               | Description                         |\n| --------------- | --------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `valid_issuers` | `[\"iggy.apache.org\"]` | Issuers accepted during validation. |","table":"table-4"},"3695":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-98","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key               | Default               | Description                           |\n| ----------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `valid_audiences` | `[\"iggy.apache.org\"]` | Audiences accepted during validation. |","table":"table-4"},"3696":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-99","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key                   | Default | Description            |\n| --------------------- | ------- | ---------------------- |\n| `access_token_expiry` | `\"1 h\"` | Access token lifetime. |","table":"table-4"},"3697":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-100","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key          | Default | Description                                        |\n| ------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `clock_skew` | `\"5 s\"` | Tolerance for clock differences during validation. |","table":"table-4"},"3698":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-101","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key          | Default | Description                             |\n| ------------ | ------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `not_before` | `\"0 s\"` | Time before which a token is not valid. |","table":"table-4"},"3699":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-102","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key               | Default      | Description                                                                                                                |\n| ----------------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `encoding_secret` | `\"\"` (empty) | Signing key. If only one secret is configured, it is used for both signing and verification. See the fallback rules below. |","table":"table-4"},"3700":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-103","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key               | Default      | Description                                                                                                           |\n| ----------------- | ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `decoding_secret` | `\"\"` (empty) | Verification key. If both secrets are set, they are used as supplied and must agree for self-issued tokens to verify. |","table":"table-4"},"3701":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-104","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"| Key                 | Default | Description                                     |\n| ------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `use_base64_secret` | `false` | Treat the configured secrets as base64-encoded. |","table":"table-4"},"3702":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-105","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"There is **no default secret**. When both secrets are empty and cluster authentication supplies no key, each server start mints a random signing key, so issued tokens **do not survive a restart** and are valid only on the node that issued them. If you configure a secret, set it through the environment (`IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET` / `IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET`), never commit it, and use a long random value."},"3703":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-106","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwt","content":"In cluster mode the secret has an extra role: a configured secret, identical on every node, makes bearer tokens valid cluster-wide and activates follower-to-primary HTTP forwarding. When both JWT secrets are empty and `cluster.auth` is enabled, the JWT key is derived from the cluster PSK. Explicit JWT secrets take precedence. Without either, tokens are node-local and forwarding stays disabled."},"3704":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-107","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwttrusted_issuers","content":"Opt-in trust of external token issuers for application-to-application authentication. With none configured, the listener accepts only self-issued tokens using the configured HMAC algorithm (`HS256`, `HS384`, or `HS512`)."},"3705":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-108","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwttrusted_issuers","content":"`issuer`, `audience`, and `jwks_url` are required per entry. `username` names the active, non-root Iggy account to which the issuer maps. Provision that account before using its tokens. The old `user_id` key is rejected."},"3706":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-109","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpjwttrusted_issuers","content":"Enabling an issuer opens an outbound JWKS fetch that is reachable before a token's signature is verified: a token naming the issuer with an unknown key id can trigger a fetch to `jwks_url`. The target is fixed (not attacker-chosen), concurrent misses coalesce onto one fetch, and repeats are rate-limited to one outbound request per issuer per short window. The same window bounds how quickly a freshly rotated signing key is picked up."},"3707":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-110","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpmetrics","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                |\n| --------- | ------- | -------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Expose Prometheus metrics. |","table":"table-5"},"3708":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-111","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpmetrics","content":"| Key        | Default      | Description                        |\n| ---------- | ------------ | ---------------------------------- |\n| `endpoint` | `\"/metrics\"` | Metrics path. Must start with `/`. |","table":"table-5"},"3709":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-112","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httpmetrics","content":"The metrics route **requires authentication**: a missing or invalid bearer credential is rejected with 401. Any authenticated user may scrape. There's no extra RBAC rule. Scrapers present a JWT or a personal access token as the bearer:"},"3710":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-113","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httptls","content":"| Key       | Default | Description  |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------ |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Serve HTTPS. |","table":"table-6"},"3711":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-114","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httptls","content":"| Key         | Default                      | Description           |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `cert_file` | `\"core/certs/iggy_cert.pem\"` | PEM certificate path. |","table":"table-6"},"3712":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-115","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httptls","content":"| Key        | Default                     | Description   |\n| ---------- | --------------------------- | ------------- |\n| `key_file` | `\"core/certs/iggy_key.pem\"` | PEM key path. |","table":"table-6"},"3713":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-116","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#httptls","content":"HTTP TLS is **file-based only**. There's no `self_signed` option for this listener. Both files must exist when enabled."},"3714":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-117","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                    |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Serve the TCP binary protocol. |","table":"table-7"},"3715":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-118","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"| Key       | Default            | Description            |\n| --------- | ------------------ | ---------------------- |\n| `address` | `\"127.0.0.1:8090\"` | Bind address and port. |","table":"table-7"},"3716":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-119","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"TCP is the highest-throughput transport. These two keys, plus the TLS block below, are the entire section."},"3717":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-120","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcptls","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                  |\n| --------- | ------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Wrap TCP connections in TLS. |","table":"table-8"},"3718":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-121","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcptls","content":"| Key           | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `self_signed` | `true`  | Generate an ephemeral loopback certificate, but only while `cert_file` does not exist on disk. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead (the server logs which way it went). `false` requires both files to exist. |","table":"table-8"},"3719":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-122","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcptls","content":"| Key         | Default                      | Description           |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `cert_file` | `\"core/certs/iggy_cert.pem\"` | PEM certificate path. |","table":"table-8"},"3720":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-123","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcptls","content":"| Key        | Default                     | Description   |\n| ---------- | --------------------------- | ------------- |\n| `key_file` | `\"core/certs/iggy_key.pem\"` | PEM key path. |","table":"table-8"},"3721":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-124","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key       | Default | Description               |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Serve the QUIC transport. |","table":"table-9"},"3722":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-125","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key       | Default            | Description            |\n| --------- | ------------------ | ---------------------- |\n| `address` | `\"127.0.0.1:8080\"` | Bind address and port. |","table":"table-9"},"3723":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-126","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key                           | Default | Description                                                      |\n| ----------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_concurrent_bidi_streams` | `256`   | Maximum concurrent bidirectional streams on one QUIC connection. |","table":"table-9"},"3724":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-127","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key           | Default    | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `initial_mtu` | `\"1200 B\"` | Initial path MTU before MTU discovery runs. 1200 bytes is the QUIC minimum. Values below it are rejected at boot. Values above the real path MTU cause packet loss until black-hole detection resets to 1200. |","table":"table-9"},"3725":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-128","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key           | Default    | Description                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `send_window` | `\"64 MiB\"` | Flow-control budget for unacknowledged data in flight to one connection. A ceiling, not a reservation. |","table":"table-9"},"3726":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-129","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key              | Default    | Description                                                                           |\n| ---------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `receive_window` | `\"64 MiB\"` | Flow-control budget for data buffered from one connection, shared by all its streams. |","table":"table-9"},"3727":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-130","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key                     | Default    | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------------- | ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `stream_receive_window` | `\"64 MiB\"` | Per-stream slice of `receive_window`. Must not exceed `receive_window`. Equality is allowed. Lower it below `receive_window` if you raise the stream cap, so one unread stream cannot pin the whole connection window. |","table":"table-9"},"3728":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-131","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key                   | Default  | Description                                                                                                                                             |\n| --------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `keep_alive_interval` | `\"10 s\"` | Keep-alive PING interval. One third of `max_idle_timeout`, so two consecutive losses fit before the idle timer closes the connection. `\"0 s\"` disables. |","table":"table-9"},"3729":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-132","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"| Key                | Default  | Description                                                                  |\n| ------------------ | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_idle_timeout` | `\"30 s\"` | Idle time before a connection is closed. `\"0 s\"` disables (not recommended). |","table":"table-9"},"3730":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-133","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"Raising `max_concurrent_bidi_streams` is a transport decision only: the server's accept loop takes one stream at a time and waits for its reply before accepting the next, so a higher cap does **not** make command handling concurrent."},"3731":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-134","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quiccertificate","content":"| Key           | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `self_signed` | `true`  | **Always** generate an ephemeral loopback certificate. Unlike TCP and WebSocket, files are ignored even when they exist on disk (the server logs a warning naming the skipped files). `false` requires both files. |","table":"table-10"},"3732":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-135","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quiccertificate","content":"| Key         | Default      | Description                                                |\n| ----------- | ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `cert_file` | `\"\"` (empty) | PEM certificate path. Required when `self_signed = false`. |","table":"table-10"},"3733":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-136","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quiccertificate","content":"| Key        | Default      | Description                                        |\n| ---------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `key_file` | `\"\"` (empty) | PEM key path. Required when `self_signed = false`. |","table":"table-10"},"3734":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-137","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                    |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Serve the WebSocket transport. |","table":"table-11"},"3735":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-138","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key       | Default            | Description            |\n| --------- | ------------------ | ---------------------- |\n| `address` | `\"127.0.0.1:8092\"` | Bind address and port. |","table":"table-11"},"3736":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-139","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key                | Default             | Description                                   |\n| ------------------ | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `read_buffer_size` | unset (`\"128 KiB\"`) | Target minimum size of the frame read buffer. |","table":"table-11"},"3737":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-140","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key                 | Default             | Description                                         |\n| ------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `write_buffer_size` | unset (`\"128 KiB\"`) | Target buffer size for batched writes before flush. |","table":"table-11"},"3738":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-141","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key                     | Default           | Description                                                                                                                           |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_write_buffer_size` | unset (unlimited) | Hard ceiling on the write buffer. Writes past it error instead of buffering. Must exceed `write_buffer_size` by at least one message. |","table":"table-11"},"3739":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-142","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key                | Default            | Description                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------ | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_message_size` | unset (`\"64 MiB\"`) | Upper bound on one inbound message after fragment reassembly. Must not exceed `message_bus.max_message_size`. |","table":"table-11"},"3740":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-143","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key              | Default            | Description                                                                             |\n| ---------------- | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_frame_size` | unset (`\"16 MiB\"`) | Upper bound on one inbound frame before reassembly. Must not exceed `max_message_size`. |","table":"table-11"},"3741":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-144","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"| Key                      | Default | Description                                                                |\n| ------------------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `accept_unmasked_frames` | `false` | Accept unmasked client frames in violation of RFC 6455. Strict by default. |","table":"table-11"},"3742":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-145","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"The size knobs are optional. Leaving one unset keeps the `compio-ws` (tungstenite) default shown in parentheses. A malformed size string fails config load."},"3743":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-146","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websockettls","content":"| Key       | Default | Description |\n| --------- | ------- | ----------- |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Serve WSS.  |","table":"table-12"},"3744":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-147","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websockettls","content":"| Key           | Default | Description                                                                                                                         |\n| ------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `self_signed` | `true`  | Same load-or-generate behavior as `[tcp.tls]`: an existing PEM pair wins, otherwise an ephemeral loopback certificate is generated. |","table":"table-12"},"3745":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-148","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websockettls","content":"| Key         | Default                      | Description           |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `cert_file` | `\"core/certs/iggy_cert.pem\"` | PEM certificate path. |","table":"table-12"},"3746":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-149","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websockettls","content":"| Key        | Default                     | Description   |\n| ---------- | --------------------------- | ------------- |\n| `key_file` | `\"core/certs/iggy_key.pem\"` | PEM key path. |","table":"table-12"},"3747":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-150","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"The `self_signed` flag doesn't mean the same thing everywhere:"},"3748":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-151","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"| Listener       | Behavior                                                                                                                                   |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| TCP, WebSocket | `self_signed = true` generates an ephemeral certificate **only while `cert_file` does not exist**. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead. |","table":"table-13"},"3749":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-152","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"| Listener | Behavior                                                                                         |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| QUIC     | `self_signed = true` **always** generates an ephemeral certificate and ignores configured files. |","table":"table-13"},"3750":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-153","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"| Listener | Behavior                             |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| HTTP     | No `self_signed` option. Files only. |","table":"table-13"},"3751":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-154","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"| Listener      | Behavior                                           |\n| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Replica plane | Separate section, `[cluster.tls]`. See Clustering. |","table":"table-13"},"3752":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-155","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tls-across-transports","content":"Ephemeral certificates **change on every start** and are meant for loopback development, not production."},"3753":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-156","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#root-data-path-and-runtime","content":"| Key    | Default        | Description                                                             |\n| ------ | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `path` | `\"local_data\"` | Base directory for all server data. Everything below is relative to it. |","table":"table-14"},"3754":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-157","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#root-data-path-and-runtime","content":"Set `path` before any TOML table header. `[runtime] path = \"runtime\"` and `[logging] path = \"logs\"` are relative to it. Data uses the fixed layout `{path}/streams/{stream_id}/topics/{topic_id}/partitions/{partition_id}`. Stream, topic, and partition path overrides are rejected."},"3755":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-158","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key    | Default  | Description                                 |\n| ------ | -------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `path` | `\"logs\"` | Log directory, relative to the root `path`. |","table":"table-15"},"3756":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-159","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key     | Default  | Description                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `level` | `\"info\"` | Filter directive in `RUST_LOG` syntax: simple levels or directives like `\"warn,server=debug,iggy=trace\"`. The `RUST_LOG` environment variable always takes precedence. |","table":"table-15"},"3757":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-160","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key            | Default | Description                           |\n| -------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `file_enabled` | `true`  | Write logs to file as well as stdout. |","table":"table-15"},"3758":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-161","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key             | Default    | Description                                                                                 |\n| --------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_file_size` | `\"500 MB\"` | Size at which a log file rotates. Zero disables size rotation. There is no hourly rotation. |","table":"table-15"},"3759":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-162","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key              | Default  | Description                                                                                                          |\n| ---------------- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max_total_size` | `\"4 GB\"` | Total log budget. Cleanup deletes the oldest archives first. Zero disables this size limit. Retention still applies. |","table":"table-15"},"3760":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-163","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key                       | Default | Description                                                   |\n| ------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `rotation_check_interval` | `\"1 h\"` | How often rotation status is checked. Avoid values below 1 s. |","table":"table-15"},"3761":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-164","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key         | Default    | Description                                          |\n| ----------- | ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `retention` | `\"7 days\"` | How long log files are kept. Avoid values below 1 s. |","table":"table-15"},"3762":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-165","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#logging","content":"| Key                      | Default  | Description                              |\n| ------------------------ | -------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `sysinfo_print_interval` | `\"10 s\"` | Interval for system-information logging. |","table":"table-15"},"3763":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-166","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#encryption","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Generate a data key for each new stream. Existing streams retain their encryption choice. Payloads and user headers use AES-256-GCM-SIV. Metadata and structural headers are not encrypted as a whole. |","table":"table-16"},"3764":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-167","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#encryption","content":"| Key   | Default      | Description                                                                                                                             |\n| ----- | ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `key` | `\"\"` (empty) | Base64 of 32 bytes. Derives the key that wraps stream data keys. Required when encryption is enabled or a client supplies a stream key. |","table":"table-16"},"3765":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-168","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#encryption","content":"| Key             | Default | Description                                                      |\n| --------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `previous_keys` | `[]`    | Up to two previous server keys retained during rotation. Secret. |","table":"table-16"},"3766":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-169","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#encryption","content":"| Key                        | Default | Description                                                                                       |\n| -------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `retired_kek_fingerprints` | `[]`    | Fingerprints of wraps to remove after rotation. Cannot name a configured current or previous key. |","table":"table-16"},"3767":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-170","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#encryption","content":"See Stream options for the rotation procedure. Environment overrides include `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_PREVIOUS_KEYS` and `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_RETIRED_KEK_FINGERPRINTS`, each accepting a JSON array."},"3768":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-171","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#external_auth","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                                                    |\n| --------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Forward logins for unknown local usernames to an HTTP service. |","table":"table-17"},"3769":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-172","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#external_auth","content":"| Key   | Default | Description                                                                                        |\n| ----- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `url` | Empty   | HTTP or HTTPS endpoint. Required when enabled and omitted from the effective configuration output. |","table":"table-17"},"3770":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-173","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#external_auth","content":"| Key       | Default | Description               |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------- |\n| `timeout` | `\"5 s\"` | Positive callout timeout. |","table":"table-17"},"3771":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-174","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#external_auth","content":"| Key                       | Default | Description               |\n| ------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------- |\n| `max_concurrent_callouts` | `64`    | Positive per-shard limit. |","table":"table-17"},"3772":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-175","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#external_auth","content":"The service maps a caller to an existing active, non-root account. Local usernames never use the callout. See External authentication for the JSON contract and failure behavior."},"3773":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-176","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory_pool","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                                                                     |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Reuse aligned buffers through the memory pool. Buffers are allocated on demand. |","table":"table-18"},"3774":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-177","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory_pool","content":"| Key    | Default   | Description                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------ | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `size` | `\"4 GiB\"` | Pool allocation budget, not a process memory limit. When the pool cannot supply a buffer within this budget, allocation continues outside it. Minimum 512 MiB. Must be a multiple of 4096 (page size). |","table":"table-18"},"3775":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-178","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory_pool","content":"| Key               | Default | Description                                                      |\n| ----------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `bucket_capacity` | `8192`  | Maximum buffers per bucket. Must be a power of two. Minimum 128. |","table":"table-18"},"3776":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-179","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory_pool","content":"The pool has 28 buckets with buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB."},"3777":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-180","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#data_maintenancemessages","content":"| Key               | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `cleaner_enabled` | `true`  | Run the segment cleaner. It deletes the oldest **sealed** segments of topics with a finite `message_expiry` or `max_topic_size`, per partition, best-effort. The active segment is never touched, and stored consumer/group offsets can hold back deletion. |","table":"table-19"},"3778":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-181","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#data_maintenancemessages","content":"| Key        | Default | Description           |\n| ---------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `interval` | `\"1 m\"` | Cleaner run interval. |","table":"table-19"},"3779":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-182","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#heartbeat","content":"| Key       | Default | Description               |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `true`  | Verify client heartbeats. |","table":"table-20"},"3780":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-183","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#heartbeat","content":"| Key        | Default  | Description                  |\n| ---------- | -------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `interval` | `\"30 s\"` | Expected heartbeat interval. |","table":"table-20"},"3781":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-184","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#heartbeat","content":"When enabled, the per-shard verifier evicts stale group-member connections after the heartbeat threshold. Evicting a link does not itself end its logical session. The metadata liveness process expires the session lease and reassigns group membership. The session timeout is 1.2 times `heartbeat.interval`, or 36 seconds by default. Periodic checks mean expiry is not an exact wall-clock deadline."},"3782":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-185","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#heartbeat","content":"SDK clients maintain heartbeats when connected. The Java blocking client delegates to the async TCP implementation and also uses its heartbeat. Keep the client heartbeat interval below the server session expiry threshold. Call the SDK connection method before using a long-lived consumer, and close it through the SDK lifecycle API."},"3783":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-186","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"Server OTLP export is unavailable in this revision: `grpc` prevents startup, and `http` does not deliver logs or traces. Leave `enabled = false`."},"3784":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-187","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"| Key       | Default | Description                  |\n| --------- | ------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Enable OpenTelemetry export. |","table":"table-21"},"3785":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-188","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"| Key            | Default  | Description            |\n| -------------- | -------- | ---------------------- |\n| `service_name` | `\"iggy\"` | Reported service name. |","table":"table-21"},"3786":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-189","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"| Key                        | Default  | Description                                 |\n| -------------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `telemetry.logs.transport` | `\"grpc\"` | Log export transport: `\"grpc\"` or `\"http\"`. |","table":"table-21"},"3787":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-190","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"| Key                       | Default                           | Description          |\n| ------------------------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------- |\n| `telemetry.logs.endpoint` | `\"http://localhost:7281/v1/logs\"` | Log export endpoint. |","table":"table-21"},"3788":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-191","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"| Key                          | Default  | Description                                   |\n| ---------------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `telemetry.traces.transport` | `\"grpc\"` | Trace export transport: `\"grpc\"` or `\"http\"`. |","table":"table-21"},"3789":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-192","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"| Key                         | Default                             | Description            |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| `telemetry.traces.endpoint` | `\"http://localhost:7281/v1/traces\"` | Trace export endpoint. |","table":"table-21"},"3790":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-193","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key              | Default       | Description                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `cpu_allocation` | `\"numa:auto\"` | Number of shards and their CPU affinity. See syntaxes below. |","table":"table-22"},"3791":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-194","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key         | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ----------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `pin_cores` | `true`  | Pin shard threads to dedicated cores, drawn from the process's allowed CPU set (cooperates with systemd `AllowedCPUs=` and container cpusets). Set `false` on hosts where the server shares cores with other workloads. |","table":"table-22"},"3792":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-195","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key              | Default | Description                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `inbox_capacity` | `65536` | Per-shard inbox capacity for consensus, connection setup and reconciliation. |","table":"table-22"},"3793":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-196","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                    | Default | Description                                                                                                                       |\n| ---------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `reply_inbox_capacity` | `1024`  | Separate per-shard channel for forwarded client replies. Size for peak reply fan-out. Dropped replies have no bus retransmission. |","table":"table-22"},"3794":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-197","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                        | Default | Description                                              |\n| -------------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `poll_completion_capacity` | `1024`  | Completed deferred reads waiting for their owning shard. |","table":"table-22"},"3795":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-198","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                      | Default  | Description                                          |\n| ------------------------ | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deferred_poll_max_wait` | `\"30 s\"` | Server cap on a requested wait, at most 600 seconds. |","table":"table-22"},"3796":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-199","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                         | Default | Description                        |\n| --------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `deferred_poll_max_pending` | `1024`  | Maximum parked requests per shard. |","table":"table-22"},"3797":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-200","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                                     | Default | Description                                                                 |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deferred_poll_max_pending_per_session` | `64`    | Maximum parked requests per session, or HTTP user without a binary session. |","table":"table-22"},"3798":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-201","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                            | Default    | Description                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------ | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deferred_poll_max_read_bytes` | `\"16 MiB\"` | Read budget cap. A first message larger than the reply budget can return alone. |","table":"table-22"},"3799":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-202","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                                | Default    | Description                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `deferred_poll_max_inflight_bytes` | `\"64 MiB\"` | Combined deferred-read reservations per shard, at most 1 GiB. |","table":"table-22"},"3800":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-203","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                     | Default | Description                                                                               |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `partition_io_capacity` | `16`    | Active partition file jobs and results awaiting acceptance per shard, from 1 through 256. |","table":"table-22"},"3801":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-204","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                      | Default | Description                                                                                                                         |\n| ------------------------ | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `partition_io_bytes_max` | Derived | Admission ceiling for retained file-job allocations. Omission selects the largest legal job charge, slightly above 4 GiB per shard. |","table":"table-22"},"3802":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-205","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                      | Default  | Description                                                             |\n| ------------------------ | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `shutdown_drain_timeout` | `\"10 s\"` | Per-shard bus drain budget on shutdown. Slow-fsync hosts may need more. |","table":"table-22"},"3803":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-206","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                      | Default   | Description                                                                                 |\n| ------------------------ | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `shutdown_poll_interval` | `\"50 ms\"` | Poll cadence for the shutdown flag. Must be less than or equal to `shutdown_drain_timeout`. |","table":"table-22"},"3804":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-207","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                     | Default  | Description                                                                                                                                |\n| ----------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `shutdown_join_timeout` | `\"30 s\"` | Hard deadline for joining shard threads at exit. A wedged shard is abandoned with an error log. Must be at least `shutdown_drain_timeout`. |","table":"table-22"},"3805":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-208","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"| Key                           | Default | Description                                                                                   |\n| ----------------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `reconcile_periodic_interval` | `\"1 s\"` | Safety tick for the partition reconciliation loop, which also wakes on every metadata commit. |","table":"table-22"},"3806":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-209","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"Valid `cpu_allocation` syntaxes:"},"3807":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-210","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"`\"all\"`: one shard per available CPU core"},"3808":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-211","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"a number, e.g. `4`: four shards on the first four CPUs in the process's allowed set when `pin_cores` is enabled"},"3809":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-212","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"a range, e.g. `\"5..8\"`: three shards on cores 5, 6, 7"},"3810":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-213","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"`\"numa:auto\"`: all NUMA nodes and cores, NUMA-aware"},"3811":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-214","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"`\"numa:nodes=0,1;cores=4;no_ht=true\"`: NUMA nodes 0 and 1, four cores each, no hyperthreads"},"3812":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-215","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"Tunables for the metadata consensus plane (shard 0's VSR replica: users, streams, topics, sessions)."},"3813":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-216","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"| Key                   | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `prepare_queue_depth` | `32`    | Uncommitted metadata ops in flight at once. Submits beyond it are rejected with a transient error the SDKs retry. Capped at 127 by the view-change wire format. |","table":"table-23"},"3814":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-217","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"| Key             | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `journal_slots` | `1024`  | In-memory index of the metadata WAL, in slots. Larger buys more headroom between forced checkpoints at the cost of memory and bigger WAL rewrites. Boot enforces `journal_slots >= 4 x max(64, prepare_queue_depth)`. |","table":"table-23"},"3815":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-218","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"| Key                 | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `clients_table_max` | `8192`  | Slots in the VSR client table: how many distinct clients (TCP/QUIC/WebSocket virtual clients and HTTP sessions together) hold live session state. When full, the client with the oldest last commit is evicted and re-registers on its next request. The HTTP session cap tracks this at half. Must be between 2 and 65536. |","table":"table-23"},"3816":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-219","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"| Key              | Default | Description                                                       |\n| ---------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `partitions_max` | `0`     | Total partition admission limit. Zero leaves this limit disabled. |","table":"table-23"},"3817":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-220","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"| Key                            | Default | Description                         |\n| ------------------------------ | ------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `change_log_resource_capacity` | `65536` | Retained resource metadata changes. |","table":"table-23"},"3818":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-221","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#metadata","content":"| Key                            | Default | Description                         |\n| ------------------------------ | ------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `change_log_activity_capacity` | `16384` | Retained activity metadata changes. |","table":"table-23"},"3819":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-222","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"Partition storage and consensus tunables share this table. Unlike `[metadata]` (one plane on shard 0), a pipeline exists per partition, so raising per-partition budgets **multiplies resource use by the partition count**."},"3820":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-223","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"`durability`, `consumer_offset_durability`, segment size, preallocation, and the message/byte flush thresholds are topic creation options. `wal_bytes_max` sizes the persistence machinery without selecting the topic's completion policy."},"3821":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-224","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                   | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `prepare_queue_depth` | `32`    | Uncommitted produce and consumer-offset ops in flight per partition. Submits past it spill into a request queue of twice this depth. Once both are full the server rejects the request with `TransientNotAccepted`. A lost rejection reply can still make the client wait for its timeout. Must be between 1 and 127. |","table":"table-24"},"3822":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-225","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                 | Default | Description                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `validate_checksum` | `true`  | Re-hash batches read from segment storage and report a mismatch instead of serving them. |","table":"table-24"},"3823":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-226","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key             | Default     | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| --------------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wal_bytes_max` | `\"256 MiB\"` | Active WAL plus queued/in-flight prepare budget per multi-replica partition when either topic policy is `persisted`. A 4 KiB multiple, from 128 MiB + 8 KiB through 4 GiB. Checkpointing reclaims history only after materialized state is synchronized. Temporary rewrites require extra disk space. Environment override: `IGGY_PARTITION_WAL_BYTES_MAX`. |","table":"table-24"},"3824":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-227","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                       | Default   | Description                                                                 |\n| ------------------------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repair_memory_bytes_max` | `\"1 GiB\"` | Node-wide budget shared by unproven repair walks and snapshot repair tails. |","table":"table-24"},"3825":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-228","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                             | Default | Description                                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `wal_group_commit_delay_micros` | `0`     | Delay before a persisted partition WAL barrier, at most 10000 microseconds. Zero disables the delay. |","table":"table-24"},"3826":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-229","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                 | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dedup_clients_max` | `4096`  | Initial slots for session-qualified retry protection per partition. A full table rejects new writers with `TransientNotAccepted`. Ordered session retirement releases slots. The first committed operation fixes this capacity, from 1 through 65536. |","table":"table-24"},"3827":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-230","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                    | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ---------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `consumer_offsets_max` | `4096`  | Durable offset keys admitted per partition, counted separately for standalone consumers and groups. Existing keys remain writable at the limit. New keys are rejected with `TooManyConsumerOffsets`. Between 1 and 262144. |","table":"table-24"},"3828":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-231","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                        | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| -------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `offset_reservation_lease` | `65536` | Offsets reserved ahead in the superblock for single-replica partitions. Crash recovery skips the unused reservation to avoid reusing acknowledged offsets. Multi-replica groups ignore it. Between 1 and 16777216. |","table":"table-24"},"3829":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-232","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                     | Default | Description                                                                                                    |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `evicted_ring_capacity` | `65536` | Maximum repair entries per replicated partition. At most 65536 entries. Single-replica partitions retain none. |","table":"table-24"},"3830":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-233","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                      | Default    | Description                                                                                              |\n| ------------------------ | ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `evicted_ring_bytes_max` | `\"64 MiB\"` | Byte ceiling for the repair ring. Older batches can leave memory and be read from disk. At most 256 MiB. |","table":"table-24"},"3831":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-234","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                               | Default      | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `transfer_served_cache_bytes_max` | `\"2176 MiB\"` | Byte budget, **per shard**, for segment payloads kept resident to serve state-transfer chunk requests. The default fits two sealed segments at the 1 GiB ceiling, each with one max-message overshoot. Serving concurrency is `floor(this / max(transfer_artifact_bytes_max, 1 GiB + 64 MiB))`, minimum one. Boot warns when it drops below two. At most `\"64 GiB\"`. |","table":"table-24"},"3832":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-235","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#partition","content":"| Key                           | Default      | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `transfer_artifact_bytes_max` | `\"1088 MiB\"` | Allocation ceiling for one received state-transfer artifact, per shard (up to four transfers run at once). Boot enforces the floor `1 GiB + message_bus.max_message_size`. The shipped value sits exactly at it. Raising `message_bus.max_message_size` alone therefore refuses boot: raise this by the same amount in the same edit. At most `\"64 GiB\"`. |","table":"table-24"},"3833":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-236","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"Tunables for the internal bus that ships consensus traffic between replicas and client traffic between shards."},"3834":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-237","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key         | Default | Description                                                                                |\n| ----------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `max_batch` | `256`   | Messages coalesced into one `writev(2)` call. Hard upper bound 512 (`IOV_MAX/2` on Linux). |","table":"table-25"},"3835":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-238","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                | Default    | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------------------ | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `max_message_size` | `\"64 MiB\"` | Wire-level cap on a single framed message, at most `\"256 MiB\"`. Values above `\"64 MiB\"` exceed the Go SDK's frame limit. Coupled to `partition.transfer_artifact_bytes_max` and `websocket.max_message_size` (see those keys). |","table":"table-25"},"3836":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-239","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                   | Default | Description                                                                     |\n| --------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `peer_queue_capacity` | `4096`  | Per-peer message queue capacity. `cluster.repair_chunk_max` must stay below it. |","table":"table-25"},"3837":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-240","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                        | Default    | Description                                           |\n| -------------------------- | ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `replica_read_buffer_size` | `\"32 KiB\"` | Replica socket read buffer for batching small frames. |","table":"table-25"},"3838":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-241","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key               | Default                  | Description                                                                            |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `connections_max` | Half the open-file limit | Node-wide client connection cap when omitted. An explicit positive value overrides it. |","table":"table-25"},"3839":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-242","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                     | Default | Description                        |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `client_queue_capacity` | `256`   | Queue capacity for client traffic. |","table":"table-25"},"3840":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-243","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                          | Default  | Description                          |\n| ---------------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| `client_write_stall_timeout` | `\"30 s\"` | Deadline for a stalled client write. |","table":"table-25"},"3841":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-244","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                              | Default     | Description                             |\n| -------------------------------- | ----------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `client_request_queue_bytes_max` | `\"256 MiB\"` | Byte budget for queued client requests. |","table":"table-25"},"3842":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-245","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                | Default | Description                                            |\n| ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `reconnect_period` | `\"1 s\"` | Interval between outbound reconnect attempts to peers. |","table":"table-25"},"3843":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-246","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key                  | Default | Description                                            |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `close_peer_timeout` | `\"2 s\"` | Per-peer close drain budget before force-cancellation. |","table":"table-25"},"3844":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-247","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key           | Default | Description                                                     |\n| ------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `close_grace` | `\"2 s\"` | Bound on a single stream shutdown in the TLS-family transports. |","table":"table-25"},"3845":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-248","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#message_bus","content":"| Key               | Default  | Description                                                                                                    |\n| ----------------- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `handshake_grace` | `\"10 s\"` | Bound on a connection's handshake phase, so a slowloris peer cannot pin per-connection resources indefinitely. |","table":"table-25"},"3846":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-249","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#personal_access_token","content":"| Key                   | Default | Description                     |\n| --------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `max_tokens_per_user` | `100`   | Maximum active tokens per user. |","table":"table-26"},"3847":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-250","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#personal_access_token","content":"| Key                                     | Default | Description                         |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `personal_access_token.cleaner.enabled` | `true`  | Periodically remove expired tokens. |","table":"table-26"},"3848":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-251","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#personal_access_token","content":"| Key                                      | Default | Description           |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `personal_access_token.cleaner.interval` | `\"1 m\"` | Cleaner run interval. |","table":"table-26"},"3849":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-252","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#consumer_group","content":"| Key                   | Default | Description                                                                                                    |\n| --------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `rebalancing_timeout` | `\"30s\"` | Maximum time a partition can remain in pending revocation before being force-transferred to the target member. |","table":"table-27"},"3850":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-253","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#consumer_group","content":"| Key                  | Default | Description                                                                                                         |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `heartbeat_interval` | `\"5s\"`  | Membership heartbeat interval advertised to consumers. Session expiry derives separately from `heartbeat.interval`. |","table":"table-27"},"3851":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-254","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#node","content":"This node's own client-facing identity. The address is read only while `cluster.enabled = false`: in cluster mode the roster answers the same question per node, so the address itself is ignored and the server says so at startup. The key is still validated in every mode, so a malformed value refuses boot even with `cluster.enabled = true`."},"3852":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-255","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#node","content":"| Key                  | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `advertised_address` | unset   | The address clients dial, published in cluster metadata. A literal IP or a DNS hostname. Left unset, the server derives it from the bind address of the first enabled listener. That fails when the listener binds a wildcard, and the server refuses to start rather than publish an address no client can use. Declaring the unspecified address (`0.0.0.0`, `::`) is refused for the same reason. |","table":"table-28"},"3853":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-256","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"Cluster mode is configured here but documented in Clustering. The section contains:"},"3854":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-257","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key       | Default | One-liner                                                    |\n| --------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `enabled` | `false` | Participate in a cluster. Requires `--replica-id` at launch. |","table":"table-29"},"3855":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-258","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key    | Default          | One-liner                                                                                                             |\n| ------ | ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `name` | `\"iggy-cluster\"` | Permanent cluster identity, hashed into every superblock on first boot. Renaming against existing data refuses start. |","table":"table-29"},"3856":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-259","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                 | Default | One-liner                                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `heartbeat_timeout` | `\"5s\"`  | Primary liveness window. A silent primary triggers a view change. At least `\"2s\"` and at least 4 x `commit_broadcast_interval`. |","table":"table-29"},"3857":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-260","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                         | Default   | One-liner                                                                       |\n| --------------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `commit_broadcast_interval` | `\"500ms\"` | How often the primary broadcasts its commit point. This is the liveness signal. |","table":"table-29"},"3858":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-261","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                           | Default   | One-liner                                       |\n| ----------------------------- | --------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `prepare_retransmit_interval` | `\"250ms\"` | Retransmit cadence for unacknowledged prepares. |","table":"table-29"},"3859":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-262","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                               | Default   | One-liner                                |\n| --------------------------------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `view_change_retransmit_interval` | `\"500ms\"` | Retransmit cadence during a view change. |","table":"table-29"},"3860":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-263","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                          | Default | One-liner                                                                                                          |\n| ---------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `view_change_status_timeout` | `\"5s\"`  | Backstop for a stalled view change. Escalates to a fresh election. At least 4 x `view_change_retransmit_interval`. |","table":"table-29"},"3861":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-264","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                                      | Default | One-liner                                            |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `request_start_view_retransmit_interval` | `\"1s\"`  | Re-request cadence for the current view's StartView. |","table":"table-29"},"3862":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-265","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                       | Default | One-liner                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `view_probe_attempts_max` | `5`     | Unanswered probes a recovering replica tolerates before electing on its recovered log. Between 1 and 100. |","table":"table-29"},"3863":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-266","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                     | Default | One-liner                                               |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repair_retry_interval` | `\"1s\"`  | Re-request cadence for a stalled journal-repair stream. |","table":"table-29"},"3864":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-267","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                            | Default | One-liner                                                                                                                                        |\n| ------------------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `repair_gap_debounce_interval` | `\"1s\"`  | How long a committed journal gap waits before repair starts, floored at 500 ms. Nonzero. Separate from retries of an already-open repair stream. |","table":"table-29"},"3865":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-268","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                | Default | One-liner                                                                                                         |\n| ------------------ | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repair_chunk_max` | `128`   | Prepares served per repair round. Must stay strictly below `message_bus.peer_queue_capacity`. Between 1 and 1024. |","table":"table-29"},"3866":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-269","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                               | Default | One-liner                                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `superblock_wedged_fatal_timeout` | `\"2m\"`  | Exit if a metadata or partition superblock remains unwritable past this window. `\"0\"` leaves it fenced indefinitely. Nonzero values must be at least `\"30s\"`. |","table":"table-29"},"3867":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-270","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"| Key                          | Default | One-liner                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `default_replication_factor` | `\"all\"` | Copies per partition for new topics that omit `replication_factor`. Accepts `all` or 1 through 32, within cluster membership. |","table":"table-29"},"3868":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-271","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"Durations are **rounded down** to the consensus 10 ms tick. Values under 10 ms become one tick."},"3869":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-272","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"Sub-sections:"},"3870":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-273","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"`[cluster.auth]`: replica-to-replica authentication (PSK plus BLAKE3 keyed-MAC handshake). `shared_secret` must be at least 32 bytes of CSPRNG output, identical on every node. Prefer `IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET`. `previous_shared_secret` enables rolling key rotation."},"3871":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-274","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"`[cluster.coordinator]`: `skip_shard_zero_for_replicas = true` and `skip_shard_zero_for_clients = false` control connection placement when more than one shard runs. `replica_connections_per_peer = 1` controls links per peer and must match on every node."},"3872":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-275","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"`[cluster.tls]`: TLS 1.3 on the replica port. Requires `cluster.auth.enabled`. The PSK authenticates the peer, TLS supplies confidentiality."},"3873":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-276","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#cluster","content":"`[[cluster.nodes]]`: the full roster, byte-identical on every node. Each entry has `name`, `ip`, `replica_id`, and `ports` (`tcp`, `quic`, `http`, `websocket`, `tcp_replica`). In cluster mode `ports` is the single source of listener ports: `tcp` and `tcp_replica` ports are always required, and every other enabled transport also needs an explicit per-node port or the server refuses to start. `advertised_address` and per-CIDR `advertised_addresses` selectors control what clients are told to dial."},"3874":{"id":"/docs/server/docker","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"page","content":"Docker & Helm","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker"},"3875":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-0","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker","content":"Run the Iggy server from the official Docker images, and deploy it with the Helm chart."},"3876":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-1","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"Docker"},"3877":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-2","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"Why these capabilities?"},"3878":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-3","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"Available images"},"3879":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-4","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#building-from-source","content":"Building from source"},"3880":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-5","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#running-the-cli-inside-the-container","content":"Running the CLI inside the container"},"3881":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-6","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#helm-charts","content":"Helm charts"},"3882":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-7","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"3883":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-8","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"Chart components"},"3884":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-9","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#key-values","content":"Key values"},"3885":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-10","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#local-source-image","content":"Local source image"},"3886":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-11","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"You can easily run the Iggy server with Docker - the official images can be found here, use `docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0`, or `apache/iggy:latest` for the newest stable release."},"3887":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-12","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"These properties of the published image matter for deployment:"},"3888":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-13","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"The working directory is `/app` and the `iggy-server` and `iggy` binaries are on `PATH` (`/usr/local/bin`). The default data directory `local_data` therefore resolves to `/app/local_data` - **mount your volume there**, or set `IGGY_PATH` and mount that path instead."},"3889":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-14","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"The image bakes in no address overrides, so the server binds the loopback defaults (`127.0.0.1`) and is **unreachable from outside the container** even with published ports. Set `IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090` (and the equivalent for every other transport you expose) alongside the `-p` flags."},"3890":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-15","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"The wildcard says nothing about where clients reach the container, so the server refuses to start until `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` supplies that address. Use `localhost` when the ports are published to the host, the compose service name when the clients are containers on the same network, and the external hostname or load balancer name when they are further away. The value reaches clients through cluster metadata, where it is the endpoint they reconnect through."},"3891":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-16","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"Below is an example `docker-compose.yml` which overrides the default configuration (see Configuration) with environment variables. If you prefer using the configuration file, you can mount it as a volume and provide the path to it with the `IGGY_CONFIG_PATH` environment variable."},"3892":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-17","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"The examples use a permissive syscall profile and unlimited locked memory for development. Production deployments can use a custom seccomp profile and a finite locked-memory budget that permit the server's required operations. See Linux tuning."},"3893":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-18","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"Or when running with `docker run`:"},"3894":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-19","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"**`SYS_NICE`** broadens scheduling and NUMA permissions. Pinning a thread owned by the process with `sched_setaffinity` does not by itself require this capability. Container policies can impose additional restrictions. See Linux affinity permissions."},"3895":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-20","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"**`seccomp:unconfined`** permits `io_uring` calls blocked by Docker's default seccomp profile. A custom profile allowing the required syscalls is an alternative to disabling filtering. See Docker seccomp profiles."},"3896":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-21","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"**`memlock: -1`** removes the process's locked-memory limit. A finite limit is valid if it covers the runtime's requirements. Neither setting overrides the container's memory limit."},"3897":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-22","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"**Host kernel 5.19 or newer** is required. Containers use the host's kernel, so these settings alone are not enough. See System requirements."},"3898":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-23","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"| Image         | Description  |\n| ------------- | ------------ |\n| `apache/iggy` | Server + CLI |","table":"table-0"},"3899":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-24","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"| Image                | Description       |\n| -------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `apache/iggy-web-ui` | Standalone Web UI |","table":"table-0"},"3900":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-25","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"| Image                 | Description        |\n| --------------------- | ------------------ |\n| `apache/iggy-connect` | Connectors runtime |","table":"table-0"},"3901":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-26","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"| Image             | Description |\n| ----------------- | ----------- |\n| `apache/iggy-mcp` | MCP server  |","table":"table-0"},"3902":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-27","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"| Image                         | Description                                  |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `apache/iggy-bench-dashboard` | Benchmark dashboard (server + WASM frontend) |","table":"table-0"},"3903":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-28","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"Images tagged `latest` are based on stable releases. Images tagged `edge` are built from the latest `master` branch."},"3904":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-29","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#building-from-source","content":"The repository root carries its own `Dockerfile` and `docker-compose.yml` for building from source (a multi-stage build: Rust compilation including the Web UI, then a minimal runtime image):"},"3905":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-30","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#building-from-source","content":"Or use `docker compose up` directly from the repository root. That image **differs from the published one**: it sets the `0.0.0.0` address of all four listeners via `ENV` and keeps its data at `/local_data`. Those wildcards are baked into the image, so a plain `docker run` of it refuses to start until you pass `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` yourself. The repository's compose file publishes the ports to the host, so it declares `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost`."},"3906":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-31","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#running-the-cli-inside-the-container","content":"The `iggy` CLI is on `PATH` in the official image. With the compose example above (container `iggy`, password `Secret123`):"},"3907":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-32","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#helm-charts","content":"Helm charts for Kubernetes deployment are available in the repository. Almost everything server-related nests under the `server:` key in `values.yaml`. The chart also bundles a Web UI deployment under `ui:`."},"3908":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-33","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**Server Deployment** - runs `apache/iggy` with the pod security context the server needs: seccomp profile `Unconfined` (for `io_uring`) plus the `IPC_LOCK` capability (for memory locking). Listener addresses are set to `0.0.0.0` via `server.env`, and the data volume mounts at `/app/local_data`. The chart supplies `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` as the in-cluster Service DNS name. Override it with `server.advertisedAddress` when clients arrive through a LoadBalancer or an Ingress. The server image tag defaults to the chart's `appVersion` (`0.9.0-edge.6` in this source revision). The quick start selects `0.10.0` explicitly."},"3909":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-34","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**Server Service** - exposes the `http` (3000), `quic` (8080/UDP), `tcp` (8090), and `websocket` (8092) ports."},"3910":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-35","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**Web UI Deployment + Service** - a separate `apache/iggy-web-ui` deployment on port 3050, enabled by default (`ui.enabled`)."},"3911":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-36","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**Secret** - root user credentials from `server.users.root` (default `iggy`/`changeit`). Point `server.users.root.existingSecret.name` at your own Secret in production. `usernameKey` and `passwordKey` select its keys."},"3912":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-37","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**PersistentVolumeClaim** - storage for `/app/local_data`, **disabled by default** (`server.persistence.enabled`), 8Gi when enabled."},"3913":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-38","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**ServiceAccount** and **Ingress** - a shared ServiceAccount and separate optional server/UI ingresses. The chart has no HPA template. Server `replicaCount` must not exceed 1. Cluster mode uses one release per node, each with its own replica ID and storage."},"3914":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-39","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"**ServiceMonitor** - Prometheus scrape config (optional). The `/metrics` endpoint requires a bearer credential like every other read, so wire a token (e.g. a personal access token stored in a Secret) into `server.serviceMonitor.authorization` - an unauthenticated scrape gets 401."},"3915":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-40","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#key-values","content":"Save the excerpt as `my-values.yaml`, customize it for your environment, and deploy with:"},"3916":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-41","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#local-source-image","content":"The repository-root `Dockerfile` builds a local image with wildcard transport binds and `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost`. The release image uses `core/server/Dockerfile` and its `/app` data path. Keep the advertised address explicit in your deployment so reconnects work from the client network. Do not infer the release image's defaults from the local development Dockerfile."},"3917":{"id":"/docs/server/durability","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"page","content":"Durability","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability"},"3918":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-0","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability","content":"What a completed write guarantees, how to select topic durability, and what survives a single-node crash."},"3919":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-1","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"Select the policy when creating the topic"},"3920":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-2","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability#the-single-node-write-path","content":"The single-node write path"},"3921":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-3","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability#flush-scheduling-is-separate","content":"Flush scheduling is separate"},"3922":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-4","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"What survives a failure"},"3923":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-5","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"Which responses prove completion"},"3924":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-6","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/durability#recovery-and-cost","content":"Recovery and cost"},"3925":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-7","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability","content":"A successful write has the guarantee selected by the topic's `durability` option. The default, `replicated`, waits for consensus commit and local application without an additional stable-storage barrier. `persisted` also requires a recoverable copy on stable storage at the replication quorum."},"3926":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-8","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability","content":"**Both policies write data to disk.** The difference is what must have completed before the server reports success. On a single node the quorum is one, so `replicated` provides no second copy and can acknowledge messages that exist only in the server process's memory. For multiple replicas, see Cluster Durability."},"3927":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-9","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"| Topic option | Default      | Controls           |\n| ------------ | ------------ | ------------------ |\n| `durability` | `replicated` | Message production |","table":"table-0"},"3928":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-10","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"| Topic option                 | Default      | Controls                                    |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------ | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `consumer_offset_durability` | `replicated` | Explicit consumer-offset stores and deletes |","table":"table-0"},"3929":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-11","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"Both accept `replicated` or `persisted`. They default **independently**: setting `durability=persisted` leaves consumer offsets at `replicated` unless that option is also supplied. Neither inherits the other."},"3930":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-12","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"For persisted messages and consumer offsets, create a topic in an existing stream:"},"3931":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-13","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"These are topic creation options, not `config.toml` settings or server environment overrides. `GetTopic` reports the effective values. Both policies are create-only. Resource updates cannot change them on an existing topic."},"3932":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-14","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#select-the-policy-when-creating-the-topic","content":"The former `enforce_fsync` topic option is rejected. Use `durability` for messages and `consumer_offset_durability` for offsets. The old server-wide `consumer_offset_enforce_fsync` setting has also been removed."},"3933":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-15","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#the-single-node-write-path","content":"With `replicated`, the server commits and applies the operation locally. Message batches can remain in the in-memory partition journal after success. Ordinary segment writes still happen when a flush trigger fires, and a write that triggers a flush can wait for that I/O, but success does not require stable-storage synchronization."},"3934":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-16","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#the-single-node-write-path","content":"With `persisted`, successful message completion follows this path:"},"3935":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-17","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#the-single-node-write-path","content":"The committed batches are flushed even below the ordinary flush thresholds. **Setting `durability=persisted` is sufficient. Setting `messages_required_to_save=1` is not required.** A single-replica partition does not use the cluster's prepare WAL."},"3936":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-18","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#the-single-node-write-path","content":"For explicit offset changes, `consumer_offset_durability=persisted` similarly waits for the required local file and directory synchronization before success. On a singleton, persisting an offset does not force earlier `replicated` messages to be flushed. Select both policies as `persisted` when both messages and their stored offsets need that protection."},"3937":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-19","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#flush-scheduling-is-separate","content":"| Topic option                | Default | Effect                                            |\n| --------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `messages_required_to_save` | `1024`  | Message-count trigger for ordinary segment writes |","table":"table-1"},"3938":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-20","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#flush-scheduling-is-separate","content":"| Topic option                        | Default | Effect                                         |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `size_of_messages_required_to_save` | `1 MiB` | Byte-count trigger for ordinary segment writes |","table":"table-1"},"3939":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-21","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#flush-scheduling-is-separate","content":"| Topic option   | Default | Effect                                         |\n| -------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `segment_size` | `1 GiB` | Soft segment limit. A whole batch can cross it |","table":"table-1"},"3940":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-22","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#flush-scheduling-is-separate","content":"A count or byte trigger, or a full active segment, can flush the committed journal prefix. Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle work can flush earlier. These thresholds are not a maximum data-loss guarantee and do not specify a periodic flush interval. A quiet `replicated` topic can retain messages in process memory while waiting for a trigger."},"3941":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-23","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#flush-scheduling-is-separate","content":"Reducing the thresholds changes batching and I/O frequency. It does not give `replicated` the stable-storage guarantee of `persisted`."},"3942":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-24","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"This table describes acknowledged messages on one node, with no later retention or deletion:"},"3943":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-25","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"| Failure                                      | `replicated`                   | `persisted`                                              |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Process crash, `SIGKILL`, panic, or OOM kill | Unflushed messages can be lost | Acknowledged messages are recoverable from local storage |","table":"table-2"},"3944":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-26","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"| Failure                                    | `replicated`                                                  | `persisted`                                                                 |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Machine crash, kernel panic, or power loss | Unflushed messages and unsynchronized file writes can be lost | Acknowledged messages are recoverable if the storage honors synchronization |","table":"table-2"},"3945":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-27","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"| Failure                                    | `replicated`                          | `persisted`                           |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| Storage device lost or irreparably damaged | No surviving local copy is guaranteed | No surviving local copy is guaranteed |","table":"table-2"},"3946":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-28","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"Completed buffered file writes normally remain in Linux's page cache after the server process dies. Messages still in the process's journal do not. Page cache alone does not protect against machine or power failure."},"3947":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-29","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#what-survives-a-failure","content":"A graceful shutdown drains work and forces a final flush of committed messages, regardless of the thresholds. This depends on the shutdown finishing and its I/O succeeding. A container or service termination that expires its grace period and sends `SIGKILL` is a process crash, not a completed graceful shutdown."},"3948":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-30","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"The policy applies to the completed server operation, not merely to enqueueing a request in a producer or receiving an early dispatch response."},"3949":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-31","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"For HTTP production:"},"3950":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-32","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"| Request                       | Response                                                    | Meaning                                              |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| Default, or `?ack=replicated` | `201 Created`, `Iggy-Durability: replicated` or `persisted` | The write completed under the topic's message policy |","table":"table-3"},"3951":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-33","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"| Request     | Response                                | Meaning                                                        |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `?ack=none` | `202 Accepted`, `Iggy-Durability: none` | Dispatch was accepted. Commit and persistence were not awaited |","table":"table-3"},"3952":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-34","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"The query value `ack=replicated` selects the awaited path even when the topic uses `persisted`. It does not override the topic policy."},"3953":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-35","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#which-responses-prove-completion","content":"**Poll auto-commit is asynchronous.** A successful poll does not confirm that its offset write committed or became durable, even with `consumer_offset_durability=persisted`. When processing needs an explicit offset completion guarantee, store the offset explicitly and await success."},"3954":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-36","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#recovery-and-cost","content":"Recovery validates segment batches and partition identity. It can truncate an incomplete tail and rebuild a missing or torn sparse index. Interior damage, gaps, or intact records beyond damaged data can require refusal or peer repair instead of truncation. A single node cannot fetch a missing copy from another replica. See Storage Engine."},"3955":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-37","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#recovery-and-cost","content":"`persisted` adds synchronization to the completion path. Its latency and throughput depend on storage, filesystem, batching, and concurrency. Measure both policies on the intended hardware rather than applying results from the removed `enforce_fsync` configuration. The benchmarking guide explains how to reproduce a workload."},"3956":{"id":"/docs/server/durability-38","page_id":"/docs/server/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/durability#recovery-and-cost","content":"The implementation references are the topic policy definitions, partition completion paths, and HTTP response handling."},"3957":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","type":"page","content":"External Authentication","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/external-authentication"},"3958":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-0","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication","content":"Delegate unknown-user logins to an HTTP service that maps callers to existing Iggy accounts."},"3959":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-1","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#configure-the-service","content":"Configure the service"},"3960":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-2","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#request-and-response","content":"Request and response"},"3961":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-3","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#failure-and-replay","content":"Failure and replay"},"3962":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-4","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication","content":"An external authentication callout delegates a login decision to an HTTP service. Iggy calls it only when the submitted username does not exist locally. A wrong password for a local user never falls back to the service. Personal access tokens and HTTP bearer validation use their existing authentication paths."},"3963":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-5","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#configure-the-service","content":"Provision the target Iggy account first. It must be active and cannot be root. Give it the permissions that mapped callers need. The service maps identities to this account. It does not create accounts or supply permissions."},"3964":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-6","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#configure-the-service","content":"`url` names the HTTPS endpoint. `IGGY_EXTERNAL_AUTH_URL` sets it from the environment and keeps it out of the file. The server sends the submitted password to this endpoint. Protect both the client connection and the callout with TLS."},"3965":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-7","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#configure-the-service","content":"The concurrency cap applies per shard, so total in-flight callouts can reach the shard count multiplied by this value. There is no per-client rate limit in the callout. Apply one at the service or network boundary if required."},"3966":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-8","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#request-and-response","content":"Iggy sends a JSON `POST`:"},"3967":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-9","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#request-and-response","content":"`transport` is `tcp`, `tcp_tls`, `ws`, `wss`, `quic` or `http`. Binary connections keep their connection identity across reconnect retries. HTTP creates a new UUID for each login request."},"3968":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-10","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#request-and-response","content":"The service must return a successful HTTP status and a JSON decision:"},"3969":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-11","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#request-and-response","content":"To reject the login:"},"3970":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-12","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#request-and-response","content":"The reason is optional. An allowed username must resolve to the provisioned account at admission. The server rejects malformed decisions and refuses redirects from the callout endpoint."},"3971":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-13","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#failure-and-replay","content":"The normal-status metadata primary performs callouts. Other nodes and view changes can produce retryable errors. Timeouts, network failures, overload, HTTP 429 and HTTP 5xx map to retryable failures. Other unsuccessful HTTP responses and invalid decisions terminate the login."},"3972":{"id":"/docs/server/external-authentication-14","page_id":"/docs/server/external-authentication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/external-authentication#failure-and-replay","content":"The SDK can replay a login. The service must accept repeated verification of the same credentials safely. One-time passwords that become invalid after the first verification do not survive this replay model. A failed external service never grants access by default."},"3973":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/introduction"},"3974":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction","content":"What the Iggy server does, and where its releases and Docker images are published."},"3975":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"System requirements"},"3976":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"Running the server"},"3977":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/introduction#authentication","content":"Authentication"},"3978":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction","content":"Iggy server is the most important part of the system as it's responsible for handling all the incoming connections, managing the data and providing the API for the clients. The server is written in Rust. It uses `io_uring` on Linux and a polling backend on macOS."},"3979":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-5","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction","content":"The releases are published to GitHub and can be found here. The official Docker images can be found here, use `docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0`, or `apache/iggy:latest` for the newest stable release."},"3980":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-6","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction","content":"If you compile the source code in release mode, linking takes longer because LTO is enabled in the `[profile.release]` section of the workspace Cargo.toml."},"3981":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-7","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"The server uses `io_uring` on Linux, which sets a minimum kernel version."},"3982":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-8","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"**Linux kernel 5.19 or newer is required.** The shard executors create their rings with `IORING_SETUP_COOP_TASKRUN` and `IORING_SETUP_TASKRUN_FLAG`, which older kernels reject, and the server refuses to start rather than run without them. Check yours with `uname -r`."},"3983":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-9","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"**Kernel 6.1 or newer is worth having.** It is the first version with the `kernel.io_uring_disabled` sysctl, so the startup diagnostics can tell you io_uring was disabled by policy instead of failing obscurely. See Linux tuning."},"3984":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-10","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"**Ubuntu 22.04 LTS ships 5.15 and will not run the server.** Its hardware enablement kernel is new enough, and so are Ubuntu 24.04 LTS and Debian 12 as they ship."},"3985":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-11","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"**WSL2 often ships incomplete `io_uring`**, missing setup flags or operations even on a kernel that reports 5.19 or newer. Run `wsl --update` first."},"3986":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-12","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"**macOS uses a polling backend** rather than `io_uring` when the server runs natively, so this requirement does not apply there. Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"3987":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-13","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#system-requirements","content":"Containers use the host's kernel, so it is the host that has to meet this requirement. See Docker & Helm."},"3988":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-14","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"One `iggy-server` binary serves both the single-node and the clustered deployment. The loaded configuration decides which one you get. The server accepts these startup flags, plus `--help` and `--version`:"},"3989":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-15","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"| Flag      | Purpose                                                                          |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--fresh` | Delete the data directory before starting. **This wipes all data** on that node. |","table":"table-0"},"3990":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-16","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"| Flag                              | Purpose                                                                                                                                       |\n| --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--with-default-root-credentials` | Set the root credentials to `iggy`/`iggy` on first start, unless `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME`/`IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` are already set. Development only. |","table":"table-0"},"3991":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-17","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"| Flag               | Purpose                                                                                              |\n| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--replica-id <N>` | Select this node's entry in `cluster.nodes`. Required when `cluster.enabled = true`. See Clustering. |","table":"table-0"},"3992":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-18","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"Configuration comes from the TOML file named by `IGGY_CONFIG_PATH`, or `core/server/config.toml` relative to the working directory. If that file is missing, the server uses the defaults embedded in the binary. Any key can be overridden with an `IGGY_`-prefixed environment variable, and a `.env` file in the working directory or its parents (or the file named by `IGGY_ENV_PATH`) is loaded at startup. See Configuration for the full reference."},"3993":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-19","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"A fresh standalone server requires `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD`, or the development flag. It never generates a password. A cluster replica without credentials waits for the root account to be replicated. Provision credentials on every replica for first cluster boot."},"3994":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-20","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"The HTTP API endpoints can be found in server.http file, which can be used with REST Client extension for VS Code."},"3995":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-21","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"In order to see the detailed logs from the server, run it with `RUST_LOG=trace` environment variable."},"3996":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-22","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"To seed the example data, start a development server with known credentials from the root of the repository:"},"3997":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-23","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"In another terminal at the repository root:"},"3998":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-24","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#running-the-server","content":"The seeder logs in as `iggy`/`iggy` by default. Pass `--username` and `--password` to use other credentials."},"3999":{"id":"/docs/server/introduction-25","page_id":"/docs/server/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/introduction#authentication","content":"For broker API commands, only the ping liveness probe and the login handshake itself (username/password, personal access token, or HTTP token refresh, all of which prove a credential) are served without an authenticated session. Every other request requires one and is subject to permissions: fetching server stats, for example, needs the `read_servers` permission (the root user has it), and even the Prometheus `/metrics` scrape **must present a bearer credential**. A stateful connection authenticates by logging in with the user's credentials or personal access token. A retained session survives transport loss. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end it. Over the HTTP API, authentication is done by providing the `Authorization` header with a `Bearer` JWT or personal access token. HTTP CORS preflight responses and embedded `/ui` static assets are public. The UI's broker API calls still require authentication."},"4000":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"page","content":"Linux tuning","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning"},"4001":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-0","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","content":"Prepare Linux hosts for Iggy and measure the effects of memory, CPU, storage, and network tuning."},"4002":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-1","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#inspect-the-host-first","content":"Inspect the host first"},"4003":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-2","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#runtime-access-and-process-limits","content":"Runtime access and process limits"},"4004":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-3","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#swapping-and-writeback","content":"Swapping and writeback"},"4005":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-4","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#huge-pages-and-mimalloc","content":"Huge pages and mimalloc"},"4006":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-5","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"Explicit large pages"},"4007":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-6","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#transparent-huge-pages","content":"Transparent huge pages"},"4008":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-7","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#cpu-placement-and-numa","content":"CPU placement and NUMA"},"4009":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-8","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#network-and-storage","content":"Network and storage"},"4010":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-9","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#apply-verify-and-reproduce","content":"Apply, verify, and reproduce"},"4011":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-10","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","content":"Host settings affect both production latency and benchmark results. Start with the distribution defaults, satisfy Iggy's runtime requirements, then change one setting at a time. The examples below are experiments for dedicated Linux hosts, not a configuration to apply unchanged to every VM."},"4012":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-11","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","content":"Use the benchmarking guide to compare throughput, p50, p99 and p99.9 latency, memory use, and errors under the same workload. Keep the server version, transport, topic durability, CPU allocation, and storage identical between runs."},"4013":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-12","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#inspect-the-host-first","content":"Run these read-only checks before changing the VM image or starting a benchmark:"},"4014":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-13","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#inspect-the-host-first","content":"The shell's limits are not necessarily the service's limits. For a running process, inspect `/proc/PID/limits`, `/proc/PID/status`, and its cgroup limits. Replace `PID` with that process's ID. Record the VM type, vCPU topology, memory limit, disk type and provisioned throughput, filesystem, and network bandwidth with the result."},"4015":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-14","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#runtime-access-and-process-limits","content":"Iggy uses `io_uring`. The kernel must implement the operations required by the server build, and the service or container must be allowed to use them. The server's startup diagnostics identify failed ring creation and unsupported operations. Where the kernel exposes them, inspect:"},"4016":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-15","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#runtime-access-and-process-limits","content":"`io_uring_disabled=0` permits ring creation. With `1`, an unprivileged service needs membership in the configured `io_uring_group`. With `2`, creation is disabled. Container syscall filters can independently deny access. Configure access for the service rather than assuming that a root shell proves the container can start. See the kernel's io_uring controls and Docker deployment guidance."},"4017":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-16","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#runtime-access-and-process-limits","content":"Size file-descriptor limits for connections, partitions, open segment files, and runtime overhead. `LimitNOFILE=65536` is an example starting budget, not an Iggy requirement. Increase it if measurements justify more. A systemd service can use the following drop-in:"},"4018":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-17","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#runtime-access-and-process-limits","content":"Unlimited locked memory is an option for a dedicated service with a planned memory budget. A finite limit is also valid if it covers the runtime's locked-memory requirements. Neither setting creates RAM or overrides a container's memory ceiling."},"4019":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-18","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#runtime-access-and-process-limits","content":"Recheck the actual process limits after restarting the service. PAM settings in `/etc/security/limits.conf` do not configure a system service. See systemd resource limits."},"4020":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-19","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#swapping-and-writeback","content":"On a host with disk-backed swap and enough RAM for the workload, try `vm.swappiness=10` and compare it with the existing value. It reduces the preference for swapping relative to reclaiming file cache. It does not disable swapping, and even `0` permits swapping under sufficient pressure. zram and zswap can warrant different values. See Linux swappiness."},"4021":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-20","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#swapping-and-writeback","content":"Leave memory for the kernel, page cache, other processes, and Iggy's buffers. Watch swap-in/out with `vmstat 1` and memory pressure in `/proc/pressure/memory`. Disabling swap does not fix an undersized memory budget."},"4022":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-21","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#swapping-and-writeback","content":"Dirty-page limits govern buffered writeback. Large percentage limits, such as `dirty_background_ratio=10` and `dirty_ratio=30`, can permit large bursts on high-memory VMs. If writeback coincides with latency spikes, compare explicit byte limits sized from disk throughput and the acceptable backlog."},"4023":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-22","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#swapping-and-writeback","content":"Byte and ratio forms are alternatives. Writing one disables its corresponding other form. These settings do not replace Iggy's persistence barriers. See Linux writeback controls."},"4024":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-23","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#huge-pages-and-mimalloc","content":"Huge pages can reduce translation overhead, but they consume memory differently from ordinary pages. Measure both throughput and tail latency. Iggy's default server build uses mimalloc. Check the startup allocator message, since builds that disable mimalloc do not use its environment options."},"4025":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-24","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#huge-pages-and-mimalloc","content":"There are three distinct controls:"},"4026":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-25","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#huge-pages-and-mimalloc","content":"| Control                      | Meaning                                                                                 |\n| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Transparent huge pages (THP) | Kernel promotion of eligible mappings. Inspect the top-level and per-size THP policies. |","table":"table-0"},"4027":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-26","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#huge-pages-and-mimalloc","content":"| Control             | Meaning                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Linux HugeTLB pools | Explicitly reserved pages. `vm.nr_hugepages` counts pages of the default size reported by `Hugepagesize`. |","table":"table-0"},"4028":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-27","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#huge-pages-and-mimalloc","content":"| Control                      | Meaning                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| mimalloc environment options | Tell the allocator which page mechanisms to request. They are not kernel pool sizes. |","table":"table-0"},"4029":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-28","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"First verify the default page size and leave sufficient ordinary memory. If `Hugepagesize` is `2048 kB`, 512 reserved pages consume 1 GiB:"},"4030":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-29","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"The requested count may not be allocated on a fragmented host. Boot-time reservation is more reliable. Reserved HugeTLB memory is unavailable for ordinary allocations or page cache, even while unused. Check NUMA placement and the actual pool, not just the requested count. See HugeTLB administration."},"4031":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-30","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"Try the allocator option on a mimalloc-enabled server, with access to the pool configured for its service account:"},"4032":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-31","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"Set allocator variables in the launching environment or the service's `Environment=` entries before process startup. The server can allocate before loading its `.env` file. Verbose allocator output is useful for checking the experiment, then turn it off for timed runs."},"4033":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-32","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"**Do not copy a Linux page count into `MIMALLOC_RESERVE_HUGE_OS_PAGES`.** That mimalloc option counts **1 GiB pages**. A value of `2048` requests 2 TiB, whereas `vm.nr_hugepages=2048` represents 4 GiB only when the default HugeTLB page size is 2 MiB. A 2 MiB pool does not provide a matching 1 GiB reservation."},"4034":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-33","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#explicit-large-pages","content":"A separate 1 GiB-page experiment requires platform support, a suitable pool, and a budget for each process. mimalloc advises normally choosing this reservation mode separately from `MIMALLOC_ALLOW_LARGE_OS_PAGES`. See mimalloc environment options. HugeTLB allocation can require `CAP_IPC_LOCK` or membership in `vm.hugetlb_shm_group`, as described in mmap permissions."},"4035":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-34","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#transparent-huge-pages","content":"Inspect the current policy instead of universally enabling or disabling THP:"},"4036":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-35","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#transparent-huge-pages","content":"Compare the existing policy with `madvise` if allocator page promotion or compaction is relevant. Direct reclaim and compaction can stall allocations. On recent kernels, per-size policies and explicit `MADV_COLLAPSE` requests also matter, so the top-level switch alone does not describe all behavior. See transparent huge-page controls."},"4037":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-36","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#transparent-huge-pages","content":"Verify actual usage in `/proc/PID/smaps`: `AnonHugePages` reports PMD-sized anonymous THP, while `Private_Hugetlb` and `Shared_Hugetlb` identify explicit huge-page mappings. A successful pool reservation alone does not prove Iggy used it."},"4038":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-37","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#cpu-placement-and-numa","content":"Allocate dedicated CPUs where possible. On the current server, `[sharding] cpu_allocation` and `pin_cores` control shard placement, and the process's allowed CPU set constrains allocation. Check the embedded configuration for the exact version being deployed. Separate server CPUs from a colocated load generator and leave capacity for kernel and network work."},"4039":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-38","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#cpu-placement-and-numa","content":"Inspect NUMA topology with `lscpu` or `numactl --hardware`. Compare physical-core-only placement with SMT when relevant. Match memory placement and any per-node huge-page reservation to the CPUs running the shards. Avoid assuming that allocating a pool on one NUMA node helps shards running on another."},"4040":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-39","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#cpu-placement-and-numa","content":"For a reproducible dedicated-host experiment, compare the `performance` CPU governor with the current governor if the guest exposes CPU frequency control. Power and thermal limits can still constrain frequency. Many cloud guests cannot control the host's governor. Record the setting rather than assuming a command changed it. See CPU performance scaling."},"4041":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-40","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#cpu-placement-and-numa","content":"Inspect CPU steal time, cgroup throttling, and per-core utilization before increasing shard counts. If one core handles most interrupts or receive processing, inspect RSS queue distribution and IRQ affinity. Do not disable irqbalance or assign every IRQ to a fixed core without measuring. See Linux network scaling."},"4042":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-41","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#network-and-storage","content":"Socket-buffer requirements depend on bandwidth and round-trip time. For example, 10 Gbit/s across 10 ms has a bandwidth-delay product of about 12.5 MB. If the connection is window-limited, inspect `net.core.rmem_max`, `net.core.wmem_max`, `net.ipv4.tcp_rmem`, and `net.ipv4.tcp_wmem`. Larger ceilings permit larger buffers, but do not force the application to use them or guarantee higher throughput."},"4043":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-42","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#network-and-storage","content":"Raise `somaxconn`, `tcp_max_syn_backlog`, or `netdev_max_backlog` only when backlog pressure or drops show the need. QUIC uses UDP, so TCP tuning does not configure its flow-control windows or UDP receive buffers."},"4044":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-43","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#network-and-storage","content":"Keep changes such as `tcp_tw_reuse`, shorter TCP timeouts, and wider ephemeral-port ranges tied to a demonstrated connection-churn problem. See TCP controls and core networking controls."},"4045":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-44","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#network-and-storage","content":"Use storage with measured sustained bandwidth and latency, and record cloud volume and instance limits. Keep free space for segments, WAL, and recovery. Compare warm-cache and cold-cache runs explicitly. Do not periodically drop caches or disable storage flushes to improve a benchmark number. Storage, retention, and the selected durability policy must match the workload being evaluated."},"4046":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-45","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#apply-verify-and-reproduce","content":"Trial a change with a runtime setting first. Keep the previous value for rollback. Persist accepted sysctl values in a dedicated `/etc/sysctl.d/` file and accepted service limits or allocator variables in that service's systemd drop-in. THP sysfs writes require a boot-time mechanism of their own. Recheck effective values after reboot and process restart."},"4047":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-46","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#apply-verify-and-reproduce","content":"Collect `vmstat 1`, `iostat -xz 1`, `mpstat -P ALL 1`, pressure counters, and network drop/retransmission counters alongside benchmark results. The iostat and mpstat tools are provided by the sysstat package. Run long enough to include segment flushes, WAL checkpoints, and sustained device limits, rather than measuring only buffered admission."},"4048":{"id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning-47","page_id":"/docs/server/linux-tuning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/linux-tuning#apply-verify-and-reproduce","content":"Iggy's topic `durability` and `consumer_offset_durability` independently default to `replicated`. `persisted` changes completion guarantees, and huge pages or sysctl settings do not replace it. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous. Record both policies, replication-group size, payload and batch sizes, warmup, CPU allocation, and the exact host changes with each benchmark result."},"4049":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","type":"page","content":"Monitoring","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/monitoring"},"4050":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-0","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring","content":"Scrape the server's Prometheus metrics: the token the endpoint needs, what it exports, and which metrics are worth alerting on."},"4051":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-1","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/monitoring#get-a-token-for-the-scraper","content":"Get a token for the scraper"},"4052":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-2","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"What's exported"},"4053":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-3","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/monitoring#what-to-alert-on","content":"What to alert on"},"4054":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-4","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/monitoring#grafana-and-opentelemetry","content":"Grafana and OpenTelemetry"},"4055":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-5","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/monitoring#topic-usage-and-replica-progress","content":"Topic usage and replica progress"},"4056":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-6","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring","content":"The server exports Prometheus metrics on its HTTP API at `/metrics`. Both the HTTP API and the metrics endpoint are on by default. See `[http.metrics]` to change the path or turn it off."},"4057":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-7","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring","content":"This page covers the server's own metrics. The connectors runtime has a separate endpoint with its own metric names. See Connectors observability."},"4058":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-8","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#get-a-token-for-the-scraper","content":"`/metrics` needs a bearer token, like every other read on the HTTP API. Any logged-in user can scrape it and gets the aggregate gauges. The per-topic series need the `read_servers` or `manage_servers` permission. There's no separate monitoring permission. Prometheus can't log in, so create a personal access token and put it in the scrape config:"},"4059":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-9","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#get-a-token-for-the-scraper","content":"The token is printed once. The Prometheus scrape config is in `[http.metrics]`."},"4060":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-10","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#get-a-token-for-the-scraper","content":"The token can read every metric the server exports, so store it like any other secret: in a Kubernetes Secret or your secrets manager, not in a file you commit. With the Helm chart, set it through `server.serviceMonitor.authorization`. See Docker & Helm."},"4061":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-11","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"The core metrics are below. The gauges are read fresh on every scrape."},"4062":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-12","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"| Metric                | Type    | What it counts    |\n| --------------------- | ------- | ----------------- |\n| `http_requests_total` | counter | HTTP API requests |","table":"table-0"},"4063":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-13","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"| Metric                                        | Type  | What it counts         |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ----- | ---------------------- |\n| `streams`, `topics`, `partitions`, `segments` | gauge | Current number of each |","table":"table-0"},"4064":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-14","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"| Metric     | Type  | What it counts  |\n| ---------- | ----- | --------------- |\n| `messages` | gauge | Messages stored |","table":"table-0"},"4065":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-15","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"| Metric  | Type  | What it counts |\n| ------- | ----- | -------------- |\n| `users` | gauge | Users          |","table":"table-0"},"4066":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-16","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"| Metric    | Type  | What it counts    |\n| --------- | ----- | ----------------- |\n| `clients` | gauge | Connected clients |","table":"table-0"},"4067":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-17","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"The names have no `iggy_` prefix, so watch for clashes if Prometheus scrapes other exporters with similar names."},"4068":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-18","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#whats-exported","content":"Each shard also exports its own lower-level metrics, labelled `shard=\"<id>\"`. These cover the write-ahead log (the `partition_wal_*` series), partition housekeeping (`partitions_*`), and requests or frames the server refused or dropped. To see the full list with descriptions, scrape a running server:"},"4069":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-19","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#what-to-alert-on","content":"**`frame_drops_total{variant=\"forward_client_send\"}`.** Any increase means a client never got a reply to a request. Most other `frame_drops_total` variants are retried or repaired automatically. This series only appears after the first drop, so on a healthy server it won't be in the scrape at all. Write the alert so that a missing series counts as no drops."},"4070":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-20","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#what-to-alert-on","content":"**`stats_rollup_underflows_total`.** It goes up when a stream, topic or partition total would have dropped below zero and was held at zero instead. A few during deletes or restores are expected. A steady rise at other times is worth a look."},"4071":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-21","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#grafana-and-opentelemetry","content":"Add Prometheus as a data source in Grafana, then build panels from the metric names above."},"4072":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-22","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#grafana-and-opentelemetry","content":"The server doesn't export metrics over OTLP. To bring them into an OpenTelemetry pipeline, scrape `/metrics` with the collector's Prometheus receiver, using the same bearer token. Server export of logs and traces over OTLP isn't available yet. See `[telemetry]`."},"4073":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-23","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#topic-usage-and-replica-progress","content":"`topic_size_bytes`, `topic_messages` and `topic_max_size_bytes` use `stream` and `topic` labels. The size-cap series is absent for unlimited topics. Renamed or deleted topics disappear from the next scrape. Retention can keep more bytes than the configured cap, so a usage ratio is not a hard storage boundary."},"4074":{"id":"/docs/server/monitoring-24","page_id":"/docs/server/monitoring","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/monitoring#topic-usage-and-replica-progress","content":"Replica counters can lag a successful send until a prepare or commit broadcast updates them. Compare node-local observations with routing and view-change events before treating a transient difference as data loss. Repair-ring metrics report retained batch bytes and released-entry accounting, not total process memory."},"4075":{"id":"/docs/server/networking","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"page","content":"Networking","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking"},"4076":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-0","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking","content":"The four transports Iggy serves simultaneously, TCP, QUIC, WebSocket and HTTP, and what each is best suited to."},"4077":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-1","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#transport-protocols","content":"Transport protocols"},"4078":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-2","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#binary-protocol","content":"Binary protocol"},"4079":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-3","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#connection-handling-across-shards","content":"Connection handling across shards"},"4080":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-4","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#tcp","content":"TCP"},"4081":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-5","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"QUIC"},"4082":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-6","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#http","content":"HTTP"},"4083":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-7","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#websocket","content":"WebSocket"},"4084":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-8","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"TLS"},"4085":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-9","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#heartbeat","content":"Heartbeat"},"4086":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-10","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/networking#cluster-networking","content":"Cluster networking"},"4087":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-11","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking","content":"Iggy supports four transport protocols simultaneously, each optimized for different use cases. All stateful protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket) use the same custom binary wire protocol, while HTTP provides a standard REST API."},"4088":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-12","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#transport-protocols","content":"| Protocol | Default Address  | Implementation                  | Best for                           |\n| -------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| TCP      | `127.0.0.1:8090` | compio + custom binary protocol | Highest throughput, lowest latency |","table":"table-0"},"4089":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-13","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#transport-protocols","content":"| Protocol | Default Address  | Implementation      | Best for                          |\n| -------- | ---------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| QUIC     | `127.0.0.1:8080` | compio-quic (quinn) | Secure connections out of the box |","table":"table-0"},"4090":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-14","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#transport-protocols","content":"| Protocol | Default Address  | Implementation | Best for                   |\n| -------- | ---------------- | -------------- | -------------------------- |\n| HTTP     | `127.0.0.1:3000` | axum           | REST API, web integrations |","table":"table-0"},"4091":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-15","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#transport-protocols","content":"| Protocol  | Default Address  | Implementation | Best for                                 |\n| --------- | ---------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| WebSocket | `127.0.0.1:8092` | compio-ws      | Browser clients, bidirectional streaming |","table":"table-0"},"4092":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-16","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#transport-protocols","content":"**Performance ranking** (lowest to highest overhead): TCP > QUIC > WebSocket > HTTP"},"4093":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-17","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#binary-protocol","content":"Every request and reply on the stateful transports starts with a fixed 256-byte header (the same Viewstamped Replication header layout the cluster uses internally), followed by the command payload. The `iggy_binary_protocol` crate provides zero-copy serialization for these headers, and foreign SDKs implement the same contract natively. See the Binary Protocol section for the full specification."},"4094":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-18","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#connection-handling-across-shards","content":"The server runs one thread and runtime per selected shard (thread-per-core, shared-nothing), with CPU selection and optional pinning controlled by `[sharding]`. All listeners (every client transport) bind on shard 0 only. Shard 0's coordinator hands accepted plaintext TCP and WebSocket connections to peer shards round-robin by transferring the file descriptor at accept time. From then on the connection lives entirely on its shard. QUIC, TCP with TLS, WebSocket with TLS, and HTTP terminate on shard 0, because their per-connection state cannot move between threads. Sockets **never migrate** after accept: an operation that touches a partition owned by another shard rides the internal message bus instead."},"4095":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-19","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tcp","content":"TCP provides the highest performance as it works directly with binary data without the overhead of HTTP or TLS handshakes (unless TLS is enabled). The full set of keys:"},"4096":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-20","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"QUIC runs the same binary protocol over UDP, on top of the quinn library, with TLS built in. It isn't quite as fast as TCP, but security comes out of the box (TLS is **mandatory**). Defaults:"},"4097":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-21","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"A few of these are deliberate rather than arbitrary:"},"4098":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-22","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"`max_concurrent_bidi_streams = 256` bounds concurrent bidirectional streams on a connection. Deferred requests can wait while other requests use separate streams."},"4099":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-23","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"`initial_mtu = \"1200 B\"` is the QUIC minimum. MTU discovery probes upward automatically, and boot rejects values below 1200."},"4100":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-24","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"`stream_receive_window` is the per-stream limit within the shared connection receive window. Both default to 64 MiB."},"4101":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-25","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#quic","content":"`keep_alive_interval` is one third of `max_idle_timeout`, so two consecutive lost keep-alives fit before the idle timer closes the connection."},"4102":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-26","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#http","content":"The HTTP API is built on axum and provides a standard REST interface. It includes JWT authentication, CORS configuration, and optional TLS."},"4103":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-27","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#http","content":"HTTP is the most accessible protocol but has the highest overhead due to JSON serialization and the stateless nature of HTTP (no persistent connections for consumer groups). You can find request examples in the server.http file."},"4104":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-28","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#http","content":"The HTTP server also hosts:"},"4105":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-29","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#http","content":"**Prometheus metrics** at `/metrics` (endpoint configurable via `[http.metrics]`). The route authenticates like every other read: scrapers must present a bearer credential (JWT or personal access token). The `/ping` API route, CORS preflight responses, and enabled `/ui` static assets are public. Login and refresh routes prove credentials in the request body."},"4106":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-30","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#http","content":"**Embedded Web UI** at `/ui`, which requires `web_ui = true` in `[http]` (**off by default**) on a server built with the `iggy-web` feature (part of the default build) and the Web UI static assets. Build the assets before compiling the server. See Configuration."},"4107":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-31","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#websocket","content":"WebSocket provides bidirectional streaming over HTTP upgrade. Iggy uses the `compio-ws` crate to connect tungstenite framing to compio's completion-based I/O. The default WebSocket read buffer is 128 KiB and the default maximum message size is 64 MiB. These are separate settings."},"4108":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-32","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#websocket","content":"The `[websocket]` section exposes the frame-tuning knobs (`read_buffer_size`, `write_buffer_size`, `max_write_buffer_size`, `max_message_size`, `max_frame_size`, `accept_unmasked_frames`). See Configuration. TLS for WebSocket has its own `[websocket.tls]` section."},"4109":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-33","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#websocket","content":"**Historical benchmark comparison** from the WebSocket implementation article (AWS i3en.3xlarge, 4 producers or consumers, 40M messages, 1,000 messages per batch, fsync enabled). These measurements predate 0.10.0:"},"4110":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-34","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#websocket","content":"Producer avg latency: TCP 2.61ms vs WebSocket 3.43ms (+31%)"},"4111":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-35","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#websocket","content":"Consumer avg latency: TCP 0.70ms vs WebSocket 1.44ms (+106%)"},"4112":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-36","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"Each transport configures TLS in its own section, and the `self_signed` semantics differ per transport:"},"4113":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-37","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"| Transport | Section     | Behavior                                                                                                                               |\n| --------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| TCP       | `[tcp.tls]` | `self_signed = true` generates an ephemeral certificate only while `cert_file` does not exist. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead. |","table":"table-1"},"4114":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-38","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"| Transport | Section           | Behavior                           |\n| --------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| WebSocket | `[websocket.tls]` | Same load-or-generate rule as TCP. |","table":"table-1"},"4115":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-39","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"| Transport | Section              | Behavior                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| --------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| QUIC      | `[quic.certificate]` | TLS is mandatory (part of the QUIC spec). `self_signed = true` **always** generates an ephemeral certificate and ignores `cert_file`/`key_file`, logging a warning if the files exist. |","table":"table-1"},"4116":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-40","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"| Transport | Section      | Behavior                                                                                     |\n| --------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTTP      | `[http.tls]` | No `self_signed` option: HTTPS requires certificate and key files in `cert_file`/`key_file`. |","table":"table-1"},"4117":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-41","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#tls","content":"With `self_signed = false`, `cert_file` and `key_file` must both exist. For production, provide proper certificate files everywhere. Ephemeral certificates **change on every start**, so a trust configuration pinned to one generated certificate does not survive a restart."},"4118":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-42","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#heartbeat","content":"The per-shard verifier evicts stale group-member connections. A link eviction does not itself end the logical session. The metadata liveness process expires its lease and rebalances membership. The session timeout is 1.2 times `heartbeat.interval`, 36 seconds by default, with periodic checks around that threshold."},"4119":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-43","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#heartbeat","content":"SDKs maintain heartbeats after connection. The Java blocking client delegates to the async TCP client and uses its heartbeat. Reconnect binds a retained session before creating a new one. See Client failover."},"4120":{"id":"/docs/server/networking-44","page_id":"/docs/server/networking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/networking#cluster-networking","content":"In cluster mode, listener ports come exclusively from each node's `cluster.nodes` ports entry, including the `tcp_replica` port for the consensus plane, and nodes can advertise different client-facing addresses per client network. Replica-to-replica traffic can be authenticated with a pre-shared key and wrapped in TLS 1.3. See Clustering configuration and Clustering security."},"4121":{"id":"/docs/server/security","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"page","content":"Security","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security"},"4122":{"id":"/docs/server/security-0","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security","content":"Authentication, authorization, transport encryption and encryption at rest in the Iggy server."},"4123":{"id":"/docs/server/security-1","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#authentication","content":"Authentication"},"4124":{"id":"/docs/server/security-2","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#username-and-password","content":"Username and password"},"4125":{"id":"/docs/server/security-3","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#personal-access-tokens-pat","content":"Personal Access Tokens (PAT)"},"4126":{"id":"/docs/server/security-4","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#authorization","content":"Authorization"},"4127":{"id":"/docs/server/security-5","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"Permission matrix"},"4128":{"id":"/docs/server/security-6","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#scoped-permissions","content":"Scoped permissions"},"4129":{"id":"/docs/server/security-7","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"How permissions cascade"},"4130":{"id":"/docs/server/security-8","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"Transport encryption (TLS)"},"4131":{"id":"/docs/server/security-9","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#data-encryption-at-rest","content":"Data encryption at rest"},"4132":{"id":"/docs/server/security-10","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#jwt-http-api","content":"JWT (HTTP API)"},"4133":{"id":"/docs/server/security-11","page_id":"/docs/server/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/security#cluster-security","content":"Cluster security"},"4134":{"id":"/docs/server/security-12","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security","content":"Iggy provides multiple layers of security covering authentication, authorization, transport encryption, and data encryption at rest."},"4135":{"id":"/docs/server/security-13","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#authentication","content":"Iggy supports local credentials and personal access tokens. HTTP also accepts configured JWT issuers. An optional external authentication service handles unknown local usernames."},"4136":{"id":"/docs/server/security-14","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#username-and-password","content":"Users authenticate with a username and password, for example via the Rust SDK's `login_user()`. Passwords are hashed using **Argon2id** (a memory-hard hashing algorithm). A fresh server requires explicit root credentials. It does not generate or log a root password. Set both environment variables:"},"4137":{"id":"/docs/server/security-15","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#username-and-password","content":"Or use the `--with-default-root-credentials` flag for development (sets root credentials to `iggy`/`iggy`)."},"4138":{"id":"/docs/server/security-16","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#username-and-password","content":"These variables initialize the root account only when it is first created. Supplied values are still validated on later starts, but they do not replace the stored credentials. Use the password-change API or `iggy user password` with the current password to change an existing account. The `--fresh` flag deletes all data. It is not needed for a password change."},"4139":{"id":"/docs/server/security-17","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#username-and-password","content":"The root user **cannot be deleted**, and its permissions are fixed: it always holds every permission."},"4140":{"id":"/docs/server/security-18","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#personal-access-tokens-pat","content":"PATs provide programmatic access with optional expiry. Each user can have up to `max_tokens_per_user` (default 100) stored tokens, including expired tokens until the cleaner removes them. Tokens are **hashed before storage** and can be revoked at any time."},"4141":{"id":"/docs/server/security-19","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#personal-access-tokens-pat","content":"An automatic cleaner removes expired tokens at a configurable interval."},"4142":{"id":"/docs/server/security-20","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#personal-access-tokens-pat","content":"Broker API commands require an authenticated session or bearer credential, except for ping and the login/refresh flows that establish or prove a credential. The Prometheus `/metrics` scrape requires a JWT or personal access token. HTTP CORS preflight responses and embedded `/ui` static assets are public. The UI's broker API calls still require authentication."},"4143":{"id":"/docs/server/security-21","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#authorization","content":"Iggy provides granular permissions at three levels:"},"4144":{"id":"/docs/server/security-22","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#authorization","content":"**Global permissions** - apply to the whole server and all streams"},"4145":{"id":"/docs/server/security-23","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#authorization","content":"**Stream permissions** - scoped to one stream and all its topics"},"4146":{"id":"/docs/server/security-24","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#authorization","content":"**Topic permissions** - scoped to one topic within a stream"},"4147":{"id":"/docs/server/security-25","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#authorization","content":"The root user has full access to everything and **bypasses all permission checks**. Other users can be assigned specific permissions using the CLI, HTTP API, SDK, or Web UI."},"4148":{"id":"/docs/server/security-26","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"The global permissions and the operations they unlock:"},"4149":{"id":"/docs/server/security-27","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation        | Required global permission |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Get server stats | `read_servers`             |","table":"table-0"},"4150":{"id":"/docs/server/security-28","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                                | Required global permission |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Diagnostic snapshot and metadata changes | `manage_servers`           |","table":"table-0"},"4151":{"id":"/docs/server/security-29","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                | Required global permission |\n| ------------------------ | -------------------------- |\n| List clients, get client | `read_servers`             |","table":"table-0"},"4152":{"id":"/docs/server/security-30","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                                                          | Required global permission |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------- |\n| Create, update, delete users. Update permissions. Change passwords | `manage_users`             |","table":"table-0"},"4153":{"id":"/docs/server/security-31","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation            | Required global permission |\n| -------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Get user, list users | `read_users`               |","table":"table-0"},"4154":{"id":"/docs/server/security-32","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                      | Required global permission |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------------- |\n| Create, update, delete streams | `manage_streams`           |","table":"table-0"},"4155":{"id":"/docs/server/security-33","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                | Required global permission |\n| ------------------------ | -------------------------- |\n| Get stream, list streams | `read_streams`             |","table":"table-0"},"4156":{"id":"/docs/server/security-34","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                     | Required global permission |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Create, update, delete topics | `manage_topics`            |","table":"table-0"},"4157":{"id":"/docs/server/security-35","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation              | Required global permission |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Get topic, list topics | `read_topics`              |","table":"table-0"},"4158":{"id":"/docs/server/security-36","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation                                         | Required global permission |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Consumer groups: create, delete, join, leave, get | `read_topics`              |","table":"table-0"},"4159":{"id":"/docs/server/security-37","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation     | Required global permission |\n| ------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Poll messages | `poll_messages`            |","table":"table-0"},"4160":{"id":"/docs/server/security-38","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"| Operation     | Required global permission |\n| ------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Send messages | `send_messages`            |","table":"table-0"},"4161":{"id":"/docs/server/security-39","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"Two kinds of implication apply on top of the table:"},"4162":{"id":"/docs/server/security-40","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"**Supersets**: every `manage_*` permission includes its `read_*` counterpart. In addition `manage_streams` includes `manage_topics`, `read_streams` includes `read_topics`, and `read_topics` includes `poll_messages`. `manage_topics` also includes `send_messages`, so `manage_streams` permits sending too."},"4163":{"id":"/docs/server/security-41","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#permission-matrix","content":"**Self-service**: an authenticated user can always read their own account, change their own password, and manage their own personal access tokens, without any of the user permissions above."},"4164":{"id":"/docs/server/security-42","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#scoped-permissions","content":"Instead of (or in addition to) global grants, permissions can be scoped per stream and per topic:"},"4165":{"id":"/docs/server/security-43","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#scoped-permissions","content":"**Stream scope**: `manage_stream`, `read_stream`, `manage_topics`, `read_topics`, `poll_messages`, `send_messages`, plus an optional per-topic map. `read_stream` includes `read_topics` and `poll_messages` within that stream."},"4166":{"id":"/docs/server/security-44","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#scoped-permissions","content":"**Topic scope**: `manage_topic`, `read_topic`, `poll_messages`, `send_messages`. `read_topic` includes `poll_messages` on that topic."},"4167":{"id":"/docs/server/security-45","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"Permissions are checked from top to bottom: global, then stream, then topic. A permission granted at a higher level covers everything below it:"},"4168":{"id":"/docs/server/security-46","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"Global `poll_messages` lets the user poll messages from **all** streams and topics."},"4169":{"id":"/docs/server/security-47","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"Stream-scoped `poll_messages` on stream 1 lets the user poll from **all topics** in stream 1, but not from other streams."},"4170":{"id":"/docs/server/security-48","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"Topic-scoped `poll_messages` on topic 5 of stream 1 lets the user poll **only** from that topic."},"4171":{"id":"/docs/server/security-49","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"If a stream has no entry in the user's stream permissions, only global permissions apply to it. The same holds for topics within a stream."},"4172":{"id":"/docs/server/security-50","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#how-permissions-cascade","content":"For example, a user that may only consume from stream 42 needs no global permissions at all: grant stream-scoped `read_stream` and `poll_messages` on stream 42, and the user can read that stream, list its topics, and poll messages from any topic in it. The read grant also permits consumer-group operations in that stream. For polling without those read and group permissions, grant only `poll_messages`."},"4173":{"id":"/docs/server/security-51","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"Each transport configures TLS in its own section, and the `self_signed` semantics differ per transport:"},"4174":{"id":"/docs/server/security-52","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"| Transport | Section     | Behavior                                                                                                                               |\n| --------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| TCP       | `[tcp.tls]` | `self_signed = true` generates an ephemeral certificate only while `cert_file` does not exist. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead. |","table":"table-1"},"4175":{"id":"/docs/server/security-53","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"| Transport | Section           | Behavior                           |\n| --------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| WebSocket | `[websocket.tls]` | Same load-or-generate rule as TCP. |","table":"table-1"},"4176":{"id":"/docs/server/security-54","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"| Transport | Section              | Behavior                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| --------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| QUIC      | `[quic.certificate]` | TLS is mandatory (part of the QUIC spec). `self_signed = true` **always** generates an ephemeral certificate and ignores `cert_file`/`key_file`, logging a warning if the files exist. |","table":"table-1"},"4177":{"id":"/docs/server/security-55","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"| Transport | Section      | Behavior                                                                                     |\n| --------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTTP      | `[http.tls]` | No `self_signed` option: HTTPS requires certificate and key files in `cert_file`/`key_file`. |","table":"table-1"},"4178":{"id":"/docs/server/security-56","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#transport-encryption-tls","content":"For production deployments, provide proper certificates via `cert_file` and `key_file`. Ephemeral certificates change on every start, so a trust configuration pinned to one generated certificate does not survive a restart. See Networking for the surrounding transport configuration."},"4179":{"id":"/docs/server/security-57","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#data-encryption-at-rest","content":"Each encrypted stream has its own AES-256-GCM-SIV data key. The server encrypts message payloads and user headers before storage and decrypts them for polling. Metadata journals, metadata snapshots and structural headers are not encrypted as a whole."},"4180":{"id":"/docs/server/security-58","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#data-encryption-at-rest","content":"When `encryption.enabled` is true, the server generates a key for each new stream. A client can also supply a stream key at creation. The server wraps that key with a key derived from `encryption.key`. Reads expose fingerprints, never keys."},"4181":{"id":"/docs/server/security-59","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#data-encryption-at-rest","content":"Set `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_KEY` to the standard base64 encoding of 32 random bytes before starting this configuration. Enabling encryption does not change existing unencrypted streams. Follow Stream options to create keyed streams and rotate the server key without re-encrypting messages."},"4182":{"id":"/docs/server/security-60","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#jwt-http-api","content":"The HTTP API uses JWT (JSON Web Tokens) for session management. Tokens are signed with HS256 by default and have configurable expiry, clock skew tolerance, and audience/issuer validation:"},"4183":{"id":"/docs/server/security-61","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#jwt-http-api","content":"Further keys (`valid_issuers`, `valid_audiences`, `not_before`, `use_base64_secret`, the signing secrets) are covered in Configuration."},"4184":{"id":"/docs/server/security-62","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#jwt-http-api","content":"**Signing secrets**: `encoding_secret` and `decoding_secret` default to empty. Without `cluster.auth`, the server generates a secure random secret on every start. That's a safe single-node default with two consequences: issued tokens **die on restart**, and in a cluster each node signs with its own key, so bearers are node-local and follower-to-primary request forwarding stays disabled. For clusters, configure an identical secret on every node (prefer the `IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET`/`IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET` environment variables over on-disk config), or enable `cluster.auth` so the JWT key derives from the cluster's shared PSK."},"4185":{"id":"/docs/server/security-63","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#jwt-http-api","content":"**Refresh tokens**: `POST /users/refresh-token` re-issues an access token from a still-valid one presented in the request body, answering the same identity shape as login, so HTTP clients can extend a session without re-sending credentials."},"4186":{"id":"/docs/server/security-64","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#jwt-http-api","content":"**Federated issuers**: the opt-in `[[http.jwt.trusted_issuers]]` list accepts tokens minted by external identity providers for application-to-application flows. Each entry names an `issuer`, `audience`, and `jwks_url`. Signatures are verified against the issuer's JWKS (fetches are rate-limited), and every token from that issuer is remapped onto the configured non-root `username`. With no entries configured, the listener accepts only self-issued tokens. See Configuration for the key reference."},"4187":{"id":"/docs/server/security-65","page_id":"/docs/server/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/security#cluster-security","content":"In cluster mode, replica-to-replica traffic can require an authenticated handshake based on a pre-shared key (with a documented rolling rotation procedure) and can be wrapped in TLS 1.3. With `cluster.auth` enabled and no JWT secrets configured, the PSK also becomes the cluster-wide JWT key source. See Clustering security."},"4188":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"page","content":"Storage Engine","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine"},"4189":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-0","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine","content":"The segmented append-only log, and how streams, topics, partitions and segments map onto files on disk."},"4190":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-1","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#directory-layout","content":"Directory layout"},"4191":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-2","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#segmented-log","content":"Segmented log"},"4192":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-3","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#on-disk-batch-format","content":"On-disk batch format"},"4193":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-4","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"Indexes"},"4194":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-5","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"Write pipeline"},"4195":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-6","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#boot-time-segment-recovery","content":"Boot-time segment recovery"},"4196":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-7","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#read-integrity","content":"Read integrity"},"4197":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-8","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#memory-pool","content":"Memory pool"},"4198":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-9","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#retention-and-cleanup","content":"Retention and cleanup"},"4199":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-10","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#metadata-plane","content":"Metadata plane"},"4200":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-11","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#encryption","content":"Encryption"},"4201":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-12","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#compression","content":"Compression"},"4202":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-13","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"Removed and relocated settings"},"4203":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-14","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#bounded-partition-file-work","content":"Bounded partition file work"},"4204":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-15","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine","content":"Iggy's storage engine is built around the concept of a **segmented append-only log**. Message data follows a hierarchy: System -> Streams -> Topics -> Partitions -> Segments. This page covers how data is stored, indexed, flushed, recovered, and cleaned up on disk."},"4205":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-16","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#directory-layout","content":"All data lives under the root `path` directory (default `local_data`, overridden with `IGGY_PATH`):"},"4206":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-17","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#directory-layout","content":"Stream, topic, and partition ids are numeric and **0-based**. Each partition directory holds pairs of `.log` and `.index` files. The filename is the segment's start offset, zero-padded to 20 digits. Recovery can create an empty active segment at a reserved offset beyond the last stored message. Next to the segments, every partition keeps its own superblock pair (replica identity and consensus state for that partition) and an `offsets/` tree for consumer offset storage. Multi-replica partitions also keep a prepare WAL when either topic durability policy is `persisted`."},"4207":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-18","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#segmented-log","content":"Each partition is a segmented log: an ordered list of **sealed** (read-only) segments plus one **active** (writable) segment. When the active segment reaches the topic's `segment_size` (default 1 GiB), it is sealed and a new active segment starts at the next offset."},"4208":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-19","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#segmented-log","content":"`segment_size` is a per-topic creation option, bounded to a 512-byte multiple between 1 MiB and 1 GiB. It's a **soft limit**: rotation fires after the append that crosses it, so the crossing batch lands whole and a sealed segment may run one batch past the configured size. `preallocate_segments` (also per topic) reserves each segment's bytes up front on filesystems that support it. See Topic options."},"4209":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-20","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#on-disk-batch-format","content":"Segments store **batch records**, not individual messages. The record is byte-identical to the wire encoding: the one layout a `SendMessages` body, the replicated prepare, the persisted segment record, and the poll reply all share. There is **no other message encoding**."},"4210":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-21","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#on-disk-batch-format","content":"The 256-byte batch header carries `partition_id`, `base_offset`, `base_timestamp`, `origin_timestamp`, `batch_length`, `batch_checksum` (XxHash3-64), and `message_count`. An optional producer section carries a producer ID, epoch and sequence. Remaining reserved bytes must be zero. Each 48-byte frame header carries `checksum` (XxHash3-64), `id` (u128), `offset_delta` (u32), `timestamp_delta` (u32), and the two lengths. A message's absolute offset is `base_offset + offset_delta`. Its server timestamp is the flat `base_timestamp`, while `timestamp_delta` resolves producer time against `origin_timestamp`. All fields are **little-endian**, and records are stored contiguously with no padding."},"4211":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-22","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#on-disk-batch-format","content":"The full byte-level tables live on the Message batches page. Absent at-rest encryption, what lands on disk is exactly what the producer sent, with the server stamping `partition_id` at admission and `base_offset` / `base_timestamp` (plus a `batch_checksum` recompute) at journal append."},"4212":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-23","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"Each segment has an accompanying `.index` file holding a **sparse index**: one 24-byte entry per flushed write, not per message."},"4213":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-24","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"| Field  | Bytes | Type | Description                          |\n| ------ | ----- | ---- | ------------------------------------ |\n| offset | 0..8  | u64  | Absolute offset of the flushed batch |","table":"table-0"},"4214":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-25","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"| Field     | Bytes | Type | Description                    |\n| --------- | ----- | ---- | ------------------------------ |\n| timestamp | 8..16 | u64  | Timestamp of the flushed batch |","table":"table-0"},"4215":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-26","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"| Field    | Bytes  | Type | Description                                         |\n| -------- | ------ | ---- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| position | 16..24 | u64  | Byte position of the batch start in the `.log` file |","table":"table-0"},"4216":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-27","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"Little-endian, no header, no padding. To serve a poll, the server binary-searches the index for the closest entry at or below the target offset (or timestamp), seeks to its `position`, and walks batch records from there. Because the index is a sparse hint, a lookup below the indexed range falls back to the segment start rather than failing."},"4217":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-28","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#indexes","content":"There is no index caching configuration: the in-memory index cache is an internal per-segment structure the server manages itself."},"4218":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-29","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"Messages are admitted, stamped, and buffered in the partition journal. Their completion path depends on the topic's durability policy:"},"4219":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-30","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"The flush thresholds and completion policies are **per-topic creation options**:"},"4220":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-31","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"`messages_required_to_save` (default `1024`): count threshold."},"4221":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-32","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"`size_of_messages_required_to_save` (default `1 MiB`): byte threshold."},"4222":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-33","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"`durability` (default `replicated`): message completion policy. `persisted` requires recoverable stable storage at the replication quorum."},"4223":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-34","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"`consumer_offset_durability` (default `replicated`): the independent policy for explicit offset stores and deletes."},"4224":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-35","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"A full active segment also triggers an ordinary flush. These are soft limits: a flush writes whole committed batches, so the actual count or size can overshoot. Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle operations can flush below the thresholds. The settings do not define a periodic flush interval. The `MessagesWriter` uses **vectored I/O** with up to 1024 buffers per syscall, so many buffered batches can land in one write."},"4225":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-36","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"With `replicated`, completion does not wait for an additional stable-storage barrier. With `persisted`, a single-replica partition flushes and synchronizes committed segment state before success. A multi-replica partition instead gates the required prepare acknowledgements on recoverable WAL history, so persisted success can precede an ordinary segment flush. `messages_required_to_save=1` is not required."},"4226":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-37","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#write-pipeline","content":"For multi-replica partitions, the disk prepare WAL is enabled when either policy is `persisted` and includes message payloads even if only the offset policy is persisted. Checkpoints synchronize materialized files before reclaiming WAL history. See Durability and Cluster Durability for completion guarantees and failure behavior."},"4227":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-38","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#boot-time-segment-recovery","content":"At startup, recovery validates batch checksums, partition identity, and the segment chain before serving data. With message durability `replicated`, it walks the log from byte zero because buffered writeback can preserve later pages before earlier ones. With `persisted`, completed durable segment flushes allow an index-anchored recovery path."},"4228":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-39","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#boot-time-segment-recovery","content":"A missing or torn sparse index can be rebuilt from valid log data. An incomplete tail can be truncated, but an interior gap, intact records after damage, or a contradiction with durable history can require recovery refusal. Recovery does not blindly truncate at the first invalid batch."},"4229":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-40","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#boot-time-segment-recovery","content":"When a prepare WAL exists, recovery reconciles it with materialized segment and offset state. Unrecoverable partition data is fenced and repaired from peers when available. It must not be replaced by an empty healthy partition. A singleton has no peer copy to fetch."},"4230":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-41","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#read-integrity","content":"Disk reads are verified before they reach a consumer:"},"4231":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-42","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#read-integrity","content":"With `validate_checksum = true` (the default), every batch a disk poll reads is re-hashed and compared against its stored checksum. A mismatch stops the disk walk and logs an error on the server. The consumer receives any valid prefix already read, or an ordinary empty poll if none was read. The protocol does not report the checksum failure to the consumer. Repeated polls can therefore wait indefinitely at damaged data. Setting it to `false` skips the re-hash and serves whatever decodes, which can hand a consumer bytes provably not the ones written. Only disable it with a corruption guard elsewhere in the stack."},"4232":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-43","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#read-integrity","content":"Binary polls reuse the stored batch layout. A reply may slice a larger stored batch and rewrite its header, and at-rest encryption requires decryption before the reply. HTTP additionally serializes the result as JSON."},"4233":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-44","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#memory-pool","content":"Iggy includes a custom memory pool to reuse buffers on the hot path. The pool has **28 buckets** with buffer sizes from 4 KiB up to 512 MiB (non-uniform spacing, denser around common message sizes, with sizes above 2 MiB rounded to hugepage-friendly steps). Components request a buffer from the appropriate bucket and return it when done."},"4234":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-45","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#memory-pool","content":"Buffers are allocated lazily. A pool miss can allocate outside the pool, so message processing is not allocation-free. Internal components can pass ownership of existing buffers without copying their contents."},"4235":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-46","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#retention-and-cleanup","content":"Two independent retention policies exist, both **per-topic options configurable at creation or update** (see Topic options). The segment cleaner enforces them:"},"4236":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-47","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#retention-and-cleanup","content":"**Size-based retention** (`max_topic_size`): the cluster has no single owner of a topic-wide total, so each partition enforces an equal share: `max_topic_size / partition_count`, counted over **sealed** bytes. The share is floored at one sealed-segment ceiling (`segment_size` plus the maximum message-bus frame, since a sealed segment can overshoot by one batch). A cap that divides below that floor is raised to it, so the policy always means at least \"keep the newest sealed segment\". Once sealed bytes exceed the budget, the oldest sealed segments are trimmed. There is no percentage-based early trigger."},"4237":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-48","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#retention-and-cleanup","content":"**Time-based retention** (`message_expiry`): sealed segments whose newest message is older than the expiry are deleted."},"4238":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-49","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#retention-and-cleanup","content":"Both policies can be active at once. They only ever touch sealed segments. Deletion also stops at the minimum committed consumer or consumer-group offset, and pending persistence checkpoints can defer it. The active segment is **never deleted by retention**, even if its messages have expired."},"4239":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-50","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#metadata-plane","content":"Metadata operations (create stream, delete topic, create user, and so on) do not use the partition storage above. They replicate through the metadata consensus group and persist in `local_data/metadata/`:"},"4240":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-51","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#metadata-plane","content":"**`journal.wal`**: the prepare journal. Each entry is a 256-byte `PrepareHeader` (the consensus header carrying checksums, view, op and commit numbers, operation discriminant, and acting user) followed by the operation's wire-format body, exactly as replicated. This is the write-ahead log: an operation is journaled before it is applied."},"4241":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-52","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#metadata-plane","content":"**`snapshot.bin`**: a **MessagePack** (rmp-serde) snapshot of the whole metadata state machine, written at checkpoints so the journal can be truncated. Recovery loads the snapshot, then replays journal entries newer than it, rebuilding the client session table alongside."},"4242":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-53","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#metadata-plane","content":"**`superblock.a` / `superblock.b`**: a ping-pong pair of checkpoint records, where the newest valid slot wins by sequence number. The superblock records which checkpoint is current and the snapshot's checksum. Boot cross-checks the pairing: a superblock pointing at a checkpoint newer than the on-disk snapshot, or a snapshot whose checksum doesn't match the record, refuses to boot rather than silently rewinding committed state."},"4243":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-54","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#metadata-plane","content":"Partition directories carry the same superblock mechanism for their own consensus state (replica identity, view), which is how a restarted replica proves it is itself rather than a blank impostor."},"4244":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-55","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#encryption","content":"Iggy encrypts message payloads and user headers with a separate AES-256-GCM-SIV key per encrypted stream. They are encrypted before storage and decrypted for polling. Metadata journals, metadata snapshots, and structural record headers remain unencrypted. The key must decode from base64 to 32 bytes. See Security."},"4245":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-56","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#compression","content":"The `compression_algorithm` topic option accepts `none` (default) and `gzip`, but it is a **placeholder today**: the value is persisted and reported back, but no message compression is applied. Payload encryption still applies when enabled. To compress today, do it client-side and tag messages via user headers - see the message headers examples in the Iggy repo."},"4246":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-57","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"Earlier releases documented several storage features and `[system.*]` keys that no longer exist:"},"4247":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-58","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"**`cache_indexes`**: removed. Index caching is internal now (see Indexes)."},"4248":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-59","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"**`[system.message_deduplication]`**: removed configuration. It does not configure the partition request deduplication table."},"4249":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-60","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"**Segment archiving / S3 backup placeholders**: removed. Delete `archive_expired` and the old `[system.segment]` table rather than retaining a `false` value."},"4250":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-61","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"**`enforce_fsync`**: replaced by the topic `durability` policy. Consumer-offset completion has its own `consumer_offset_durability` policy. Both default independently to `replicated`."},"4251":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-62","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"**`[system.*]` tables**: removed or moved to root tables. Retention, segment size, preallocation, and flush thresholds are per-topic options."},"4252":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-63","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#removed-and-relocated-settings","content":"The config loader rejects the old `[system]` table and `IGGY_SYSTEM_*` environment mappings. The full mapping is in Relocated configuration keys."},"4253":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-64","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#bounded-partition-file-work","content":"Partition file operations run as bounded jobs. `sharding.partition_io_capacity` limits active jobs and results waiting for their owner. `partition_io_bytes_max` limits retained allocations. Exhausted capacity queues work instead of letting one slow disk operation block every partition on its shard."},"4254":{"id":"/docs/server/storage-engine-65","page_id":"/docs/server/storage-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/storage-engine#bounded-partition-file-work","content":"The current storage format is incompatible with 0.9 data directories. Boot checks it before metadata replay. Preserve old files for rollback and use a fresh directory for 0.10. See Upgrading."},"4255":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","type":"page","content":"Stream Options","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/stream-options"},"4256":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-0","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options","content":"Stream names, per-stream encryption keys and server key rotation."},"4257":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-1","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"Select encryption at creation"},"4258":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-2","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/stream-options#create-a-stream-with-the-cli","content":"Create a stream with the CLI"},"4259":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-3","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"Read the catalog"},"4260":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-4","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"Rotate the server encryption key"},"4261":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-5","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options","content":"A stream accepts options when it is created and when it is updated. Use `name` to rename a stream. Use `encryption_key` at creation to supply a data key for that stream. The server rejects changes or resets to an existing stream key."},"4262":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-6","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"Each encrypted stream uses a separate AES-256-GCM-SIV data key. The server encrypts message payloads and user headers before storage and decrypts them for reads. Metadata journals, snapshots and structural headers are not encrypted as a whole."},"4263":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-7","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"| Creation request | Server configuration         | Result             |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| No stream key    | `encryption.enabled = false` | Unencrypted stream |","table":"table-0"},"4264":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-8","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"| Creation request | Server configuration                              | Result                        |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| No stream key    | `encryption.enabled = true` with a configured key | Server generates a stream key |","table":"table-0"},"4265":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-9","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"| Creation request    | Server configuration             | Result                       |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Explicit stream key | Server encryption key configured | Stream uses the supplied key |","table":"table-0"},"4266":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-10","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"An explicit key must contain 32 random bytes. The API accepts raw bytes or standard padded base64. Configure a server encryption key even when the client supplies the stream key. The server wraps the data key, which means it encrypts the key before storing it. The wrapping key derives from `encryption.key`."},"4267":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-11","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"The stream key travels in the create request. Use a transport with TLS. The server never returns the data key or its wrapped bytes. Reads report `encryption_key_source` as `stream`, `server` or `none`, and fingerprints for encrypted streams."},"4268":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-12","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#select-encryption-at-creation","content":"Enabling or disabling server encryption affects creation defaults. It does not change the encryption of existing streams. Renaming a stream preserves its key and stored messages."},"4269":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-13","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#create-a-stream-with-the-cli","content":"With a server encryption key configured, create a private key file and pass it to the CLI:"},"4270":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-14","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#create-a-stream-with-the-cli","content":"`--stream-key-file` configures server-side encryption for this stream. The CLI's separate `--encryption-key` option encrypts payloads on the client."},"4271":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-15","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"| Option | Access | Meaning                                 |\n| ------ | ------ | --------------------------------------- |\n| `name` | Update | Resource name, from 1 through 255 bytes |","table":"table-1"},"4272":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-16","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"| Option           | Access                  | Meaning         |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------- | --------------- |\n| `encryption_key` | Create only, write only | Stream data key |","table":"table-1"},"4273":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-17","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"| Option                  | Access    | Meaning                      |\n| ----------------------- | --------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `encryption_key_source` | Read only | Origin of the key, or `none` |","table":"table-1"},"4274":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-18","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"| Option                       | Access    | Meaning                     |\n| ---------------------------- | --------- | --------------------------- |\n| `encryption_key_fingerprint` | Read only | Fingerprint of the data key |","table":"table-1"},"4275":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-19","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"| Option                       | Access    | Meaning                                                         |\n| ---------------------------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `encryption_kek_fingerprint` | Read only | Comma-separated fingerprints of the keys that wrap the data key |","table":"table-1"},"4276":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-20","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#read-the-catalog","content":"A fingerprint identifies a key without exposing it. It consists of 16 lowercase hexadecimal characters. It cannot replace a key during recovery."},"4277":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-21","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"A key-encryption key (KEK) wraps stream data keys. Rotation changes the wraps and does not re-encrypt messages. The metadata primary replicates these changes. Each stream can hold up to three wraps."},"4278":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-22","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"Set `encryption.key` to the new key and add the old key to `encryption.previous_keys` on every node. Restart nodes with the updated configuration. Retain the old key during this phase."},"4279":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-23","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"Make sure that every node uses this configuration and every encrypted stream reports the new KEK fingerprint. The primary adds new wraps while preserving old ones."},"4280":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-24","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"Empty `previous_keys` and add the old fingerprint to `retired_kek_fingerprints` on every node. The primary removes those wraps."},"4281":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-25","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"Make sure that every stream no longer reports the retired fingerprint. Clear `retired_kek_fingerprints` on every node."},"4282":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-26","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"`previous_keys` accepts at most two keys. Retire an old fingerprint before adding a fourth wrap. Do not discard keys needed by retained backups. Losing the only usable wrapping key makes the affected streams unreadable."},"4283":{"id":"/docs/server/stream-options-27","page_id":"/docs/server/stream-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/stream-options#rotate-the-server-encryption-key","content":"See Configuration for environment variables and Security for transport protection."},"4284":{"id":"/docs/server/testing","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","type":"page","content":"Testing with Iggy","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/testing"},"4285":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-0","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing","content":"Run the Iggy Docker image from your own test suite, with examples for Rust, Python and Node.js."},"4286":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-1","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"What the container needs"},"4287":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-2","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/testing#rust","content":"Rust"},"4288":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-3","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/testing#python","content":"Python"},"4289":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-4","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/testing#nodejs","content":"Node.js"},"4290":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-5","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing","content":"Iggy has no embedded, in-process test server. The server ships as a binary, not a library, so a test suite can't start it in-process. To test against a real server, start the published Docker image when your tests start and stop it when they finish. The examples below use each language's `testcontainers` library to do that."},"4291":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-6","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"These settings apply whichever language your tests are in:"},"4292":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-7","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"**`seccomp=unconfined`** as a security option. Docker's default seccomp profile blocks the `io_uring` calls the server needs."},"4293":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-8","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"**`IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD`.** A fresh standalone server requires them or the explicit development flag."},"4294":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-9","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"**`IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090` and `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost`.** By default the server listens on `127.0.0.1` inside the container, which a mapped port can't reach. Listening on all addresses also needs an advertised address, or the server won't start. Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"4295":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-10","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"Wait for the `server client listeners started` log line before connecting. The server logs `server running; waiting on shard threads` earlier, before the port is open, and connecting on that line fails some of the time."},"4296":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-11","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#what-the-container-needs","content":"If the server exits with `io_uring was denied locked memory`, the container's locked-memory limit is too low for it. Raise it, as the `--ulimit memlock=-1:-1` flag does in Docker & Helm."},"4297":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-12","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#rust","content":"This example uses `testcontainers` 0.27 and the `iggy` crate 0.12, which goes with server 0.10.0."},"4298":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-13","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#rust","content":"Run it with `cargo test`."},"4299":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-14","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#python","content":"This example uses `testcontainers`, `pytest` and `pytest-asyncio` with `apache-iggy` 0.10.0:"},"4300":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-15","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#python","content":"Run it with `pytest -v`."},"4301":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-16","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#nodejs","content":"This example uses `testcontainers` with `apache-iggy` 0.11.0, the Node.js SDK that goes with server 0.10.0, and the built-in `node:test` runner. `testcontainers` needs Node.js 22.19 or newer."},"4302":{"id":"/docs/server/testing-17","page_id":"/docs/server/testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/testing#nodejs","content":"Save it as `iggy.test.mjs` and run it with `node --test`."},"4303":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"page","content":"Topic Options","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options"},"4304":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-0","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options","content":"Per-topic retention, durability and segment layout options, set when the topic is created."},"4305":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-1","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"The catalog"},"4306":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-2","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options#setting-options","content":"Setting options"},"4307":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-3","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options#create-only-vs-updatable","content":"Create-only vs updatable"},"4308":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-4","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options#replication-factor","content":"Replication factor"},"4309":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-5","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options#discovering-the-catalog","content":"Discovering the catalog"},"4310":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-6","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/topic-options#durability","content":"Durability"},"4311":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-7","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options","content":"Retention, durability, and segment layout are per-topic decisions, made when the topic is created. They used to be server-wide `[system.*]` keys, which gave every knob two homes. The server now refuses to boot on the old keys, and each topic carries its own values instead."},"4312":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-8","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options","content":"Options are key-value pairs sent with `CreateTopic`. Unknown keys are **rejected at the edge, never skipped**: a silently ignored knob would leave the client on server defaults with no way to find out. Streams and users have their own catalogs. See Stream options for stream keys and names. User updates accept `username` and `status`."},"4313":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-9","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option           | Default   | Constraints                           | Description                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `max_topic_size` | unlimited | finite values at least `segment_size` | Topic-wide limit on sealed segment bytes, divided across partitions. The cleaner deletes eligible oldest sealed segments when a partition exceeds its share. |","table":"table-0"},"4314":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-10","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option           | Default | Constraints | Description                                                                                   |\n| ---------------- | ------- | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `message_expiry` | none    |             | Delete the oldest sealed segments whose newest message timestamp is older than this duration. |","table":"table-0"},"4315":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-11","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option                    | Default   | Constraints                       | Description                                                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------- | --------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `partition_resize_policy` | `mutable` | `mutable` or `fixed`, create only | `fixed` rejects partition additions and deletions. `mutable` permits layout changes without a bounded cutover or per-key ordering across layouts. |","table":"table-0"},"4316":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-12","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option               | Default                         | Constraints                                                      | Description                                |\n| -------------------- | ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `replication_factor` | Server default, initially `all` | 1 through 32 and no greater than cluster membership, create only | Number of nodes that store each partition. |","table":"table-0"},"4317":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-13","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option                  | Default | Constraints      | Description                                                   |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `compression_algorithm` | `none`  | `none` or `gzip` | Placeholder: stored and reported, no compression applied yet. |","table":"table-0"},"4318":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-14","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option         | Default | Constraints                                                                   | Description                                                               |\n| -------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `segment_size` | 1 GiB   | 0 selects the default. Otherwise a 512-byte multiple from 1 MiB through 1 GiB | Soft size limit per segment: a segment may close one whole batch past it. |","table":"table-0"},"4319":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-15","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option       | Default      | Constraints                 | Description                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------ | ------------ | --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `durability` | `replicated` | `replicated` or `persisted` | Message completion policy. `persisted` requires recoverable stable-storage copies at the replication quorum before success. |","table":"table-0"},"4320":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-16","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option                       | Default      | Constraints                 | Description                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------ | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `consumer_offset_durability` | `replicated` | `replicated` or `persisted` | Completion policy for explicit consumer-offset stores and deletes, independent of message durability. |","table":"table-0"},"4321":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-17","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option                      | Default | Constraints                | Description                                         |\n| --------------------------- | ------- | -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `messages_required_to_save` | 1024    | non-zero, at most 16777216 | Flush the journal once it holds this many messages. |","table":"table-0"},"4322":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-18","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option                              | Default | Constraints                          | Description                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `size_of_messages_required_to_save` | 1 MiB   | 0 selects the default. At most 1 GiB | Flush the journal once it holds this many bytes. Paired with the message count. Whichever threshold trips first flushes. |","table":"table-0"},"4323":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-19","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"| Option                 | Default | Constraints                                           | Description                                                             |\n| ---------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `preallocate_segments` | `false` | `segment_size` x partitions at most 64 GiB per create | Reserve each segment's bytes up front where the filesystem supports it. |","table":"table-0"},"4324":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-20","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"Both retention policies can be active at once. The active segment is **never touched**, and its bytes are excluded from the size limit. A segment whose end offset exceeds the lowest stored consumer or consumer-group offset is retained. With no stored offsets, this barrier is absent. Deletion is done by the server's segment cleaner (`[data_maintenance.messages]`, enabled by default)."},"4325":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-21","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"Both durability policies write data to disk and default independently to `replicated`. Neither inherits the other. Flush thresholds schedule ordinary segment writes. Required persistence, capacity pressure, or lifecycle operations can flush earlier. They do not weaken the `persisted` completion guarantee."},"4326":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-22","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"Value forms are forgiving: byte sizes accept a raw number of bytes or a string like `\"128 MiB\"`, expiry accepts microseconds or a humantime string like `\"7 days\"`, booleans accept `true`/`false`. Create admission re-parses and re-encodes what you send, so a string `segment_size=128MiB` is stored as the number it names."},"4327":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-23","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#the-catalog","content":"`preallocate_segments` requests a `segment_size` reservation for each partition's segment file when it is opened, including during topic creation and rotation. On Linux this reserves disk space without changing the file's logical length. Unsupported or failed reservations log a warning and fall back to ordinary allocation. With the default 1 GiB segment size, the request is 1 GiB per partition up front, which is why it's opt-in and why one create is **capped at 64 GiB** of requested reservation."},"4328":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-24","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#setting-options","content":"At creation, every interface takes the same keys:"},"4329":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-25","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#setting-options","content":"In the CLI, both durability policies have named flags. `--set` is repeatable and covers other storage options, such as `segment_size`. `compression_algorithm`, `message_expiry`, and `max_topic_size` also have first-class parameters. Run `iggy options topic` to discover what the server accepts."},"4330":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-26","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#setting-options","content":"SDKs expose typed durability values in their topic creation options. In Rust, set `TopicCreateOptions::durability` and `TopicCreateOptions::consumer_offset_durability` to `Durability::Replicated` or `Durability::Persisted`. The HTTP API takes `\"durability\"` and `\"consumer_offset_durability\"` as string values in the create body's `options` map."},"4331":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-27","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#setting-options","content":"Keys absent from the wire request are resolved by the admitting server and stored as **derived** entries. Typed SDKs can send their default durability values explicitly. Binary `GetTopic` responses carry explicit and derived blocks. HTTP reports an `explicit` flag per option. Both report option values and the provenance of the request. Updates preserve the values and provenance of keys outside the patch."},"4332":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-28","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#create-only-vs-updatable","content":"Updates use UpdateResource with a set/reset patch. SDKs retain resource-specific update methods, but these methods send the shared command. Only `name`, `message_expiry` and `max_topic_size` can change on a topic."},"4333":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-29","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#create-only-vs-updatable","content":"An absent key keeps its value. In HTTP, `null` also leaves a value unchanged. A name in `reset` returns that option to the server default and marks it as derived. Do not send the same key in both sets. Unknown keys, read-only keys and create-only changes are rejected. An empty patch, including a patch with only null values or zero sentinels, is rejected."},"4334":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-30","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#create-only-vs-updatable","content":"HTTP updates use a flat JSON object, not a nested `options` object:"},"4335":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-31","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#create-only-vs-updatable","content":"`compression_algorithm`, `segment_size`, both durability policies, flush thresholds, `preallocate_segments`, `partition_resize_policy` and `replication_factor` are create-only. Select them before creating the topic. A finite size limit must still be at least the segment size."},"4336":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-32","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#replication-factor","content":"When `replication_factor` is absent, the server resolves `cluster.default_replication_factor` and stores the result with the topic. The default `all` uses every cluster node. A later server configuration change does not change existing topics."},"4337":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-33","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#replication-factor","content":"A factor of one stores one copy. Replicated durability can acknowledge it before stable storage. A factor of two requires both replicas to make progress. Three copies tolerate one unavailable replica. See Cluster durability for larger quorum rules."},"4338":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-34","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#replication-factor","content":"The example needs a cluster with at least three nodes. The metadata group still spans the full cluster, independent of each topic's factor."},"4339":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-35","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#discovering-the-catalog","content":"Ask the server which keys it accepts, with their types, defaults, and descriptions:"},"4340":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-36","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#discovering-the-catalog","content":"For HTTP, set `IGGY_TOKEN` to a valid JWT or personal access token:"},"4341":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-37","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#discovering-the-catalog","content":"The Rust SDK exposes the same call as `describe_options`. The scope is `topic`, `stream`, or `user`."},"4342":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-38","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#discovering-the-catalog","content":"Discovery matters most on the binary transports: TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket carry **only an error code** for a rejected key, not its name, so the catalog is how a client finds out what this server supports. Over HTTP the error message names the offending key directly."},"4343":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-39","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#durability","content":"`replicated` waits for quorum commit and application without an additional stable-storage barrier. `persisted` also requires recoverable stable-storage copies at the required quorum. On a single node that means recoverable local persistence before success. In a cluster it uses durable prepare history. Setting `messages_required_to_save=1` is not required for `persisted`."},"4344":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-40","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#durability","content":"The former `enforce_fsync` option is rejected. Use `durability` for messages and `consumer_offset_durability` for explicit offset changes. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous, and HTTP `ack=none` confirms dispatch only, regardless of these policies."},"4345":{"id":"/docs/server/topic-options-41","page_id":"/docs/server/topic-options","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/topic-options#durability","content":"See Durability for single-node failure behavior and Cluster Durability for the quorum and WAL guarantees."},"4346":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"page","content":"Upgrading from 0.9 to 0.10","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading"},"4347":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-0","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading","content":"Prepare a fresh data directory, matching clients and updated configuration for Apache Iggy 0.10.0."},"4348":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-1","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading#preserve-data-before-the-upgrade","content":"Preserve data before the upgrade"},"4349":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-2","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading#upgrade-clients-and-tools","content":"Upgrade clients and tools"},"4350":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-3","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Review server configuration"},"4351":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-4","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-consumer-checkpoints","content":"Review consumer checkpoints"},"4352":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-5","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-encryption","content":"Review encryption"},"4353":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-6","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/upgrading#check-the-cutover","content":"Check the cutover"},"4354":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-7","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 changes the wire protocol, data directory format and public SDK APIs. Upgrade the server and clients together."},"4355":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-8","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#preserve-data-before-the-upgrade","content":"There is no migration from a 0.9.x data directory. Keep a backup and export messages that you need with the old server and compatible clients. Start 0.10.0 with a separate, empty data directory. Import the retained data through a compatible client. Do not point the new server at the old directory."},"4356":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-9","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#preserve-data-before-the-upgrade","content":"Do not use a rolling upgrade between server versions. Every cluster node must run the same server version. Keep the old deployment and its data available until you finish the cutover checks."},"4357":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-10","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#upgrade-clients-and-tools","content":"Server 0.10.0 uses protocol 0.12.0. The server rejects incompatible binary clients at login. Use the versions in Server compatibility. The 0.9 documentation remains available for the old deployment."},"4358":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-11","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#upgrade-clients-and-tools","content":"Review API changes before recompiling applications. Resource creation uses typed options. Updates change only the fields supplied through set/reset patches. Stream and topic purge, flush unsaved buffer, and the old UpdateStream, UpdateTopic and UpdateUser wire commands are removed. The SDK update methods use UpdateResource internally."},"4359":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-12","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#upgrade-clients-and-tools","content":"Rust stream builders take stream and topic names, without caller-assigned IDs. Python requires Python 3.11 or later. The SDK guides describe the release APIs and examples."},"4360":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-13","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Compare your configuration with the 0.10 reference. Do not copy obsolete keys into the new deployment."},"4361":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-14","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Set `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` before first boot. The server no longer generates a root password. Use `--with-default-root-credentials` only for development."},"4362":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-15","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Remove `consumer_group.session_timeout`. Session expiry derives from `heartbeat.interval` with a factor of 1.2. `consumer_group.heartbeat_interval` controls membership heartbeat pacing."},"4363":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-16","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Replace `user_id` with `username` in `http.jwt.trusted_issuers`. The mapped account must exist and cannot be root. HTTP clients and the Web UI must log in again because old self-issued access and refresh tokens are rejected."},"4364":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-17","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Supply a valid `logging.level`. An invalid filter stops startup. Log files rotate by size, with retention and total-size cleanup."},"4365":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-18","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Keep `cluster.coordinator.replica_connections_per_peer` identical on all nodes. Cluster membership is limited to 32 nodes."},"4366":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-19","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"Keep node clocks synchronized. Authenticated HTTP forwarding uses short-lived proofs."},"4367":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-20","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-server-configuration","content":"New topics inherit `cluster.default_replication_factor`, which defaults to `all`. Select a different factor at topic creation with `replication_factor`. Existing topics retain their stored value. See Multi-leader partitions."},"4368":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-21","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-consumer-checkpoints","content":"A checkpoint records how far a consumer processed a partition. The current poll API defaults to manual commits. Rust high-level consumers also default to manual commits, and the stream builder starts with `PollingStrategy::next()`."},"4369":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-22","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-consumer-checkpoints","content":"Store offsets after processing, or select an explicit automatic commit policy. Without stored offsets, a restarted consumer can read retained messages again. Committing on read can advance the offset before application processing. See Deferred polling."},"4370":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-23","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-consumer-checkpoints","content":"Connector sinks use deferred polling. Replace `poll_interval` with `max_wait` and `min_count`. The runtime rejects a sink configuration that still sets `poll_interval`."},"4371":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-24","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#review-encryption","content":"Encryption is selected when a stream is created. Each encrypted stream has its own data key. Enabling server encryption does not encrypt an existing unencrypted stream. Keep all keys required to read retained streams and backups. See Stream options for key creation and rotation."},"4372":{"id":"/docs/server/upgrading-25","page_id":"/docs/server/upgrading","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/upgrading#check-the-cutover","content":"Run producers and consumers against the new deployment before moving application traffic. Test resource creation, permissions, message payloads and headers, checkpoints, and reconnects. For a cluster, also test requests to different partition primaries and node failure. An accepted write and a processed message are different events. Select the required durability policies and commit behavior explicitly."},"4373":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting"},"4374":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"What the most common first-run failures look like, what causes them, and how to fix them."},"4375":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Client hangs with no output"},"4376":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Docker container can't be reached"},"4377":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Server exits with an io_uring error in a container"},"4378":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Kernel too old"},"4379":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#missing-root-credentials","content":"Missing root credentials"},"4380":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"\"Already exists\" error when running the CLI again"},"4381":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-cannot-complete-login","content":"Client cannot complete login"},"4382":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#numa-memory-binding-fails","content":"NUMA memory binding fails"},"4383":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"This page covers the problems people most often hit the first time they run Apache Iggy. Each entry shows what you see, what causes it, and how to fix it."},"4384":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"To collect diagnostic data for a bug report instead, see the `snapshot` command in CLI commands."},"4385":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"**What you see:** the client starts and then prints nothing. It doesn't fail and it doesn't exit."},"4386":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"**Cause:** nothing is listening on the address the client connects to, usually `127.0.0.1:8090`. The server isn't running, is bound to a different address, or hasn't finished starting. By default the SDKs retry the connection forever, and the retries are only logged if your application has logging set up, so all you see is silence."},"4387":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"**Fix:** check the server has started before starting the client. It logs a `server client listeners started` line that includes the address, for example `tcp=127.0.0.1:8090`. You can also check the port directly:"},"4388":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"To make the client fail with an error instead of retrying forever, set `reconnection_retries=0` on the connection string, or call `.with_reconnection_max_retries(Some(0))` on the Rust client builder. Turning on logging in your application also shows each reconnect attempt."},"4389":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-15","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"**What you see:** the server is running in Docker with `-p 8090:8090`, but clients on the host can't connect. On Docker Desktop the connection may be accepted and then reset straight away."},"4390":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-16","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"**Cause:** the image doesn't override the server's addresses, so it binds `127.0.0.1` inside the container, where the published port can't reach it."},"4391":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-17","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"**Fix:** set `IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090`, and the equivalent for any other transport you expose, along with `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS`. See the `docker run` and Compose examples in Docker & Helm."},"4392":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-18","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"**What you see:** the server exits at startup with one of these:"},"4393":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-19","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"On a machine with more cores, the same problems can show up as `failed to create io_uring runtime for shard N` instead."},"4394":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-20","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"**Cause:** Docker's default seccomp profile blocks the `io_uring` system calls the server needs, or the container's locked-memory limit is too small for the server's io_uring rings."},"4395":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-21","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"**Fix:** run the container with `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` and `--ulimit memlock=-1:-1`, or with a custom seccomp profile and a locked-memory limit big enough for the server. See Why these capabilities? and Linux tuning."},"4396":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-22","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"**What you see:** the server exits with an `io_uring Invalid Argument (EINVAL)` report that includes a line like:"},"4397":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-23","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"**Cause:** on Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer for the io_uring features it uses. WSL2 kernels can be missing io_uring features even on a newer version."},"4398":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-24","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"**Fix:** upgrade the host kernel to 5.19 or newer. Under WSL2, run `wsl --update`. Containers use the host's kernel, so it's the host that needs upgrading. See System requirements."},"4399":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-25","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#missing-root-credentials","content":"A fresh standalone server refuses to start without root credentials. Set both `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` before first boot, or use `--with-default-root-credentials` for a disposable development instance. The server no longer generates a password."},"4400":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-26","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#missing-root-credentials","content":"A cluster replica without credentials waits for the root account to arrive through replication. Provision the same initial credentials on all replicas so any promoted node can initialize it. Once root is stored, environment changes do not reset its password. Do not delete a data directory to change an existing password."},"4401":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-27","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"**What you see:** running `iggy stream create dev` a second time fails:"},"4402":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-28","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"The name is missing from the message because of a known bug where error details are lost between the server and the client (#3735)."},"4403":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-29","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"**Cause:** the stream was created the first time. The CLI reports the server's error rather than skipping streams that already exist."},"4404":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-30","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"**Fix:** nothing needs fixing. The stream exists and can be used as it is. Check with `iggy stream list` before creating it if you're running a setup script more than once."},"4405":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-31","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-cannot-complete-login","content":"Make sure that the client matches the server's protocol. Server 0.10.0 uses protocol 0.12.0 and rejects incompatible clients during login. A malformed old client can report a disconnect rather than a useful compatibility error."},"4406":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-32","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-cannot-complete-login","content":"Use Rust SDK 0.12.0 or Python SDK 0.10.0 with this server. See Server compatibility for the other languages. Also make sure that credentials match the stored account, not just the current bootstrap environment."},"4407":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-33","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#numa-memory-binding-fails","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding when the detected topology has one NUMA node. On a multi-node topology, container policy or host restrictions can still reject memory binding."},"4408":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-34","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#numa-memory-binding-fails","content":"Set `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding fits the deployment. Inspect the host topology and allowed CPU set before changing production placement. See Linux tuning."},"4409":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"page","content":"An event-driven service with a consumer group","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Tutorials"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service"},"4410":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","content":"Build a small order-processing service in Python: key messages by customer, share a topic between two service instances with a consumer group, and see them take over from each other and resume."},"4411":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"4412":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#start-the-server","content":"Start the server"},"4413":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"The producer"},"4414":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"The service"},"4415":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Run it"},"4416":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"Running it again"},"4417":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","content":"You will build a small order-processing service. A producer writes order events to a topic, keyed by customer. Two copies of the service share the topic through a consumer group. You stop one and watch the other take over, then start it again and see it resume from stored progress."},"4418":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"You need Docker, Python 3.11 or later, and the Python SDK in a virtual environment. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer, but macOS has no kernel requirement (see System requirements)."},"4419":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"Consumer groups need a binary transport, so this tutorial uses TCP. HTTP cannot join a group (see Consumer groups and the FAQ)."},"4420":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#start-the-server","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"4421":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Save as `orders_producer.py`."},"4422":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"`Partitioning.messages_key` hashes the key to pick the partition, so orders for one customer share a partition and stay in order. The connection string comes from the environment, so the same script works against a server on another host (see Connection strings)."},"4423":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Run it:"},"4424":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"The following output illustrates the message shape. Customer choices, offsets and partition scheduling vary."},"4425":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-15","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"Save as `order_service.py`."},"4426":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-16","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"`consume_messages` runs until the shutdown event is set. Ctrl-C, or a SIGTERM from a supervisor, sets it, and the current message finishes before the call returns. The instance name is only used in the log."},"4427":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-17","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"The automatic policy counts a message after its handler succeeds. A handler exception ends the run and leaves that message uncounted. A later run can read it again. Await consumer shutdown to finish pending progress and leave the group."},"4428":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-18","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"**One instance.** Start the service in a second terminal:"},"4429":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-19","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"It is the only member, so it gets all three partitions and drains what the producer wrote:"},"4430":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-20","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"**Scale out.** In a third terminal start a second instance, then run the producer again:"},"4431":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-21","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"The server rebalances the group. The following example shows `svc-b` reading partition 2 while `svc-a` reads 0 and 1. Rebalances and retries can replay messages."},"4432":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-22","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"**Scale in.** Press Ctrl-C in the `svc-a` terminal. It prints `[svc-a] shutting down` and exits. Run the producer again. `svc-b` now owns every partition and receives all 15 orders (output trimmed):"},"4433":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-23","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"**Resume.** Start `svc-a` again. It prints only its `joined` line, because the group's stored offsets mean nothing is replayed. Run the producer once more and `svc-a` gets partition 2 back:"},"4434":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-24","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"When a partition moves, the server stops the old owner polling it before handing it to the new one, and waits up to 30 seconds by default (see How does consumer group rebalancing work?)."},"4435":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-25","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"The producer creates the stream and topic only if they are missing, so it can be run any number of times. A restarted service resumes from the group's stored offset. To start from nothing, stop the container, and `--rm` removes it along with its data."},"4436":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-26","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"The service is the same consumer shape as the high-level consumer example in the repository, with a JSON handler and signal handling added."},"4437":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"page","content":"A Grafana dashboard for the broker","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Tutorials"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard"},"4438":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","content":"Run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana in Docker Compose, and watch the broker on a dashboard."},"4439":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"4440":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"The stack"},"4441":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#start-it","content":"Start it"},"4442":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#create-a-token-for-prometheus","content":"Create a token for Prometheus"},"4443":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#send-some-messages","content":"Send some messages"},"4444":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#open-grafana","content":"Open Grafana"},"4445":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#what-the-server-exports","content":"What the server exports"},"4446":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#running-it-again","content":"Running it again"},"4447":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","content":"You will run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana, and watch the broker on a dashboard as you send it messages."},"4448":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#before-we-start","content":"All you need is Docker with Compose, since everything else runs in containers."},"4449":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"Create a directory with these files."},"4450":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"`compose.yml`:"},"4451":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"The server flags are explained on Docker & Helm, and metrics are on by default. Grafana is on port 3001 because the server already uses 3000."},"4452":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"`prometheus/prometheus.yml`:"},"4453":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-15","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"Create an empty token file:"},"4454":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-16","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"`grafana/provisioning/datasources/prometheus.yaml`:"},"4455":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-17","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"`grafana/provisioning/dashboards/iggy.yaml`:"},"4456":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-18","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"`grafana/dashboards/iggy.json`:"},"4457":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-19","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#start-it","content":"Prometheus starts scraping straight away, but the server turns it away because it has no token. You get the same answer from curl:"},"4458":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-20","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#start-it","content":"The Prometheus targets page also shows the `iggy` job as down."},"4459":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-21","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#create-a-token-for-prometheus","content":"Create a personal access token with the CLI and write it to the file Prometheus reads:"},"4460":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-22","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#create-a-token-for-prometheus","content":"Check that the token works:"},"4461":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-23","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#create-a-token-for-prometheus","content":"Within a few seconds the Prometheus targets page shows the `iggy` job as up."},"4462":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-24","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#send-some-messages","content":"Use the CLI to create a stream and topic, then send 60 messages to it:"},"4463":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-25","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#send-some-messages","content":"Check the message count in Prometheus:"},"4464":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-26","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#open-grafana","content":"Open http://localhost:3001, sign in as `admin` with password `admin`, and find the \"Apache Iggy broker\" dashboard in the Iggy folder. Messages stored reads 60 and the rate panels show the burst, so send another and watch them move."},"4465":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-27","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#what-the-server-exports","content":"The server exports about forty metrics, including `http_requests_total`, `streams`, `topics`, `partitions`, `segments`, `messages`, `users` and `clients`. To list them all:"},"4466":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-28","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#running-it-again","content":"To stop everything:"},"4467":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-29","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#running-it-again","content":"Your files stay in the directory, so `docker compose up -d` starts it all again. The server starts empty each time, so create a new token and send some messages before you open the dashboard."},"4468":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-30","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#running-it-again","content":"To add a panel, edit `grafana/dashboards/iggy.json` and Grafana picks up the change within a few seconds. Watch the denied requests panel: if it starts to climb, the server is too busy to take more requests. The server's metrics settings are under `[http.metrics]` in the configuration reference."},"4469":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"page","content":"Iggy Web UI","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Web UI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start"},"4470":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-0","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start","content":"The Iggy Web UI dashboard, for monitoring server health and browsing streams, topics, messages and users."},"4471":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-1","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#how-it-connects","content":"How it connects"},"4472":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-2","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#running-modes","content":"Running modes"},"4473":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-3","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#embedded-mode","content":"Embedded mode"},"4474":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-4","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#standalone-mode","content":"Standalone mode"},"4475":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-5","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#local-development","content":"Local development"},"4476":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-6","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"Features"},"4477":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-7","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/web_ui/start#docker-compose-example","content":"Docker Compose example"},"4478":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-8","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start","content":"Iggy Web UI provides a comprehensive dashboard for Iggy server. It allows you to monitor the server's health, streams, topics, browse the messages, users and more. The dashboard is built with SvelteKit and TypeScript and is available as open-source as well as the Docker image on Docker Hub."},"4479":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-9","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start","content":"These instructions target Iggy server 0.10.0. Use a matching source checkout for local builds."},"4480":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-10","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start","content":"Log in again after upgrading from 0.9. Existing self-issued access and refresh tokens are not valid for the new release."},"4481":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-11","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#how-it-connects","content":"The Web UI is a purely client-side application: every call to the Iggy HTTP API is issued by your browser, not by the Web UI container or server. Two things follow from this:"},"4482":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-12","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#how-it-connects","content":"`PUBLIC_IGGY_API_URL` must be a URL that the **browser** can reach (e.g. `http://localhost:3010`), not an internal Docker network hostname like `http://iggy:80`."},"4483":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-13","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#how-it-connects","content":"The Iggy server must allow cross-origin requests from the Web UI origin. CORS is enabled for all origins by default (`[http.cors]` in the server config). If you restricted `allowed_origins`, add the Web UI origin."},"4484":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-14","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#how-it-connects","content":"In embedded mode neither applies: the UI is served from the same origin as the HTTP API and uses relative URLs."},"4485":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-15","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#embedded-mode","content":"The Web UI is compiled directly into the Iggy server binary via the `iggy-web` cargo feature, which is enabled by default (official binaries and Docker images include it). To serve it at the `/ui` endpoint, flip the `web_ui` flag (disabled by default):"},"4486":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-16","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#embedded-mode","content":"When building from source, generate the static assets before compiling the server. Run from the repository root:"},"4487":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-17","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#embedded-mode","content":"The feature alone does not generate these assets. A server binary built without them returns 404 at `/ui`."},"4488":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-18","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#embedded-mode","content":"If the server is compiled without the `iggy-web` feature and `web_ui = true`, a warning is logged but the server continues to run normally."},"4489":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-19","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#standalone-mode","content":"Start an Iggy server with HTTP enabled, as shown in Getting started, then run the Web UI as a separate container:"},"4490":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-20","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#standalone-mode","content":"`PUBLIC_IGGY_API_URL` is consumed by the browser: if the Iggy server also runs in Docker, use the host-published HTTP port here, not the container's network alias."},"4491":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-21","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#local-development","content":"The Web UI lives in the `web/` folder of the repository. Use a Node.js version supported by the frontend toolchain (`^20.19.0 || ^22.13.0 || >=24`, for example Node.js 22.13 or later in the 22.x series) and `npm` (`pnpm` and `yarn` are not part of the supported workflow):"},"4492":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-22","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#local-development","content":"Point the UI at your Iggy server's HTTP API via a `.env` file in the `web/` folder, e.g. `PUBLIC_IGGY_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000`."},"4493":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-23","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"The Web UI provides the following pages and functionality:"},"4494":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-24","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Overview** - server health and key metrics"},"4495":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-25","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Streams** - list, create, and manage streams"},"4496":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-26","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Topics** - create and delete topics, and edit their name and message expiry"},"4497":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-27","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Partitions** - view partition details per topic"},"4498":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-28","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Messages** - browse messages per partition with built-in decoders:"},"4499":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-29","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"JSON decoder (formatted JSON output)"},"4500":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-30","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"String decoder (UTF-8 text)"},"4501":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-31","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"XML decoder"},"4502":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-32","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Users** - list and create users, including their initial permissions"},"4503":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-33","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"**Settings** - Web UI preferences"},"4504":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-34","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"Topic updates use the HTTP set/reset contract. Omitted fields keep their current values. See Topic options for mutable fields and creation-only policies. Some settings require the CLI or HTTP API."},"4505":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-35","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#features","content":"The Clients, Logs and terminal routes are placeholders hidden from navigation. The Server settings page displays information, but saving settings is disabled. Editing and deleting existing users, changing their permissions and bulk user actions are also placeholders. See the roadmap for what is planned."},"4506":{"id":"/docs/web_ui/start-36","page_id":"/docs/web_ui/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/web_ui/start#docker-compose-example","content":"Here's the full example of the `docker-compose.yml` file that starts the Iggy server, initializes it with the `my-stream` stream and `my-topic` topic with the help of Iggy CLI, and starts the Iggy Web UI:"}},"count":4506},"sorting":{"language":"english","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{"content":{"docs":{"1":2817,"2":3698,"3":1955,"4":4181,"5":1878,"6":3451,"7":2895,"8":3353,"9":2622,"10":2168,"11":1988,"12":3485,"13":3011,"14":3618,"15":4224,"16":3071,"17":2913,"18":2255,"19":2260,"20":4364,"21":3317,"22":3096,"23":2104,"24":2392,"25":3650,"26":3892,"27":3565,"28":4120,"29":3778,"30":3890,"31":3514,"32":3734,"33":2433,"34":3349,"35":3693,"36":3882,"37":1662,"38":1656,"39":1661,"40":1653,"41":1648,"42":1629,"43":1659,"44":1635,"45":1634,"46":1941,"47":1650,"48":1655,"49":1642,"50":1643,"51":1644,"52":1654,"53":1657,"54":1646,"55":1645,"56":1647,"57":1637,"58":1636,"59":1638,"60":1641,"61":1640,"62":1631,"63":1632,"64":1626,"65":1627,"66":1630,"67":1628,"68":1625,"69":1658,"70":1660,"71":1651,"72":1649,"73":1652,"74":1633,"75":1639,"76":1624,"77":1622,"78":1623,"79":2297,"80":3668,"81":613,"82":2996,"83":3504,"84":2291,"85":1902,"86":3844,"87":3769,"88":2062,"89":3253,"90":3499,"91":3286,"92":4259,"93":2639,"94":2136,"95":2231,"96":2596,"97":2642,"98":3149,"99":3150,"100":2143,"101":2144,"102":3151,"103":3586,"104":2401,"105":3587,"106":3306,"107":3304,"108":3272,"109":3308,"110":3595,"111":3307,"112":3594,"113":2594