on and receives all 15 orders (output trimmed):"],[4430,"**Scale out.** In a third terminal start a second instance, then run the producer again:"],[2378,"**SDKs** - client libraries for 9 languages (Rust, Python, Java, Go, Node.js, C#, C++, PHP, Swift), most with runnable examples."],[3911,"**Secret** - root user credentials from `server.users.root` (default `iggy`/`changeit`). Point `server.users.root.existingSecret.name` at your own Secret in production. `usernameKey` and `passwordKey` select its keys."],[4249,"**Segment archiving / S3 backup placeholders**: removed. Delete `archive_expired` and the old `[system.segment]` table rather than retaining a `false` value."],[2665,"**Segment.** Part of a partition, stored on disk as a data file and an index file. Retention deletes old segments whole. See Segment."],[3190,"**SegmentClient** - delete segments"],[2062,"**Selected-column `json`**: V2 parses the cell as JSON. V3 serializes the column's existing JSON value, so a string containing JSON remains a JSON string."],[2064,"**Selected-column `raw`**: the column must contain standard base64 text, which is decoded into raw bytes."],[2063,"**Selected-column `text`**: V2 emits the cell as UTF-8. V3 emits strings directly and serializes other JSON values as text."],[4163,"**Self-service**: an authenticated user can always read their own account, change their own password, and manage their own personal access tokens, without any of the user permissions above."],[1005,"**Self-signed mode** (`self_signed = true`): the node auto-generates a certificate at boot and the dialer accepts ANY peer certificate. This gives you encrypted replica traffic without certificate management, but **no acceptor authentication at all**: peer authenticity rests entirely on the PSK handshake. Use CA mode when the replica network is not trusted."],[356,"**Send messages. Code: 101.**"],[631,"**Server ↔ SDK client**, everything a client sends or receives:"],[637,"**Server ↔ server**, the replica-to-replica consensus traffic that never appears on a client connection:"],[3908,"**Server Deployment** - runs `apache/iggy` with the pod security context the server needs: seccomp profile `Unconfined` (for `io_uring`) plus the `IPC_LOCK` capability (for memory locking). Listener addresses are set to `0.0.0.0` via `server.env`, and the data volume mounts at `/app/local_data`. The chart supplies `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` as the in-cluster Service DNS name. Override it with `server.advertisedAddress` when clients arrive through a LoadBalancer or an Ingress. The server image tag defaults to the chart's `appVersion` (`0.9.0-edge.6` in this source revision). The quick start selects `0.10.0` explicitly."],[3909,"**Server Service** - exposes the `http` (3000), `quic` (8080/UDP), `tcp` (8090), and `websocket` (8092) ports."],[3913,"**ServiceAccount** and **Ingress** - a shared ServiceAccount and separate optional server/UI ingresses. The chart has no HPA template. Server `replicaCount` must not exceed 1. Cluster mode uses one release per node, each with its own replica ID and storage."],[3914,"**ServiceMonitor** - Prometheus scrape config (optional). The `/metrics` endpoint requires a bearer credential like every other read, so wire a token (e.g. a personal access token stored in a Secret) into `server.serviceMonitor.authorization` - an unauthenticated scrape gets 401."],[4503,"**Settings** - Web UI preferences"],[2666,"**Shard.** One thread in the server that owns a set of partitions. See Architecture."],[4184,"**Signing secrets**: `encoding_secret` and `decoding_secret` default to empty. Without `cluster.auth`, the server generates a secure random secret on every start. That's a safe single-node default with two consequences: issued tokens **die on restart**, and in a cluster each node signs with its own key, so bearers are node-local and follower-to-primary request forwarding stays disabled. For clusters, configure an identical secret on every node (prefer the `IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET`/`IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET` environment variables over on-disk config), or enable `cluster.auth` so the JWT key derives from the cluster's shared PSK."],[2430,"**Single binary deployment** without an external broker or database. Operating-system libraries are still required by dynamically linked builds"],[1561,"**Single destination table**"],[1306,"**Sink connector config (connectors/protobuf_sink.toml):**"],[1894,"**Sink connector config (connectors/stdout.toml):**"],[3428,"**Sink data producer** - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."],[3302,"**sink-data-producer** - bulk data generation for sink connectors"],[3224,"**sinkdataproducer** - high-volume data generation (100 batches of 1000 to 1099 messages) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."],[4236,"**Size-based retention** (`max_topic_size`): the cluster has no single owner of a topic-wide total, so each partition enforces an equal share: `max_topic_size / partition_count`, counted over **sealed** bytes. The share is floored at one sealed-segment ceiling (`segment_size` plus the maximum message-bus frame, since a sealed segment can overshoot by one batch). A cap that divides below that floor is raised to it, so the policy always means at least \"keep the newest sealed segment\". Once sealed bytes exceed the budget, the oldest sealed segments are trimmed. There is no percentage-based early trigger."],[1305,"**Source connector config (connectors/protobuf_source.toml):**"],[2186,"**Source connector config (connectors/random.toml):**"],[2446,"**Standalone** - as a separate container via `docker pull apache/iggy-web-ui:0.5.0`"],[1080,"**State persistence** applies to source connectors and stores the optional checkpoint bytes supplied by the plugin, using a local file or an HTTP backend. The SDK provides MessagePack helpers. The runtime acknowledges a source batch after forwarding it to Iggy and saving its optional checkpoint. See state storage."],[1100,"**Statically Typed**: Ensures type safety and compile-time checks, reducing runtime errors."],[362,"**Store consumer offset. Code: 121.**"],[1577,"**store_access_key_id**: The optional access key ID of the object storage."],[1580,"**store_class**: The storage class to use. **Currently, only S3-compatible storage is supported.**"],[1581,"**store_path_style_access**: Use path-style S3 URLs (`http://host/bucket/key`). Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` for stores that require virtual-hosted-style URLs."],[1579,"**store_region**: The region of the object storage. Required. For S3-compatible stores that ignore it, supply any placeholder value."],[1578,"**store_secret_access_key**: The optional secret key used to upload data to the object storage. Supply both credential fields or omit both to use the default AWS credential provider chain."],[1576,"**store_url**: The URL of the object storage for data uploads."],[3422,"**Stream builder** - examples using the `IggyStream`, `IggyStreamProducer`, and `IggyStreamConsumer` builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."],[4145,"**Stream permissions** - scoped to one stream and all its topics"],[4165,"**Stream scope**: `manage_stream`, `read_stream`, `manage_topics`, `read_topics`, `poll_messages`, `send_messages`, plus an optional per-topic map. `read_stream` includes `read_topics` and `poll_messages` within that stream."],[3301,"**stream-builder** - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"],[2667,"**Stream.** A container for related topics. For example, a `dev` stream and a `production` stream can each have their own `orders` topic. See Stream."],[3225,"**streambuilder** - a combined producer and consumer in a single class. It deletes its `test_stream` stream after the run."],[3187,"**StreamClient** - create, get, list, update, delete streams"],[4495,"**Streams** - list, create, and manage streams"],[1414,"**String columns**: non-null, non-string values are converted to their JSON string representation. Nulls remain null."],[4162,"**Supersets**: every `manage_*` permission includes its `read_*` counterpart. In addition `manage_streams` includes `manage_topics`, `read_streams` includes `read_topics`, and `read_topics` includes `poll_messages`. `manage_topics` also includes `send_messages`, so `manage_streams` permits sending too."],[1560,"**Support for REST catalogs**"],[1559,"**Support for S3-compatible storage**"],[373,"**Sync consumer group. Code: 606.**"],[3186,"**SystemClient** - ping, stats, snapshot"],[2097,"**Table Polling**: Incrementally fetch rows using a tracking column"],[1570,"**tables**: The names of the Iceberg tables you want to statically route Iggy messages to. The name should include the table’s namespace, separated by a dot (`.`). Ignored when `dynamic_routing = true`."],[2485,"**TCP and WebSocket** connections, plaintext or TLS, are handed off round-robin across the shards. Shard 0's coordinator duplicates the socket's file descriptor, ships a connection-setup frame to the target shard, and drops its own handle, so the owning shard serves the connection from then on - before a single byte is read. For TLS-wrapped TCP and secure WebSocket (WSS), the target shard runs the TLS handshake. Shard 0 is part of the rotation by default. `skip_shard_zero_for_clients = true` under `[cluster.coordinator]` takes it out."],[3426,"**TCP TLS** - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with custom CA certificates. Stop the plain server and start a TLS-enabled server from the repository root:"],[3300,"**tcp-tls** - TLS-encrypted TCP connections"],[3028,"**TCP** - binary protocol for optimal performance and lower latency (recommended). Over TCP the SDK speaks the VSR consensus framing, the only wire protocol the server accepts. See Connection semantics"],[2823,"**TcpTls** - TLS-encrypted TCP connection"],[3227,"**tcptls** - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with CA certificate verification. Requires a TLS-enabled server. Run this server command from the repository root:"],[2535,"**There is an extra level above topics.** Every topic lives in a stream, so you create a stream before you create its topics."],[2400,"**Thread per core shared nothing design** together with `io_uring` guarantee the best possible performance on modern `Linux` systems"],[4237,"**Time-based retention** (`message_expiry`): sealed segments whose newest message is older than the expiry are deleted."],[1217,"**timeout** (optional): HTTP request timeout (default: 10s)"],[1413,"**Timestamp columns**: ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 strings (e.g. `\"2021-11-11T22:11:58Z\"`) are converted to microsecond timestamps. Integer timestamps pass through as epoch microseconds. Space-separated timestamps such as `\"2021-11-11 22:11:58\"` are interpreted as UTC. Invalid timestamp strings fail the batch."],[2421,"**TLS** support for all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTPS)"],[3341,"**TLS**: set `transport: 'TLS'` and pass Node `tls.ConnectionOptions` in `options`. See the tcp-tls example."],[3407,"**TLS**: use `TcpConfig` with `tls_enabled` and `tls_ca_file`, or the TLS options in a connection string. The certificate must match `tls_domain`, which defaults to the dialed host. The getting-started example shows the full setup."],[3410,"**Topic options**: `create_topic(..., options=TopicOptions(...))` accepts typed options. `TopicOptions.raw` accepts extra keys as `dict[str, str]`, validated by the server. `client.describe_options(\"topic\")` lists the keys a server accepts."],[4146,"**Topic permissions** - scoped to one topic within a stream"],[4166,"**Topic scope**: `manage_topic`, `read_topic`, `poll_messages`, `send_messages`. `read_topic` includes `poll_messages` on that topic."],[2668,"**Topic.** A named set of partitions for one kind of message, such as orders or user events. See Topic."],[3188,"**TopicClient** - create, get, list, update, delete topics"],[4496,"**Topics** - create and delete topics, and edit their name and message expiry"],[1338,"**Transform Configuration**: Create a new converter to change its configuration. `load_schema()` can reload its existing source. Without a descriptor, JSON-to-protobuf conversion produces JSON text in `Payload::Proto`, not a schema-encoded binary message."],[1079,"**Transforms** run inside the runtime process, not inside the plugins. They are applied after decoding for sources (before sending to Iggy) and after consuming from Iggy for sinks (before forwarding to the external system)."],[1324,"**Transforms**: `proto_convert` supports JSON-to-protobuf schema encoding for scalar fields, including floating-point numbers and numeric enum values. Bytes and already-encoded nested messages use base64 strings. It logs and omits fields it cannot encode. Its protobuf-to-JSON path exposes Any metadata or raw-data metadata rather than decoding a custom message descriptor. Converting protobuf to `flat_buffer` or `avro` rewraps bytes without transcoding them."],[1527,"**Transient errors** (retried): network errors, HTTP 429, 500, 502, 503, 504."],[3175,"**Transport selection** via scheme:"],[2956,"**Typed message (de)serialization**"],[3984,"**Ubuntu 22.04 LTS ships 5.15 and will not run the server.** Its hardware enablement kernel is new enough, and so are Ubuntu 24.04 LTS and Debian 12 as they ship."],[2722,"**Unknown option keys are hard errors.** The parser rejects the whole string with `InvalidConnectionString` instead of ignoring the key, so a typo fails fast rather than silently falling back to a default."],[1092,"**unwrap_envelope** - promote a nested field of a JSON envelope to the top-level payload."],[380,"**Update permissions. Code: 36.**"],[1088,"**update_fields** - update existing fields with static or computed values such as `timestamp_millis` or `uuid_v7`."],[477,"**Update** requests are patches: keys absent from the block are left alone, never reset. A client built before a key existed cannot erase it."],[1573,"**uri**: The URI of the Iceberg catalog."],[1219,"**url_templates** (optional): Custom URL templates for API endpoints. Supports variable substitution with `{key}` and `{version}` placeholders."],[2402,"**User authentication and authorization** with granular permissions, Personal Access Tokens (PAT) and Argon2id password hashing"],[2669,"**User headers.** Optional key and value pairs sent with a message, kept separate from the payload. See Serialising messages."],[3409,"**User headers**: `SendMessage(data, user_headers=...)` accepts a plain `dict` with `str`, `bytes`, `bool`, `int`, or `float` values. `HeaderKey`/`HeaderValue` give explicit control over the wire type. See the message-headers examples."],[3194,"**UserClient** - create, get, list, update, delete users, change password, update permissions"],[2730,"**Username and password**, separated by a colon. Both parts must be non-empty:"],[4502,"**Users** - list and create users, including their initial permissions"],[2058,"**V2** uses an inclusive cursor, such as `range(start: time(v: \"$cursor\"))`. Flux sorts and limits each table separately, so the example groups its selected numeric field into one table before sorting. A persisted count skips rows already acknowledged at the cursor timestamp. `$limit` is the base batch size plus that count, capped at eleven times the base size. Dense timestamp groups can exhaust the skip allowance and produce a stuck-cursor error without advancing the cursor. A strict `>` query can lose unseen ties and is unsuitable for this skip-count scheme."],[2059,"**V3** requires strict `WHERE time > '$cursor'`. Inclusive `>=` templates are rejected. A full batch containing one timestamp retains the previous cursor, advances `$offset`, and doubles the next query size up to the configured cap. A full batch containing several timestamps defers the final timestamp group to the next poll. Reaching the inflation cap emits no messages, resets the effective size to the base size, preserves the offset, and records a circuit-breaker failure. The breaker opens only when its failure threshold is reached. Setting the cap factor to `0` disables these guards and can skip unseen tied rows."],[2398,"**Very high throughput** for both writes and reads"],[1047,"**View change**: replicas exchange `StartViewChange` and `DoViewChange`, then the new primary sends `StartView`."],[2670,"**VSR.** Viewstamped Replication, the protocol Apache Iggy uses to keep replicas in step. See Viewstamped Replication."],[1572,"**warehouse**: The warehouse value sent to the REST catalog. Its meaning depends on the catalog service. Data file destinations come from each table’s metadata."],[3910,"**Web UI Deployment + Service** - a separate `apache/iggy-web-ui` deployment on port 3050, enabled by default (`ui.enabled`)."],[2376,"**Web UI** - SvelteKit dashboard for stream/topic management, message browsing with JSON/string/XML decoders, and user management."],[4401,"**What you see:** running `iggy stream create dev` a second time fails:"],[4385,"**What you see:** the client starts and then prints nothing. It doesn't fail and it doesn't exit."],[4392,"**What you see:** the server exits at startup with one of these:"],[4396,"**What you see:** the server exits with an `io_uring Invalid Argument (EINVAL)` report that includes a line like:"],[4389,"**What you see:** the server is running in Docker with `-p 8090:8090`, but clients on the host can't connect. On Docker Desktop the connection may be accepted and then reset straight away."],[2061,"**Whole-row `json`**: V3 emits a flat object with native JSON types. `include_metadata = false` removes only the cursor column. V2 emits an envelope with `measurement`, `field`, `timestamp`, `value` and `row`. Its parser reads CSV cells as strings and ignores the `#datatype` and `#default` values. Payload conversion tries bool, integer and finite float, then string. An empty cell becomes null. Thus a string field containing `\"42\"` becomes the number `42` in V2 but stays a string in V3. With metadata disabled, V2's `row` retains only `_time` and `_value`. The envelope remains."],[2407,"**Works directly with binary data**, avoiding enforced schema and serialization/deserialization overhead"],[3985,"**WSL2 often ships incomplete `io_uring`**, missing setup flags or operations even on a kernel that reports 5.19 or newer. Run `wsl --update` first."],[3568,"*Historical CLI screenshot. Server-start and cleanup flags shown there have been removed. Use the commands below and the current `iggy-bench --help`.*"],[3566,"*Historical dashboard screenshot showing a 0.5.0 run, not 0.10.0 measurements.*"],[751,"`--after` resumes from a returned cursor. `--limit` accepts 1 through 1000 and defaults to 100. `--interval` defaults to one second while following. `--since` and `--until` use microseconds since the epoch. A follower rebuilds from current state when its saved history expires."],[3591,"`--durability` and `--consumer-offset-durability` independently default to `replicated`. Place them before the benchmark kind. For example, this keeps consumer offsets replicated while measuring persisted message acknowledgments:"],[3080,"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory** (`local_data` by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."],[3218,"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory** (`local_data` by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."],[3111,"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory**. `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` take precedence over the flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials, including credentials recovered from cluster peers. The snippets use `iggy`/`iggy`. Run the producer before the consumer."],[3501,"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory**. Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. In a cluster, a fresh replica can recover credentials from peers. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."],[3172,"`--fresh` **wipes this replica's local data directory**. In a cluster, it can recover stored credentials from another replica. The environment takes precedence over the default-credentials flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. This setup is for *development only*. Use a disposable data directory. See Connection Strings for the authentication details."],[3594,"`--max-wait` defaults to one second, and `--min-count` defaults to one. `--max-bytes` defaults to at least 1 MiB, increased when needed to fit the configured batch. Place these flags before the benchmark kind."],[771,"`--network host` works on **Linux**. On macOS and Windows, point the CLI at the host instead: `--tcp-server-address host.docker.internal:8090`."],[913,"`--replica-id` is required and must match exactly one `cluster.nodes` entry"],[735,"`--store-token` and an expiry are mutually exclusive: stored tokens **never expire**."],[795,"`--store-token` is mutually exclusive with an expiry: stored tokens **never expire**. They're also **namespaced per server address**, so a token stored for one server isn't visible when connecting to another. Alternatively, create a token with an expiry and pass its value with `-t`:"],[4270,"`--stream-key-file` configures server-side encryption for this stream. The CLI's separate `--encryption-key` option encrypts payloads on the client."],[3085,"`--tcp-server-address` - server address, default `127.0.0.1:8090`"],[3087,"`--tls-ca-file` - path to a CA certificate file"],[3088,"`--tls-domain` - TLS server domain name for SNI"],[3086,"`--tls` - enable TLS"],[903,"`--with-default-root-credentials` sets the root user to `iggy`/`iggy` unless `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` are already exported. These values initialize the root user **only on its first creation**. Later starts recover the stored user. Supplied environment values are still validated at boot."],[785,"`-n, --token-name <name>`: a personal access token stored in the platform keyring under that name."],[3588,"`-o/--output-dir` defaults to `performance_results`. Optional flags (`--remark`, `--gitref`, extra info) annotate the run. Generated charts use the in-repo report library (`core/bench/report`). The runner (`core/bench/runner`) executes benchmarks across Git revisions. You can browse or compare results in the dashboard (`core/bench/dashboard`), which also powers the public benchmarking platform. A prebuilt dashboard image is available: `docker pull apache/iggy-bench-dashboard:edge`."],[699,"`-q, --quiet` suppresses ordinary status output. `stats` still prints its result, and `pat create` still prints an unstored token. Interactive password changes also print their confirmation. `-d, --debug <file>` writes verbose logs to a file."],[786,"`-t, --token <token>`: a personal access token passed directly."],[787,"`-u, --username <user>` with `-p, --password <password>`. When `-p` is omitted, the CLI prompts for the password interactively (or reads one line from stdin when piped)."],[791,"`-u`, `-t` and `-n` are **mutually exclusive**. Avoid passing the password inline with `-p`: it lands in your **shell history**. Prefer `iggy login`, the interactive prompt, or the environment variables."],[2567,"`.index` file - positional and time indexes for fast lookups"],[2566,"`.log` file - the actual message data"],[2495,"`\"all\"` - one shard per available CPU core"],[3807,"`\"all\"`: one shard per available CPU core"],[2498,"`\"numa:auto\"` - automatically detect NUMA topology and select physical cores, avoiding sibling hyperthreads"],[3810,"`\"numa:auto\"`: all NUMA nodes and cores, NUMA-aware"],[2499,"`\"numa:nodes=0,1;cores=4;no_ht=true\"` - explicit NUMA nodes, with the same number of cores taken from each listed node and hyperthread avoidance"],[3811,"`\"numa:nodes=0,1;cores=4;no_ht=true\"`: NUMA nodes 0 and 1, four cores each, no hyperthreads"],[3873,"`[[cluster.nodes]]`: the full roster, byte-identical on every node. Each entry has `name`, `ip`, `replica_id`, and `ports` (`tcp`, `quic`, `http`, `websocket`, `tcp_replica`). In cluster mode `ports` is the single source of listener ports: `tcp` and `tcp_replica` ports are always required, and every other enabled transport also needs an explicit per-node port or the server refuses to start. `advertised_address` and per-CIDR `advertised_addresses` selectors control what clients are told to dial."],[3608,"`[[http.jwt.trusted_issuers]]`"],[887,"`[cluster.auth]` configures the pre-shared-key replica handshake and `[cluster.tls]` wraps replica connections in TLS. Both are covered in Security."],[3870,"`[cluster.auth]`: replica-to-replica authentication (PSK plus BLAKE3 keyed-MAC handshake). `shared_secret` must be at least 32 bytes of CSPRNG output, identical on every node. Prefer `IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET`. `previous_shared_secret` enables rolling key rotation."],[3871,"`[cluster.coordinator]`: `skip_shard_zero_for_replicas = true` and `skip_shard_zero_for_clients = false` control connection placement when more than one shard runs. `replica_connections_per_peer = 1` controls links per peer and must match on every node."],[1000,"`[cluster.tls]` wraps every replica connection on the `tcp_replica` port in TLS 1.3 (ALPN `iggy-replica`) before the replica handshake runs:"],[3872,"`[cluster.tls]`: TLS 1.3 on the replica port. Requires `cluster.auth.enabled`. The PSK authenticates the peer, TLS supplies confidentiality."],[3633,"`[cluster]`"],[3631,"`[consumer_group]`"],[3623,"`[data_maintenance.messages]`"],[3620,"`[encryption]`"],[3621,"`[external_auth]`"],[3624,"`[heartbeat]`"],[3606,"`[http.cors]`"],[3607,"`[http.jwt]`"],[3609,"`[http.metrics]`"],[3610,"`[http.tls]`"],[3605,"`[http]`"],[3619,"`[logging]`"],[3622,"`[memory_pool]`"],[3629,"`[message_bus]`"],[3627,"`[metadata]`"],[3632,"`[node]`"],[3628,"`[partition]`"],[3630,"`[personal_access_token]`"],[3614,"`[quic.certificate]`"],[3613,"`[quic]`"],[3626,"`[sharding]`"],[3612,"`[tcp.tls]`"],[3611,"`[tcp]`"],[3625,"`[telemetry]`"],[3616,"`[websocket.tls]`"],[3615,"`[websocket]`"],[4058,"`/metrics` needs a bearer token, like every other read on the HTTP API. Any logged-in user can scrape it and gets the aggregate gauges. The per-topic series need the `read_servers` or `manage_servers` permission. There's no separate monitoring permission. Prometheus can't log in, so create a personal access token and put it in the scrape config:"],[1194,"`/usr/lib`, `/usr/lib64`, `/lib`, `/lib64`, `/usr/local/lib`, `/usr/local/lib64`"],[1189,"`<FIELD>` sets one lowercased top-level plugin configuration key. Underscores are preserved (`A_B` becomes `a_b`, not nested `a.b`), and nested plugin configuration keys cannot be set through environment variables. `FORMAT` is reserved and is listed separately as `..._PLUGIN_CONFIG_FORMAT`. The passthrough template does not guarantee that a plugin accepts every field."],[1188,"`<KEY>` is the configured connector key, uppercased. These per-connector overrides are available only with the local configuration provider."],[1187,"`<N>` is a vector index from `0` through `255` for connector stream fields. Whole-value vectors are listed once without a placeholder."],[838,"`advertised_address`"],[839,"`advertised_addresses`"],[3005,"`AutoCommit.Default` stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. `CommitAfter.Processing` counts a message when enumeration advances or a `ConsumeAsync` handler succeeds. A handler exception ends consumption and leaves that message uncounted. `CommitAfter.Marked` supports explicit progress marking after concurrent work."],[2834,"`AutoLoginSettings`"],[1824,"`aws_access_key_id` and `aws_secret_access_key` supply explicit staging credentials. `s3_endpoint` supports an alternate S3 endpoint. The complete example configuration includes a local staging service setup. Replace its credentials and role before use."],[451,"`Balanced`: `kind = 1`, `length = 0`, no value. The binary client resolves it to an explicit partition before sending."],[3521,"`batch_length` limits messages in a direct request. `linger_time` sets the minimum gap between sequential direct sends. It does not buffer separate calls. Use the high-level producer's background mode when you need a buffer."],[3465,"`batch_size` and `batch_length` are flush triggers checked after adding a send. The final send can take the buffer past the byte threshold. The linger deadline starts when an empty buffer receives a send. The first trigger reached causes a flush. Each worker also has a bounded queue, which can make dispatch wait independently of `failure_mode`. A send larger than the entire byte budget fails under every backpressure mode. Nonzero `max_buffer_size` and `max_in_flight` values must not exceed `tokio::sync::Semaphore::MAX_PERMITS`. Constructing the dispatcher with a larger value panics."],[2080,"`batch_size` defaults to 100 and `batch_timeout` to 10 milliseconds after the first message. `poll_timeout` defaults to one second. `request_capacity` defaults to 32. The source keeps one batch in flight until the runtime reports its result."],[1703,"`binary` stores BSON Binary with the generic subtype. Use `schema = \"raw\"` and no payload-changing transform to preserve original bytes."],[2077,"`broker_url` and a nonempty `subscriptions` list are required. Broker URLs accept `mqtt://`, `mqtts://` or `ssl://`. Supply `username` and `password` together when the broker requires credentials. `subscription_qos` can override the default QoS for a filter already listed in `subscriptions`. Each value must be zero, one or two."],[2145,"`capture_operations` accepts uppercase `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE`. An empty array emits none. Filtering happens after reading the slot. Qualified table names match the schema and table. Unqualified names match that table name in any schema. An empty `tables` array captures all tables."],[3361,"`cargo-php`"],[3121,"`client.ParseConnectionString` returns TCP options from a connection string. Pass them to `client.WithTcp` when constructing the client. It accepts credentials, TLS and reconnect options. Use `GetMetadataChanges` to read resource or activity changes without listing the entire catalog. Raw requests can target a replica with `SendBinaryRequestToReplica`. See Metadata changes for restart and retention behavior."],[3550,"`client.reader` returns batches from explicit partitions or a group assignment. `client.consumerGroup` yields individual messages. A consumer needs `initialize()` before iteration:"],[864,"`cluster.default_replication_factor` defaults to `\"all\"`. It applies to new topics whose creation request omits `replication_factor`. An explicit value must be from 1 through 32 and no greater than the cluster node count. The resolved value is stored with the topic. Changing this default does not change existing topics."],[871,"`cluster.nodes` is the full roster of cluster members and is intended to be **byte-identical on every node**. The running node finds its own entry through the `--replica-id` CLI flag. Environment variables replace the entire `cluster.nodes` array rather than merging individual fields into TOML entries. If you configure any roster entry through environment variables, provide the complete roster that way."],[1452,"`columns` and `where` are forwarded verbatim to Doris and evaluated as SQL expressions. Keep this config trusted."],[1775,"`commit_timeout_secs = 10` requests a time-based commit. It is not a ten-second delivery deadline. Retention uses the document's `timestamp` field. The example requests seven-day retention checked daily. The timestamp mapping accepts Unix timestamps and formats search results as nanoseconds, with millisecond fast-field precision."],[3483,"`CommitPolicy::OnRead` submits offsets with the read and can advance them before processing. The consumer refuses this policy with a client-side encryptor because decryption can fail after the store. Use manual or automatic commits instead."],[3006,"`CommitPolicy.OnRead` submits the offset before processing. It cannot be combined with a consumer encryptor. See Deferred polling."],[2924,"`CommitPolicy.OnRead` submits the offset with the read, before application processing. `CommitPolicy.Auto()` is available to readers and consumers, but not a one-shot poll. The deferred polling guide explains limits and cancellation."],[4450,"`compose.yml`:"],[4335,"`compression_algorithm`, `segment_size`, both durability policies, flush thresholds, `preallocate_segments`, `partition_resize_policy` and `replication_factor` are create-only. Select them before creating the topic. A finite size limit must still be at least the segment size."],[4426,"`consume_messages` runs until the shutdown event is set. Ctrl-C, or a SIGTERM from a supervisor, sets it, and the current message finishes before the call returns. The instance name is only used in the log."],[3368,"`consumeMessages()` requires a finite limit. A callback exception ends the run. With automatic commits after processing, the failed message stays uncounted. `PollOptions` carries the commit policy. Use `CommitPolicy::manual()`, `CommitPolicy::onRead()` or `CommitPolicy::auto(...)`."],[4223,"`consumer_offset_durability` (default `replicated`): the independent policy for explicit offset stores and deletes."],[955,"`consumer_offset_durability=persisted` with replicated messages still retains message payloads through WAL segment references. A durable offset's predecessor history must remain recoverable."],[2605,"`Consumer.Single(name)` is a consumer that polls on its own, and the server stores its offset under that name. With `auto_commit=True` the offset is stored after the poll returns, without waiting for the store to finish, so a crash straight afterwards can deliver the last batch again."],[3367,"`consumerGroup()` creates and joins a group by default. It reads assigned partitions and stores no offsets unless you select a policy or commit checkpoints. This example processes five messages:"],[3378,"`createStream($name, $encryption_key)` accepts the standard padded base64 of a 32-byte stream key. `createTopic` accepts named creation arguments, including `replication_factor`. Stream and topic updates change only named fields and reset keys. `describeOptions()` lists each catalog. User and PAT administration are not exposed as typed PHP methods in this release. Reads expose encryption fingerprints, never key material."],[473,"`CreateStream`, `CreateTopic`, `CreateUser` and `UpdateResource` end with a key-value **options block** that reuses the user-headers TLV encoding. The block runs from its start to the end of the payload (no length prefix, and the validator requires exact consumption) and may be empty."],[3112,"`CreateTopic` accepts `*iggcon.TopicOptions`. A nil value selects server defaults. `CreateStream` accepts `*iggcon.StreamOptions`. `UpdateTopic`, `UpdateStream` and `UpdateUser` accept typed patches. `DescribeOptions` lists the keys and mutability flags."],[1249,"`DELETE /sinks/{key}/configs`: delete one configuration version, chosen by the `version` query parameter or the locally saved active selection."],[1260,"`DELETE /sources/{key}/configs`: delete one configuration version, chosen by the `version` query parameter or the locally saved active selection."],[2134,"`delete_after_read` and `processed_column` stage cleanup keys during polling and modify PostgreSQL only after `Ack`. A Nack or failed poll leaves the rows available for replay. Cleanup is separate from delivery and checkpointing: a cleanup error stops the source, and a crash after checkpointing but before cleanup can leave already-delivered rows unmodified in PostgreSQL. Cleanup across tables is not atomic."],[2912,"`DescribeOptionsAsync(OptionsScope.Topic)` returns the catalog. Set fields in `TopicUpdate` to change them. `Reset` returns a supported key to its server default. Omitted fields keep their stored values. Compression cannot be updated. See Topic options."],[2932,"`DescribeOptionsAsync` enumerates the option keys a server accepts for a resource scope (`OptionsScope.Topic`, `OptionsScope.Stream`, or `OptionsScope.User`), with the kind, default, and description of each. This is the discovery surface for the topic options dictionary:"],[4222,"`durability` (default `replicated`): message completion policy. `persisted` requires recoverable stable storage at the replication quorum."],[3820,"`durability`, `consumer_offset_durability`, segment size, preallocation, and the message/byte flush thresholds are topic creation options. `wal_bytes_max` sizes the persistence machinery without selecting the topic's completion policy."],[954,"`durability=persisted` does not make an offset response persisted when `consumer_offset_durability=replicated`."],[498,"`EvictionHeader`: server to client, connection rejection with no body."],[1270,"`exactly_once=true` enables producer batch identity for a source. The plugin must reproduce the same messages from its saved state. The runtime stores producer ID, epoch, sequence and partition with the plugin checkpoint before sending."],[390,"`expiry` is a duration in **microseconds**. Two sentinel values exist: `0` (server default, which currently means no expiry) and `u64::MAX` (never expires)."],[2270,"`field_mappings` renames input JSON fields or fields decoded from Avro. Text input is not field-mapped. Text-to-Avro parses JSON before encoding. Raw-to-Avro rewraps existing bytes without validating them. Avro-to-Text emits base64 and Avro-to-Raw preserves the bytes. All three `conversion_options` keys are accepted but have no effect. Unsupported conversion pairs pass their input through unchanged."],[2259,"`field` must not be empty. Messages that aren't JSON objects, or that don't contain the field, pass through unchanged."],[449,"`flag = 1` means the id is set. `flag = 0` means none (the value bytes are then zero). Group polls and group offset writes require an explicit assigned partition. Plain-consumer polls and consumer-offset reads use partition 0 when the id is omitted."],[2602,"`from_connection_string` takes the transport, host and credentials in one string, `iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port`. See Connection strings for the other options. Creating a stream or topic that already exists is an error, so the producer checks first and only creates what's missing. That's also what makes it safe to run again."],[1241,"`GET /`: welcome message."],[1242,"`GET /health`: process liveness response. It does not check connector health. Inspect `/stats`, `/sources` or `/sinks` for connector status."],[1244,"`GET /metrics`: Prometheus metrics, available when `[http.metrics]` is enabled (path configurable via its `endpoint` key)."],[1250,"`GET /sinks/{key}/configs/{version}`: configuration details for a specific version."],[1251,"`GET /sinks/{key}/configs/active`: active configuration details."],[1253,"`GET /sinks/{key}/configs/plugin`: sink plugin config, including the optional `format` query parameter to specify the config format."],[1247,"`GET /sinks/{key}/configs`: list of configuration versions for the sink."],[1254,"`GET /sinks/{key}/transforms`: sink transforms to be applied to the fields."],[1246,"`GET /sinks/{key}`: sink details."],[1245,"`GET /sinks`: list of sinks."],[1261,"`GET /sources/{key}/configs/{version}`: configuration details for a specific version."],[1262,"`GET /sources/{key}/configs/active`: active configuration details."],[1264,"`GET /sources/{key}/configs/plugin`: source plugin config, including the optional `format` query parameter to specify the config format."],[1258,"`GET /sources/{key}/configs`: list of configuration versions for the source."],[1265,"`GET /sources/{key}/transforms`: source transforms to be applied to the fields."],[1257,"`GET /sources/{key}`: source details."],[1256,"`GET /sources`: list of sources."],[1152,"`GET /stats` on the HTTP API returns a JSON snapshot of the runtime: version, process ID, CPU and memory usage (cgroup-aware in containers), uptime, the source/sink totals, and a `connectors` array with per-connector details - key, name, type, plugin version, `enabled`, message counters, error count, and the current status."],[1243,"`GET /stats`: runtime statistics (process info plus per-connector status and message counters)."],[1508,"`GET` and `HEAD` are accepted but unusual for delivering data: the request still carries a body, which some servers reject. The sink logs a warning at startup when `GET` or `HEAD` is combined with a batch mode other than `individual`."],[3379,"`getMetadataChanges()` follows the metadata log. `sendBinaryRequestToReplica()` sends supported non-replicated commands to a selected node. See Metadata changes."],[3553,"`getMetadataChanges` exposes the metadata log. `sendRawRequest(code:payload:replica:)` targets a replica for supported non-replicated commands. See Metadata changes."],[3258,"`getMetadataChanges` follows metadata history, and raw requests can target a named replica. See Metadata changes and Client failover."],[4456,"`grafana/dashboards/iggy.json`:"],[4455,"`grafana/provisioning/dashboards/iggy.yaml`:"],[4454,"`grafana/provisioning/datasources/prometheus.yaml`:"],[860,"`heartbeat_timeout` must be at least `2s` and at least 4x `commit_broadcast_interval`, so one delayed broadcast never trips an election on a healthy primary."],[1753,"`id` stores the full unsigned 128-bit message ID as a decimal, without stream, topic, partition or offset in the key. Offset and checksum values are cast to signed 64-bit integers, and partition IDs to signed 32-bit integers. Values above those signed ranges appear negative. Message and origin timestamps are interpreted as Unix microseconds. Zero becomes the Unix epoch. A timestamp outside the date library's range falls back to the current time. `created_at` is generated by PostgreSQL when inserting."],[1206,"`If-Match`, `If-None-Match`, `Idempotency-Key` and `Content-Type` are managed by the runtime and cannot be overridden in `request_headers`."],[792,"`iggy login` authenticates once and stores a session token in the platform's secure credential store: Secret Service on Linux and the BSDs, Keychain on macOS, Credential Manager on Windows. Subsequent commands use the cached token automatically. This requires the default `login-session` feature and an available credential-store backend. Linux and BSD need a running Secret Service provider on the D-Bus session."],[790,"`iggy login` tries the supplied credentials before a cached session, so it can replace an expired login. Other commands fail if their cached token is rejected. They do not retry with the next credential source in the same invocation."],[793,"`iggy session status` only checks whether a token exists in the local keyring. An expired token **still reports as active**. On TCP, QUIC and WebSocket, run `iggy me` to verify the session against the server. HTTP does not support `me`. Use an authenticated command such as `iggy stream list` with the required permissions."],[1671,"`iggy_checksum` and `iggy_message_id` are strings. Offsets and message timestamps remain numbers. Message timestamps are microseconds, while `iggy_ingested_at` uses current wall-clock milliseconds. Disabling metadata leaves payload fields intact and still injects a missing primary key."],[1150,"`iggy_connector_errors_total` - errors encountered."],[1147,"`iggy_connector_messages_consumed_total` - messages consumed from Iggy (sink)."],[1149,"`iggy_connector_messages_filtered_total` - messages intentionally dropped by transforms returning `Ok(None)`, excluding transform errors."],[1148,"`iggy_connector_messages_processed_total` - messages passed to the sink plugin in batches that returned success. A failed batch adds an error and no processed messages. The runtime cannot infer partial delivery inside the plugin."],[1145,"`iggy_connector_messages_produced_total` - messages received from a source plugin's poll."],[1146,"`iggy_connector_messages_sent_total` - messages sent to Iggy (source)."],[1143,"`iggy_connectors_sinks_running` - sinks in the `Running` status."],[1142,"`iggy_connectors_sinks_total` - configured sink connectors."],[1141,"`iggy_connectors_sources_running` - sources in the `Running` status."],[1140,"`iggy_connectors_sources_total` - configured source connectors."],[1516,"`iggy_id` is a 32-character lowercase hex string (no dashes)."],[2506,"`IGGY_SHARD_RUNTIME_CAPACITY` and `IGGY_SHARD_EVENT_INTERVAL` override the two numeric defaults. On macOS, compio uses its polling driver and a blocking pool for file I/O."],[2597,"`IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS` and `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` are needed because the server binds `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default, which the published port can't reach. The Rust tutorial's Starting the Iggy server section explains each flag."],[1517,"`iggy_timestamp` is the message timestamp in Unix epoch microseconds. `include_checksum` and `include_origin_timestamp` add the corresponding metadata fields. Both default to `false`."],[924,"`iggy-server --fresh` deletes the configured data directory before boot. In cluster mode this wipes **that replica only**: it rejoins the cluster and refills its state by transfer from the other replicas. Wiping a quorum of replicas at the same time **destroys committed data**, and a service unit that always passes `--fresh` re-transfers the whole dataset on every restart."],[3652,"`iggy-server` accepts these four startup options, plus `--help` (`-h`) and `--version` (`-V`):"],[2799,"`iggy::ffi::from_connection_string()` accepts these strings. `new_connection()` instead takes an `IggyClientConfig`: `{}` selects TCP at `127.0.0.1:8090` without automatic login, and its `server_address` field overrides that address. Binary clients authenticate from their connection string during `connect()`. HTTP requires an explicit `login_user()` call. See connection strings for the supported options and credential syntax."],[2830,"`IggyClientConfigurator`"],[3038,"`IggyClientConfigurator` also exposes TCP socket buffers, TLS, heartbeat, response-frame limits, reconnection with exponential backoff (on by default), auto-login, and message encryption. HTTP clients require explicit login. The TCP connection settings are not applied to HTTP. See Client configuration for the full reference."],[2866,"`IggyClientConfigurator` exposes the full set of connection options - buffer sizes, TLS, heartbeat, automatic reconnection with exponential backoff, and auto-login:"],[3010,"`IggyConsumerBuilder<T>` accepts an `IDeserializer<T>` and exposes `ReceiveDeserializedAsync`. Keep the typed builder in a variable before configuring it because inherited fluent methods return the base builder type. Use `WithPollOptions` to select the commit policy, and inspect each message status before accepting its progress."],[3528,"`IggyConsumerConfig::default()` names `test_stream`, `test_topic` and `test_consumer`. It uses a group, `next()`, a five-second deferred wait and manual commits. It retries initialization five times at three-second intervals. It does not create a missing stream or topic by default."],[2804,"`IggyException::OperationId()` identifies retained uncertain operations. `ResolveOperation` retries a mutation with its retained identity, without returning the original payload. `AbandonOperation` frees local state without undoing the result. A lost committing poll reports `fetch_progress_uncertain` and blocks partition progress until abandonment."],[3441,"`IggyProducer` batches writes and `IggyConsumer` reads messages through a partition reader. Both share the client's routing, session and recovery behavior. The consumer manages group membership and tracks checkpoints. Select when it stores those checkpoints through `PollOptions`."],[3527,"`IggyStream::with_client_from_connection_string` also creates the client. Its result contains the client, producer and consumer. Resource creation flags remain explicit on the consumer configuration."],[3526,"`IggyStreamProducer::build(&client, &producer_config)` and `IggyStreamConsumer::build(&client, &consumer_config)` reuse a connected client. To build both, combine the configuration:"],[1630,"`include_metadata = false` omits those three values. It does not disable the independently controlled `iggy_checksum` and `iggy_origin_timestamp` fields."],[1473,"`index_mapping` is sent verbatim as the body of the create-index request, so it must be a structured object, not a JSON string embedded in TOML. It is used only when the connector creates an index. It does not update an existing index and is ignored when `create_index_if_not_exists = false`. That setting disables the startup existence check too. Express the mapping as nested TOML tables:"],[4099,"`initial_mtu = \"1200 B\"` is the QUIC minimum. MTU discovery probes upward automatically, and boot rejects values below 1200."],[4016,"`io_uring_disabled=0` permits ring creation. With `1`, an unprivileged service needs membership in the configured `io_uring_group`. With `2`, creation is disabled. Container syscall filters can independently deny access. Configure access for the service rather than assuming that a root shell proves the container can start. See the kernel's io_uring controls and Docker deployment guidance."],[2501,"`io_uring` is **completion-based** - you submit I/O requests to a submission queue (SQ), and the kernel completes them and places results in a completion queue (CQ). Both queues are shared ring buffers between user space and kernel. Submissions and completions can be batched to reduce syscalls. This is fundamentally better for disk I/O."],[835,"`ip`"],[874,"`ip` is **not the bind interface** for the client transports. `tcp`, `quic`, `http`, and `websocket` bind whatever their own `address` settings say. The roster supplies only their ports. The defaults bind loopback, so a cluster spread across hosts needs each transport's `address` set to `0.0.0.0` or the routable interface. The server warns at startup when a bind cannot serve the advertised `ip`."],[917,"`ip` must be a **literal IP address**. Use `advertised_address` when clients can't reach it (see Configuration)"],[3705,"`issuer`, `audience`, and `jwks_url` are required per entry. `username` names the active, non-root Iggy account to which the issuer maps. Provision that account before using its tokens. The old `user_id` key is rejected."],[2609,"`iter_messages()` is the simplest way to read from a group. For a long-running service with its own shutdown signal, use `consume_messages(callback, shutdown_event)` instead."],[1701,"`json` parses the payload and converts it to a BSON value under `payload`, including objects, arrays and scalars. Invalid JSON or an unsupported BSON conversion rejects the whole chunk before insertion."],[4101,"`keep_alive_interval` is one third of `max_idle_timeout`, so two consecutive lost keep-alives fit before the idle timer closes the connection."],[2256,"`key_pattern` variants: `exact`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, `contains`, `regex` - each takes a string, e.g. `key_pattern = { contains = \"name\" }`."],[445,"`kind = 1`: consumer."],[446,"`kind = 2`: consumer group."],[447,"`kind = 3`: externally managed group offsets. This kind cannot poll or join native consumer groups."],[1446,"`Label Already Exists` is accepted only when the existing job is `FINISHED`. `RUNNING` and `CANCELLED` are retried. `Publish Timeout` is accepted as committed, although rows may not yet be visible. Deduplication lasts only while Doris retains the label. Check the retention settings for your Doris version. Doris 4.0.3 uses `streaming_label_keep_max_second` (default 12 hours) for Stream Load. See Doris FE configuration."],[4098,"`max_concurrent_bidi_streams = 256` bounds concurrent bidirectional streams on a connection. Deferred requests can wait while other requests use separate streams."],[1355,"`max_request_bytes` defaults to 8 MiB and is clamped from 64 KiB through 9 MiB. The sink splits batches to stay below the API request limit. `max_retries` defaults to three total attempts, including the first. Retry delay starts at one second and is capped at 30 seconds. Each request and startup operation has a 30-second default timeout."],[2083,"`max_retries` defaults to five retries after the first broker acknowledgement attempt. If these attempts fail, the source abandons that acknowledgement and continues. Broker redelivery can then duplicate the message. QoS 0 has no acknowledgement token and cannot use this recovery mechanism."],[1628,"`message_id` is always a string field and `offset` is always a tag."],[4220,"`messages_required_to_save` (default `1024`): count threshold."],[453,"`MessagesKey`: `kind = 3`, `length = 1..255`, value is a routing key hashed to a partition."],[357,"`metadata_length` counts the bytes from `stream_id` through `messages_count` inclusive, so a reader can skip straight to the batch. The producer leaves `partition_id`, `base_offset`, and `base_timestamp` zero in the batch header, and the server stamps them. Every checksum is producer-computed and verified at admission. The reserved regions must be zero."],[3289,"`metadata.change_log_resource_capacity` defaults to 65536 records. `metadata.change_log_activity_capacity` defaults to 16384 records. Each budget also limits record bodies to 128 MiB. A busy activity log does not evict resource history. Nodes can retain different depths."],[834,"`name`"],[3334,"`node consumer-group.mjs` does the same from the group's stored offset."],[3333,"`node consumer.mjs` prints only the new messages, because `PollingStrategy.Next` continues after the stored offset. With nothing new to read, it prints nothing."],[3332,"`node producer.mjs` finds the existing stream and topic and appends ten more messages."],[3251,"`org.apache.iggy.Iggy` is the unified entrypoint for building clients. `Iggy.tcpClientBuilder().blocking()` returns the same builder as `IggyTcpClient.builder()` used above."],[452,"`PartitionId`: `kind = 2`, `length = 4`, value is a u32 partition id."],[4422,"`Partitioning.messages_key` hashes the key to pick the partition, so orders for one customer share a partition and stay in order. The connection string comes from the environment, so the same script works against a server on another host (see Connection strings)."],[2055,"`payload_column` extracts one column. Without it, every `payload_format` setting emits whole-row JSON. With it, the selected `json`, `text` or `raw` format applies, and the destination `streams.schema` should match. Selecting a column that is missing from a row causes a poll error."],[1637,"`payload_format` selects the stored representation after stream decoding and transforms. For arbitrary bytes, use `schema = \"raw\"` with `payload_format = \"base64\"` and no payload-changing transform. For plain text, use `schema = \"text\"` with `payload_format = \"text\"`. JSON stream decoding can change the original byte representation."],[1825,"`payload_format=\"json\"` writes a string column, not a native Redshift SUPER value. Use Redshift JSON parsing when structured queries need that representation. `varbyte` preserves the serialized payload as bytes."],[378,"`permissions_len` and the permissions bytes are present **only** when `has_permissions = 1`. When it is 0 the options block follows immediately. The user catalog includes `username` and `status` for updates. Creation carries those values in its fixed fields. `status`: 1 = active, 2 = inactive."],[3955,"`persisted` adds synchronization to the completion path. Its latency and throughput depend on storage, filesystem, batching, and concurrency. Measure both policies on the intended hardware rather than applying results from the removed `enforce_fsync` configuration. The benchmarking guide explains how to reproduce a workload."],[961,"`persisted` protects acknowledged operations across process and power failures when the required storage copies remain intact and the storage honors synchronization. Recovery and availability still require the protocol's quorums. It cannot protect against destruction of all durable copies, nor does it prevent configured retention or explicit deletion."],[2931,"`PingAsync` is **more than a bare ping** over TCP: it also re-syncs the assignment of every consumer group this client has joined, at the cost of one extra round trip per joined group. The client already pings on its own every `HeartbeatInterval`, so an explicit ping is only needed to force an assignment refresh sooner. `GetCurrentAddress()` is useful under leader redirection, when the client may have followed the cluster roster to a different node than the one it was configured with (see Connection semantics)."],[2079,"`plugin_config.tls.ca_file` selects a CA bundle. `client_cert_file` and `client_key_file` must be supplied together for mutual TLS. Certificate hostname validation uses the host in `broker_url`. A separate server-name override is not available."],[81,"`poll_messages` requires `update` as well as `read` when `auto_commit` is true or the `next` strategy is used. The `next` strategy enables auto-commit automatically. Storing an offset requires `update`. Deleting an offset requires `delete`. PAT creation and deletion require `create` and `delete`, respectively."],[3373,"`PollingStrategy::timestamp()` and `timestampMicros()` expect microseconds since the Unix epoch. Use `PollingStrategy::timestampSeconds()` for PHP `time()` values."],[3255,"`PollOptions.waitFor(Duration.ofSeconds(5))` enables deferred polling. `CommitPolicy.manual()` is the default. A reader or consumer also accepts `CommitPolicy.auto(...)`. One-shot polls reject automatic policies. The async client exposes a partition reader through `client.reader(...)`. A blocking wrapper is available as `org.apache.iggy.client.blocking.reader.PartitionReader`."],[3525,"`PollOptions` defaults to manual commits. `CommitPolicy::Auto(AutoCommit::default())` stores progress after processing every 10,000 messages or five seconds. `CommitPolicy::OnRead` stores with the read, before application processing. Read the commit contract before selecting a policy."],[3053,"`PollOptions` separates read limits from the polling strategy and consumer identity. A strategy selects the starting position, such as a specific offset or the next stored offset."],[837,"`ports`"],[915,"`ports` is the single source of listener ports: every enabled transport needs an explicit per-node port, otherwise the server **refuses to start**"],[1248,"`POST /sinks/{key}/configs`: add a new configuration version for the sink."],[1255,"`POST /sinks/{key}/restart`: restart the sink connector."],[1259,"`POST /sources/{key}/configs`: add a new configuration version for the source."],[1266,"`POST /sources/{key}/restart`: restart the source connector."],[2013,"`POST /topics/{topic_path}` uses `auth_bearer_token`. If that field is omitted, the named path has no authentication. `POST /e/{endpoint_id}` uses a secret path with optional bearer or HMAC authentication. Generate secret endpoint IDs and keep their URLs private."],[4327,"`preallocate_segments` requests a `segment_size` reservation for each partition's segment file when it is opened, including during topic creation and rotation. On Linux this reserves disk space without changing the file's logical length. Unsupported or failed reservations log a warning and fall back to ordinary allocation. With the default 1 GiB segment size, the request is 1 GiB per partition up front, which is why it's opt-in and why one create is **capped at 64 GiB** of requested reservation."],[2263,"`preserve_unknown_fields`, `validate_messages`, `type_url_prefix`, and `strict_mode` are accepted but have no effect. `pretty_json` affects JSON text output, and `include_metadata` adds fields on the supported protobuf-to-JSON paths."],[4282,"`previous_keys` accepts at most two keys. Retire an old fingerprint before adding a fourth wrap. Do not discard keys needed by retained backups. Losing the only usable wrapping key makes the affected streams unreadable."],[995,"`previous_shared_secret` enables a rolling key rotation without an authentication outage. The retiring key is accepted *for verification only*. Every MAC a node produces uses `shared_secret`. Rotate in three rolls, restarting every node after each step:"],[4452,"`prometheus/prometheus.yml`:"],[414,"`protocol_version` is the packed version of the protocol the client was built against. `sdk_name` identifies the SDK (for example `rust-sdk`, `go-sdk`). `sdk_version` is the SDK's own build version."],[4490,"`PUBLIC_IGGY_API_URL` is consumed by the browser: if the Iggy server also runs in Docker, use the host-published HTTP port here, not the container's network alias."],[4482,"`PUBLIC_IGGY_API_URL` must be a URL that the **browser** can reach (e.g. `http://localhost:3010`), not an internal Docker network hostname like `http://iggy:80`."],[2993,"`publisher.SendAsync(batch)` **takes ownership** - do not dispose the batch yourself. Its buffer returns to the pool once the batch is sent (immediately, or after the background flush)."],[1252,"`PUT /sinks/{key}/configs/active`: activate a specific configuration version for the sink."],[1263,"`PUT /sources/{key}/configs/active`: activate a specific configuration version for the source."],[3369,"`reader()` reads batches from explicit partitions. `groupReader()` reads an existing group's assignment. Close a reader with `close()`. A timeout on `next(timeout_micros)` returns null and keeps a later batch available for the next call."],[2803,"`Reader` reads batches from explicit partitions or a group assignment. `StandaloneConsumer` and `GroupConsumer` yield individual messages. Call `Close` before destruction. Manual commits are the default, and reader waits default to five seconds."],[3485,"`ReadError` identifies the failed partition and contains its underlying error. Recoverable read failures are retried. A history change, uncertain read or undecodable message can stop a partition. Inspect the error before calling `seek(partition_id, strategy)` to select a new start. A `skip` continuation lets an application deliberately move past a record it cannot decode."],[3040,"`ReceiveAsync` polls **indefinitely** - pass a `CancellationToken` or `break` out of the loop to stop."],[3009,"`ReceiveRentedAsync` exposes payloads from a pooled buffer. Dispose each yielded message before moving on, and copy bytes that must survive disposal. Read limits and commit policies are the same as for `ReceiveAsync`."],[2833,"`ReconnectionSettings`"],[2985,"`RentedMessageBatchBuilder`:"],[863,"`repair_chunk_max` must be between 1 and 1024, and strictly below `message_bus.peer_queue_capacity`: each repair frame rides the per-peer message-bus queue, and a full round above that capacity would overrun the queue and drop frames."],[836,"`replica_id`"],[914,"`replica_id` values must be unique and contiguous from `0`"],[4343,"`replicated` waits for quorum commit and application without an additional stable-storage barrier. `persisted` also requires recoverable stable-storage copies at the required quorum. On a single node that means recoverable local persistence before success. In a cluster it uses durable prepare history. Setting `messages_required_to_save=1` is not required for `persisted`."],[975,"`replication_factor` selects how many nodes store each partition of a topic. The value is fixed at topic creation. It must be from 1 through 32 and no greater than the cluster node count."],[497,"`ReplyHeader`: server to client, answers one request."],[496,"`RequestHeader`: client to server."],[818,"`resolve_operation` and its language equivalents retry a retained mutation. `abandon_operation` frees local state without undoing or determining the server result. `OperationNotRetained` does not prove that nothing committed. Keep the same client for recovery and handle the error before retrying application work."],[2266,"`schema_path`, `include_paths`, and `preserve_unknown_fields` have no effect. `root_table_name` only labels decoding metadata. `verify_buffers` checks the minimum buffer length (four bytes), not a schema-aware verifier. `buffer_size_hint` sets the initial encoder capacity. `pretty_json`, `include_metadata`, and `strict_mode` have no effect."],[4208,"`segment_size` is a per-topic creation option, bounded to a 512-byte multiple between 1 MiB and 1 GiB. It's a **soft limit**: rotation fires after the append that crosses it, so the crossing batch lands whole and a sealed segment may run one batch past the configured size. `preallocate_segments` (also per topic) reserves each segment's bytes up front on filesystems that support it. See Topic options."],[3489,"`send_binary_request(code, payload)` sends a raw request through a binary transport. `send_binary_request_to_replica(code, payload, replica_id)` targets a replica. Session-management commands remain restricted so a raw call cannot replace the client's session state. HTTP returns `FeatureUnavailable` for binary calls."],[3490,"`send_http_request(method, path, body)` sends an HTTP request and returns its raw body. Binary transports return `FeatureUnavailable` for that call. Use typed APIs for commands whose route, ownership or recovery identity the SDK must manage."],[2933,"`SendBinaryRequestAsync` is an escape hatch that sends a raw command code with a prebuilt payload and returns the raw response bytes, for commands the typed API does not cover:"],[3117,"`SendMessages` returns the placements reported by the server. An uncertain mutation retains its frozen request and original identity. Reconnection binds the existing session before attempting a new registration. `ResolveOperation` retries the retained identity, and `AbandonOperation` releases local state without undoing the server result. Keep the same client for recovery. A fresh send after losing receipt access can duplicate work."],[2984,"`SendMessagesAsync` accepts messages with `ReadOnlyMemory<byte>` payloads, which can share backing storage. On hot paths, build a `RentedMessageBatch` instead - every payload is written into a single buffer rented from the shared array pool - and hand it to `SendAsync`:"],[2916,"`SendMessagesAsync` returns a `SendMessagesResponse` whose `Confirmations` carry `StreamId`, `TopicId`, `PartitionId`, and `BaseOffset` (the offset of the first message of the batch) per committed batch. An empty list reports no assigned offsets. A single-message overload is also available: `SendMessagesAsync(streamId, topicId, partitioning, message)`."],[2209,"`serialize_state()` encodes the state to MessagePack via `ConnectorState::serialize()`. `generate_messages()` samples the half-open `messages_range` (lower bound included, upper bound excluded), capped by the remaining `max_count`. Each message carries a JSON-serialized `Record` with `payload_size` random alphanumeric characters of text. A non-increasing range returns `Error::InvalidConfigValue` from `poll()` without advancing state."],[998,"`shared_secret = new` alone, closing the window"],[997,"`shared_secret = new`, `previous_shared_secret = old` on every node"],[996,"`shared_secret = old`, `previous_shared_secret = new` on every node"],[4221,"`size_of_messages_required_to_save` (default `1 MiB`): byte threshold."],[2002,"`state.auto_save_interval` and `state.tracked_fields` do not affect the runtime plugin. Its JSON snapshot is a direct file write, separate from the runtime's atomic checkpoint protocol, and snapshot errors only warn. If enabled, account for this second snapshot when resetting or restoring progress. Removing only the runtime state file does not reset the plugin snapshot."],[353,"`strategy` is a polling strategy. `count` is the requested number of messages. `auto_commit = 1` asks the server to advance the consumer offset. A primary submits a quorum offset write before replying, but the poll does not wait for that write to commit. Partition reads route to their current primary. See Client failover."],[4100,"`stream_receive_window` is the per-stream limit within the shared connection receive window. Both default to 64 MiB."],[2640,"`StreamCreateOptions` configures stream creation. `TopicCreateOptions` selects partitions, retention and storage policies. Omitted optional fields use server defaults. The default message durability is `replicated`. Select `Durability::Persisted` when successful sends must wait for stable storage. See Topic options."],[1702,"`string` and its `text` alias require valid UTF-8 and store a BSON string."],[866,"`superblock_wedged_fatal_timeout` defaults to `2m`. It controls how long repeated superblock persistence failures can continue before the process exits. The affected consensus group stays fenced while persistence is failing. `0`, `disabled`, or `unlimited` disables the process exit, leaving the group fenced. A nonzero value must be at least `30s`."],[3116,"`tcp.WithTLSValidateCertificate` controls certificate validation. The client also accepts `client.WithLogger` for a custom `*slog.Logger` and runs a built-in heartbeat loop (every 5 seconds by default) once connected."],[916,"`tcp` and `tcp_replica` ports are **always required**, even when client TCP is disabled. `tcp_replica` carries replica-to-replica consensus traffic"],[2832,"`TlsSettings`"],[4073,"`topic_size_bytes`, `topic_messages` and `topic_max_size_bytes` use `stream` and `topic` labels. The size-cap series is absent for unlimited topics. Renamed or deleted topics disappear from the next scrape. Retention can keep more bytes than the configured cap, so a usage ratio is not a hard storage boundary."],[3967,"`transport` is `tcp`, `tcp_tls`, `ws`, `wss`, `quic` or `http`. Binary connections keep their connection identity across reconnect retries. HTTP creates a new UUID for each login request."],[3964,"`url` names the HTTPS endpoint. `IGGY_EXTERNAL_AUTH_URL` sets it from the environment and keeps it out of the file. The server sends the submitted password to this endpoint. Protect both the client connection and the callout with TLS."],[728,"`user create` and `user permissions` accept the same permission flags:"],[2257,"`value_pattern` variants: `equals` (any JSON value), `contains` (substring), `regex`, `greater_than`, `less_than`, `between` (e.g. `{ between = [1.0, 10.0] }`), and the parameterless checks `is_null`, `is_not_null`, `is_string`, `is_number`, `is_boolean`, `is_object`, `is_array` (written as plain strings, e.g. `value_pattern = \"is_number\"`)."],[2183,"`verbose` and `benchmark` are optional and default to `false`. `verbose` switches the connector's per-batch logging to info level, and `benchmark` emits per-batch timing events for performance measurement."],[861,"`view_change_status_timeout` must be at least 4x `view_change_retransmit_interval`, so a few dropped view-change messages retransmit instead of escalating into a fresh cluster-wide election."],[862,"`view_probe_attempts_max` must be between 1 and 100."],[3522,"`with_topic_durability(Durability::Persisted)` selects the policy when the builder creates a topic. It does not change an existing topic. Client-side `encryptor` configuration is separate from stream encryption."],[2930,"> **Note:** Join/Leave are TCP-only and throw `FeatureUnavailableException` on HTTP."],[2939,"> **Note:** TCP-only - throws `FeatureUnavailableException` on HTTP."],[2891,"> **Warning:** TCP reconnection is **on by default** with **unlimited retries**. A failed pass tries the known roster addresses before consuming one retry. Connection and TLS handshake failures can be retried. An invalid local CA file stops after the pass, and rejected login credentials are surfaced. Pass a `CancellationToken` or set `MaxRetries` to bound connection attempts, or set `Enabled = false` to disable reconnection. Request replay also has its own deadline and requires configured or remembered login credentials."],[845,"> `cluster.name` is a **permanent on-disk identity**, not a label. It's hashed into the cluster id stamped into every metadata and partition superblock on first boot, and the consensus layer derives its cluster id from the name unconditionally. Nodes with different names cannot connect to each other, and changing the name later makes the server refuse to start against existing data."],[907,"> `cluster.name` is a **permanent on-disk identity**. It is hashed into the cluster id stamped into every metadata and partition superblock on first boot. Changing the name later makes the server **refuse to start** against the existing data, so renaming a cluster means starting from an empty data directory."],[1023,"> Iggy hasn't reached a 1.0 production release yet. The cluster protocol and configuration described here can still change between pre-release versions."],[502,"| `command`  | Value | Direction        | Meaning                                         |\n| ---------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `Eviction` | 13    | server to client | Connection rejected, no per-request correlation |"],[501,"| `command` | Value | Direction        | Meaning               |\n| --------- | ----- | ---------------- | --------------------- |\n| `Reply`   | 8     | server to client | Answer to one request |"],[500,"| `command` | Value | Direction        | Meaning           |\n| --------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------------- |\n| `Request` | 5     | client to server | A command request |"],[1967,"| `payload_format`    | Stored payload and encoding                                     |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `base64` / `binary` | Base64 of the payload bytes, with `payload_encoding = \"base64\"` |"],[1964,"| `payload_format` | Stored payload and encoding                                                                                                         |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `auto`           | JSON values as `json`. Text/Proto variants as `text`. Raw/Avro/FlatBuffer bytes as `base64`, even when raw bytes contain valid JSON |"],[1965,"| `payload_format` | Stored payload and encoding                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `json`           | A JSON value, parsing other payload variants as JSON. Invalid JSON is rejected |"],[1966,"| `payload_format` | Stored payload and encoding                                                |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `text`           | A string, decoding other payload bytes as UTF-8. Invalid UTF-8 is rejected |"],[2369,"| Capability         | Details                                                               |\n| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **AI Integration** | MCP server with 40+ tools for LLM-driven message streaming management |"],[2366,"| Capability      | Details                                                                                                         |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Performance** | Thread-per-core + io_uring on Linux, binary message views, configurable memory pool with a default 4 GiB budget |"],[2370,"| Capability     | Details                                                                                                                                          |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **Management** | Web UI dashboard (embedded or standalone), CLI with shell completions, Prometheus metrics. Connector OpenTelemetry export (broker export limits) |"],[2368,"| Capability     | Details                                                                                                                         |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Connectors** | 17 sink and 6 source plugins including PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, Apache Iceberg, S3, with data transforms |"],[2371,"| Capability     | Details                                                                                                            |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **Clustering** | Built on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) consensus. Single node by default, multi-node via `[cluster]` configuration |"],[2372,"| Capability     | Details                                                                       |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Deployment** | Single server process, Docker & Helm, configurable CPU/NUMA affinity on Linux |"],[2365,"| Capability    | Details                                                    |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Transport** | TCP, QUIC, WebSocket (custom binary protocol), HTTP (REST) |"],[2367,"| Capability   | Details                    