res no offsets by default."],[2815,"A consumer that creates and joins a consumer group, stores each offset after handling the yielded message and requesting the next one, and surfaces polling errors:"],[823,"A contacted HTTP node can forward to the correct primary in one hop. The route and request carry a signed relay proof, and the destination authenticates the bearer independently. Configure shared JWT key material and synchronize clocks. Proofs expire after ten seconds."],[2643,"A continuation carries the next position and the partition history that produced it. Do not advance an offset by the number of returned messages because offsets can contain gaps. A checkpoint stores progress under that history. If the partition is recreated, the server refuses a stale checkpoint."],[3940,"A count or byte trigger, or a full active segment, can flush the committed journal prefix. Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle work can flush earlier. These thresholds are not a maximum data-loss guarantee and do not specify a periodic flush interval. A quiet `replicated` topic can retain messages in process memory while waiting for a trigger."],[3271,"A cursor identifies a position in this log. Save the returned `next` value after applying the corresponding records. Cursor text starts with `mc1_`. Current-state page tokens start with `cs1_`. Treat both as opaque values and use the SDK parsers."],[1993,"A custom `query` must be a structured object. In TOML that means nested tables (an inline JSON string won't work):"],[1411,"A custom S3 endpoint restricts the default credential chain. Set `AWS_FORCE_CREDENTIAL_LOAD=true` in the runtime environment when shared profile or SSO credentials are required. Static keys, web identity, container credentials and instance metadata remain available without that override."],[1183,"A default configuration is embedded in the binary and always merged as the base, so omitted settings inherit its values. A local provider still needs a nonempty `connectors.config_dir`."],[978,"A factor of one provides no replica redundancy. A factor of two stops writes when either copy is unavailable. With three copies, the partition can tolerate one unavailable replica. See Durability for the quorum rules and persistence policies."],[4337,"A factor of one stores one copy. Replicated durability can acknowledge it before stable storage. A factor of two requires both replicas to make progress. Three copies tolerate one unavailable replica. See Cluster durability for larger quorum rules."],[4097,"A few of these are deliberate rather than arbitrary:"],[4276,"A fingerprint identifies a key without exposing it. It consists of 16 lowercase hexadecimal characters. It cannot replace a key during recovery."],[2615,"A first program with the low-level Rust SDK: create a stream and topic, send messages, then poll them back."],[2588,"A first program with the Python SDK: create a stream and topic, send messages, then read them back on their own and as a consumer group."],[667,"A frame's absolute position is derived, not stored: offset = `base_offset + offset_delta`. The server-stamped time is the flat batch `base_timestamp` (the delta does not apply to it). Producer time resolves as `origin_timestamp + timestamp_delta`."],[4399,"A fresh standalone server refuses to start without root credentials. Set both `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` before first boot, or use `--with-default-root-credentials` for a disposable development instance. The server no longer generates a password."],[3993,"A fresh standalone server requires `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD`, or the development flag. It never generates a password. A cluster replica without credentials waits for the root account to be replicated. Provision credentials on every replica for first cluster boot."],[4224,"A full active segment also triggers an ordinary flush. These are soft limits: a flush writes whole committed batches, so the actual count or size can overshoot. Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle operations can flush below the thresholds. The settings do not define a periodic flush interval. The `MessagesWriter` uses **vectored I/O** with up to 1024 buffers per syscall, so many buffered batches can land in one write."],[2937,"A full snapshot can exceed the default 64 MiB reply-frame bound, in which case the reply is **refused and the connection dropped**. Raise `MaxResponseFrameSize` before requesting large snapshots."],[3947,"A graceful shutdown drains work and forces a final flush of committed messages, regardless of the thresholds. This depends on the shutdown finishing and its I/O succeeding. A container or service termination that expires its grace period and sends `SIGKILL` is a process crash, not a completed graceful shutdown."],[4437,"A Grafana dashboard for the broker"],[3062,"A group consumer uses its assigned partitions. Assignment changes and primary changes require a refresh before further reads. Use the SDK reader or consumer to manage this state. A raw fetch caller must also supply the route and ownership information required by the protocol."],[4277,"A key-encryption key (KEK) wraps stream data keys. Rotation changes the wraps and does not re-encrypt messages. The metadata primary replicates these changes. Each stream can hold up to three wraps."],[2262,"A loaded descriptor is used for JSON-to-protobuf encoding. Without one, that direction produces JSON text in `Payload::Proto`. Protobuf-to-JSON conversion does not decode a custom message descriptor: it parses text as JSON, exposes an Any envelope, or returns metadata containing base64 raw data. Protobuf-to-Avro/FlatBuffers conversion only rewraps the bytes."],[1588,"A lookup failure skips the affected message. It does not fail the batch. Each batch loads its destination tables again. The route field is included in the JSON row, but is ignored by the writer if the target schema has no matching column."],[1970,"A malformed record is skipped while valid records in its chunk are still submitted. A failed chunk does not stop later chunks, and the last error is returned. The sink checks HTTP and SQL statement statuses. Its own processed counter counts records submitted in successful statements, including records ignored by `INSERT IGNORE`. Runtime counters instead reflect whether the whole callback succeeded. Neither is a count of newly inserted rows."],[1387,"A message whose payload type does not match the chosen format is skipped with an error log. The rest of the batch is still sent. A batch with no serializable payloads returns success without an insert. In `row_binary` mode, a JSON row whose values cannot be converted to the column types fails the whole batch before any insert request, so it does not enter the plugin's insert retry loop."],[4228,"A missing or torn sparse index can be rebuilt from valid log data. An incomplete tail can be truncated, but an interior gap, intact records after damage, or a contradiction with durable history can require recovery refusal. Recovery does not blindly truncate at the first invalid batch."],[412,"A new logical session authenticates through the register handshake: command code **40** (`LOGIN_REGISTER`, username and password) or **45** (`LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT`, personal access token). Both ride `operation = 1` (`Register`) with `session = 0`, `request = 0`, and a non-zero `client` id. Mint a fresh id for a new logical client. A reconnect retains its request identities and binds the existing session. A matching registration retry preserves its epoch."],[3290,"A node can lag behind the metadata primary. A cursor that is valid elsewhere can receive Behind from that node. Wait or use a node that has caught up. Expired and Reset require rebuilding, not a loop over the same stale position."],[2162,"A non-increasing `messages_range` returns a configuration error when message generation is attempted. The SDK logs poll errors and continues polling. Each generated JSON record contains a random UUID `id`, fixed `title = \"Hello\"` and `name = \"World\"`, and random alphanumeric `text`. `payload_size` controls only that text, not the total serialized record size."],[788,"A nonempty `IGGY_TOKEN` environment variable."],[591,"A nonzero header `status` has an empty body. With `status = 0`, decode the result section for the operations above: it can report either a committed outcome or a pre-commit transient rejection. Its presence alone does not prove commitment. A matching final result also needs the committed-result marker at byte 220. Replayed PAT creation cannot return the original secret and can report `PersonalAccessTokenAlreadyExists` with that proof. A successful login-register reply starts with `count = 0`, followed by the login response payload. An empty Register body is a terminal failure, not a successful login."],[3808,"a number, e.g. `4`: four shards on the first four CPUs in the process's allowed set when `pin_cores` is enabled"],[2496,"A numeric value (e.g. `4`) - exactly N shards, pinned to the first N CPUs in the process's allowed CPU set when pinning is enabled"],[2651,"A one-line definition of each term, with a link to the page that explains it."],[3066,"A one-shot poll rejects an automatic policy because it has no continuing processing state. On-read commits can precede application processing. A failover before offset replication can also deliver the last batch again. Use manual commits or automatic commits after processing when messages must be processed before their offsets advance."],[2585,"A partition primary orders writes for that partition. Different partitions can have different primaries. `replication_factor` determines how many nodes store each partition. Metadata has its own primary and spans the full cluster."],[3061,"A partition reader groups targets by their current primary and uses FetchMessages. Each fetch applies `count`, `min_count` and `max_bytes` across its targets together. The reader can keep several fetches active. It can return a partition with messages without waiting for a quiet partition first."],[2704,"A payload can be up to 64 MB, and a message's user headers up to 100 KB in total. The whole send request also has to fit the server's request limit, which by default is 64 MiB over TCP and 2 MB over HTTP."],[2922,"A poll reads one explicit partition. Use a reader for several partitions or a consumer group assignment. `PollOptions` defaults to no wait and manual commits."],[972,"A primary orders writes for one consensus group. Iggy has one metadata group and a separate group for each partition. Multi-leader partitions let different nodes lead different partitions at the same time."],[2814,"A publisher that creates the stream and topic if missing, batches sends in the background, and retries failures:"],[2497,"A range (e.g. `\"5..8\"`) - shards on CPUs 5, 6 and 7 when pinning is enabled. Those CPUs must be allowed for the process"],[3809,"a range, e.g. `\"5..8\"`: three shards on cores 5, 6, 7"],[3008,"A read error that stops a partition ends enumeration with `ReadException`. Inspect `StopsPartition`, `IsResumable` and `Skip` before selecting a new start with `SeekAsync`. Automatic retries do not discard an undecodable record without an application decision."],[3277,"A rebuild reads Current pages, then follows changes from the returned anchor. Keep the rebuilt state private until a change page reports no more records and reaches at least the last current page's `head_op`. A sequence of current pages alone does not establish that the mirror caught up."],[558,"A reconnect uses BindSession. Ending the session loses access to unresolved receipts. A fresh operation can duplicate an earlier uncertain write. SDKs retain uncertain operations and block conflicting work until resolution or abandonment."],[3060,"A reply can contain fewer than `min_count` messages when the wait ends. The first message can exceed `max_bytes` and return alone, so a large message does not prevent progress. Treat the byte budget as a batching limit, not an absolute allocation guarantee."],[816,"A request rejected before admission can be retried within its deadline. A lost reply after sending is different: the operation can already be committed. Retained-operation SDKs preserve its original identity and payload instead of submitting a fresh mutation."],[959,"A restarting replica loads segment and offset state and, when enabled, reconciles the durable prepare WAL with that state. It also restores durable consensus state and follows the recovery protocol before serving as a healthy replica. It does not treat every locally recovered prepare as committed."],[3032,"A retained session can survive transport loss. Reconnect binds it before registering a new session. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end the session. An uncertain mutation retains its request identity for recovery. Keep the same client and use `ResolveOperationAsync` or `AbandonOperationAsync` when an exception reports a retained operation. Abandonment does not undo the server result."],[556,"A retry keeps the original client, session, request number, logical request stamp and arguments. New mutations use new request numbers. Registration uses request zero. Do not infer that a low-numbered request succeeded because a later request committed."],[1356,"A row that cannot be encoded or accepted is logged and dropped while other rows can proceed. Transient request failures retry with jittered exponential backoff. The `_default` stream does not provide exactly-once append semantics. A lost response can lead to duplicate rows."],[3078,"A runnable getting-started example lives in the examples/go directory of the core repository. Its `go.mod` carries a `replace` directive pointing at the in-repo SDK, so the example always builds against the SDK source in the same checkout. The examples are exercised in CI via `scripts/run-examples-from-readme.sh --language go`."],[3122,"A runnable getting-started example with producer, consumer, and TLS flags lives in examples/go. See the examples page for how to run it."],[4034,"A separate 1 GiB-page experiment requires platform support, a suitable pool, and a budget for each process. mimalloc advises normally choosing this reservation mode separately from `MIMALLOC_ALLOW_LARGE_OS_PAGES`. See mimalloc environment options. HugeTLB allocation can require `CAP_IPC_LOCK` or membership in `vm.hugetlb_shm_group`, as described in mmap permissions."],[484,"A served partition context is 24 bytes:"],[956,"A shared barrier can also persist co-batched operations with the weaker policy. That incidental persistence does not strengthen what their earlier `replicated` acknowledgements promised."],[1292,"A source produces messages to the configured stream and topic. A sink consumes messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s):"],[1158,"A source that supplies checkpoint bytes gets a file named `source_{key}.state` (e.g. `local_state/source_random.state`) holding the raw state bytes - by convention MessagePack produced by the SDK helpers. Sinks have no state files. Their progress lives in Iggy consumer groups."],[814,"A stale link does not always mean the logical session ended. The SDK closes that link and tries to bind again. A terminal session refusal requires new registration. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end the session. A password or PAT change does not revoke a session already established with those credentials."],[2644,"A standalone consumer does not join a group. Two applications using its same identity can change the same stored offset. Use a consumer group to assign partitions among service instances. The high-level consumer manages those assignments and grouped fetches."],[3524,"A standalone consumer uses `ConsumerKind::Consumer`. Select its partitions with `with_partitions`. The default configuration selects partition zero. Group consumers use the server assignment instead. `ExternalGroup` represents externally managed offsets and cannot poll."],[4261,"A stream accepts options when it is created and when it is updated. Use `name` to rename a stream. Use `encryption_key` at creation to supply a data key for that stream. The server rejects changes or resets to an existing stream key."],[419,"A successful login is a normal `Reply` with `status = 0`. Its body includes an empty result section before the login payload:"],[3925,"A successful write has the guarantee selected by the topic's `durability` option. The default, `replicated`, waits for consensus commit and local application without an additional stable-storage barrier. `persisted` also requires a recoverable copy on stable storage at the replication quorum."],[2060,"A timestamp watermark is not change-data capture. Late inserts, edits and backdated points at or below an acknowledged watermark can be missed, and changing the order of tied rows invalidates offset-based progress. Neither version tracks deletions. The connector's timestamp-plus-position message IDs can also collide for distinct rows across batches. They are not unique database-row keys."],[2641,"A topic's first partition is zero. `Partitioning::partition_id(0)` selects it explicitly. `balanced()` distributes batches across partitions, and `messages_key()` selects a partition from a key. Ordering applies within a partition."],[2233,"A transform receives each decoded message and returns either the (possibly modified) message, or nothing - in which case the message is dropped from the batch. A transform can also return an error. A source rejects the whole batch and its checkpoint on a transform error. A sink drops the affected message and continues processing the batch."],[2693,"A type field in a header"],[2692,"A type field in the payload"],[2817,"A typed publisher/consumer pair that (de)serializes a record as JSON. The typed builders are configured via statements rather than one fluent chain - the `With*` methods return the untyped base builder, so `Build()` **must be called on the typed builder variable** (see Typed consumer):"],[471,"A user headers block (inside a message frame) is a flat run of TLV fields:"],[2380,"About"],[5,"Access is limited in two layers. The `[permissions]` section of the MCP server's configuration can restrict it to read-only operations, and the Iggy user it connects as keeps its own permissions on the server. For production use, connect as a dedicated user with the minimum permissions it needs."],[2492,"Across nodes, the SDK uses a partition route before this shard-local dispatch. Partition primaries are distributed across eligible nodes and rebalanced by the metadata primary. Each topic selects its own replication factor at creation. See Multi-leader partitions."],[3592,"Add `--consumer-offset-durability persisted` to request that policy too. These are create-time options and have no effect on existing topics with `--reuse-streams`. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous, so poll latency is not an acknowledged offset-store measurement. `iggy-bench examples` shows all benchmark kinds, transports, policy combinations, and available topic-option examples."],[3546,"Add `.product(name: \"Iggy\", package: \"iggy\")` to your target dependencies. Build the server and client from the same release source."],[2276,"Add a match arm to the `from_config` function deserializing your config."],[2274,"Add a new module implementing the `Transform` trait."],[2275,"Add a variant to the `TransformType` enum."],[709,"Add or remove partitions of a topic."],[2914,"Add partitions to or remove them from an existing topic:"],[4071,"Add Prometheus as a data source in Grafana, then build panels from the metric names above."],[3245,"Add the dependency under the `project` element in `pom.xml`:"],[2920,"Add typed custom headers to messages. Build keys with `HeaderKey.FromString` and values with the `HeaderValue.From*` factories:"],[2223,"add_fields"],[2231,"Adding a new transform"],[1211,"Additional mechanics of the local provider:"],[2192,"Additionally, our source connector will have its own state, which can be used e.g. to track the overall progress or store some relevant information when producing the data from the actual external sources or tooling."],[2239,"Adds top-level fields to the message payload, replacing any existing value with the same key. Each field has a `key` and a `value`, which is either static or computed at runtime:"],[815,"Advertised endpoints must be reachable from the client network. The SDK uses the endpoints returned by cluster metadata. See Configuration for network selectors and TLS names."],[2108,"After a polling cycle, read the produced records:"],[3643,"After boot the server writes the effective configuration, including the addresses it actually bound, to `{path}/runtime/current_config.toml` with the default runtime subdirectory."],[2995,"After disposal, `batch.Messages` and every payload's `Span` throw `ObjectDisposedException` instead of reading recycled pool memory."],[1919,"After saving the configuration files above, start the runtime. This explicitly enables the sink if you tried the optional disabling override:"],[2217,"After saving the configuration files above, start the runtime. This explicitly enables the source if you tried the optional disabling override:"],[666,"After the 48 header bytes: `payload` (`payload_length` bytes), then `user_headers` (`user_headers_length` bytes). The payload comes **first**."],[85,"After the broker is ready, run the MCP server on the same network:"],[1860,"After the health request succeeds, create the bucket using MinIO's client. These commands use Docker host networking on Linux:"],[1943,"After the health request succeeds, save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory. Its plugin path assumes the runtime starts from the checkout root."],[415,"After the prefix:"],[1787,"After verifying or creating the index, the sink probes the ingest endpoint with an empty body before accepting messages. This waits for the queue on Quickwit versions that return 404 while it starts. These probes submit no documents and retry HTTP 404, 429, 5xx and network failures. Each readiness check uses `max_open_retries` and `open_retry_max_delay`."],[1271,"All batches go to one partition, zero when omitted. Each plugin batch is one request. `batch_length` and `linger_time` do not apply in this mode. Use a topic with `persisted` durability and upgrade every node and reader before enabling producer-aware batches."],[841,"All clustering settings live in the `[cluster]` section of the server configuration. The authoritative defaults with detailed comments are in `core/server/config.toml`. Every value can also be set through an `IGGY_`-prefixed environment variable, for example `IGGY_CLUSTER_ENABLED=true` or `IGGY_CLUSTER_NODES_0_IP=10.0.1.5`."],[4205,"All data lives under the root `path` directory (default `local_data`, overridden with `IGGY_PATH`):"],[629,"All multi-byte integers are **little-endian** unless stated otherwise."],[1569,"All options below are required except the credential pair and `store_path_style_access`. `tables` and `dynamic_route_field` must be present even when the selected routing mode does not use them."],[2896,"All properties are init-only: build the settings with an object initializer or the static factories `AutoLoginSettings.For(username, password)` and `AutoLoginSettings.ForPersonalAccessToken(token)`. TCP reconnects use configured auto-login credentials first, then credentials remembered from a successful manual login. Without either, a reconnect cannot restore the authenticated session."],[402,"All section headers precede the records. Records follow in table order. A failed section has no records. Inspect every section status before accepting progress. See Deferred polling for SDK use."],[2487,"All shards, **including shard 0**, own partitions and serve partition requests."],[1300,"All the available transforms and their configuration options are documented on the **Transforms page**."],[1117,"All the connectors are implemented as Rust libraries and can be used as a part of the connector runtime. The runtime is responsible for managing the lifecycle of the connectors and providing the necessary infrastructure for the connectors to run. For more information, please refer to the **runtime documentation**."],[2635,"All the data used by the server will be persisted under the `local_data` directory, unless specified differently in the configuration."],[3331,"All three samples can be run again without changes:"],[4448,"All you need is Docker with Compose, since everything else runs in containers."],[4038,"Allocate dedicated CPUs where possible. On the current server, `[sharding] cpu_allocation` and `pin_cores` control shard placement, and the process's allowed CPU set constrains allocation. Check the embedded configuration for the exact version being deployed. Separate server CPUs from a colocated load generator and leave capacity for kernel and network work."],[1999,"Along with each successful poll, including an empty one, the connector returns a MessagePack checkpoint containing its timestamp watermark and counters. After sending the batch, the runtime saves that checkpoint and acknowledges it. With the default file state backend, it uses `source_<key>.state` under the runtime `[state]` path (default `local_state`). The runtime also supports an HTTP state backend. On restart, the saved checkpoint is passed to the plugin. Invalid MessagePack state logs a warning and starts fresh."],[1900,"Also, when implementing the sink connector, make sure to use the `sink_connector!` macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink with the runtime."],[2194,"Also, when implementing the source connector, make sure to use the `source_connector!` macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the source with the runtime."],[2459,"Also, when running the container, **make sure to include the additional capabilities**, as you can find in docker-compose file:"],[2633,"Alternatively, build from source by cloning the repository and running:"],[3362,"Alternatively, skip the install and point PHP at the built library per invocation:"],[3484,"Always await `consumer.shutdown()` before dropping it. Shutdown flushes automatic progress, adjusts on-read progress to delivered messages, and leaves the group. It is not a substitute for successful application processing. See Deferred polling for the shared contract."],[4333,"An absent key keeps its value. In HTTP, `null` also leaves a value unchanged. A name in `reset` returns that option to the server default and marks it as derived. Do not send the same key in both sets. Unknown keys, read-only keys and create-only changes are rejected. An empty patch, including a patch with only null values or zero sentinels, is rejected."],[4141,"An automatic cleaner removes expired tokens at a configurable interval."],[2255,"An empty `keep_fields` list together with an empty `patterns` list leaves the payload unchanged. Invalid regular expressions fail configuration loading. Numeric `between` comparisons include both endpoints."],[1160,"An empty or missing state file supplies no previous checkpoint when the connector starts. Loading fails if the file cannot be read or its parent directory is unavailable at load time. Stop the runtime before deleting a checkpoint to reset that source on its next start. Deleting it while the connector runs does not reset its in-memory position."],[4409,"An event-driven service with a consumer group"],[4266,"An explicit key must contain 32 random bytes. The API accepts raw bytes or standard padded base64. Configure a server encryption key even when the client supplies the stream key. The server wraps the data key, which means it encrypts the key before storing it. The wrapping key derives from `encryption.key`."],[3962,"An external authentication callout delegates a login decision to an HTTP service. Iggy calls it only when the submitted username does not exist locally. A wrong password for a local user never falls back to the service. Personal access tokens and HTTP bearer validation use their existing authentication paths."],[3129,"An idempotent producer lets a partition recognize a batch it already accepted. Each batch carries a producer ID, epoch and sequence. This identity survives a reconnect under a new client session. It is separate from the ID field of an individual message."],[3487,"An idempotent producer prevents duplicate appends when the same producer batch is retried. Enable it with `.idempotent()` before `build()` and call `init()` to obtain a producer ID. The default is disabled, and this mode requires a binary transport."],[418,"An incompatible protocol version is answered with a 256-byte `Eviction` frame, reason `14` (`IncompatibleProtocol`), carrying the accepted window at bytes 144 (max) and 148 (min) as packed u32 versions. A body without a decodable `ClientVersionInfo` prefix gets reason `15` (`MalformedLogin`) with a zero window. Bad credentials get reasons 9-11. See EvictionHeader for the frame layout."],[1798,"An individual serialized document exceeding 8 MiB, including its newline, is logged and skipped. Other documents and later chunks are still attempted, and the last error is returned. Successful chunks are not rolled back. The runtime logs/counts plugin callback errors and continues without replaying the failed batch. It auto-commits while polling. A failed callback can therefore coexist with stored documents, and a successful callback can include documents rejected by Quickwit. Runtime processed counts do not prove successful indexing. The sink provides no end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[1708,"An insert error containing only duplicate-key errors (`11000`) and no write-concern error is treated as success. This covers all unique indexes, not only `_id`, and does not compare payload contents. Reusing a message ID in one stream/topic/partition preserves the existing document. A conflict on another unique index can also discard a distinct message while runtime reports it processed. Account for this when choosing collection indexes."],[3486,"An uncertain committing read can require `abandon_operation` before seeking. Abandon does not undo a store. The source consumer example shows explicit recovery choices."],[46,"An uncertain result is not proof that the operation failed. Keep the same MCP process for retained-operation recovery. Abandoning a request does not make a fresh retry safe from duplicates."],[1901,"And finally, each sink should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID via expected `new()` method."],[2195,"And finally, each source should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID and optional state via expected `new()` method."],[1921,"And that's all, enjoy using the sink connector!"],[2218,"And that's all, enjoy using the source connector!"],[483,"Any other value is rejected. The option is a **placeholder today**: the value is validated, persisted, and echoed back, but it does not enable automatic payload compression or decompression. To compress today, do it client-side and tag messages via user headers - see the message headers examples in the Iggy repo."],[1419,"Apache Doris Sink"],[4354,"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 changes the wire protocol, data directory format and public SDK APIs. Upgrade the server and clients together."],[2598,"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."],[2624,"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."],[2681,"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."],[4420,"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."],[4407,"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding when the detected topology has one NUMA node. On a multi-node topology, container policy or host restrictions can still reject memory binding."],[2354,"Apache Iggy documentation: how Iggy organizes data into streams, topics, partitions and segments, and where to start."],[3,"Apache Iggy includes an MCP server, so LLM applications can work with a running Iggy server. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs."],[1020,"Apache Iggy replicates clusters with **Viewstamped Replication Revisited (VSR)**. VSR keeps an ordered state machine consistent across replicas and elects a new primary when the current primary fails."],[3354,"API notes"],[2539,"Append-only log"],[4010,"Apply, verify, and reproduce"],[1061,"Architecture"],[2464,"Architecture"],[3647,"Array-valued environment settings replace the corresponding TOML array. For an indexed array such as `cluster.nodes`, supply every entry and its required fields through environment variables. A single field override does not merge into the existing TOML roster."],[381,"As in `CreateUser`, the length field is conditional: with `has_permissions = 0` the payload ends at the flag."],[2636,"As long as the Iggy server is running, we're good to go. If you'd like to play with the configuration (e.g. change the addresses, ports, caching or so) you will find `config.toml` under `core/server` in the repository."],[922,"Ask any node for the cluster state with the CLI:"],[4339,"Ask the server which keys it accepts, with their types, defaults, and descriptions:"],[1906,"async-trait"],[2201,"async-trait"],[4328,"At creation, every interface takes the same keys:"],[881,"At most 16 selectors per node. Boot rejects duplicate `client_cidr` entries on one node (compared truncated, so `10.0.1.0/16` duplicates `10.0.0.0/16`) and any two nodes advertising one `host:port` to overlapping client sets."],[4227,"At startup, recovery validates batch checksums, partition identity, and the segment chain before serving data. With message durability `replicated`, it walks the log from byte zero because buffered writeback can preserve later pages before earlier ones. With `persisted`, completed durable segment flushes allow an index-anchored recovery path."],[2198,"At this point, we can expose the required `new()` method, which will be used by the runtime to create a new instance of the source connector. The `id` is assigned by the runtime, and represents the unique identifier of the source connector. The `state` is an optional connector state (e.g. persisted by the file or HTTP state backend), which will be provided by the runtime, given that the connector has persisted its own state before the runtime was restarted. The `ConnectorState::deserialize()` helper decodes the MessagePack bytes back into our `State` struct, falling back to a fresh state when nothing was persisted or decoding fails."],[318,"Authentication"],[760,"Authentication"],[1486,"Authentication"],[2838,"Authentication"],[3977,"Authentication"],[4123,"Authentication"],[2310,"Authentication and authorization"],[840,"Authentication and TLS"],[993,"Authentication is **off by default**, which leaves replica traffic unauthenticated. Enabling it is a **coordinated-restart change**: flip every node in one restart."],[4122,"Authentication, authorization, transport encryption and encryption at rest in the Iggy server."],[4126,"Authorization"],[3437,"Automatic commits"],[2406,"Available client SDK in multiple languages"],[1063,"Available Connectors"],[3878,"Available images"],[11,"Available tools"],[2921,"Available value factories: `FromString`, `FromBool`, `FromBytes`, `FromUInt8`, `FromInt32`, `FromInt64`, `FromInt128`, `FromUInt32`, `FromUInt64`, `FromUInt128`, `FromFloat`, `FromDouble`, `FromGuid`."],[2230,"avro_convert"],[2518,"Awaited writes apply committed operations before success is returned. A singleton with `durability=persisted` synchronizes local segment state before replying."],[3433,"Background producer configuration"],[3580,"Balanced consumer group (`bcg`): N consumers assigned to M consumer groups polling from K partitions:"],[3579,"Balanced producer (`bp`): N producers sending to M partitions in K streams with balanced partitioning:"],[3581,"Balanced producer and consumer group (`bpcg`), in parallel:"],[641,"Batch header (256 bytes)"],[1484,"Batch Modes"],[1555,"Batches and table commits"],[2073,"Batching"],[1397,"Batching & Transactions"],[1346,"Batching and failures"],[2481,"Before diving into the architecture details, here's an overview of a message's journey from client to disk:"],[3642,"Before the environment is read, the server loads a `.env` file from the working directory or its parents, or from the path named by `IGGY_ENV_PATH`. Existing environment values take precedence over the `.env` file."],[2208,"Before we make use of the `Source` trait, let's define the internal payload of the message that will be produced (e.g. as if it was pulled from some external database or so)."],[2589,"Before we start"],[2616,"Before we start"],[4411,"Before we start"],[4439,"Before we start"],[3891,"Below is an example `docker-compose.yml` which overrides the default configuration (see Configuration) with environment variables. If you prefer using the configuration file, you can mount it as a volume and provide the path to it with the `IGGY_CONFIG_PATH` environment variable."],[3558,"Benchmark kinds"],[3555,"Benchmarking"],[2909,"Besides `Global`, `Permissions.Streams` scopes permissions to specific streams (keyed by numeric stream id), and each `StreamPermissions` can in turn scope down to specific topics:"],[3454,"Besides `send()`, the producer also offers `send_one(message)`, `send_with_partitioning(messages, partitioning)` to override the partitioning per call, and `send_to(stream, topic, messages, partitioning)` to target a different stream and topic."],[2998,"Besides the collection overload, `SendAsync` also accepts a single item (`SendAsync(order, messageId: null, userHeaders: null)`) or a collection of `(data, messageId, userHeaders)` tuples when you need per-message ids or headers."],[3254,"Beyond streams, topics, and messages, the client exposes consumer groups, partitions, users, and personal access tokens through `client.consumerGroups()`, `client.partitions()`, `client.users()`, and `client.personalAccessTokens()`. The SDK's custom exception types inherit from `IggyException`. Failed futures can wrap them in `CompletionException` when joined."],[3320,"Beyond the basics"],[3399,"Beyond the basics"],[2800,"Beyond the generated `lib.rs.h` FFI surface, `foreign/cpp/include/iggy.hpp` ships typed C++ helpers: `CompressionAlgorithm`, `MaxTopicSize`, `Expiry`, `PollingStrategy`, `IdKind`, `TopicOption`, `SnapshotCompression`, and `SystemSnapshotType`, plus the `IggyException` error type. They construct and validate the kind strings and values the raw FFI calls expect."],[1198,"Beyond these sections, the runtime configuration also supports `[http]` for the HTTP API (see below), and `[telemetry]`, `[logging]`, and `[http.metrics]` covered on the **Observability page**."],[1341,"BigQuery"],[813,"Binary login registers a logical session and a binding secret. Additional connections and reconnects bind that session. The client can therefore keep connections to several partition primaries without creating an unrelated identity for every link."],[4232,"Binary polls reuse the stored batch layout. A reply may slice a larger stored batch and rewrite its header, and at-rest encryption requires decryption before the reply. HTTP additionally serializes the result as JSON."],[4078,"Binary protocol"],[625,"Binary Protocol"],[626,"Binary protocol 0.12.0: one wire format across TCP, QUIC and WebSocket, covering both the client and replica planes."],[408,"Bind a retained session"],[422,"BindSession, code 15, authenticates another connection for the same logical session without another Register:"],[3215,"Blocking vs. async"],[4195,"Boot-time segment recovery"],[858,"Boot-time validation enforces these constraints:"],[3929,"Both accept `replicated` or `persisted`. They default **independently**: setting `durability=persisted` leaves consumer offsets at `replicated` unless that option is also supplied. Neither inherits the other."],[2552,"Both approaches have advantages and disadvantages. The message broker is a more mature concept, but the message streaming platform is gaining more and more popularity, especially in the cloud-native world. And you can achieve much higher performance and throughput with the message streaming platform, since it acts as a simple database, being optimized for the append-only operations and can be queried in a very efficient way."],[3084,"Both binaries accept the same flags:"],[4325,"Both durability policies write data to disk and default independently to `replicated`. Neither inherits the other. Flush thresholds schedule ordinary segment writes. Required persistence, capacity pressure, or lifecycle operations can flush earlier. They do not weaken the `persisted` completion guarantee."],[3636,"Both listeners here bind a wildcard, which tells the server nothing about where clients reach it, so `[node]` has to name that address explicitly - without it this file is refused at boot."],[4238,"Both policies can be active at once. They only ever touch sealed segments. Deletion also stops at the minimum committed consumer or consumer-group offset, and pending persistence checkpoints can defer it. The active segment is **never deleted by retention**, even if its messages have expired."],[3536,"Both programs accept `--tcp-server-address`, `--tls`, `--tls-ca-file` and `--tls-domain`. The example README provides the development TLS commands."],[413,"Both request bodies begin with the `ClientVersionInfo` prefix, so the server can gate on the version before touching credentials:"],[4324,"Both retention policies can be active at once. The active segment is **never touched**, and its bytes are excluded from the size limit. A segment whose end offset exceeds the lowest stored consumer or consumer-group offset is retained. With no stored offsets, this barrier is absent. Deletion is done by the server's segment cleaner (`[data_maintenance.messages]`, enabled by default)."],[4203,"Bounded partition file work"],[4142,"Broker API commands require an authenticated session or bearer credential, except for ping and the login/refresh flows that establish or prove a credential. The Prometheus `/metrics` scrape requires a JWT or personal access token. HTTP CORS preflight responses and embedded `/ui` static assets are public. The UI's broker API calls still require authentication."],[783,"Broker commands require credentials, except for `ping`. For commands other than `login`, the CLI resolves credentials in this order, **first match wins**:"],[4234,"Buffers are allocated lazily. A pool miss can allocate outside the pool, so message processing is not allocation-free. Internal components can pass ownership of existing buffers without copying their contents."],[1869,"Buffers are cleared before upload. A failed upload loses those messages and stops processing the remainder of that poll. The sink tries to write an additional `.lost` object containing the offset range, message count and error, using the same retry policy. This marker contains no payload and may also fail. Graceful close attempts to flush remaining buffers, logs failures, and still returns success. Neither runtime counters nor shutdown success prove that every message was stored, and the sink provides no end-to-end delivery or deduplication guarantee."],[1348,"Build `iggy_connector_bigquery_sink` with the same release source as the runtime. Add this connector file to the local provider, then set your project, dataset and table:"],[2011,"Build `iggy_connector_http_source` from the same release source as the runtime. Create the destination stream and topic. Add this connector file through the local provider:"],[2076,"Build `iggy_connector_mqtt_source` from the same release source as the runtime. Create the destination stream and topic before starting the connector. Add this file to the local provider:"],[898,"Build `iggy-server` and the `iggy` CLI, run a single development node, then bring up a three-node cluster on one machine. See Configuration for the full `[cluster]` reference."],[3516,"Build a consumer"],[3515,"Build a producer"],[2686,"Build a producer and a consumer as two applications: Getting started."],[4410,"Build a small order-processing service in Python: key messages by customer, share a topic between two service instances with a consumer group, and see them take over from each other and resume."],[3351,"Build and install"],[890,"Build from source"],[889,"Build the binaries and run a three-node Apache Iggy cluster"],[3292,"Build the local Node.js SDK and run its TypeScript examples."],[1917,"Build the matching plugin, runtime and CLI from the repository root:"],[2215,"Build the matching plugin, runtime and CLI from the repository root:"],[3416,"Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. Start the server from the repository root in a separate terminal. The examples use `iggy`/`iggy`. For a new local instance with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides:"],[899,"Build the server and CLI from the repository root:"],[1106,"Build the server, CLI, runtime and quick-start plugins:"],[88,"Build with the `systemd` cargo feature to enable systemd readiness and watchdog notifications:"],[2787,"Building from source"],[3879,"Building from source"],[1072,"Building the connectors"],[2621,"Building the consumer"],[2620,"Building the producer"],[2432,"Built on **Viewstamped Replication (VSR)** consensus: runs as a single node by default, with multi-node clustering available via the `[cluster]` configuration"],[2429,"Built-in **benchmarking app** to test the performance, with published results on the benchmarks dashboard"],[2427,"Built-in **CLI** to manage the streaming server installable via `cargo install iggy-cli --version 0.15.0 --locked`"],[2428,"Built-in **Web UI** dashboard (Svelte) that can be embedded directly in the server binary or run as a standalone container"],[1472,"Bulk actions do not specify document IDs. Elasticsearch generates them, so replaying a message can create another document. The connector does not provide end-to-end exactly-once delivery."],[1490,"Bulk REST API Ingestion"],[590,"Business or transient rejection: one entry `{ index: 0, result: error_code }` and no payload."],[2137,"By default (no `payload_column`), each row is wrapped in a JSON envelope:"],[2320,"By default, Kafka topic `orders` maps to Iggy stream `kafka`, topic `orders`. `IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_STREAM` changes the default stream. `IGGY_KAFKA_TOPIC_MAP_PATH` selects a TOML mapping file:"],[1179,"By default, runtime will look for the configuration file, to decide which connectors to load and how to configure them."],[2413,"By offset (using the indexes)"],[2414,"By timestamp (using the time indexes)"],[4023,"Byte and ratio forms are alternatives. Writing one disables its corresponding other form. These settings do not replace Iggy's persistence barriers. See Linux writeback controls."],[3012,"C# SDK"],[3170,"C++ and PHP are included in the source archive. Swift uses the root Swift Package Manager manifest. See Upgrading before replacing an existing deployment."],[2785,"C++ SDK"],[398,"CancelDeferredRequest is code 108. It has an empty body and identifies the original request through the header request number on the same authenticated connection. The cancel command has no success reply. A committing deferred read still has one terminal reply that tells the caller whether the cancel won or an offset was stored."],[3049,"Cancellation and uncertain results"],[3068,"Cancellation removes a parked request when the server receives it. If a committing poll loses its reply, the client cannot assume that its offset stayed unchanged. SDK recovery tracks the uncertain operation and blocks conflicting work until the application resolves or abandons it. Abandoning permits later work but does not undo an offset that already committed."],[2503,"Capacity: an io_uring submission queue of 4096 entries (by default)"],[2935,"Capture a system snapshot as a compressed archive:"],[756,"Cargo"],[307,"Catalog"],[2148,"CDC always builds a JSON envelope with the operation and parsed columns. Do not combine it with polling payload extraction options. Deletes contain replica-identity columns. Updates may also supply `old_data` from an `old-key` tuple. Quoted `test_decoding` values, including JSONB and arrays, remain strings. Unchanged TOAST values are represented as null, which does not establish that the database value is null. Envelope timestamps are generated while polling, not PostgreSQL transaction commit times."],[2094,"CDC Mode"],[3883,"Chart components"],[895,"Check cluster metadata"],[3246,"Check Maven Central for the latest published version. Snapshot builds are available from the ASF snapshot repository."],[742,"Check that the server is up and measure the response time. `-c, --count <N>` sends N pings (default: 1)."],[4460,"Check that the token works:"],[4353,"Check the cutover"],[4463,"Check the message count in Prometheus:"],[2690,"Choose a format for your message payloads, and send more than one kind of message to the same topic."],[3046,"Choose read limits"],[3048,"Choose when to commit"],[933,"Choosing the policies"],[23,"Claude Desktop integration"],[2384,"CLI"],[1389,"ClickHouse deduplication depends on the table engine, query settings, identical retry data, and the retained deduplication window. `ReplicatedMergeTree` enables a deduplication log by default. Non-replicated `MergeTree` can also deduplicate when `non_replicated_deduplication_window` is positive. Its default is zero. See ClickHouse insert deduplication for the settings and limits."],[1358,"ClickHouse Sink"],[681,"client"],[4381,"Client cannot complete login"],[2829,"Client configuration"],[2828,"Client configuration and the full low-level IIggyClient API surface for the C# SDK."],[802,"Client Failover"],[1058,"Client failover: reads, writes, and leader redirection from the client side"],[4375,"Client hangs with no output"],[1030,"client sessions, request fencing, and duplicate detection"],[505,"Client to server. 256 bytes."],[3239,"Client types and configuration"],[879,"Clients whose transport-level peer IP falls inside `client_cidr` are handed `address` instead of the catch-all. The **longest matching prefix** wins. Clients matching no selector fall back to `advertised_address`, then `ip`. `address` takes the same forms as `advertised_address` (literal IP or hostname, never a port)."],[3252,"Close clients when finished: `client.close()`, `asyncClient.close().join()`, and `httpClient.close()`. The HTTP close method declares `IOException`."],[682,"cluster"],[825,"Cluster Configuration"],[927,"Cluster Durability"],[3853,"Cluster mode is configured here but documented in Clustering. The section contains:"],[4086,"Cluster networking"],[737,"Cluster operations. The only subcommand is `metadata` (alias `m`)."],[4133,"Cluster security"],[983,"Cluster Security"],[2476,"Clustering"],[1021,"Clustering is built into the standard `iggy-server` binary. There's **no separate build, binary, or Cargo feature**: the same server runs single-node or clustered, and `cluster.enabled` in the configuration decides which one you get. Clustering is **disabled by default**."],[387,"Codes **38** and **44** are the pre-register login shapes. The server rejects both with a `MalformedLogin` eviction."],[326,"Codes 14, 35, 102, 103, 104, 107, 123, 124, 204, 205, 304 and 305 are retired. They are not alternate names for current commands. Use UpdateResource for resource patches and topic durability for persistence requirements."],[1415,"Coercions cover nested structs, arrays of strings or timestamps, and arrays of structs. Nested arrays, maps, and variant columns pass through without these coercions."],[4047,"Collect `vmstat 1`, `iostat -xz 1`, `mpstat -P ALL 1`, pressure counters, and network drop/retransmission counters alongside benchmark results. The iostat and mpstat tools are provided by the sysstat package. Run long enough to include segment flushes, WAL checkpoints, and sustained device limits, rather than measuring only buffered admission."],[745,"Collect server troubleshooting data into an archive. This requires `manage_servers` because the archive can include logs and host diagnostics."],[2522,"Coming from Apache Kafka"],[94,"Command discriminants"],[669,"Command Reference"],[3602,"Command-line flags"],[305,"Commands"],[696,"Commands that address an existing stream, topic, user, or consumer group accept its numeric ID or name (for example, `iggy topic get dev events`). Numeric IDs identify the same resources only when those are the IDs assigned by the server. The numeric stream/topic IDs inside permission specifications are an exception: names are not accepted there."],[634,"Commands: the command codes and per-command payload layouts."],[1557,"Commit rate and maintenance"],[100,"CommitHeader"],[101,"CommitReplyHeader"],[2297,"Committing an offset before application processing, including poll-time auto-commit, can provide **at-most-once** processing: a crash can lose unprocessed messages. Committing only after successful processing supports **at-least-once** processing, with duplicates possible after a crash. Recovery also depends on retention and the configured message and consumer-offset durability. Iggy does not provide an atomic transaction between a consumer offset and an external side effect. **Exactly-once** application effects require an idempotent operation or an atomic deduplication-and-effect transaction. Unique message IDs alone do not provide that guarantee."],[2782,"Committing fetches use a separate pool with the same configured limits. That pool stores target metadata, not reply bodies, and retains unknown progress across sessions. Exhausting a limit rejects or delays further work according to the operation path. See Client failover."],[3143,"Common operations"],[1422,"Common Options"],[1483,"Common Options"],[1605,"Common Options"],[1648,"Common Options"],[1831,"Common Options"],[1935,"Common Options"],[2030,"Common Options"],[2279,"Common questions about Apache Iggy, including how it compares to Kafka and other message brokers."],[4036,"Compare the existing policy with `madvise` if allocator page promotion or compaction is relevant. Direct reclaim and compaction can stall allocations. On recent kernels, per-size policies and explicit `MADV_COLLAPSE` requests also matter, so the top-level switch alone does not describe all behavior. See transparent huge-page controls."],[4360,"Compare your configuration with the 0.10 reference. Do not copy obsolete keys into the new deployment."],[3128,"Compatibility and recovery"],[3946,"Completed buffered file writes normally remain in Linux's page cache after the server process dies. Messages still in the process's journal do not. Page cache alone does not protect against machine or power failure."],[436,"Compression"],[4201,"Compression"],[331,"Compression codes: 1 = Stored, 2 = Deflated, 3 = Bzip2, 4 = Zstd, 5 = Lzma, 6 = Xz. Snapshot type codes: 1 = FilesystemOverview, 2 = ProcessList, 3 = ResourceUsage, 4 = Test, 5 = ServerLogs, 6 = ServerConfig, 100 = All."],[702,"Compression is a **required positional**: `none` or `gzip`. It is stored as topic metadata, but compression is not applied. Expiry is a human-readable duration (`7d`, `1day 12h`, `unlimited`). Omitting expiry at creation selects no expiration."],[2936,"Compression methods: `Stored`, `Deflated`, `Bzip2`, `Zstd`, `Lzma`, `Xz`. Snapshot types: `FilesystemOverview`, `ProcessList`, `ResourceUsage`, `Test`, `ServerLogs`, `ServerConfig`, `All`."],[2536,"Concepts"],[2533,"Concepts explains each of these in more detail."],[2410,"Configurable server features (e.g. caching and transport protocols), plus per-topic segment size, durability and flush thresholds"],[10,"Configuration"],[1068,"Configuration"],[1343,"Configuration"],[1360,"Configuration"],[1393,"Configuration"],[1421,"Configuration"],[1456,"Configuration"],[1482,"Configuration"],[1604,"Configuration"],[1647,"Configuration"],[1678,"Configuration"],[1714,"Configuration"],[1765,"Configuration"],[1801,"Configuration"],[1811,"Configuration"],[1830,"Configuration"],[1875,"Configuration"],[1925,"Configuration"],[1934,"Configuration"],[1975,"Configuration"],[2006,"Configuration"],[2029,"Configuration"],[2071,"Configuration"],[2089,"Configuration"],[2152,"Configuration"],[2167,"Configuration"],[2222,"Configuration"],[3319,"Configuration"],[3596,"Configuration"],[3992,"Configuration comes from the TOML file named by `IGGY_CONFIG_PATH`, or `core/server/config.toml` relative to the working directory. If that file is missing, the server uses the defaults embedded in the binary. Any key can be overridden with an `IGGY_`-prefixed environment variable, and a `.env` file in the working directory or its parents (or the file named by `IGGY_ENV_PATH`) is loaded at startup. See Configuration for the full reference."],[1550,"Configuration example"],[1281,"Configuration Example"],[3638,"Configuration is resolved in three layers. Later layers win:"],[1170,"Configuration Options"],[1551,"Configuration Options"],[1895,"Configuration properties can be overridden using environment variables. Supported scalar fields and indexed stream entries use the pattern: `IGGY_CONNECTORS_SINK_[KEY]_[PROPERTY]`"],[2188,"Configuration properties can be overridden using environment variables. Supported scalar fields and indexed stream entries use the pattern: `IGGY_CONNECTORS_SOURCE_[KEY]_[PROPERTY]`"],[1167,"Configuration Providers"],[1056,"Configuration: every `[cluster]` setting and its validation rules"],[2331,"Configure `metadata.partitions_max` on Iggy to bound topic creation. Its default is zero, which means no cap. DescribeAcls presents Iggy permissions, but CreateAcls and DeleteAcls are not supported. The authentication reference describes the exact limits."],[2959,"Configure a publisher, initialize it, then send. `InitAsync` validates (and optionally creates) the stream and topic:"],[3514,"Configure a Rust producer and consumer by stream and topic names."],[2187,"Configure one `[[streams]]` destination per source instance. The runtime currently retains only the last producer when multiple entries are supplied."],[3959,"Configure the service"],[3540,"Connect and create resources"],[758,"Connecting to a server"],[763,"Connection contexts"],[2836,"Connection events"],[4079,"Connection handling across shards"],[404,"Connection lifecycle"],[633,"Connection lifecycle: protocol versioning and the login-register handshake."],[3016,"Connection semantics"],[3142,"Connection string"],[3431,"Connection string"],[3495,"Connection string"],[2789,"Connection strings"],[2705,"Connection Strings"],[3549,"Connection strings accept `iggy://` and `iggy+tcp://`. TLS uses system roots or the configured CA file. `tls_domain` overrides the name checked in the certificate. The parser accepts pending-operation limits, but does not implement retained mutation limits."],[3102,"Connection strings and metadata"],[1238,"Connector runtime has an HTTP API which is **enabled by default** at `127.0.0.1:8081`. It can be disabled by setting the `enabled` flag to `false` in the `[http]` section."],[4370,"Connector sinks use deferred polling. Replace `poll_interval` with `max_wait` and `min_count`. The runtime rejects a sink configuration that still sets `poll_interval`."],[2386,"Connectors"],[1076,"Connectors are dynamically loaded shared libraries (`.so` on Linux, `.dylib` on macOS, `.dll` on Windows) loaded at runtime via `dlopen2`. Message batches and metadata crossing the FFI boundary between the runtime and plugins are serialized using **postcard**, a compact binary format. Plugin configuration is passed as JSON. The SDK initializes one Tokio runtime per loaded plugin library, shared by its connector instances. All plugins run in the same process."],[95,"Consensus and history"],[846,"Consensus runs on a **fixed 10ms tick**. Every duration below is converted to whole ticks: values are **rounded down** to a multiple of 10ms, and anything under 10ms is raised to a single tick rather than firing sooner. `repair_gap_debounce_interval` has an additional 500ms floor."],[828,"Consensus timing"],[1548,"Consume messages from Iggy topics and store them in Iceberg tables, with REST catalogs and S3-compatible storage."],[429,"Consumer"],[2592,"Consumer"],[3020,"Consumer"],[3098,"Consumer"],[3238,"Consumer"],[3316,"Consumer"],[3398,"Consumer"],[3435,"Consumer"],[4111,"Consumer avg latency: TCP 0.70ms vs WebSocket 1.44ms (+106%)"],[2948,"Consumer builder options"],[3436,"Consumer configuration"],[2593,"Consumer group"],[2809,"Consumer group"],[3317,"Consumer group"],[316,"Consumer groups"],[2545,"Consumer groups"],[2857,"Consumer groups"],[2947,"Consumer groups"],[3101,"Consumer groups"],[3353,"Consumer groups"],[18,"Consumer Groups"],[2929,"Consumer groups coordinate message consumption across multiple consumers, load-balancing partitions between members."],[4419,"Consumer groups need a binary transport, so this tutorial uses TCP. HTTP cannot join a group (see Consumer groups and the FAQ)."],[2573,"Consumer groups provide horizontal scaling for message consumption. When multiple consumers join the same consumer group, the server automatically distributes partitions among group members so that each partition has **at most one member permitted to poll it** within that group. When members join or leave, the server triggers a **cooperative partition rebalancing** with a pending revocation phase (configurable timeout, default 30s) to let the previous owner finish processing and commit before handoff. Application processing and offset-commit ordering still determine whether failures cause skips or duplicates."],[315,"Consumer offsets"],[19,"Consumer Offsets"],[2563,"Consumer offsets and consumer group offsets"],[2928,"Consumer offsets are keyed by consumer + stream + topic + partition. Note the argument order - the `Consumer` comes first:"],[807,"Consumer progress"],[683,"consumer-group"],[684,"consumer-offset"],[2568,"Consumers can poll the messages in multiple ways:"],[2851,"Consuming messages"],[2949,"Consuming with rented buffers"],[894,"Containers"],[3987,"Containers use the host's kernel, so it is the host that has to meet this requirement. See Docker & Helm."],[685,"context"],[796,"Contexts are named connection profiles (transport, addresses, TLS settings, credentials) for working with multiple environments. Subcommands: `list`, `use`, `create`, `delete`, `show`."],[799,"Contexts are stored in `contexts.toml`, and the active context name in `.active_context`, both under the Iggy home directory: `~/.iggy` by default, overridable with the `IGGY_HOME` environment variable. The `default` context always exists and **cannot be deleted or recreated through context commands**. Deleting the currently active context switches back to `default`. Context names may contain letters, digits, hyphens and underscores. Passwords and raw tokens supplied to `context create` are stored as plaintext in `contexts.toml` (owner-only permissions on Unix). Use a stored token name when the credential should stay in the keyring."],[2927,"Control where consumption starts:"],[2918,"Control which partition receives each message:"],[695,"Conventions used throughout:"],[2268,"Converts JSON to a raw Avro datum and Avro data to JSON. The schema can be given inline (`schema_json`) or as a file (`schema_path`). The inline schema takes precedence. This example can be added to the Random source with `streams.schema = \"avro\"`:"],[2264,"Converts JSON to the SDK's generic FlatBuffer representation, or exposes FlatBuffer bytes as metadata, base64 text, or raw bytes. It does not interpret an external `.fbs` schema. This example can be added to a JSON-producing source with `streams.schema = \"flat_buffer\"`:"],[2260,"Converts messages between Protocol Buffers and other formats. This example exposes the `type_url` and base64 `value` of an Any envelope received as raw bytes. See the **SDK documentation** for a complete runtime configuration and the schema loading details:"],[2505,"Cooperative task running enabled"],[832,"Coordinator placement"],[2472,"CPU allocation modes"],[4008,"CPU placement and NUMA"],[3118,"Crash durability follows the topic's `durability` policy: `replicated` confirms replication, while `persisted` also waits for the required replicas to persist the message data. See Durability. An empty confirmation list is a valid success but does not by itself prove that new messages were appended."],[2163,"Create `my-stream` and `my-topic` before starting the runtime, or change the sample to use an existing destination. Configure one destination per source instance."],[1931,"Create `my-stream` and `my-topic` before starting the runtime, or change the sample to use existing resources. The Random source configuration produces JSON to the same destination."],[4449,"Create a directory with these files."],[4459,"Create a