erred consumer workloads"],[3044,"Deferred Polling"],[3052,"Deferred polling lets the server wait for messages before answering a read. The read returns when enough messages are available or its wait ends. A quiet partition does not need a repeated request loop."],[322,"Deferred polls"],[3240,"Deferred reads and recovery"],[1173,"Deferred sink reads"],[3958,"Delegate unknown-user logins to an HTTP service that maps callers to existing Iggy accounts."],[1235,"Delete sink config: `DELETE {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs`"],[1236,"Delete source config: `DELETE {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs`"],[2938,"Delete up to N of the oldest sealed segments from a partition. The active segment is retained:"],[710,"Delete up to the requested number of oldest sealed segments of a partition. The active segment is retained."],[2224,"delete_fields"],[351,"Deletes up to `segments_count` oldest sealed segments of the partition. The active segment is retained."],[1481,"Deliver messages from Iggy streams to any HTTP endpoint, from webhooks and REST APIs to serverless functions."],[2309,"Delivery and durability"],[2009,"Delivery and retries"],[2074,"Delivery behavior"],[3100,"Delivery semantics"],[1424,"Delivery Semantics"],[1650,"Delivery Semantics"],[1681,"Delivery Semantics"],[1719,"Delivery Semantics"],[1768,"Delivery Semantics"],[1835,"Delivery Semantics"],[1937,"Delivery Semantics"],[1391,"Delta Lake Sink"],[888,"Deploy a Cluster"],[1055,"Deploy a cluster: build the binaries and run a three-node cluster"],[1492,"Deployment & Performance"],[486,"DescribeOptions entries report mutability and visibility. Bit 0 means updatable, bit 1 resettable, bit 2 secret, bit 3 read-only and bit 4 critical. A critical key must be understood before a node serves the resource. Unknown client option keys are rejected."],[1033,"deterministic simulation and cross-SDK BDD coverage"],[4190,"Directory layout"],[1192,"Directory of the runtime binary (filename only)"],[4022,"Dirty-page limits govern buffered writeback. Large percentage limits, such as `dirty_background_ratio=10` and `dirty_ratio=30`, can permit large bursts on high-memory VMs. If writeback coincides with latency spikes, compare explicit byte limits sized from disk throughput and the acceptable backlog."],[1752,"Disabling `include_metadata` omits the five offset/timestamp/stream/topic/partition columns. Checksum and origin timestamp flags work independently. `id`, `payload` and `created_at` are always created. With `auto_create_table = false`, provision a compatible table yourself. Startup does not check its existence or schema."],[4309,"Discovering the catalog"],[4342,"Discovery matters most on the binary transports: TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket carry **only an error code** for a rejected key, not its name, so the catalog is how a client finds out what this server supports. Over HTTP the error message names the offending key directly."],[4230,"Disk reads are verified before they reach a consumer:"],[2996,"Dispose the builder only when abandoning it before `Build()`. A `using` on the builder is safe either way (it becomes a no-op after `Build()`)."],[2347,"Do not mix `LISTENERS` with the single-listener advertised host/port or SASL toggle. Use matching `ADVERTISED_LISTENERS`. TLS needs `IGGY_KAFKA_TLS_CERT_FILE` and `IGGY_KAFKA_TLS_KEY_FILE`. The bridge hop uses `IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_TLS_ENABLED`, `IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_TLS_DOMAIN` and `IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_TLS_CA_FILE`."],[3132,"Do not treat a message ID as a deduplication key. Recreating a producer or submitting the same business event as a new batch is not the same as retrying the original producer batch. Consumers still need application-level handling for repeated work."],[4356,"Do not use a rolling upgrade between server versions. Every cluster node must run the same server version. Keep the old deployment and its data available until you finish the cutover checks."],[24,"Docker"],[757,"Docker"],[2388,"Docker"],[3876,"Docker"],[3874,"Docker & Helm"],[4477,"Docker Compose example"],[4376,"Docker container can't be reached"],[2295,"Docker must allow the required `io_uring` calls and provide enough locked-memory allowance. The supplied Compose configuration uses `SYS_NICE`, `seccomp:unconfined` and unlimited memlock. Those settings are not universal requirements for every host. See Docker and configuration."],[1649,"Document Mapping"],[104,"DoViewChangeHeader"],[3917,"Durability"],[4310,"Durability"],[2710,"Duration values"],[3868,"Durations are **rounded down** to the consensus 10 ms tick. Values under 10 ms become one tick."],[1785,"Durations require units and must be positive. Invalid or zero durations prevent startup. Unknown plugin keys are rejected. There is no plugin `batch_size` option: `batch_length` limits the polled batch, and the sink splits it by serialized byte size without accumulating across polls."],[1553,"Dynamic Routing"],[1554,"Dynamic routing configuration example"],[1287,"Dynamic Schema Loading"],[3488,"Each chunk carries a producer ID, epoch and per-partition sequence. This is separate from message IDs and session retry protection. Upgrade all topic readers to compatible 0.10 clients before writing these batches. See Idempotent producers for limits."],[2483,"Each configured **shard** (an instance of `IggyShard`) has its own single-threaded `compio` async runtime. With `pin_cores = true`, Linux shard threads are pinned to their selected CPUs via `sched_setaffinity`. NUMA allocation modes also bind memory via `hwlocality`. Setting `pin_cores = false` disables both bindings. Shared metadata and inter-shard communication still require synchronization."],[359,"Each confirmation is 20 bytes. The server reports one partition per append. A zero count or an empty successful body supplies no offset information. A retry can return the retained result rather than append again. Retry coverage is bounded, and crash durability depends on the topic's durability policy."],[121,"Each connection starts with an eight-byte placement preamble before TLS and the replica handshake:"],[2147,"Each connector needs its own slot. Reusing one slot across connectors divides its changes between them. `pg_logical_slot_get_changes` consumes slot progress before delivery, including changes excluded by the filters. A later failure cannot replay those changes from the slot, and the plugin checkpoint does not contain a recovery LSN."],[3083,"Each consumer reads partition `0` independently, starting from offset `0` or the earliest retained message. Running several consumers reads the same messages. These examples do not join a consumer group."],[1995,"Each cycle the connector waits `polling_interval`, then runs a search against `index` with the configured `query`, `size = batch_size`, sorted ascending by `timestamp_field` (`@timestamp` when unset). Every hit with `_source` becomes one JSON message. Elasticsearch `_id` is not copied into the payload or used as the Iggy message ID. Headers and timestamps are also unset by this plugin."],[4179,"Each encrypted stream has its own AES-256-GCM-SIV data key. The server encrypts message payloads and user headers before storage and decrypts them for polling. Metadata journals, metadata snapshots and structural headers are not encrypted as a whole."],[4262,"Each encrypted stream uses a separate AES-256-GCM-SIV data key. The server encrypts message payloads and user headers before storage and decrypts them for reads. Metadata journals, snapshots and structural headers are not encrypted as a whole."],[3306,"Each example has a script in `examples/node/package.json`. Set `DEBUG=iggy:examples*` to see its progress."],[1205,"Each logical save has an `Idempotency-Key`, reused across retries and resolution of an uncertain write. The server must replay the original outcome for that key without applying the write again."],[1417,"Each nonempty batch passed to the plugin is written to Parquet buffers and appended in a single Delta log transaction. Empty batches create no transaction in `consume()`. The stream's `batch_length` is a polling limit, so it influences Delta commit granularity: small batches produce many small files and table versions, so prefer larger batches for production tables (and compact periodically). The plugin has no retry loop for failed batches, though the Delta library retries eligible commit conflicts and storage requests. A write or commit failure clears the writer buffers and returns a connector error. The runtime uses consumer auto-commit and does not replay that failed batch, so the Delta transaction does not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[2561,"Each partition in the thread-per-core architecture is owned by **exactly one shard per replica** and includes:"],[4207,"Each partition is a segmented log: an ordered list of **sealed** (read-only) segments plus one **active** (writable) segment. When the active segment reaches the topic's `segment_size` (default 1 GiB), it is sealed and a new active segment starts at the next offset."],[3135,"Each partition retains the last five batches of each producer and at most 4096 producers. A producer idle for 24 hours can expire. A retry outside this retained history can append a duplicate. These limits also apply to the gateway and source connector paths."],[1445,"Each poll is split into chunks of at most `batch_size` messages. Each chunk gets a deterministic label based on `label_prefix`, the target table, stream, topic, partition, and first/last offsets. Transient failures (HTTP 5xx/408/429, transport errors, empty or unreadable successful responses) are retried under the same label. Backoff starts at `retry_delay`, doubles for each retry, applies ±20% jitter, and never exceeds `max_retry_delay`. Other HTTP 4xx responses, `Fail` status, and nonempty malformed responses are permanent errors and are not retried."],[1756,"Each poll is split into chunks of at most `batch_size` messages. One multi-row `INSERT` writes each chunk. A duplicate primary key, incompatible table, invalid payload or other terminal error rejects that chunk. Later chunks are still attempted. The plugin logs failures, increments its private insertion-error count by the failed chunk size, and returns success after attempting all chunks. Both its processed count and runtime's processed count can therefore include messages that were never stored. Runtime error counts do not expose these insert failures."],[1706,"Each polled batch is split into chunks of at most `batch_size` messages. Chunks are inserted immediately with unordered `insert_many`. Other documents can succeed when one is rejected. Later chunks are still attempted after a failure, and the last chunk error is returned. There is no accumulation across polls and no transaction covering the batch."],[1672,"Each polled batch is split into requests of at most `batch_size` documents, with no accumulation across polls. The first failed chunk stops the loop. Later chunks are not attempted. Earlier chunks may already be indexed."],[1600,"Each runtime batch creates one append commit per destination table. Larger batches reduce snapshot and manifest growth. Use `batch_length=1000`, `min_count=1000` and `max_wait=\"5s\"` in the stream entry when this latency fits the workload. The reply byte budget can finish a read earlier."],[1330,"Each Rust example below is a complete `connector-sdk-example/src/main.rs`. Run it from the repository root so the relative schema path resolves:"],[2565,"Each segment consists of:"],[4212,"Each segment has an accompanying `.index` file holding a **sparse index**: one 24-byte entry per flushed write, not per message."],[33,"Each setting can also be overridden using `IGGY_MCP_<SECTION>_<KEY>`, for example `IGGY_MCP_IGGY_USERNAME` or `IGGY_MCP_HTTP_ADDRESS`. Nested settings use the same underscore convention, such as `IGGY_MCP_IGGY_TLS_ENABLED`. Set `IGGY_MCP_ENV_PATH` to load a particular dotenv file. Otherwise `.env` is searched for in the current directory and its parents. Existing environment variables take precedence over dotenv values."],[4068,"Each shard also exports its own lower-level metrics, labelled `shard=\"<id>\"`. These cover the write-ahead log (the `partition_wal_*` series), partition housekeeping (`partitions_*`), and requests or frames the server refused or dropped. To see the full list with descriptions, scrape a running server:"],[1853,"Each stream/topic/partition has its own buffer, retained across polls. Rotation is checked after appending a complete formatted record. Size mode counts record bytes but excludes JSON newlines, commas and array brackets, so an object can exceed `max_file_size`. Message-count mode does not also enforce the size threshold. Uploads use one `PutObject`, with no multipart upload. Choose thresholds and message sizes that fit the store's single-upload limit. There is no timer flush: a partial buffer waits until its threshold is reached or the connector closes."],[782,"Each transport also exposes reconnection and tuning flags (retry counts, intervals, QUIC window sizes and MTU). Run `iggy --help` for the complete list with defaults."],[4112,"Each transport configures TLS in its own section, and the `self_signed` semantics differ per transport:"],[4173,"Each transport configures TLS in its own section, and the `self_signed` semantics differ per transport:"],[3586,"Each transport subcommand accepts a trailing `output` subcommand that persists the run (raw JSON data and charts):"],[4246,"Earlier releases documented several storage features and `[system.*]` keys that no longer exist:"],[2359,"Ecosystem"],[3574,"Eight kinds are available, each with a short alias:"],[1009,"Either way, matching JWT key material lets a bearer token minted on one node verify on another. If JWT keys are derived from the PSK, rotating the PSK also changes the JWT key: `previous_shared_secret` applies only to replica authentication. Use independently configured, shared JWT keys when HTTP tokens must remain valid across PSK rotation. Forwarded requests dial the primary at its roster `ip` and `ports.http`. The follower verifies the caller's bearer locally before forwarding, and the primary re-authenticates the request through its normal stack."],[1454,"Elasticsearch Sink"],[1973,"Elasticsearch Source"],[4473,"Embedded mode"],[4280,"Empty `previous_keys` and add the old fingerprint to `retired_kek_fingerprints` on every node. The primary removes those wraps."],[2306,"Enable the bridge"],[3706,"Enabling an issuer opens an outbound JWKS fetch that is reachable before a token's signature is verified: a token naming the issuer with an unknown key id can trigger a fetch to `jwks_url`. The target is fixed (not attacker-chosen), concurrent misses coalesce onto one fetch, and repeats are rate-limited to one outbound request per issuer per short window. The same window bounds how quickly a freshly rotated signing key is picked up."],[4268,"Enabling or disabling server encryption affects creation defaults. It does not change the encryption of existing streams. Renaming a stream preserves its key and stored messages."],[1002,"Enabling TLS is also a coordinated-restart change: a TLS dialer can't talk to a plaintext acceptor or vice versa."],[4200,"Encryption"],[4371,"Encryption is selected when a stream is created. Each encrypted stream has its own data key. Enabling server encryption does not encrypt an existing unencrypted stream. Keep all keys required to read retained streams and backups. See Stream options for key creation and rotation."],[3582,"End-to-end producing consumer (`e2e`): each task alternates producing and consuming, measuring latency from the messages' producer timestamps:"],[3583,"End-to-end producing consumer group (`e2ecg`): N producing consumers assigned to M consumer groups:"],[2319,"Enter a message in the producer, then read it from another terminal:"],[3417,"Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. `--fresh` deletes this replica's local data. Use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password. A new cluster requires explicit credentials."],[1857,"Environment credentials use `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` / `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, with optional `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN` / `AWS_SECURITY_TOKEN`. The file path is `AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE` or `~/.aws/credentials`. This plugin reads its `[default]` section and does not select `AWS_PROFILE`. Web identity uses `AWS_ROLE_ARN` and `AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE`. The container path uses `AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_RELATIVE_URI`. Explicit config credentials have no session-token option. Use the environment or credentials file for a temporary key pair and token."],[1876,"Environment Variable Overrides"],[2168,"Environment Variable Overrides"],[3599,"Environment variables"],[3752,"Ephemeral certificates **change on every start** and are meant for loopback development, not production."],[1363,"Error Handling & Delivery Semantics"],[3375,"Errors are thrown as exceptions under `Iggy\\Exception`: `AuthenticationException`, `ConnectionException`, `NotFoundException`, `TransientException`, and the `IggyException` base."],[2504,"Event interval: poll the I/O driver after 128 scheduler ticks (roughly task polls) by default"],[884,"Every `address` must be routable from inside its own `client_cidr`: leader-aware clients redial whatever address metadata advertises, and a selector pointing at an unreachable host strands them mid-redirect."],[698,"Every `list` command accepts `-l, --list-mode <table|list>` (default: `table`)."],[3644,"Every configuration key can be overridden with an `IGGY_` variable. The name is the TOML path, uppercased, with dots turned into underscores:"],[1700,"Every document has a generated `_id` of `stream:topic:partition:message_id` and a `payload` field. The offset is not part of the ID. Payload fields remain nested under `payload`, so a payload's own `_id` does not replace the connector's ID. Message headers are not stored."],[859,"Every duration must be **nonzero**. The `0`, `disabled`, and `unlimited` sentinels all parse to a zero duration and are rejected. There's no way to switch the liveness window off."],[168,"Every header begins with the common 128-byte frame prefix shown in Framing. The tables below list the remaining fields in wire order. Multi-byte integers are little-endian. Reserved fields are not extension space for clients. RepairPrepare uses the PrepareHeader layout with its repair command value."],[670,"Every iggy CLI command with its arguments, flags, and a runnable example"],[495,"Every message on a binary transport is a fixed **256-byte header** followed by an optional body. The header is a `#[repr(C)]` struct decoded by pointer cast (zero-copy), so field offsets are fixed and enforced at compile time in the server. Three header shapes cross the client boundary:"],[905,"Every node loads the same configuration. Nodes are told apart *only* by `--replica-id`, which selects that node's own entry in the `cluster.nodes` roster."],[981,"Every node must run the same server version and the same `cluster.coordinator.replica_connections_per_peer`. There is no rolling upgrade between 0.9 and 0.10. The roster supports at most 32 nodes."],[886,"Every node must run the same server version. Do not mix 0.9 and 0.10 nodes during an upgrade."],[2364,"Every partition is an append-only log. Messages are **immutable** once written, consumers can start from a chosen offset, and retained messages can be replayed. Retention removes old data, and offsets can contain gaps."],[4093,"Every request and reply on the stateful transports starts with a fixed 256-byte header (the same Viewstamped Replication header layout the cluster uses internally), followed by the command payload. The `iggy_binary_protocol` crate provides zero-copy serialization for these headers, and foreign SDKs implement the same contract natively. See the Binary Protocol section for the full specification."],[920,"Every roster field can be provided through typed environment variables instead of TOML, which suits container deployments:"],[3173,"Every SDK has connection-string support. Accepted schemes, credentials and options differ by language. Java currently accepts username/password TCP strings with `tls` and `tls_ca_file` only. They provide a compact way to configure the client, including the transport protocol, credentials, server address, and options."],[3467,"Every send method returns a `SendMessagesResponse` carrying the commit confirmations of the chunks the send was split into. The list is **empty** whenever the server sends no confirmation payload, and always for a `background` producer, which hands the messages to a dispatcher and returns before the send happens. Branch on `confirmations.is_empty()` instead of indexing. A retried chunk may already have been committed by an earlier attempt, so a confirmation offset does not imply uniqueness. Direct-send completion follows the topic's durability policy."],[1961,"Every submitted record contains `id`, `iggy_message_id`, `payload` and `payload_encoding`. `include_metadata` adds `iggy_stream`, `iggy_topic`, `iggy_partition_id`, `iggy_offset`, `iggy_timestamp` and `iggy_schema`. The checksum and origin timestamp have separate flags. `iggy_headers` appears only when enabled and nonempty."],[608,"Eviction reasons:"],[494,"EvictionHeader"],[1174,"Exactly-once sources"],[1488,"Example Configurations"],[2806,"Examples"],[3073,"Examples"],[3103,"Examples"],[3211,"Examples"],[3241,"Examples"],[3291,"Examples"],[3321,"Examples"],[3356,"Examples"],[3382,"Examples"],[3400,"Examples"],[3413,"Examples"],[3531,"Examples"],[3043,"Examples - producer, consumer-group, and typed-message samples, plus links to runnable projects"],[3347,"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. See Examples for the list and how to run them."],[3294,"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. They use the SDK from the same checkout:"],[2303,"Examples are available in the repository for multiple languages: Rust (basic, getting-started, message-envelope, message-headers, multi-tenant, new-sdk, sink-data-producer, stream-builder, tcp-tls), C#, Go, Java, Node.js, PHP, Python and Swift. Docker Compose files and Helm charts are also provided."],[947,"Except for two replicas, the replication quorum is `min(ceil(n / 2), 3)` and the view-change quorum is `n - replication_quorum + 1`. Two replicas require both for either quorum. The quorums therefore intersect. These are the implemented quorum rules, not a configurable acknowledgement count."],[1112,"Execute `cargo run --example sink-data-producer --release`, which sends 100 batches of messages to previously created `qw` stream and `records` topic (this will be used by the Quickwit sink connector)."],[4006,"Explicit large pages"],[1007,"explicitly configured `http.jwt` signing secrets, identical on every node, or"],[908,"Export the settings shared by all three processes:"],[1293,"Expose the implementation with `sink_connector!(MySink)` or `source_connector!(MySource)`. The sink constructor has the signature `new(id: u32, config: MySinkConfig) -> Self`. The source constructor also takes `state: Option<ConnectorState>`. The **sink guide** and **source guide** provide complete plugin implementations and build setup."],[3957,"External Authentication"],[3961,"Failure and replay"],[1016,"Failure handling"],[950,"Failure independence matters. A shared power loss, an OOM cascade, or a crash triggered on every replica can destroy multiple volatile copies at once. A process-only failure can lose messages still in the processes' journals, even while the kernels and their page caches remain alive. Replication count alone does not establish a bound on that loss."],[1595,"Fan-out is not atomic across tables. A write or commit error stops work on the remaining tables in that batch. An earlier table may already be committed. The Iceberg library refreshes metadata before committing and retries eligible commit errors according to the table's retry properties. The plugin does not replay a failed batch. The runtime records a plugin error and continues polling with consumer auto-commit, so messages can be lost after a failed or skipped write. Replaying an already committed batch can create duplicates."],[2278,"FAQ"],[1066,"Features"],[1549,"Features"],[1713,"Features"],[2088,"Features"],[2382,"Features"],[4476,"Features"],[2447,"Features include stream/topic/partition management, a message browser with JSON/string/XML decoders, and user listing and creation with initial permissions. Editing or deleting existing users and changing their permissions are placeholders. The logs and terminal pages are also placeholders, and the server settings page has a disabled Save action rather than an operational configuration editor."],[2852,"Fetching messages"],[399,"FetchMessages is code 109:"],[1124,"Field transformations (depending on the supported payload formats) can be applied to the messages either before they are sent to the specified topic (e.g. when produced by the source connectors), or before consumed by the sink connectors. To add a new transformation, implement the `Transform` trait in a new module under the SDK's `transforms` directory, add a variant to the `TransformType` enum, and extend its `from_config` function - the runtime picks it up without any further changes. Each transform may have its own, custom configuration."],[472,"Fields pair up: first the key, then the value. Keys and values can both use the header kinds above. Every `length` must be 1..=255, kind 0 is rejected, and the block must consume its byte range exactly. The wire validator preserves unknown non-zero kind codes. A typed SDK accessor may still reject a kind it does not recognize."],[2226,"filter_fields"],[3452,"Finally, you can use the `send()` method to send the messages to the topic. The producer **never needs to be a mutable binding**: both `init()` and `send()` take `&self`, so a plain `let producer` is enough for its whole life. Here's how you can send the messages:"],[3569,"First build the project in release mode:"],[4029,"First verify the default page size and leave sufficient ordinary memory. If `Hugepagesize` is `2048 kB`, 512 reserved pages consume 1 GiB:"],[2415,"First/Last N messages"],[455,"Fixed 9 bytes:"],[2229,"flat_buffer_convert"],[3921,"Flush scheduling is separate"],[3261,"Follow resource and activity changes, retain a position and rebuild after history expires."],[1006,"Follower nodes forward control-plane HTTP requests (stream, topic, user, and similar metadata operations) to the current metadata primary, so **any node can answer any control-plane request**. This requires JWT key material that every node can verify, from either of two sources:"],[1156,"For `transport = \"http\"`, use complete signal URLs, for example `http://localhost:4318/v1/logs` for logs and `http://localhost:4318/v1/traces` for traces. The runtime does not append those paths."],[1107,"For a debug build, omit `--release` and replace `target/release` with `target/debug` in both the commands and plugin paths. Make sure that the plugins specified in `core/connectors/runtime/example_config/connectors/` directory under `path` are available. The configuration must be provided in `toml` format. The example directory also enables connectors for ClickHouse, Delta Lake, Apache Doris, Apache Iceberg, and InfluxDB. Without their backing services (or their compiled plugins) these are reported with the `Error` status, but they don't block the remaining connectors. Set `enabled = false` in their files to skip them entirely."],[2994,"For a direct low-level send, pass `batch.Messages` to `client.SendMessagesAsync(...)` yourself, `await` it, then dispose the batch (`using var batch = builder.Build()`)."],[1755,"For a JSONB query example, a TEXT search example, and recommended indexes, see the upstream postgres_sink README."],[1566,"For a local catalog without authentication, create `nyc.users` with `id` (long) and `name` (string):"],[1730,"For a local PostgreSQL instance matching the example credentials:"],[1771,"For a local Quickwit instance with support for the example's 0.9 index configuration:"],[1497,"For a local receiver, run this in a separate terminal:"],[1448,"For a manual redrive, preserve the label inputs and chunk boundaries, including both `batch_length` and `batch_size`. Poll sizes can also vary, so unchanged settings alone do not guarantee the same labels. Changed boundaries or expired labels can produce duplicates. This is not an end-to-end exactly-once guarantee."],[4040,"For a reproducible dedicated-host experiment, compare the `performance` CPU governor with the current governor if the guest exposes CPU frequency control. Power and thermal limits can still constrain frequency. Many cloud guests cannot control the host's governor. Record the setting rather than assuming a command changed it. See CPU performance scaling."],[1914,"For a sink that only prints JSON, replace the `if self.print_payload` block in `consume()` with `print_json_messages(messages, &messages_metadata);` and add this function. Unsupported payloads are logged and skipped by this variant:"],[1431,"For a table with columns `id` (`BIGINT`) and `name` (`STRING`), create the Iggy resources and send a row:"],[1403,"For a table with columns `id` (long) and `name` (string), create the Iggy resources and send a row:"],[3036,"For an existing server, use its configured credentials. Create a client with `IggyClientFactory.CreateClient`, then call `ConnectAsync`:"],[936,"For an operation that waits for completion:"],[2997,"For automatic object serialization, use `IggyPublisherBuilder<T>` with an `ISerializer<T>`:"],[3366,"For binary transports, credentials in the connection string take effect on `connect()`. HTTP requires `loginUser()` explicitly. Alternatively, construct the client with a plain address (`new \\Iggy\\Client('127.0.0.1:8090')`) and call `loginUser('iggy', 'iggy')` explicitly."],[3999,"For broker API commands, only the ping liveness probe and the login handshake itself (username/password, personal access token, or HTTP token refresh, all of which prove a credential) are served without an authenticated session. Every other request requires one and is subject to permissions: fetching server stats, for example, needs the `read_servers` permission (the root user has it), and even the Prometheus `/metrics` scrape **must present a bearer credential**. A stateful connection authenticates by logging in with the user's credentials or personal access token. A retained session survives transport loss. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end it. Over the HTTP API, authentication is done by providing the `Authorization` header with a `Bearer` JWT or personal access token. HTTP CORS preflight responses and embedded `/ui` static assets are public. The UI's broker API calls still require authentication."],[4172,"For example, a user that may only consume from stream 42 needs no global permissions at all: grant stream-scoped `read_stream` and `poll_messages` on stream 42, and the user can read that stream, list its topics, and poll messages from any topic in it. The read grant also permits consumer-group operations in that stream. For polling without those read and group permissions, grant only `poll_messages`."],[1896,"For example, to disable the sink with key `stdout` at the next runtime start:"],[2189,"For example, to disable the source with key `random` at the next runtime start:"],[2627,"For examples in other programming languages, see the SDK section."],[3936,"For explicit offset changes, `consumer_offset_durability=persisted` similarly waits for the required local file and directory synchronization before success. On a singleton, persisting an offset does not force earlier `replicated` messages to be flushed. Select both policies as `persisted` when both messages and their stored offsets need that protection."],[3949,"For HTTP production:"],[4340,"For HTTP, set `IGGY_TOKEN` to a valid JWT or personal access token:"],[2999,"For JSON you don't need a custom serializer - the built-in `SystemTextJsonSerializer<T>` (in `Apache.Iggy.Publishers`) writes System.Text.Json output directly into the send buffer, with optional `JsonSerializerOptions`:"],[2293,"For maximum throughput and lowest latency, use **TCP**. If you need built-in encryption without configuring TLS separately, **QUIC** is a good choice. **WebSocket** works well for browser-based clients. **HTTP** is the most accessible but has the highest overhead due to JSON serialization. HTTP connections can be reused. Consumer groups can be created, inspected, and deleted, but there is no join/leave membership over HTTP."],[1538,"For multi-instance deployment patterns (Docker, Kubernetes, fan-out topologies), connection pool tuning, and full performance analysis, see the upstream http_sink README."],[4226,"For multi-replica partitions, the disk prepare WAL is enabled when either policy is `persisted` and includes message payloads even if only the offset policy is persisted. Checkpoints synchronize materialized files before reclaiming WAL history. See Durability and Cluster Durability for completion guarantees and failure behavior."],[2630,"For our purpose, we will focus on the basic scenario in order to keep things simple. Before we begin implementing the consumer and producer apps, we need to start the Iggy streaming server."],[3930,"For persisted messages and consumer offsets, create a topic in an existing stream:"],[4178,"For production deployments, provide proper certificates via `cert_file` and `key_file`. Ephemeral certificates change on every start, so a trust configuration pinned to one generated certificate does not survive a restart. See Networking for the surrounding transport configuration."],[1514,"For production throughput with JSON-representable payloads, prefer `ndjson` or `json_array`, which collapse N round trips per poll cycle into one."],[2082,"For QoS 1 and QoS 2, the source defers broker acknowledgements until Iggy accepts the batch. An Iggy failure causes a negative batch result, and the plugin retains the batch for replay. A crash after Iggy accepts it but before the broker receives the acknowledgement can produce duplicates."],[2626,"For real-world applications, you might typically prefer the **high-level SDK**, which provides `IggyProducer` and `IggyConsumer` builders with auto-batching, consumer groups, offset management, reconnection, and retry logic out of the box. Check the Stream Builder docs for the most ergonomic API."],[2826,"For TcpTls, run from the repository root so `core/certs/iggy_ca_cert.pem` resolves. Start a separate matching server with the example TLS certificate and root credentials:"],[3091,"For the code walkthrough, see the quick start on the intro page, which follows the same example."],[1546,"For the complete list and implementation context, see the upstream http_sink README."],[935,"For the creation options and single-node behavior, see Durability."],[3512,"For the full getting started tutorial, see Getting Started. For the high-level producer/consumer builders, see High-level SDK and Stream Builder."],[3310,"For the quick-start code itself, see the Node.js SDK intro."],[3392,"For the quick-start code itself, see the Python SDK intro."],[2457,"For the root Compose build, run the included CLI with `docker exec -it iggy-server /iggy`. In published images, its path is `/usr/local/bin/iggy`."],[3404,"For the source version described here, install from the repository root with Rust and Cargo available:"],[1981,"For this local example, start Elasticsearch with authentication disabled:"],[3108,"For unreleased changes, use the local SDK replacement shown in the repository examples. A published edge tag need not contain every change in your checkout."],[703,"For updates, supply expiry and `--max-topic-size` explicitly. Omitted values are sent as `server_default` (zero): the server retains the previous expiry/size fields but reports zero in their option entries. See Topic options for this inconsistency."],[1615,"For V3, replace the entire `[plugin_config]` section. Unknown options are rejected, so remove the V2 `org` and `bucket` fields:"],[801,"For zsh, add `~/.zfunc` to `fpath` before running `compinit` in your shell configuration."],[2707,"Format"],[1639,"Format names are case-insensitive. `utf8` aliases `text` and `raw` aliases `base64`. An unrecognized `payload_format` logs a warning and falls back to `json`."],[1704,"Format names are case-insensitive. Formatting happens after stream decoding and transforms, so JSON decoding can change the original bytes."],[1749,"Format names are case-insensitive. Unrecognized names silently use `bytea`. JSONB accepts objects, arrays and scalars, but PostgreSQL rejects `\\u0000` in JSONB and zero bytes in TEXT. JSON decoding and JSONB storage can change the original representation. Message headers are not stored. See PostgreSQL JSON types for JSONB restrictions."],[2026,"Forward the provider's delivery ID and use it for application deduplication. An Iggy message ID stays stable for a batch replay, but a new HTTP request receives a new ID. The source reference describes endpoint management, persistence and metrics."],[117,"ForwardCompletionHeader"],[824,"Forwarding does not make every ambiguous operation safe to replay. HTTP `ack=none` confirms dispatch only. A deferred read that commits uses POST, and a lost reply can leave its progress uncertain. See Cluster security."],[115,"ForwardLogoutHeader"],[116,"ForwardLogoutResultHeader"],[113,"ForwardRegisterHeader"],[114,"ForwardRegisterResultHeader"],[96,"Frame integrity"],[487,"Framing"],[632,"Framing: the 256-byte headers every client-facing message rides in."],[3534,"From `examples/swift`, run the producer and then the consumer:"],[1327,"From the matching repository root, create an example crate and schema directory:"],[3305,"From the repository root, build the local SDK before installing the examples. The stream-builder example creates a stream and topic, sends and consumes three messages, and deletes its resources:"],[3089,"From the repository root, start a disposable server with TLS enabled and the development certificates:"],[2405,"Fully operational RESTful API which can be optionally enabled"],[4183,"Further keys (`valid_issuers`, `valid_audiences`, `not_before`, `use_base64_secret`, the signing secrets) are covered in Configuration."],[1852,"Further options cover credentials (`access_key_id` / `secret_access_key`), retries (`max_attempts` defaults to `3`, `retry_delay` to `1s`), and `path_style` addressing (auto-enabled when `endpoint` is set, overridable with `false`). `max_retries` is an alias for `max_attempts`. `0` and `1` both make one outer upload attempt. Invalid retry durations prevent startup. `0s` is allowed. Output format names are case-insensitive, with `jsonl` and `jsonlines` aliases for `json_lines`. See the upstream s3_sink README for the full reference."],[1506,"Further options cover retries (`max_retries`, `retry_delay`, `retry_backoff_multiplier`, `max_retry_delay`), success status codes, payload size limits, TLS, connection pooling, health checks, and verbose logging. See the upstream http_sink README for the full list with defaults."],[1667,"Further options cover task polling (`task_timeout` `30s`, `task_poll_interval` `100ms`) and transient retries (`max_retries` `3`, `retry_delay` `500ms`, `max_retry_delay` `5s`, `max_open_retries` `5`). Both retry limits count retries after the first request, so `max_retries = 3` permits up to four attempts within the operation deadline. Backoff doubles from `retry_delay`, adds ±20% jitter and is capped at `max_retry_delay`. Reversed delay bounds are swapped. Invalid duration strings warn and fall back to `1s`. There is no single deadline for the whole startup sequence. See the upstream meilisearch_sink README for details."],[1442,"Further options cover the label prefix, connect timeout, retry backoff (`retry_delay`, `max_retry_delay`), `max_filter_ratio`, a `where` filter, and redirect security (`allow_insecure_redirect`, `allowed_redirect_hosts`). See the upstream doris_sink README for the full table with defaults."],[800,"Generate completions for bash, zsh, fish, elvish or powershell with `--generate`:"],[2151,"Generate random messages into Iggy streams for testing, benchmarking and verifying a connector pipeline."],[4051,"Get a token for the scraper"],[1225,"Get active configs: `GET {base_url}/configs/active`"],[1231,"Get active sink config: `GET {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/active`"],[1234,"Get active source config: `GET {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/active`"],[1226,"Get active versions: `GET {base_url}/configs/active/versions`"],[1230,"Get sink config by version: `GET {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/{version}`"],[1229,"Get sink configs: `GET {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs`"],[1233,"Get source config by version: `GET {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/{version}`"],[1232,"Get source configs: `GET {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs`"],[403,"GetMetadataChanges is code 17. It supports Earliest, Latest, After and Current starts, group selection, bounded result counts and optional time bounds. The request codec defines its flags and position encodings. See Metadata changes for the follow and rebuild contract."],[394,"GetPartitionRoute is code 105. Its request contains stream and topic identifiers followed by a u32 partition ID. The response is exactly 25 bytes:"],[9,"Getting started"],[3076,"Getting started"],[2587,"Getting started (Python)"],[2614,"Getting started (Rust)"],[921,"Give each container a static IP and put that IP in the roster: `cluster.nodes[*].ip` does **not** accept hostnames or Docker service names. The repository ships a working reference in `bdd/docker-compose.cluster.yml`, a real two-node cluster used by the BDD suites."],[2349,"Give every gateway the same `IGGY_KAFKA_BROKERS` list and a unique `IGGY_KAFKA_NODE_ID`. Put each gateway beside an Iggy node and point its bridge at that node. Clients list multiple gateways in `bootstrap.servers`. Each gateway can route requests to any Iggy partition primary."],[4168,"Global `poll_messages` lets the user poll messages from **all** streams and topics."],[732,"Global permission tokens (long / short): `manage_servers`/`m_srv`, `read_servers`/`r_srv`, `manage_users`/`m_usr`, `read_users`/`r_usr`, `manage_streams`/`m_str`, `read_streams`/`r_str`, `manage_topics`/`m_top`, `read_topics`/`r_top`, `poll_messages`/`p_msg`, `send_messages`/`s_msg`."],[2649,"Glossary"],[3092,"Go SDK"],[3235,"Gradle"],[4054,"Grafana and OpenTelemetry"],[3480,"Group membership is managed by the reader. The old `auto_join_consumer_group`, `poll_interval`, `batch_length` and enum-based auto-commit consumer configuration are replaced by the current reader and `PollOptions` API."],[323,"Grouped fetches"],[2827,"Guide"],[3041,"Guide - client configuration reference and the full API surface: auth, streams, topics, partitions, publishing, consuming, offsets, consumer groups, system operations"],[3263,"Handle each response"],[93,"Handshake"],[2512,"Having in mind that stream consists of topics, which might have one or more partitions assigned, and each partition consists of segments, we can visualize the structure of the Iggy data directory as follows:"],[433,"Header kinds"],[97,"Header layouts"],[2703,"Header values don't have to be strings. They can also be numbers, booleans or raw bytes, so you can add things like a trace ID next to the type, as the typed-headers example shows. The message-compression example uses a header in the same way to record how the payload was compressed."],[1802,"Headers and failure handling"],[2831,"Heartbeat"],[4085,"Heartbeat"],[3881,"Helm charts"],[2389,"Helm Charts