Apache Kafka's topics, partitions, consumer groups and offsets map to Apache Iggy, and what works differently."],[1163,"How configuration is resolved"],[3598,"How configuration loads"],[3556,"How Iggy is benchmarked, the tooling that ships with it, and the public platform for comparing results."],[2356,"How Iggy organizes data"],[4471,"How it connects"],[2468,"How it works"],[967,"How partition primaries, replication factors and client routing distribute work across Iggy nodes."],[4129,"How permissions cascade"],[1164,"How plugins are resolved"],[1977,"How Polling Works"],[1162,"How the connector runtime loads plugins, resolves its configuration, and manages connector lifecycles."],[405,"How the protocol version is packed and checked, and the login-register handshake that authenticates a connection."],[2524,"How the terms map"],[4082,"HTTP"],[2024,"HTTP 200 confirms an in-memory enqueue. It does not confirm an Iggy write or durable storage. Process crashes and shutdown can lose accepted events. Sender retries and replayed batches can also create duplicates."],[1797,"HTTP 429, 5xx and network failures can retry. Calculated waits use exponential backoff with jitter and the configured cap. An integer-seconds `Retry-After` on a 429 or any 5xx can lengthen the calculated wait past `retry_max_delay`, up to one hour, but never shortens it. HTTP-date and unparsable values fall back to the calculated wait. Other HTTP statuses stop that request's retry loop. Readiness has its separate attempt budget. There is no circuit breaker or sink deduplication key, so retrying an accepted request can produce duplicates. `max_retries = 1` disables the sink's HTTP retry loop."],[1641,"HTTP 429, all 5xx responses, and network errors are retried. `max_retries = 3` allows the initial attempt plus two retries. `0` and `1` both allow one attempt. Backoff starts at `retry_delay`, doubles, adds ±20% jitter, and is capped by `retry_max_delay`. An integer-seconds `Retry-After` on a 429 or any 5xx can lengthen the calculated delay past `retry_max_delay`, up to one hour, but never shortens it. HTTP-date and unparsable values fall back to the calculated delay. Startup health checks use a separate attempt budget and cap, retrying any failed check."],[1172,"HTTP API"],[980,"HTTP can forward a request to the correct primary in one hop. It needs the cluster authentication and shared JWT configuration described in Security. Keep clocks synchronized because forwarding proofs expire after ten seconds."],[1169,"HTTP Configuration Provider"],[808,"HTTP forwarding"],[4103,"HTTP is the most accessible protocol but has the highest overhead due to JSON serialization and the stateless nature of HTTP (no persistent connections for consumer groups). You can find request examples in the server.http file."],[2714,"HTTP options"],[3050,"HTTP reads"],[3445,"HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Consumer groups require a binary transport. Use an ordinary consumer with an explicit partition over HTTP."],[3682,"HTTP sessions hold live server-side session state (they count against `metadata.clients_table_max`). Consumer group management (create, get, delete) is available over HTTP. Group membership (join, leave) is not and needs a stateful transport."],[1480,"HTTP Sink"],[2004,"HTTP Source"],[1533,"HTTP success is determined by the response status. The sink does not inspect a successful response body for per-item failures."],[3713,"HTTP TLS is **file-based only**. There's no `self_signed` option for this listener. Both files must exist when enabled."],[988,"HTTP tokens across the cluster"],[4334,"HTTP updates use a flat JSON object, not a nested `options` object:"],[4005,"Huge pages and mimalloc"],[4024,"Huge pages can reduce translation overhead, but they consume memory differently from ordinary pages. Measure both throughput and tail latency. Iggy's default server build uses mimalloc. Check the startup allocator message, since builds that disable mimalloc do not use its environment options."],[1547,"Iceberg Sink"],[3439,"Idempotent producer"],[3123,"Idempotent Producers"],[428,"Identifier"],[2837,"Identifiers"],[444,"Identifies a consumer or a consumer group in message and offset commands:"],[1449,"If `max_filter_ratio` permits malformed rows, Doris can accept a load while dropping those rows. The plugin logs a warning when Doris reports them. The `where` option deliberately excludes rows before loading."],[586,"If `status` is nonzero, this reply reports a pre-commit rejection. It does not settle an earlier uncertain attempt. The body is empty."],[4171,"If a stream has no entry in the user's stream permissions, only global permissions apply to it. The same holds for topics within a stream."],[2697,"If every message on a topic has the same shape, send the encoded value as the payload and decode it the same way on the other side. Getting started does this with plain strings."],[1222,"If not customized, the HTTP provider uses the following RESTful URL patterns:"],[4296,"If the server exits with `io_uring was denied locked memory`, the container's locked-memory limit is too low for it. Raise it, as the `--ulimit memlock=-1:-1` flag does in Docker & Helm."],[4488,"If the server is compiled without the `iggy-web` feature and `web_ui = true`, a warning is logged but the server continues to run normally."],[2526,"If you already know Apache Kafka, most of Apache Iggy will feel familiar. This page maps the Kafka terms you know to Iggy's and lists what works differently. For when to choose one or the other, see Apache Iggy or Apache Kafka."],[3980,"If you compile the source code in release mode, linking takes longer because LTO is enabled in the `[profile.release]` section of the workspace Cargo.toml."],[1583,"If you don't know the names of the Iceberg tables you want to route data to in advance, you can use the dynamic routing feature. Insert a top-level field in your JSON messages with the name of the Iceberg table the message should be routed to. The Iggy connector will parse this field at runtime and route the message to the correct table."],[1598,"If your source emits envelope-wrapped JSON, use the `unwrap_envelope` transform to extract the inner data field before it reaches the sink:"],[753,"Iggy CLI"],[2443,"Iggy CLI can be installed with `cargo install iggy-cli --version 0.15.0 --locked` and then simply accessed by typing `iggy` in your terminal. It supports named connection contexts (profiles) for managing multiple server connections, shell completions for bash/zsh/fish/elvish/powershell, and session-based login through platform credential stores on Linux, macOS and Windows when the default `login-session` feature is enabled."],[3567,"Iggy comes with a built-in benchmarking tool, `iggy-bench`. It's written in Rust and uses the `tokio` runtime for asynchronous I/O, mimicking the example client applications, so you can use it to estimate the performance of the server in your environment. It is part of the core repository and lives in the `core/bench` directory."],[4244,"Iggy encrypts message payloads and user headers with a separate AES-256-GCM-SIV key per encrypted stream. They are encrypted before storage and decrypted for polling. Metadata journals, metadata snapshots, and structural record headers remain unencrypted. The key must decode from base64 to 32 bytes. See Security."],[2534,"Iggy has its own protocol, and the Kafka gateway serves Kafka producers, consumers and supported admin APIs. Run it as a separate service with the Iggy bridge enabled. The gateway has a defined API subset and does not support transactions. Native applications can use an Iggy SDK or the HTTP API."],[4290,"Iggy has no embedded, in-process test server. The server ships as a binary, not a library, so a test suite can't start it in-process. To test against a real server, start the published Docker image when your tests start and stop it when they finish. The examples below use each language's `testcontainers` library to do that."],[4233,"Iggy includes a custom memory pool to reuse buffers on the hot path. The pool has **28 buckets** with buffer sizes from 4 KiB up to 512 MiB (non-uniform spacing, denser around common message sizes, with sizes above 2 MiB rounded to hugepage-friendly steps). Components request a buffer from the appropriate bucket and return it when done."],[2394,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming log **built from the ground up** using low-level I/O with **thread-per-core shared nothing architecture**, `io_uring` and `compio` for maximum speed and efficiency."],[2291,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform that stores messages in an append-only log format, similar to Kafka. However, Iggy is designed for high performance and low latency using `io_uring` and a thread-per-core architecture. Iggy uses its own binary protocol rather than the Kafka protocol. Clients can use an Iggy SDK, implement the documented binary protocol, or use the HTTP API."],[2548,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform: messages are stored in a form of an **append-only log**. You can create multiple streams, consisting of topics, which might have one or more partitions assigned, e.g. to divide consumption among independent consumers. Multi-node replication provides redundancy. You can think of Iggy as an alternative to Kafka or RabbitMQ streams."],[2373,"Iggy is more than just a server. The project includes a full ecosystem of tools:"],[2553,"Iggy is the latter, a message streaming platform."],[1962,"Iggy message IDs, partition IDs, offsets, timestamps and checksums are stored as strings. Timestamps retain Iggy's microsecond values, not SurrealDB datetime values. Record IDs encode stream/topic UTF-8 bytes as hex, then append partition, offset and the 32-digit hexadecimal message ID. Changing the payload or transforms does not change that identity. Changing any identity component creates a different key."],[2301,"Iggy migrated from Tokio to a thread-per-core architecture with `compio` (which uses `io_uring` on Linux) starting with version 0.6.0. The published v0.5.0 versus v0.7.0 comparison reports 92% lower P9999 latency for its 16-producer, 16-stream, 40-million-message workload, and 18% higher throughput for the corresponding fsync workload. These are historical workload results, not a guarantee for 0.10.0. You can read the full story in the thread-per-core io_uring blog post."],[2392,"Iggy provides **exceptionally high throughput and performance** while utilizing minimal computing resources."],[2448,"Iggy provides a highly performant and modular **runtime** for statically typed, yet dynamically loaded connectors. You can ingest data from external sources and push the data to Iggy streams, or fetch data from Iggy streams and forward it to external systems. **Create your own Rust plugins** by simply implementing either the `Source` or `Sink` trait and **build custom pipelines for the data processing**."],[2300,"Iggy provides a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server with 40+ tools for LLM integration, connectors for piping data to/from external systems (e.g. PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, S3, Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit), Prometheus metrics, and OpenTelemetry traces/logs in the connectors runtime. Broker OpenTelemetry export has runtime limitations described in configuration. The HTTP API works with REST clients."],[4143,"Iggy provides granular permissions at three levels:"],[4134,"Iggy provides multiple layers of security covering authentication, authorization, transport encryption, and data encryption at rest."],[3147,"Iggy provides official client SDKs in multiple languages. The **Rust SDK** is the most feature-complete, offering both a high-level API (with auto-batching, consumer groups, offset management, retry logic) and a low-level API for direct protocol operations."],[2294,"Iggy runs as a single server process without an external coordination service. Native library requirements depend on the build. The provided Linux images include hwloc and udev libraries. The Linux shard runtime requires `io_uring` flags introduced in kernel 5.19. The server starts with around 20 MB of RAM. The default 4 GiB memory-pool setting is a budget, with buffers allocated on demand and fallback allocations outside the pool."],[3966,"Iggy sends a JSON `POST`:"],[3978,"Iggy server is the most important part of the system as it's responsible for handling all the incoming connections, managing the data and providing the API for the clients. The server is written in Rust. It uses `io_uring` on Linux and a polling backend on macOS."],[627,"Iggy speaks one binary protocol over its three binary transports: TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket. Commands, responses, data models, and status codes are the same on all of them. The HTTP transport is separate: it exposes JSON REST endpoints for a subset of these operations, listed in server.http."],[1040,"Iggy splits replication by namespace:"],[4087,"Iggy supports four transport protocols simultaneously, each optimized for different use cases. All stateful protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket) use the same custom binary wire protocol, while HTTP provides a standard REST API."],[4135,"Iggy supports local credentials and personal access tokens. HTTP also accepts configured JWT issuers. An optional external authentication service handles unknown local usernames."],[2299,"Iggy supports TLS on all transport protocols, Argon2id password hashing, granular per-stream/per-topic permissions, Personal Access Tokens for programmatic access, and optional AES-256-GCM-SIV encryption of stored message payloads. For the HTTP API, JWT tokens are used for session management. See the Security documentation for details."],[2502,"Iggy uses **compio** as its async runtime, which provides a driver-disaggregated architecture on top of io_uring (Linux) and IOCP (Windows). Each shard gets its own compio executor configured with:"],[4015,"Iggy uses `io_uring`. The kernel must implement the operations required by the server build, and the service or container must be allowed to use them. The server's startup diagnostics identify failed ring creation and unsupported operations. Where the kernel exposes them, inspect:"],[2482,"Iggy uses a **thread-per-core shared nothing architecture** combined with `io_uring` for maximum performance. This design has been proven by systems like ScyllaDB and Redpanda, both built on the Seastar framework."],[2514,"Iggy uses a custom memory pool with 28 buckets holding buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB. The default pool size is 4 GiB with up to 8192 buffers per bucket. Buffers are allocated on demand and reused. Requests that exceed the pool budget can allocate outside it. Frozen buffers can be shared between components without copying their contents. The pool is page-aligned (4096-byte multiples) and requires a **minimum of 512 MiB**."],[2363,"Iggy uses a hierarchical model: **streams** contain **topics**, topics contain **partitions**, and partitions store messages in **segments** on disk. This gives you flexible multi-tenancy, parallel processing, and efficient storage all in one."],[934,"Iggy uses Viewstamped Replication to commit partition operations. The topic's `durability` and `consumer_offset_durability` options select the storage guarantee required for message production and explicit offset changes, respectively. Both default independently to `replicated`. Both continue to write data to disk."],[4469,"Iggy Web UI"],[4478,"Iggy Web UI provides a comprehensive dashboard for Iggy server. It allows you to monitor the server's health, streams, topics, browse the messages, users and more. The dashboard is built with SvelteKit and TypeScript and is available as open-source as well as the Docker image on Docker Hub."],[1908,"iggy_connector_sdk"],[2203,"iggy_connector_sdk"],[940,"Iggy's replication quorum is not a strict majority for every group size:"],[4204,"Iggy's storage engine is built around the concept of a **segmented append-only log**. Message data follows a hierarchy: System -> Streams -> Topics -> Partitions -> Segments. This page covers how data is stored, indexed, flushed, recovered, and cleaned up on disk."],[4048,"Iggy's topic `durability` and `consumer_offset_durability` independently default to `replicated`. `persisted` changes completion guarantees, and huge pages or sysctl settings do not replace it. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous. Record both policies, replication-group size, payload and batch sizes, warmup, CPU allocation, and the exact host changes with each benchmark result."],[2946,"IggyConsumer"],[2471,"IggyNamespace"],[3430,"IggyProducer and IggyConsumer: batching, consumer groups and offset commits without the boilerplate."],[2942,"IggyPublisher"],[2941,"IggyPublisher and IggyConsumer for C#, with background batching, retries and automatic offset commits."],[3903,"Images tagged `latest` are based on stable releases. Images tagged `edge` are built from the latest `master` branch."],[3469,"Import `futures_util::StreamExt` to use `next()`. The mutable consumer drives reads and retries when polled. A checkpoint keeps the partition history and ownership that delivered the message. Committing it after recreation or an ownership change is refused. Process messages before committing their checkpoints."],[1352,"In `mapped` mode, each payload must be a JSON object. Top-level fields map to table columns by name. Unknown fields are ignored. Required columns without defaults must have non-null values. Missing or null repeated columns become empty arrays."],[1353,"In `raw` mode, `payload_column` defaults to `payload`. A JSON column requires valid JSON. A STRING column requires text or UTF-8 bytes. A BYTES column accepts raw bytes from any payload type. Other required columns must have defaults."],[1385,"In `row_binary` mode the schema is captured once at startup and the insert stream is positional. An `ALTER TABLE` on the target while the connector runs can silently corrupt inserted rows, so restart the connector after any schema change. The self-describing `json_each_row` format (including `string` mode with `string_format = \"json_each_row\"`) maps values by field name, but changed column names, types, or constraints can still make inserts fail. `string` passthrough with `csv` or `tsv` sends plain positional ClickHouse formats mapped by the current table column order."],[3997,"In another terminal at the repository root:"],[3703,"In cluster mode the secret has an extra role: a configured secret, identical on every node, makes bearer tokens valid cluster-wide and activates follower-to-primary HTTP forwarding. When both JWT secrets are empty and `cluster.auth` is enabled, the JWT key is derived from the cluster PSK. Explicit JWT secrets take precedence. Without either, tokens are node-local and forwarding stays disabled."],[3683,"In cluster mode, HTTP requests use the metadata or partition primary as appropriate. A contacted node can forward in one hop when shared JWT key material enables signed relay proofs. Partition polls also use the partition route. See Client failover."],[4120,"In cluster mode, listener ports come exclusively from each node's `cluster.nodes` ports entry, including the `tcp_replica` port for the consensus plane, and nodes can advertise different client-facing addresses per client network. Replica-to-replica traffic can be authenticated with a pre-shared key and wrapped in TLS 1.3. See Clustering configuration and Clustering security."],[4187,"In cluster mode, replica-to-replica traffic can require an authenticated handshake based on a pre-shared key (with a documented rolling rotation procedure) and can be wrapped in TLS 1.3. With `cluster.auth` enabled and no JWT secrets configured, the PSK also becomes the cluster-wide JWT key source. See Clustering security."],[912,"In cluster mode:"],[4484,"In embedded mode neither applies: the UI is served from the same origin as the HTTP API and uses relative URLs."],[1520,"In JSON batch modes, raw, FlatBuffer, Protobuf and Avro payload variants are base64-encoded with an explicit marker:"],[3995,"In order to see the detailed logs from the server, run it with `RUST_LOG=trace` environment variable."],[2139,"In polling mode, an existing `payload_column` bypasses the envelope. Use `bytea` or `raw` for a BYTEA column with `schema = \"raw\"`, `text` for a text column with `schema = \"text\"`, and `json_direct`, `jsonb` or `jsonb_direct` for JSON/JSONB with `schema = \"json\"`. A null BYTEA/text payload becomes empty bytes, which the Iggy message builder rejects. The entire batch is Nacked. Use non-null, non-empty BYTEA/text payloads. A null JSON/JSONB payload becomes JSON `null` and can be delivered."],[2779,"In Rust, construct clients inside a Tokio runtime, which QUIC needs even before `connect()`. The following snippet assumes an async function returning `Result<_, IggyError>` and the matching Rust SDK setup."],[4329,"In the CLI, both durability policies have named flags. `--set` is repeatable and covers other storage options, such as `segment_size`. `compression_algorithm`, `message_expiry`, and `max_topic_size` also have first-class parameters. Run `iggy options topic` to discover what the server accepts."],[3415,"In the core repository, you can find the following examples using the Rust SDK."],[2583,"In the SDK message representation, the header is followed by payload bytes and then optional user-header bytes."],[1109,"In the terminal that will run the connectors, set the runtime configuration path:"],[1458,"Index mapping"],[1646,"Index messages from Iggy streams as documents in a Meilisearch index through the official Rust SDK."],[1455,"Index messages from Iggy streams into an Elasticsearch index using the bulk API."],[4193,"Indexes"],[1774,"Indexing happens asynchronously. Search after the split has committed:"],[1471,"Individual document errors inside a successful bulk response are logged and counted in the plugin's closing statistics, but the plugin still returns success. The runtime's processed-message count can therefore include documents rejected by Elasticsearch. Monitor the document-error logs as well as runtime errors."],[1632,"InfluxDB identifies points by measurement/table, tags and timestamp. Removing stream, topic or partition tags can merge distinct messages that share an offset and timestamp. Moving those values to fields does not preserve their identity. See the V2 and V3 duplicate-point rules."],[1602,"InfluxDB Sink"],[2027,"InfluxDB Source"],[1614,"InfluxDB V2 organizes data as `org` + `bucket`. V3 uses a single `db` field instead. Omitting `version` defaults to `\"v2\"` for backward compatibility. The write body, batching, and retry behavior are identical between versions. The connector handles the differing endpoints, auth header styles, and precision spellings internally."],[2039,"InfluxDB V2 takes an `org` and a Flux query. For V3, replace the entire `[plugin_config]` section below, including the V2-only `org`. The `my-db` database must contain a `cpu` table. This example assumes `host` is its only tag."],[1362,"Insert Formats"],[1677,"Insert messages from Iggy streams into a MongoDB collection, one document per message."],[1359,"Insert messages from Iggy streams into ClickHouse tables over the HTTP interface."],[1386,"Insert requests retry HTTP 408, 429, and 5xx responses, plus network and timeout errors. Other unsuccessful HTTP statuses fail immediately. `max_retries` is the total attempt limit, with at least one attempt even when set to `0`. Before retry number `n` (starting at 1), the delay is sampled from zero through `min(retry_delay * 2^n, 60)` seconds. With the defaults, there are at most three attempts and the first retry waits between zero and two seconds. The startup ping and, in `row_binary` mode, schema fetch use the same limit and backoff but retry every error."],[2465,"Inside the Iggy server: how work is scheduled across cores, how requests are routed, and how a message reaches disk."],[4041,"Inspect CPU steal time, cgroup throttling, and per-core utilization before increasing shard counts. If one core handles most interrupts or receive processing, inspect RSS queue distribution and IRQ affinity. Do not disable irqbalance or assign every IRQ to a fixed core without measuring. See Linux network scaling."],[4039,"Inspect NUMA topology with `lscpu` or `numactl --hardware`. Compare physical-core-only placement with SMT when relevant. Match memory placement and any per-node huge-page reservation to the CPUs running the shards. Avoid assuming that allocating a pool on one NUMA node helps shards running on another."],[736,"Inspect the clients currently connected to the server. Clients are **connections, not users**."],[4035,"Inspect the current policy instead of universally enabling or disabling THP:"],[4002,"Inspect the host first"],[754,"Install the Iggy CLI, connect to a server, authenticate, and manage connection contexts."],[2599,"Install the SDK in a virtual environment:"],[755,"Installation"],[3014,"Installation"],[3095,"Installation"],[3233,"Installation"],[3313,"Installation"],[3395,"Installation"],[4164,"Instead of (or in addition to) global grants, permissions can be scoped per stream and per topic:"],[3443,"Instead of providing the configuration for the client, you can use the connection string. It's a string that contains all the necessary information to connect to the Iggy API, and it works with all four transports:"],[411,"Integer order equals semver order. The value tracks the `iggy_binary_protocol` crate release. Under 0.x the compatibility gate is minor-scoped: the server accepts a client whose packed version is at least the server's minimum and whose `major.minor` is at most the server's. Patch releases never change the wire, so the upper bound ignores patch. The implemented minimum is the current version with its patch component zeroed. Compatibility across minor versions after 1.0.0 requires a future change to that calculation."],[470,"Integers are little-endian. `Bool` is one byte."],[1178,"Internally, dlopen2 loads plugin libraries and resolves their C FFI symbols. Plugins execute inside the runtime process."],[3491,"Intro"],[1,"Introduction"],[1059,"Introduction"],[3138,"Introduction"],[3973,"Introduction"],[2473,"io_uring and compio"],[1491,"IoT / Sensor Relay (binary payloads)"],[4429,"It is the only member, so it gets all three partitions and drains what the producer wrote:"],[1913,"It's also important to note, that the supported format(s) might vary depending on the connector implementation. For example, you might expect `JSON` as the payload format, which can be then easily parsed and processed by upstream components such as data transforms, but at the same time, you could support the other formats and let the user decide which one to use."],[2213,"It's also important to note, that the supported format(s) might vary depending on the connector implementation. For example, you might use `JSON` as the payload format, which can be then easily parsed and processed by downstream components such as data transforms, but at the same time, you could support the other formats and let the user decide which one to use."],[2783,"Java accepts only TCP schemes, username/password credentials, `tls` and `tls_ca_file`. It rejects PAT connection strings and other options. Go's `client.ParseConnectionString` returns options for `client.WithTcp`. Node.js constructors accept a string except `SimpleClient`, which wraps an existing raw client. C# exposes its connection-string factory described in the C# guide."],[3230,"Java SDK"],[2858,"Joining and leaving"],[4499,"JSON decoder (formatted JSON output)"],[1864,"JSON Lines and JSON Array contain wrapper objects with the decoded/transformed payload under `payload`. JSON payloads retain their JSON value. Text and Proto variants become strings. Raw bytes containing valid JSON are parsed, while other raw bytes, Avro and FlatBuffer variants become base64 strings. Those strings do not carry an encoding tag."],[1668,"JSON object payloads are indexed as documents directly. JSON arrays and scalars are wrapped in a `value` field, since Meilisearch documents must be objects. Raw payloads are parsed as JSON when possible and otherwise indexed in `data` as base64, with `data_type = \"raw\"` and `data_encoding = \"base64\"`. Text payloads land in `text` with `data_type = \"text\"`. These mappings apply after stream decoding and transforms. Use `schema = \"raw\"` or `schema = \"text\"` for those representations. Unsupported payload variants are skipped with a warning and counted in the plugin's private error counter. A successful callback after these drops can still count the whole batch as processed in runtime statistics."],[1474,"JSON objects are indexed as documents directly. Other JSON values are forwarded without being wrapped as objects and can be rejected by Elasticsearch."],[1412,"JSON values are coerced to the Delta table schema before writing:"],[4132,"JWT (HTTP API)"],[3126,"Kafka gateway"],[2304,"Kafka Gateway"],[2324,"Kafka offsets identify the next record to read. The gateway stores externally managed group offsets in Iggy. These are separate from native Iggy consumer group membership. Deleting a Kafka topic deletes its backing Iggy topic, but retains the stream."],[4365,"Keep `cluster.coordinator.replica_connections_per_peer` identical on all nodes. Cluster membership is limited to 32 nodes."],[3595,"Keep `min_count` within the smallest `messages-per-batch` value. After warmup, consumer-only runs restart their reads from offset zero, including group consumers. Record wait and byte limits with results because they change batching and latency."],[3593,"Keep both policies, replication-group size, batch and message sizes, CPU allocation, and storage consistent between runs. Run long enough to include flushes and checkpoints, and report tail latency, errors, memory pressure, and client utilization alongside throughput."],[4044,"Keep changes such as `tcp_tw_reuse`, shorter TCP timeouts, and wider ephemeral-port ranges tied to a demonstrated connection-churn problem. See TCP controls and core networking controls."],[1762,"Keep each actual chunk within PostgreSQL's 65,535 query-parameter limit. Each row binds `2 + 5 * include_metadata + include_checksum + include_origin_timestamp` parameters, so the default flags allow at most 7,281 rows per statement. Larger chunks are rejected by SQLx. `batch_length` also limits how many rows reach a poll."],[2458,"Keep in mind that running the container on operating systems other than Linux, where the Docker is running in the VM, might result in the performance degradation."],[2193,"Keep in mind, that the produced messages will be sent further to the specified stream, however it's already the responsibility of the runtime to handle the delivery."],[4366,"Keep node clocks synchronized. Authenticated HTTP forwarding uses short-lived proofs."],[2898,"Keep the same key for clients that exchange encrypted messages and for later reads. The generated key above lasts only for this example. For a custom scheme, implement `IMessageEncryptor`."],[2251,"Keeps or drops fields of the payload based on key and value patterns:"],[4378,"Kernel too old"],[2358,"Key capabilities"],[1282,"Key Configuration Options"],[3884,"Key values"],[4331,"Keys absent from the wire request are resolved by the admitting server and stored as **derived** entries. Typed SDKs can send their default durability values explicitly. Binary `GetTopic` responses carry explicit and derived blocks. HTTP reports an `explicit` flag per option. Both report option values and the provenance of the request. Updates preserve the values and provenance of keys outside the patch."],[1493,"Known Limitations"],[1299,"Last but not least, the different `transforms` are available, to transform (add, update, delete etc.) the particular fields of the data being processed via external configuration. It's as simple as adding a new transform to the `transforms` section of the particular connector configuration file:"],[1032,"leader redirection in the client SDKs"],[999,"Leave `previous_shared_secret` **empty outside a rotation**. It has the same 32-byte floor and the same environment-variable preference as the primary key (`IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_PREVIOUS_SHARED_SECRET`), and it must differ from `shared_secret`. An identical value is refused at boot because the rotation would be a no-op."],[4021,"Leave memory for the kernel, page cache, other processes, and Iggy's buffers. Watch swap-in/out with `vmstat 1` and memory pressure in `/proc/pressure/memory`. Disabling swap does not fix an undersized memory budget."],[2475,"Left-right metadata"],[440,"Length-prefixed UTF-8 string, used for stream, topic, group, user, and token names:"],[1898,"Let's implement the example sink connector, which will simply print the messages to the standard output."],[2191,"Let's implement the example source connector, which generates a random batch of messages at the configured interval and optionally stops after a total message limit."],[4000,"Linux tuning"],[1863,"List the uploaded objects:"],[2311,"Listener and bridge configuration"],[2329,"Listeners support `PLAINTEXT`, `SSL`, `SASL_PLAINTEXT` and `SASL_SSL`. SASL uses PLAIN against Iggy credentials. SSL listeners can map verified client certificates to Iggy users. Configure TLS for both the Kafka listener and the separate gateway-to-Iggy connection when both hops need encryption."],[1191,"Literal path (relative to the working directory)"],[4216,"Little-endian, no header, no padding. To serve a poll, the server binary-searches the index for the closest entry at or below the target offset (or timestamp), seeks to its `position`, and walks batch records from there. Because the index is a sparse hint, a lookup below the indexed range falls back to the segment start rather than failing."],[1420,"Load JSON messages from Iggy streams into a pre-created Apache Doris table through the Stream Load HTTP API."],[1203,"Load uses `GET` by default, or `POST`. Return `200` with the stored bytes and a strong `ETag`, or `404` when no state exists. An empty `200` body is valid state."],[4475,"Local development"],[1168,"Local File Provider"],[3885,"Local source image"],[2509,"Lock-free reads on all shards (no contention on the hot path)"],[4480,"Log in again after upgrading from 0.9. Existing self-issued access and refresh tokens are not valid for the new release."],[1128,"Logging"],[383,"Login and logout are part of the connection lifecycle:"],[761,"Login sessions"],[407,"Login-register"],[691,"login, logout, session"],[1127,"Logs, Prometheus metrics, the stats endpoint, OpenTelemetry export and per-source state files for the connector runtime."],[4279,"Make sure that every node uses this configuration and every encrypted stream reports the new KEK fingerprint. The primary adds new wraps while preserving old ones."],[4281,"Make sure that every stream no longer reports the retired fingerprint. Clear `retired_kek_fingerprints` on every node."],[4405,"Make sure that the client matches the server's protocol. Server 0.10.0 uses protocol 0.12.0 and rejects incompatible clients during login. A malformed old client can report a disconnect rather than a useful compatibility error."],[738,"Manage consumer groups of a topic. Subcommands: `create`, `delete`, `get`, `list`."],[749,"Manage login sessions backed by the platform keyring. `iggy login [EXPIRY]` stores a session token (default expiry: 15 minutes, `none` disables expiry), `iggy logout` removes it, and `iggy session status` reports whether a token exists locally. See login sessions for the full flow."],[740,"Manage named connection profiles. Subcommands: `list`, `use`, `create`, `delete`, `show`. See connection contexts for details."],[734,"Manage personal access tokens for the authenticated user. Subcommands: `create`, `delete`, `list`."],[700,"Manage streams, the top-level containers for topics. Subcommands: `create`, `delete`, `update`, `get`, `list`. The legacy creation flags `stream create -s`, `topic create -t`, and `consumer-group create -g` are accepted but ignored. IDs are always assigned by the server."],[701,"Manage topics within a stream. Subcommands: `create`, `delete`, `update`, `get`, `list`. These examples require an existing `dev` stream."],[727,"Manage users and their permissions. Subcommands: `create`, `delete`, `get`, `list`, `name`, `status`, `password`, `permissions`."],[2841,"Managing users"],[882,"Matching is **per address family**: `0.0.0.0/0` matches no IPv6 client and `::/0` matches no IPv4 client, so covering both families takes one selector per family. IPv4-mapped IPv6 CIDRs (`::ffff:10.0.0.0/104`) match like their IPv4 form only at prefix length 96 or longer."],[883,"Matching sees the transport-level peer address, so clients behind a proxy or load balancer match the proxy's network, not their own."],[3234,"Maven"],[7,"MCP Server"],[688,"me"],[1645,"Meilisearch Sink"],[2478,"Memory pool"],[4197,"Memory pool"],[1153,"Memory values are in bytes. `run_time` is elapsed microseconds, and `start_time` is microseconds since the Unix epoch."],[675,"message"],[2521,"Message `durability` and `consumer_offset_durability` default independently to `replicated`. Both policies write data to disk. `persisted` adds a stable-storage requirement at completion. See Durability."],[639,"Message batches"],[636,"Message batches: the one layout messages travel and rest in."],[3585,"Message count, batch size, message size, and both durability flags precede the kind subcommand. Producer, consumer, stream, and partition counts belong to the selected kind. `iggy-bench --help` and `iggy-bench examples` show the full surface."],[2835,"Message encryption"],[2546,"Message format"],[642,"Message frame header (48 bytes)"],[3374,"Message IDs and checksums are returned as decimal strings. Offset and timestamp getters return PHP integers and are limited to `PHP_INT_MAX`."],[677,"message poll"],[676,"message send"],[2538,"Message streaming"],[17,"Messages"],[314,"Messages"],[4218,"Messages are admitted, stamped, and buffered in the partition journal. Their completion path depends on the topic's durability policy:"],[1640,"Messages are serialized to line protocol and each polled batch is split into chunks of at most `batch_size` messages, one HTTP POST per chunk. The final partial chunk is written immediately. The connector does not accumulate messages across polls. A serialization error rejects its entire chunk. Later chunks are still attempted after a failure, and the first error is returned after the loop."],[2516,"Messages arrive on the owning shard and are buffered in the partition journal."],[1094,"Messages can be decoded and encoded using the following formats: **JSON**, **Raw bytes**, **Text**, **Protocol Buffers**, **FlatBuffers**, and **Avro**. For Avro, the schema can be provided per stream via the `avro_schema_json` or `avro_schema_path` keys in the `[[streams]]` section."],[2700,"Messages can carry user headers, which are key and value pairs kept separate from the payload. Put the type in a header and send the payload as it is:"],[2515,"Messages flow through a multi-stage write pipeline:"],[643,"Messages travel and rest in exactly one layout, the **batch**: the `SendMessages` body, the replicated record, the persisted segment record, and the poll reply all share it. There is no other message encoding."],[692,"metadata"],[1027,"metadata and partition replication"],[324,"Metadata changes"],[3260,"Metadata Changes"],[1485,"Metadata Envelope"],[974,"Metadata leadership does not tell a client where every message write belongs. Clients discover a partition's current primary and send partition requests there. A route also identifies the partition's history and ownership generation, so an old request cannot act on a replacement partition."],[4239,"Metadata operations (create stream, delete topic, create user, and so on) do not use the partition storage above. They replicate through the metadata consensus group and persist in `local_data/metadata/`:"],[4199,"Metadata plane"],[810,"Metadata reads can use a replica's local state. A caller that needs a known metadata change can carry a minimum metadata operation and wait for that node to catch up. Counters on other nodes can lag a successful write until a later prepare or commit broadcast."],[1044,"Metadata reads can use local replicated state. Partition requests route to the current partition primary. Writes return after the required VSR commit or return a retryable error when the replica is changing view or catching up. See Client failover for how the SDKs handle both."],[3370,"Methods are camelCase and classes live in the `Iggy` namespace, for example `Iggy\\Client` and `Iggy\\SendMessage`."],[1138,"Metrics are served by the runtime HTTP API when the `[http.metrics]` section is enabled (the HTTP API itself is on by default at `127.0.0.1:8081`). Metrics are disabled by default. Change `enabled` to `true` to expose them:"],[1165,"Minimal configuration"],[1833,"MinIO example"],[960,"Missing required history, checksum failures, or contradictory materialized state must not become an empty healthy partition. Recovery can fence a partition and repair it from peers. Storage errors withhold successful completion. A locally failed application of a committed operation fences the partition and initiates server shutdown."],[4379,"Missing root credentials"],[2065,"Missing selected columns, invalid JSON cells in V2, and invalid base64 reject the poll. V3 also rejects any row with a missing, non-string or invalid cursor. V2 can emit rows without valid cursors if another row supplies a usable watermark. Keep the cursor column in every row. V3 timestamps without a timezone suffix are treated as UTC, preserving nanoseconds. Keep database timestamps in the payload when consumers need them. The runtime does not transfer the plugin's timestamp fields into broker message metadata."],[2387,"Model Context Protocol"],[1676,"MongoDB Sink"],[4049,"Monitoring"],[2811,"More examples"],[982,"More replica connections per peer can spread replication traffic across shards. This does not change the replication factor or quorum. Retain separate data directories for nodes and advertise addresses reachable by clients."],[1291,"Moreover, it contains both, the `decoders` and `encoders` modules, implementing either `StreamDecoder` or `StreamEncoder` traits, which are used when consuming or producing data from/to Iggy streams. All the supported schemas - **JSON**, **Raw bytes**, **Text**, **Protocol Buffers**, **FlatBuffers**, and **Avro** - have both a decoder and an encoder."],[2069,"MQTT"],[966,"Multi-Leader Partitions"],[1213,"Multiple files sharing the same connector `key` form a version history (each file carries its own `version`). The active version of each connector is persisted in `{config_dir}/.active_versions.toml` and can be selected via the HTTP API for the next process startup. Without a saved selection, the highest version is used. Changing this selection does not update a running connector. The connector restart endpoint currently loads the highest local version, which can differ from the selected active version."],[2412,"Multiple ways of polling the messages:"],[2221,"Mutate, filter and convert messages as they pass through the connector runtime, and how each transform is configured."],[427,"Name"],[1959,"Namespace, database and table names must start with an ASCII letter or underscore and contain only ASCII letters, digits and underscores. Endpoint URLs cannot contain credentials, a path beyond `/`, a query or a fragment. An explicit `http://` or `https://` scheme takes precedence over `use_tls`. Payload-format and authentication-scope names are case-insensitive. Unknown values prevent startup."],[1592,"Namespace: `nyc`"],[2717,"Native SDK differences"],[964,"Neither policy turns HTTP `ack=none` or a poll's auto-commit into an awaited durable result. See Which responses prove completion."],[4009,"Network and storage"],[4075,"Networking"],[1123,"New connector can be built simply by implementing either `Sink` or `Source` trait. Please check the **sink** or **source** documentation, as well as the existing examples under `core/connectors/sinks` and `core/connectors/sources`."],[2355,"New in 0.10.0"],[1043,"New partitions spread their primaries across eligible nodes, and the metadata primary rebalances them one at a time. Each group can have a different primary. Metadata writes route through the metadata primary. Partition writes route through the primary for that partition."],[973,"New partitions spread their primaries across eligible nodes. The metadata primary also rebalances existing primaries one at a time. This restores the distribution after changes such as a failed node returning. Each partition still has one primary at a time, and its messages retain a partition-local order."],[4367,"New topics inherit `cluster.default_replication_factor`, which defaults to `all`. Select a different factor at topic creation with `replication_factor`. Existing topics retain their stored value. See Multi-leader partitions."],[2416,"Next N messages for the specific consumer"],[1019,"Next steps"],[2623,"Next steps"],[2679,"Next steps"],[3021,"Next steps"],[872,"Node name, unique within the roster and non-empty."],[833,"Node roster"],[4289,"Node.js"],[3311,"Node.js SDK"],[1968,"Non-raw headers are stored as strings, including numbers and booleans. Raw headers use `{\"data\":\"AQID\",\"iggy_header_encoding\":\"base64\"}`. Payload JSON is sent as SurrealQL values, so destination schema, numeric and other value constraints still apply."],[1518,"Nonempty message headers become `iggy_headers`: non-raw values are strings, and raw values use a base64 `data` object with `iggy_header_encoding = \"base64\"`."],[1025,"normal operation with quorum commits"],[2210,"Now implement `Source` for `RandomSource`. Each `poll()` waits for `interval`, builds a batch without exceeding `max_count`, and stages a candidate checkpoint. The runtime sends the batch, saves its optional checkpoint, and calls `on_batch_result()`. Only `Ack` commits the in-memory count. `Nack` discards the candidate. After restart, `new()` receives the last persisted checkpoint. Once the limit is reached, polls return empty messages and no new state."],[1912,"Now, let's implement the `Sink` trait for our `StdoutSink` struct."],[4382,"NUMA memory binding fails"],[1354,"NUMERIC and BIGNUMERIC values must fit the declared precision and scale without rounding. Strings preserve decimal precision when JSON floating-point values cannot. INTERVAL and RANGE columns are unsupported."],[875,"Numeric replica id for VSR consensus, `0`-based. Ids must be unique and strictly less than the roster size, which makes them contiguous from `0`. Rosters with more than 32 replicas are rejected at startup."],[442,"Numeric: `kind = 1`, `length = 4`, value is a u32."],[1478,"Object documents are enriched before indexing with `_iggy_offset`, `_iggy_stream`, `_iggy_topic`, `_iggy_partition`, and `_iggy_timestamp`. These fields replace matching payload fields. `_iggy_timestamp` is the connector's processing time in Unix epoch milliseconds, not the message's stored timestamp. `_iggy_headers` is added or replaced only when message headers are present."],[1126,"Observability"],[3453,"Of course, you can provide the message headers, custom binary serialization etc. as it's the same `IggyMessage` type as the one used in the low-level API (use `IggyMessage::builder()` to set headers or a custom ID). The producer will take care of the rest, including the retries, partitioning, batching etc."],[2856,"Offset management"],[2040,"Omitting the plugin `version` defaults to `\"v2\"` for backward compatibility. It is separate from the connector entry's numeric configuration version. Unknown plugin keys and unsupported versions are rejected. The query must contain `$cursor` outside comments. Keep `$limit` in the query so the connector can control its size. With stuck-batch detection enabled, V3 also requires `$offset` and an ascending `ORDER BY`. Startup checks use text matching, so they do not prove that a query implements the required ordering."],[4020,"On a host with disk-backed swap and enough RAM for the workload, try `vm.swappiness=10` and compare it with the existing value. It reduces the preference for swapping relative to reclaiming file cache. It does not disable swapping, and even `0` permits swapping under sufficient pressure. zram and zswap can warrant different values. See Linux swappiness."],[4393,"On a machine with more cores, the same problems can show up as `failed to create io_uring runtime for shard N` instead."],[2219,"On a side note, if you'd like to process the messages consumed from the Iggy stream instead, you can implement your own **Sink connector** too :)"],[1922,"On a side note, if you'd like to produce the messages to the Iggy stream instead, you can implement your own **Source connector** too :)"],[1918,"On an empty broker, create the configured stream and topic:"],[2216,"On an empty broker, create the destination using the credentials from the quick start:"],[3363,"On macOS the library is `libiggy_php.dylib`."],[1184,"On startup the runtime loads environment variables from the first `.env` file found in the working directory or its parents, or from the file pointed to by `IGGY_CONNECTORS_ENV_PATH`."],[2903,"On the high-level builders, `WithEncryptor(...)` is only valid when the builder creates its own client. For an external client, set `MessageEncryptor` on the configurator instead."],[2551,"On the other hand, Kafka is a **message streaming platform** that stores messages in an append-only log and serves consumer fetch requests. The consumers are responsible for reading the messages from the log and processing them. Multiple consumer groups can read the same retained log without a separate stored copy for each group, although their fetch requests still consume CPU and network resources. The consumers can read the messages from the beginning, or from the specific offset, thus you can replay the messages. The typical message streaming platform follows the so-called dumb pipes and smart endpoints pattern."],[474,"On top of the TLV walk, options enforce: string keys only (kind 2, valid UTF-8), no duplicate keys, at most 1024 entries, and at most 100000 bytes total."],[82,"On top of this, the Iggy user account used by the MCP server has its own granular permissions. For production use, create a dedicated user with the minimum required permissions."],[1159,"On Unix, the file backend writes and synchronizes a `.tmp` file, atomically renames it over the previous checkpoint, and synchronizes the parent directory. Newly created files have owner-only permissions (`0600`), since state may carry cursors or tokens."],[4192,"On-disk batch format"],[772,"Once installed, run `iggy` without arguments to print an overview of the available commands, or see the command reference."],[819,"Once the original session ends, the client cannot recover its receipt through a new login. Bounded client retention can also prevent recovery. The SDK guides describe each language's error types and limits. Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution."],[1536,"One `iggy-connectors` runtime process can host many connectors: the local config provider imports every connector TOML file under its `config_dir`, and each entry gets its own plugin instance and consume tasks in the same process. Each connector entry has exactly one `[plugin_config]` block and therefore one destination URL. To send to multiple destinations, add multiple HTTP sink entries with distinct keys and URLs (in the same runtime or separate ones). For fan-out from a single topic to multiple endpoints, point multiple sink entries at the same topic with different `consumer_group` names so each maintains its own offset."],[3988,"One `iggy-server` binary serves both the single-node and the clustered deployment. The loaded configuration decides which one you get. The server accepts these startup flags, plus `--help` and `--version`:"],[2691,"One kind of message"],[1212,"Only `*.toml` files directly inside the directory are loaded - hidden files (starting with `.`) and `Cargo.toml` are skipped."],[957,"Only the `replicated` / `replicated` combination avoids the disk prepare WAL entirely."],[4444,"Open Grafana"],[4464,"Open http://localhost:3001, sign in as `admin` with password `admin`, and find the \"Apache Iggy broker\" dashboard in the Iggy folder. Messages stored reads 60 and the rate panels show the burst, so send another and watch them move."],[1131,"OpenTelemetry"],[971,"Operate the cluster"],[490,"Operation discriminants"],[13,"Operation recovery"],[3704,"Opt-in trust of external token issuers for application-to-application authentication. With none configured, the listener accepts only self-issued tokens using the configured HMAC algorithm (`HS256`, `HS384`, or `HS512`)."],[2860,"Option catalog"],[438,"Option catalog flags"],[2424,"Optional **data encryption**: server-side at rest with AES-256-GCM-SIV, and client-side with AES-256-GCM"],[2417,"Optional **poll auto-commit**, with processing and failure semantics explained in concepts"],[877,"Optional client-facing address handed out in cluster metadata and leader redirects. Set it when `ip` is a private replica-network address unreachable by clients (Docker, Kubernetes, NAT). Accepts a literal IP or an RFC 1123 DNS hostname: ASCII letters, digits, `-` and `.`, no port, no trailing dot. When unset, clients receive `ip`."],[2261,"Optional keys: `schema_path`, `message_type`, `field_mappings` (rename JSON input fields, e.g. `field_mappings = { \"old\" = \"new\" }`), `descriptor_set` (pre-compiled descriptor bytes instead of a `.proto` file), and the inactive `schema_registry_url`."],[2265,"Optional keys: `schema_path`, `root_table_name`, `field_mappings`. JSON encoding uses a generic key/value representation, so its output is not a buffer for an arbitrary generated table type. FlatBuffer-to-JSON returns buffer metadata and base64 bytes, not decoded table fields."],[2269,"Optional keys: `schema_path`, `schema_json`, `field_mappings`. JSON encoding and Avro-to-JSON decoding require a schema. Input values must match its field types. The wire payload is one raw Avro datum, not an Avro object container file. Decoding rejects trailing bytes."],[2425,"Optional metadata support in the form of **message headers**"],[430,"Optional partition id"],[3037,"Optionally, provide an `ILoggerFactory` for diagnostics (defaults to `NullLoggerFactory.Instance`). The console logger requires the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console` package:"],[686,"options"],[1361,"Options"],[1394,"Options"],[3446,"Options are appended as query parameters. For TCP:"],[4312,"Options are key-value pairs sent with `CreateTopic`. Unknown keys are **rejected at the edge, never skipped**: a silently ignored knob would leave the client on server defaults with no way to find out. Streams and users have their own catalogs. See Stream options for stream keys and names. User updates accept `username` and `status`."],[435,"Options block"],[3508,"Options can be appended as query parameters:"],[2736,"Options typed as durations take human-readable values such as `5s`, `500ms`, or `1m`, unless a table below says the value is a plain number. Heartbeat and reconnection intervals must be greater than zero. The reconnection cooldown can be zero."],[3542,"Options, metadata and recovery"],[1052,"or track the repository directly with `iggy = { git = \"https://github.com/apache/iggy\" }`."],[3905,"Or use `docker compose up` directly from the repository root. That image **differs from the published one**: it sets the `0.0.0.0` address of all four listeners via `ENV` and keeps its data at `/local_data`. Those wildcards are baked into the image, so a plain `docker run` of it refuses to start until you pass `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` yourself. The repository's compose file publishes the ports to the host, so it declares `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost`."],[4137,"Or use the `--with-default-root-credentials` flag for development (sets root credentials to `iggy`/`iggy`)."],[2460,"Or when running with `docker run`:"],[3893,"Or when running with `docker run`:"],[2519,"Ordinary segment writes use per-topic count and byte thresholds (defaults: 1024 messages, 1 MiB). Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle work can flush earlier. The `MessagesWriter` uses **vectored I/O** in chunks of up to 1024 buffers. Partial writes can require more than one I/O submission."],[503,"Other `command` values (Prepare, PrepareOk, view-change traffic, and so on) are replica-to-replica consensus messages and never appear on a client connection. They are documented on the Server-to-server page."],[1477,"Other payload formats are skipped with a warning."],[3676,"Other removed sections, such as `[message_saver]`, `[system.state]`, `[system.backup]`, `[system.compression]`, and `[tcp.socket]`, must also be removed. Consult the embedded configuration for the deployed binary rather than relying on ignored legacy fields."],[1525,"Other valid headers work the same way: use `x-api-key = \"my-secret-api-key\"` for API-key schemes, or `Authorization = \"Basic dXNlcm5hbWU6cGFzc3dvcmQ=\"` (base64 of `username:password`) for basic auth. Multiple headers are combined per request. Configure header secrets in a protected connector TOML file. The local provider supports environment overrides for flat plugin fields, such as `URL`, but not nested headers or JSON objects. `HEADERS_AUTHORIZATION` creates an unused flat field, and a JSON object supplied through `HEADERS` is parsed as a string and fails plugin initialization."],[2092,"Output Format"],[1423,"Output Formats"],[1834,"Output Formats"],[1114,"Overriding the configuration via environment variables follows the same rules as the main Iggy server."],[1874,"Overview"],[2166,"Overview"],[2992,"Ownership rules:"],[673,"partition"],[2542,"Partition"],[437,"Partition context"],[4243,"Partition directories carry the same superblock mechanism for their own consensus state (replica identity, view), which is how a restarted replica proves it is itself rather than a blank impostor."],[4253,"Partition file operations run as bounded jobs. `sharding.partition_io_capacity` limits active jobs and results waiting for their owner. `partition_io_bytes_max` limits retained allocations. Exhausted capacity queues work instead of letting one slow disk operation block every partition on its shard."],[3511,"Partition IDs are **0-based**: the first partition of a topic is partition `0`. The consumer uses the default consumer identity and requests auto-commit. Its offset can advance before application processing finishes. Use explicit offset storage after successful processing when that distinction matters."],[3371,"Partition ids are 0-based: for a topic with one partition, use `0`."],[166,"Partition incarnation fields fence an old partition history. Consumer ownership generations separately fence old group members. PrimaryRebalance moves a partition primary under metadata coordination. PrepareProgress lets a slow replica report received, held and committed progress while it catches up."],[2853,"Partition reader"],[1010,"Partition requests use the current partition route and can be forwarded in one hop to its primary. The contacted node signs the route and request with a relay proof. The destination rejects invalid or stale proofs and authenticates the caller separately. Proofs live for ten seconds, so synchronize node clocks. An uncertain result is returned without treating it as proof that a fresh replay is safe. `ack=none` confirms dispatch only."],[811,"Partition requests use the partition route. It identifies the primary, view, partition incarnation and metadata progress. An incarnation identifies one history of a partition. Recreating a partition changes that identity. Consumer group operations also carry an ownership generation, so a former member cannot store progress for a new owner."],[321,"Partition routes"],[2919,"Partition selection is resolved **client-side** on both transports. Balanced partitioning round-robins over the topic's partitions, and key-based routing hashes the key with xxHash32 modulo the partition count, consistent with the Rust SDK for the same encoded key bytes and partition count. Strings use UTF-8, integers use little-endian bytes, and GUID keys use `Guid.ToByteArray()`."],[3819,"Partition storage and consensus tunables share this table. Unlike `[metadata]` (one plane on shard 0), a pipeline exists per partition, so raising per-partition budgets **multiplies resource use by the partition count**."],[2517,"Partition VSR replicates prepares. With `durability=persisted`, a multi-replica group requires recoverable prepare-WAL copies at the replication quorum before commit."],[2491,"Partition-to-shard assignment is **deterministic**: the packed `IggyNamespace` is hashed with `Murmur3`, and the upper 16 bits of the hash are taken modulo the shard count (the upper bits are used because Murmur3 has weak lower bits for small integer inputs)."],[431,"Partitioning"],[2849,"Partitioning strategies"],[3145,"Partitioning strategies"],[312,"Partitions"],[2846,"Partitions"],[16,"Partitions & Segments"],[2361,"Partitions distribute their primaries across cluster nodes. Topics can select their replication factor. Consumers can wait for messages and fetch several partitions in one request. Streams have separate encryption keys, and resource updates use set/reset patches."],[680,"pat"],[1854,"Path templates support `{stream}`, `{topic}`, `{partition}`, `{date}`, `{hour}`, and `{timestamp}`. The time-based variables derive from the first message timestamp in each buffer: date/hour use UTC, and `{timestamp}` is epoch milliseconds. File names embed the partition (zero-padded to 5 digits, to prevent cross-partition collisions) and the offset range (zero-padded to 20 digits), e.g. `application_logs/api_requests/2026-03-16/14/00000-00000000000000000000-00000000000000000999.jsonl` before adding the configured prefix."],[4140,"PATs provide programmatic access with optional expiry. Each user can have up to `max_tokens_per_user` (default 100) stored tokens, including expired tokens until the cleaner removes them. Tokens are **hashed before storage** and can be revoked at any time."],[2093,"Payload Column Extraction"],[1716,"Payload Format"],[1606,"Payload Formats"],[2032,"Payload Formats"],[1963,"Payload formatting operates after the runtime decoder and transforms:"],[1459,"Payload handling"],[1767,"Payload Shapes"],[308,"Payloads"],[1812,"Payloads and delivery"],[327,"Payloads below are the request **body** (the bytes after the 256-byte header). Types reference the shared encodings. Empty payload means the body has zero bytes."],[2716,"Pending-operation limits"],[1151,"Per-batch stage timings are recorded in the `iggy_connector_stage_duration_seconds` histogram independently of the `benchmark` flag, labeled with `connector_key`, `connector_type`, and `stage`. Both connector types record `decode`, `prepare`, and `total`. Sinks also record `ffi`, while sources record `iggy_send` and successful checkpoint writes record `state_save`. Finite buckets range from 50 microseconds to 5 seconds."],[878,"Per-client-network overrides for split networks, where one catch-all `advertised_address` would route in-VPC clients out through the public side:"],[1144,"Per-connector counters, labeled with `connector_key` and `connector_type` (`source` or `sink`):"],[876,"Per-node listener ports: `tcp`, `quic`, `http`, `websocket`, and `tcp_replica`. In cluster mode `ports` is the **single source of listener ports**: every enabled transport needs an explicit per-node port, otherwise the server refuses to start. `tcp` and `tcp_replica` are always required, even when client TCP is disabled. `tcp_replica` carries consensus traffic. Port `0` is rejected, and no two roster entries may claim the same `ip:port`."],[4304,"Per-topic retention, durability and segment layout options, set when the topic is created."],[3561,"Performance"],[1720,"Performance Considerations"],[2474,"Performance: Tokio vs Thread-per-Core"],[4127,"Permission matrix"],[22,"Permissions"],[679,"Permissions"],[4167,"Permissions are checked from top to bottom: global, then stream, then topic. A permission granted at a higher level covers everything below it:"],[931,"Persisted completion"],[1028,"persistent WAL recovery and snapshots"],[319,"Personal access tokens"],[762,"Personal access tokens"],[2842,"Personal access tokens"],[4125,"Personal Access Tokens (PAT)"],[3509,"Personal Access Tokens are also supported. Any credential without a colon is treated as a PAT. Paste the token exactly as the server returned it, with **no prefix**:"],[3360,"PHP 8.3 or newer with `php-config` (non-thread-safe builds only, no ZTS)"],[3348,"PHP SDK"],[3372,"PHP strings are passed as named identifiers, including strings that contain only digits. PHP integers are passed as numeric identifiers."],[687,"ping"],[3577,"Pinned consumer (`pc`): N consumers, each polling its own stream-topic with a single partition:"],[3576,"Pinned producer (`pp`): N producers, each sending to its own stream-topic with a single partition:"],[3578,"Pinned producer and consumer (`ppc`), in parallel:"],[968,"Place partition primaries"],[2455,"Please note that the images tagged as `latest` are based on the official, stable releases, while the `edge` ones are updated directly from latest version of the `master` branch."],[1119,"Please refer to the **Sink documentation** for the details about the configuration and the sample implementation."],[1122,"Please refer to the **Source documentation** for the details about the configuration and the sample implementation."],[1457,"Plugin config options"],[1679,"Plugin config options"],[1715,"Plugin config options"],[1766,"Plugin config options"],[1926,"Plugin config options"],[1976,"Plugin config options"],[2090,"Plugin config options"],[2153,"Plugin config options"],[4492,"Point the UI at your Iggy server's HTTP API via a `.env` file in the `web/` folder, e.g. `PUBLIC_IGGY_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000`."],[1631,"Point timestamps come from the Iggy message timestamp in microseconds, converted to `precision`. A zero timestamp uses the current wall-clock time. Millisecond and second precision discard finer timestamp digits."],[1974,"Poll documents from an Elasticsearch index into Iggy streams, incrementally when a timestamp field is configured."],[2028,"Poll InfluxDB on an interval and produce the resulting rows into an Iggy stream, on both InfluxDB V2 and V3."],[726,"Polled messages can be replayed later, for example into another topic:"],[2091,"Polling and Delivery"],[2544,"Polling messages"],[2133,"Polling stages its cursor and processed-row count until the runtime forwards the batch, saves its checkpoint and sends `Ack`. `Nack` discards that candidate so the next poll can read the rows again. A failure after forwarding can therefore duplicate messages. IDs are random UUIDs for each poll, so replayed rows do not retain a stable message ID."],[2855,"Polling strategies"],[3144,"Polling strategies"],[2797,"Polling strategies are selected by kind string: `offset`, `timestamp`, `first`, `last`, or `next`. Partitioning kinds for sending are `balanced`, `partition_id`, and `messages_key`."],[432,"Polling strategy"],[2854,"Polling with rented buffers"],[396,"PollMessagesDeferred is code 106. It extends the PollMessages body:"],[441,"Polymorphic identifier of a stream, topic, user, or consumer group:"],[1711,"Postgres Sink"],[2086,"Postgres Source"],[2144,"PostgreSQL must run with `wal_level = logical` and have `test_decoding` installed and allowed. The login needs access to the logical-decoding SQL functions. This backend uses ordinary SQL connections, not a replication-protocol connection. Any proxy must support its SQL and slot operations."],[885,"Prefer literal IPs over hostnames. SDKs differ in how they compare an advertised hostname against the address they dialed, and a mismatch costs a reconnect on every fresh connect."],[992,"Prefer the `IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET` environment variable over storing the secret in TOML. The value is masked in logs and never written into the runtime configuration snapshot."],[4347,"Prepare a fresh data directory, matching clients and updated configuration for Apache Iggy 0.10.0."],[164,"Prepare carries an operation and its body. PrepareOk acknowledges the required replication and persistence work. Commit broadcasts the commit point and a liveness token. CommitReply proves that peers still respond. A primary that cannot reach a quorum relinquishes leadership."],[4001,"Prepare Linux hosts for Iggy and measure the effects of memory, CPU, storage, and network tuning."],[3562,"Prepare the host and topic policies"],[98,"PrepareHeader"],[99,"PrepareOkHeader"],[102,"PrepareProgressHeader"],[952,"Prepares can be forwarded while local persistence is pending. Once enough replicas have met the required barrier, the operation can commit and the primary can apply it and reply. An acknowledged message can be recovered through the WAL before ordinary segment materialization and index updates finish. Recovery does not depend on first reaching a segment flush threshold."],[3141,"Prerequisites"],[3493,"Prerequisites"],[4348,"Preserve data before the upgrade"],[3124,"Prevent duplicate appends of a retried producer batch and understand the limits of that guarantee."],[2547,"Primaries, copies and checkpoints"],[439,"Primitives the command payloads are built from."],[1924,"Print messages from Iggy streams to standard output, for debugging and verifying a connector pipeline."],[741,"Print the server's option catalog for one scope: `topic`, `stream` or `user`, with key kinds, defaults, and bounds. All three scopes have catalogs. `stream update` and `topic update` accept `--set KEY=VALUE` and `--reset KEY`. A stream key is supplied at creation through `--stream-key-file`."],[1920,"Produce JSON messages to this topic, for example with the Random source, to see the sink output."],[2591,"Producer"],[2808,"Producer"],[3019,"Producer"],[3097,"Producer"],[3237,"Producer"],[3315,"Producer"],[3397,"Producer"],[3432,"Producer"],[3575,"Producer and consumer counts default to six. Pinned workloads also default to six streams. Set explicit counts to match the workload and available CPUs."],[2807,"Producer and consumer samples for the C# SDK, built on the high-level publisher and consumer."],[4110,"Producer avg latency: TCP 2.61ms vs WebSocket 3.43ms (+31%)"],[1283,"Programmatic Encoder and Decoder Configuration"],[1286,"Programmatic Usage"],[1129,"Prometheus metrics"],[2426,"Prometheus metrics and **OpenTelemetry** logs & traces in the connector runtime. Server export has known limitations."],[4457,"Prometheus starts scraping straight away, but the server turns it away because it has no token. You get the same answer from curl:"],[2258,"Promotes a nested field of a JSON envelope to the top-level payload. Given `{ \"table\": \"...\", \"operation\": \"...\", \"data\": { ... } }`, setting `field = \"data\"` replaces the whole payload with the contents of `data`:"],[2228,"proto_convert"],[1340,"Protocol Buffers provide efficient binary serialization compared to JSON"],[1280,"Protocol Buffers Support"],[406,"Protocol version"],[3963,"Provision the target Iggy account first. It must be active and cannot be root. Give it the permissions that mapped callers need. The service maps identities to this account. It does not create accounts or supply permissions."],[2023,"Public `GET /health` returns success only while every serving instance on the listener has a healthy poll path. A stalled path or a batch receiving negative acknowledgements for over 60 seconds makes it unavailable. An instance does not publish routes until its first poll starts."],[2943,"Publisher builder options"],[2847,"Publishing messages"],[2944,"Publishing with rented batches"],[3026,"Put a sample in `IggySample/Program.cs` and run `dotnet run --project IggySample`. For console logging, add its package to that project:"],[4288,"Python"],[3403,"Python 3.11 or newer is required. Install the published package in a virtual environment:"],[3393,"Python SDK"],[3157,"Python, C++, and PHP wrap the Rust SDK, so they inherit all transport protocols when using connection strings. Java and C# implement TCP and HTTP natively. Go and Node.js currently support TCP *only*."],[2281,"Q: Are there plans to support Kafka protocol in Iggy?"],[2287,"Q: Can I use Iggy with my existing tooling?"],[2285,"Q: Does Iggy support exactly-once delivery?"],[2286,"Q: How do I secure my Iggy deployment?"],[2284,"Q: How does consumer group rebalancing work?"],[2289,"Q: Is clustering/replication available?"],[2283,"Q: What are the system requirements?"],[2290,"Q: What examples are available?"],[2288,"Q: What happened to the Tokio-based runtime?"],[2280,"Q: What is the difference between Iggy and traditional message brokers like Kafka?"],[2282,"Q: What transport protocol should I use?"],[3272,"Queries can select resource or activity groups. Current-state queries must include parent groups, such as streams when topics are selected. Limits range from 1 through 1000. Time bounds are not valid with Latest or Current starts."],[2068,"Query and parsing failures leave the acknowledged cursor unchanged and are logged as poll errors. They do not increment the runtime's forwarding-error counter or change its running status. While its circuit breaker is open, the plugin waits and returns empty batches without checkpoints. On cooldown expiry it permits another query. A V3 response reporting that the database was not found is treated as an empty result, so create the database before starting the source."],[1945,"Query the stored records:"],[1718,"Querying the Data"],[4081,"QUIC"],[2760,"QUIC certificate validation is disabled by default. When enabled, it uses the platform verifier and the connection-string configuration's server name, `localhost`. For a different certificate name, use the Rust QUIC configuration builder's `with_server_name()` method. Values above QUIC's 62-bit limit for `max_concurrent_bidi_streams`, `receive_window`, or `max_idle_timeout` are rejected with `InvalidConfiguration`."],[2712,"QUIC options"],[4096,"QUIC runs the same binary protocol over UDP, on top of the quinn library, with TLS built in. It isn't quite as fast as TCP, but security comes out of the box (TLS is **mandatory**). Defaults:"],[3496,"Quick example"],[2788,"Quick start"],[3018,"Quick start"],[3096,"Quick start"],[3236,"Quick start"],[3314,"Quick start"],[3352,"Quick start"],[3396,"Quick start"],[3882,"Quick start"],[1067,"Quick Start"],[2675,"Quickstart"],[1763,"Quickwit Sink"],[928,"Quorum completion, persisted prepare history, and the failures each topic policy covers."],[1799,"RabbitMQ"],[2550,"RabbitMQ is a **message broker** with both queues and streams. Traditional queues deliver messages to consumers and remove them after acknowledgement, or on delivery when automatic acknowledgement is used, so acknowledged messages cannot be replayed from the queue. Multiple consumers can share a queue and divide its messages. Independent subscribers that each need a copy use separate queues bound to an exchange. Queues normally use FIFO (First In, First Out) order, but priorities, redelivery and competing consumers can affect the observed order. See RabbitMQ's queue semantics. The typical message broker follows the so-called smart pipes and dumb endpoints pattern."],[857,"Raise `heartbeat_timeout` on oversubscribed hosts where scheduling stalls fake primary death. Otherwise keep the defaults unless tests show a specific scheduling or network problem."],[4043,"Raise `somaxconn`, `tcp_max_syn_backlog`, or `netdev_max_backlog` only when backlog pressure or drops show the need. QUIC uses UDP, so TCP tuning does not configure its flow-control windows or UDP receive buffers."],[3730,"Raising `max_concurrent_bidi_streams` is a transport decision only: the server's accept loop takes one stream at a time and waits for its reply before accepting the next, so a higher cap does **not** make command handling concurrent."],[2150,"Random Source"],[1794,"Raw arrays, scalars and malformed JSON retain their original bytes in the raw wrapper. Avro and FlatBuffer payload variants use that raw path. Proto uses the text wrapper. Which variant arrives depends on the runtime decoder and transforms."],[2861,"Raw binary requests"],[3070,"Raw HTTP defaults differ from `PollOptions`: count is 10, wait is one second, and request timeout is 11 seconds. Minimum count is one and the byte budget is 1 MiB."],[1866,"Raw output concatenates payload bytes without delimiters and ignores both inclusion flags. To preserve the original payload bytes, use `schema = \"raw\"` and no transform that changes them. Decoding JSON before raw output can reserialize it."],[1475,"Raw payloads are parsed as JSON when possible, and wrapped as `{ \"data\": \"<base64>\", \"data_type\": \"raw\" }` when that fails."],[3440,"Raw requests"],[584,"Read 256 bytes, check `command`. `13` (`Eviction`) rejects the connection: map the reason and close that link. `8` (`Reply`) continues."],[2362,"Read about multi-leader partitions, deferred polling, stream options, idempotent producers, metadata changes, external authentication and the Kafka gateway."],[3146,"Read and recovery APIs"],[3438,"Read errors"],[4196,"Read integrity"],[2070,"Read MQTT subscriptions and acknowledge broker messages after Iggy accepts their batch."],[750,"Read or follow the metadata change log. The command needs root or `manage_servers` and prints one JSON page per line:"],[739,"Read or set the stored offset of a consumer for one partition. Subcommands: `get`, `set`. Both accept `-k, --kind <consumer|consumer-group>` (default: `consumer`)."],[3047,"Read several partitions"],[4259,"Read the catalog"],[585,"Read the remaining `size - 256` body bytes."],[1022,"Read the VSR paper used by the project for the protocol specification."],[374,"Read-only: a member asks for its current partition assignment and the group generation, so it can select partitions to poll client-side."],[3541,"Readers and consumers"],[2792,"Readers and recovery"],[822,"Readers group targets by primary and refresh routes after changes. They can stop a partition on a history change or an undecodable message. Inspect the reported error before seeking past it."],[89,"Readiness is sent after the HTTP listener starts or the stdio MCP session initializes. When systemd enables the watchdog, the server sends keep-alive notifications at half the configured watchdog interval. SIGINT, SIGTERM, or stdio client disconnect stops the server and sends a stopping notification."],[1786,"Readiness uses `GET /health/readyz`, retrying any failed probe. The index check uses `GET /api/v1/indexes/<index_id>`. Only a 404 triggers creation with `POST /api/v1/indexes`. If creation receives an error status, a successful index recheck allows startup to continue. A successful existence check does not validate the supplied mapping against the existing one. Requests retain any path prefix from `url`."],[2005,"Receive webhooks through authenticated HTTP paths and forward their payloads to Iggy."],[4019,"Recheck the actual process limits after restarting the service. PAM settings in `/etc/security/limits.conf` do not configure a system service. See systemd resource limits."],[805,"Reconnect and bind"],[3924,"Recovery and cost"],[932,"Recovery and failure limits"],[3954,"Recovery validates segment batches and partition identity. It can truncate an incomplete tail and rebuild a missing or torn sparse index. Interior damage, gaps, or intact records beyond damaged data can require refusal or peer repair instead of truncation. A single node cannot fetch a missing copy from another replica. See Storage Engine."],[1809,"Redshift"],[3941,"Reducing the thresholds changes batching and I/O frequency. It does not give `replicated` the stable-storage guarantee of `persisted`."],[3604,"Reference"],[826,"Reference for the [cluster] settings, consensus timing, and node roster"],[1607,"Reliability"],[3603,"Relocated configuration keys"],[4362,"Remove `consumer_group.session_timeout`. Session expiry derives from `heartbeat.interval` with a factor of 1.2. `consumer_group.heartbeat_interval` controls membership heartbeat pacing."],[3675,"Remove the old tables and environment variables after migrating their values. Topic policies cannot be configured through TOML or `IGGY_` overrides, and the former `enforce_fsync` topic option is also rejected. Both new durability policies independently default to `replicated`. Explicitly select `persisted` at topic creation where required. Neither can change after topic creation."],[4202,"Removed and relocated settings"],[2248,"Removes the listed top-level fields from the message payload:"],[118,"Repair and state transfer"],[108,"RepairRangeReplyHeader"],[1589,"Replace `[plugin_config]` in the complete connector file above and append the transform below. `tables = []` is intentional: the route field chooses the destination in this mode."],[2684,"Replace `src/main.rs` with:"],[4363,"Replace `user_id` with `username` in `http.jwt.trusted_issuers`. The mapped account must exist and cannot be root. HTTP clients and the Web UI must log in again because old self-issued access and refresh tokens are rejected."],[2012,"Replace both sample tokens. Keep the admin listener off the public load balancer. Terminate TLS in front of the public listener. Instances that share a listener must use the same address. Topic paths and endpoint IDs must be unique across those instances."],[1534,"Replace the corresponding blocks in the complete configuration above, and supply your own endpoint URLs and credentials."],[1861,"Replace the earlier `[plugin_config]` table with this local configuration. One message per file makes the example upload immediately:"],[3115,"Replace the quick-start client constructor with the following TCP options. Start the TLS server as shown on the examples page. The development CA path below is relative to `examples/go`:"],[985,"Replica authentication"],[1031,"replica authentication and optional TLS"],[990,"Replica authentication uses a cluster-wide pre-shared key (PSK) and a BLAKE3 keyed-MAC handshake. With `cluster.auth.enabled = true`, every replica peer must complete the authenticated handshake or be rejected:"],[984,"Replica authentication, shared-secret rotation, and TLS for replica traffic"],[167,"Replica control frames use an XxHash3-64 frame seal widened to u128. Prepare and RepairPrepare use a prepare identity hash, and a parent identity links the history. Metadata prepare bodies have their own seal. Message batches carry their batch checksums. These unkeyed hashes detect corruption, not a malicious peer. Authentication and TLS provide separate protection."],[4074,"Replica counters can lag a successful send until a prepare or commit broadcast updates them. Compare node-local observations with routing and view-change events before treating a transient difference as data loss. Repair-ring metrics report retained batch bytes and released-entry accounting, not total process memory."],[91,"Replica links, consensus commands, history fences and the header layouts used by Apache Iggy 0.10.0."],[1029,"replica rejoin with journal repair"],[987,"Replica TLS"],[989,"Replica-to-replica security has two layers, both in the `[cluster]` configuration: a pre-shared-key handshake that authenticates peers (`[cluster.auth]`) and optional TLS that encrypts the replica connection (`[cluster.tls]`)."],[123,"ReplicaHello, ReplicaChallenge and ReplicaFinish establish the connection. `[cluster.auth]` enables the shared-key BLAKE3 MAC. `[cluster.tls]` encrypts traffic with TLS 1.3 and ALPN `iggy-replica`. TLS requires replica authentication. See Cluster security for configuration and key rotation."],[119,"Replicas use dedicated TCP listeners and 256-byte headers. Client listeners reject replica control commands. The replica protocol has no mixed-version upgrade path. Run the same server version and `replica_connections_per_peer` on every node."],[930,"Replicated completion"],[4308,"Replication factor"],[1015,"Replication model"],[588,"Replies for all metadata operations, including their server-internal rewrites, and for the partition-plane consumer-offset writes (`StoreConsumerOffset`, `DeleteConsumerOffset`) are **result-framed**. Non-empty Register replies use the same framing. The body starts with a result section ahead of the typed payload."],[592,"Replies to non-replicated commands aren't result-framed: after `status = 0` the body is the response payload directly."],[492,"ReplyHeader"],[2824,"Repository examples require .NET 10 and reference the local SDK. Build and run from the same checkout as the server:"],[3960,"Request and response"],[491,"Request numbering"],[2470,"Request routing"],[489,"RequestHeader"],[297,"RequestPrepares asks for a retained operation range. RepairPrepare sends the original prepares. RepairDone terminates the stream, and RangeEvicted reports history that is no longer retained. Requests are bounded by `repair_chunk_max` and the peer queue capacity."],[107,"RequestPreparesHeader"],[2488,"Requests are routed between shards using **message passing** (via `crossfire` bounded mpsc channels), so partition state remains on its owning shard. The routing logic splits operations into two planes:"],[106,"RequestStartViewHeader"],[111,"RequestStateChunkHeader"],[109,"RequestStateTransferHeader"],[3350,"Requirements"],[3539,"Requirements and installation"],[162,"Reserved and unused ping/pong forms do not establish a session. Reply and Eviction are client-bound and rejected on replica ingress. A replica Request uses the internal routed-request header, including its group and metadata watermark."],[896,"Reset a replica"],[2056,"Resilience defaults are `timeout = \"10s\"`, `max_retries = 3`, `retry_delay = \"1s\"`, `retry_max_delay = \"5s\"`, `max_open_retries = 10`, `open_retry_max_delay = \"60s\"`, `circuit_breaker_threshold = 5`, and `circuit_breaker_cool_down = \"30s\"`. Attempt counts include the first attempt and are clamped to at least one. Query retries cover network errors, HTTP 429 and 5xx, with exponential backoff and jitter. An integer-seconds `Retry-After` on a 429 or any 5xx can lengthen the calculated delay past `retry_max_delay`, up to one hour, but never shortens it. HTTP-date and unparsable values fall back to the calculated delay. Other HTTP failures and malformed successful responses return poll errors. Invalid duration strings warn and fall back to `1s`. Zero durations are accepted. `verbose_logging` defaults to `false`."],[1633,"Resilience options include `timeout` (`30s`), `max_retries` (`3` total write attempts), `retry_delay` (`1s`), `retry_max_delay` (`5s`), `max_open_retries` (`10` total health-check attempts), `open_retry_max_delay` (`60s`), `circuit_breaker_threshold` (`5`), and `circuit_breaker_cool_down` (`30s`). Invalid duration strings warn and fall back to `1s`. See the upstream influxdb_sink README for the full list."],[817,"Resolution requires a matching final committed-result proof. A successful status or a nonzero log position alone is insufficient. A committed business rejection is also a definitive result. An uncertain operation remains retained when resolution times out."],[3209,"Resource creation accepts typed options. Updates use patches that change only supplied fields and can reset supported defaults. See Topic options and Stream options."],[21,"Resource options"],[3355,"Resource options and recovery"],[320,"Resource patches"],[693,"Resource update examples"],[2008,"Responses and health"],[493,"Result section"],[806,"Retain uncertain mutations"],[3380,"Retained mutation errors expose an operation ID. Use `resolveOperation()` on the same client to retry its retained identity, or `abandonOperation()` to accept the unknown result and release local state. A lost committing poll throws `FetchProgressUncertain` and blocks progress for the partition until abandonment."],[3127,"Retained producer history"],[4198,"Retention and cleanup"],[3265,"Retention and node freshness"],[4311,"Retention, durability, and segment layout are per-topic decisions, made when the topic is created. They used to be server-wide `[system.*]` keys, which gave every knob two homes. The server now refuses to boot on the old keys, and each topic carries its own values instead."],[1487,"Retry & Delivery Semantics"],[1278,"Retry utilities"],[4358,"Review API changes before recompiling applications. Resource creation uses typed options. Updates change only the fields supplied through set/reset patches. Stream and topic purge, flush unsaved buffer, and the old UpdateStream, UpdateTopic and UpdateUser wire commands are removed. The SDK update methods use UpdateResource internally."],[4351,"Review consumer checkpoints"],[4352,"Review encryption"],[4350,"Review server configuration"],[909,"Root credentials are **mandatory on the first boot of each replica** when clustering is enabled. Restarts recover the stored root user. The shared secret must be **at least 32 bytes**. See Security for how it's used and rotated."],[3618,"Root data path and runtime"],[4260,"Rotate the server encryption key"],[986,"Rotating the shared secret"],[970,"Route clients"],[803,"Route requests to partition primaries and handle reconnects without losing an uncertain operation's identity."],[2007,"Routes and authentication"],[1345,"Row mapping"],[1594,"Rows are converted to Arrow using the target table schema, written to Parquet, then appended in an Iceberg transaction per destination table. Partitioned tables split a batch by the default partition spec. Writers can roll over into multiple files, so there is no fixed one-file-per-batch guarantee for either partitioned or unpartitioned tables."],[880,"Rules and caveats:"],[2612,"Run `consumer_group.py` again and it prints every message from offset 0, because it asks for `PollingStrategy.First()`. That keeps the example repeatable. A long-running group consumer would leave the polling strategy at its default and keep `auto_commit` on, so it carries on from the group's stored offset instead."],[2611,"Run `consumer.py` again and it carries on from its stored offset, printing only the new messages rather than the first ten again."],[1108,"Run `docker compose -f examples/rust/src/sink-data-producer/docker-compose.yml up -d`, which will start the Quickwit server to be used by an example sink connector. At this point, you can access the Quickwit UI at http://localhost:7280 - check this dashboard again later on, after the `events` index will be created."],[1186,"Run `iggy-connectors --list-config-env-vars` to print the supported names and templates, then exit before dotenv and configuration loading, runtime creation, plugin loading, or other startup activity. The output is sorted and uses these placeholders:"],[34,"Run `iggy-mcp --list-config-env-vars` to print every supported MCP configuration variable and exit before loading dotenv or configuration files or creating the Tokio runtime. The output is sorted and contains names only. If a configuration field is an indexed vector, its template uses `<N>` for indices from `0` through `255`. Whole-value vectors are listed once without a placeholder."],[3657,"Run `iggy-server --list-config-env-vars` to print every supported server variable and exit before loading dotenv or configuration files or creating runtime state. The output is sorted, contains names only, and uses `<N>` for indexed vector entries. Most indexed fields accept `0` through `255`. `cluster.nodes.<N>.advertised_addresses.<N>` accepts `0` through `15` for its inner index. Whole-value vector fields are listed once without a placeholder."],[892,"Run a three-node cluster"],[4438,"Run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana in Docker Compose, and watch the broker on a dashboard."],[8,"Run and configure the Apache Iggy MCP server, including transport, Iggy credentials and TLS."],[3082,"Run both from the `examples/go` directory:"],[3219,"Run each example from the `examples/java` directory with its Gradle task. Run the producer before its consumer. The ordinary consumers keep their cursor in memory and start from offset `0` or the earliest retained message on each run. The multi-tenant consumer uses consumer groups and server-managed offsets."],[3498,"Run from your application crate to add the published SDK:"],[4415,"Run it"],[2685,"Run it with `cargo run`. Partition IDs are **0-based**, so the first partition of a topic is partition `0`."],[4298,"Run it with `cargo test`."],[4300,"Run it with `pytest -v`."],[2608,"Run it with `python consumer_group.py`. With the producer run twice, so 20 messages on the topic, it prints every offset from 0 to 19 and then stops:"],[2604,"Run it with `python consumer.py`, after the producer:"],[2601,"Run it with `python producer.py`:"],[4423,"Run it:"],[2305,"Run Kafka producers, consumers and admin clients against Iggy through the Kafka gateway."],[891,"Run one development node"],[4372,"Run producers and consumers against the new deployment before moving application traffic. Test resource creation, permissions, message payloads and headers, checkpoints, and reconnects. For a cluster, also test requests to different partition primaries and node failure. An accepted write and a processed message are different events. Select the required durability policies and commit behavior explicitly."],[2312,"Run several gateways"],[2816,"Run several instances of the consumer to see the group load-balance partitions between members."],[2622,"Run the applications"],[897,"Run the cluster test suites"],[4285,"Run the Iggy Docker image from your own test suite, with examples for Rust, Python and Node.js."],[2676,"Run the Iggy server and send and receive your first message with the Rust SDK, in one file."],[3875,"Run the Iggy server from the official Docker images, and deploy it with the Helm chart."],[2610,"Run the producer again and it finds the stream and topic already there, and adds another 10 messages at offsets 10 to 19."],[2645,"Run the producer first:"],[3110,"Run the server from the repository root with disposable development data and no root credential overrides:"],[3532,"Run the Swift producer and consumer from the Apache Iggy source release."],[1105,"Run these commands from the root of the same Iggy source checkout used for the server and plugins. This guide targets server 0.10.0."],[1754,"Run these queries with `docker exec -it iggy-postgres psql -U iggy -d iggy`, or your PostgreSQL client:"],[4013,"Run these read-only checks before changing the VM image or starting a benchmark:"],[3259,"Runnable examples covering the async client, message headers and envelopes, multi-tenant setups, and TLS live in examples/java. See the examples page for how to run them."],[3216,"Runnable examples live in the examples/java directory of the core repository as a standalone Gradle project. The project builds against the in-repo SDK (via an `includeBuild` substitution), so the examples always match the SDK source in the same checkout. Java 17 or newer is required. The included `gradlew` wrapper downloads the pinned Gradle version on first run."],[3074,"Runnable Go examples from the core repository, and how to start a server they can connect to."],[3212,"Runnable Java examples from the core repository, built with Gradle, and how to start a server for them."],[3557,"Running benchmarks"],[2594,"Running it again"],[4416,"Running it again"],[4446,"Running it again"],[4472,"Running modes"],[3880,"Running the CLI inside the container"],[3214,"Running the examples"],[3293,"Running the examples"],[3384,"Running the examples"],[3318,"Running the samples again"],[3976,"Running the server"],[1069,"Runtime"],[1161,"Runtime"],[4003,"Runtime access and process limits"],[1175,"Runtime is responsible for managing the lifecycle of the connectors and providing the necessary infrastructure for the connectors to run."],[2000,"Runtime state persistence requires no plugin configuration. To reset its position with the file backend, stop the runtime and delete the source's state file."],[1130,"Runtime stats"],[1139,"Runtime-level gauges:"],[4287,"Rust"],[3359,"Rust and Cargo"],[2781,"Rust binary transport strings accept `max_pending_operations` and `max_pending_operation_bytes`. Defaults are 256 operations and 67108864 bytes. Both must be positive. The byte budget includes frozen headers and bodies, so it must fit the largest mutation you need to send."],[3125,"Rust producer"],[4359,"Rust stream builders take stream and topic names, without caller-assigned IDs. Python requires Python 3.11 or later. The SDK guides describe the release APIs and examples."],[979,"Rust, Go, Java, C# and Node.js have native partition routing. Python, C++ and PHP use the Rust SDK through bindings. Swift has its own TCP client. Follow the individual SDK guides for supported recovery operations."],[1395,"S3 example"],[1828,"S3 Sink"],[1877,"Sample implementation"],[2169,"Sample implementation"],[4425,"Save as `order_service.py`."],[4421,"Save as `orders_producer.py`."],[4302,"Save it as `iggy.test.mjs` and run it with `node --test`."],[1369,"Save the configuration below in the runtime's connector directory. Set the endpoint, credentials, database, and table for your deployment."],[3915,"Save the excerpt as `my-values.yaml`, customize it for your environment, and deploy with:"],[3326,"Save the following snippets as `producer.mjs` and `consumer.mjs` in the project where you installed the SDK. Run `node producer.mjs` before `node consumer.mjs`. A consumer group sample follows them."],[2638,"Save the producer in `src/bin/producer.rs` and the consumer in `src/bin/consumer.rs`. The programs use resource names, so they do not depend on the IDs assigned to other streams."],[906,"Save this as `/tmp/iggy-vsr.toml`:"],[1328,"Save this as `connector-sdk-example/Cargo.toml`. The path dependency uses the SDK from the same checkout as the runtime:"],[2607,"Save this as `consumer_group.py`:"],[2603,"Save this as `consumer.py`:"],[2600,"Save this as `producer.py`:"],[1329,"Save this as `schemas/user.proto`:"],[1567,"Save this complete connector file in the runtime's connector directory:"],[1498,"Save this configuration in the runtime's connector directory:"],[1983,"Save this connector entry in the runtime's connector directory. Start the runtime from the checkout root so the plugin path resolves."],[1685,"Save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory and replace the MongoDB URI and destination names for your deployment. Startup parses the URI and pings the target database. `max_pool_size`, when set, overrides the URI's pool setting. Other connection options come from the MongoDB driver and URI."],[1655,"Save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory. Replace the URL and API key with your Meilisearch endpoint and credentials. The key needs permission to inspect the index, submit documents and read tasks, plus create the index when needed. Startup checks health, checks the index, and waits for index creation to succeed even when `wait_for_tasks = false`. Set `primary_key` to match an existing index. A mismatch only produces a startup warning. The connector does not change the index key, and document tasks can fail."],[2106,"Save this entry in the runtime's connector directory and start the runtime from the checkout root:"],[1204,"Save uses `PUT` by default, or `POST`/`PATCH`, with `Content-Type: application/octet-stream`. Atomically enforce `If-Match` for a known ETag or `If-None-Match: *` for a new checkpoint. Successful `200`, `201` or `204` responses must include the new strong `ETag`."],[393,"Scope 1 selects a topic with stream and topic identifiers. Scope 2 selects a stream. Scope 3 selects a user. Empty patches, duplicate keys and keys that are both set and reset are rejected. The catalog defines update and reset permissions for each key."],[334,"Scope: 1 = topic, 2 = stream, 3 = user. Returns the option catalog for the scope: keys, value kinds, defaults, and descriptions. See Options block."],[4128,"Scoped permissions"],[4050,"Scrape the server's Prometheus metrics: the token the endpoint needs, what it exports, and which metrics are worth alerting on."],[1274,"SDK"],[3782,"SDK clients maintain heartbeats when connected. The Java blocking client delegates to the async TCP implementation and also uses its heartbeat. Keep the client heartbeat interval below the server session expiry threshold. Call the SDK connection method before using a long-lived consumer, and close it through the SDK lifecycle API."],[3264,"SDK methods"],[1290,"SDK provides the commonly used structs and traits such as `Sink` and `Source`, along with the `sink_connector` and `source_connector` macros to be used when developing connectors."],[3210,"SDKs also expose metadata changes and raw requests to a selected replica. Recovery APIs differ. Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution or pending mutation limits. It has abandonment for uncertain committing reads. Read each language guide before implementing retries after an uncertain write."],[4330,"SDKs expose typed durability values in their topic creation options. In Rust, set `TopicCreateOptions::durability` and `TopicCreateOptions::consumer_offset_durability` to `Durability::Replicated` or `Durability::Persisted`. The HTTP API takes `\"durability\"` and `\"consumer_offset_durability\"` as string values in the create body's `options` map."],[4119,"SDKs maintain heartbeats after connection. The Java blocking client delegates to the async TCP client and uses its heartbeat. Reconnect binds a retained session before creating a new one. See Client failover."],[1998,"Search and JSON-decoding failures, `timed_out: true`, and a nonzero `_shards.failed` count are returned as poll errors without advancing the watermark. The next polling cycle retries from the previous watermark. Poll errors are logged by the SDK. They do not increment the runtime's forwarding-error counter or change the runtime connector status. There is no per-query retry loop or configured HTTP request timeout. The source stages a candidate watermark and applies it only after runtime `Ack`. `Nack` keeps the previous watermark so the next search can fetch the rejected records again. Fetched-document and byte counters include these repeated attempts. This does not remove the timestamp limitations above or provide exactly-once delivery."],[3646,"Secret configuration includes: `http.jwt.encoding_secret`, `http.jwt.decoding_secret`, `encryption.key`, `encryption.previous_keys`, `external_auth.url`, `cluster.auth.shared_secret`, and `cluster.auth.previous_shared_secret`. Prefer setting them through their environment variables (`IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET`, `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET`, and so on) instead of storing them in a file. Secret values are masked in logs and **never serialized** into `runtime/current_config.toml`."],[3600,"Secrets"],[4121,"Security"],[1425,"Security Notes"],[1057,"Security: replica authentication, key rotation, and TLS"],[6,"See **MCP Server** for how to run and configure it, the full list of tools, and client setup."],[4283,"See Configuration for environment variables and Security for transport protection."],[3510,"See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."],[2682,"See Docker & Helm for what each option does."],[4345,"See Durability for single-node failure behavior and Cluster Durability for the quorum and WAL guarantees."],[865,"See Multi-leader partitions for primary placement and the availability consequences of each factor."],[3767,"See Stream options for the rotation procedure. Environment overrides include `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_PREVIOUS_KEYS` and `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_RETIRED_KEK_FINGERPRINTS`, each accepting a JSON array."],[1093,"See the **Transforms documentation** for the configuration of each transform."],[3011,"See the C# examples for complete producer and consumer programs."],[3346,"See the foreign/node README for the full API walkthrough."],[674,"segment"],[2543,"Segment"],[2863,"Segment management"],[4191,"Segmented log"],[313,"Segments"],[4209,"Segments store **batch records**, not individual messages. The record is byte-identical to the wire encoding: the one layout a `SendMessages` body, the replicated prepare, the persisted segment record, and the poll reply all share. There is **no other message encoding**."],[3262,"Select a starting point"],[969,"Select copies per topic"],[4257,"Select encryption at creation"],[2085,"Select the destination topic's durability before creation. An Iggy acknowledgement under `replicated` does not imply stable-storage persistence. The MQTT source reference includes broker setup and recovery examples."],[3919,"Select the policy when creating the topic"],[475,"Semantics:"],[711,"Send and poll messages. Subcommands: `send` and `poll`. The flush command is removed."],[2678,"Send and receive a message"],[3434,"Send confirmations"],[1800,"Send Iggy messages to RabbitMQ with the connector runtime."],[1810,"Send Iggy messages to Redshift with the connector runtime."],[1764,"Send messages from Iggy streams to a Quickwit index over HTTP, creating the index when it does not exist."],[4443,"Send some messages"],[2957,"Sending and receiving over **rented, pooled buffers** to avoid separate payload copies on TCP"],[2848,"Sending messages"],[1909,"serde"],[2204,"serde"],[2689,"Serialising messages"],[12,"Server"],[409,"Server 0.10.0 uses binary protocol 0.12.0. Clients must use a compatible release."],[3159,"Server 0.10.0 uses binary protocol 0.12.0. Upgrade clients and server together. There is no fallback to the 0.9 protocol. The table lists the component versions released with each server version."],[4357,"Server 0.10.0 uses protocol 0.12.0. The server rejects incompatible binary clients at login. Use the versions in Server compatibility. The 0.9 documentation remains available for the old deployment."],[3051,"Server capacity"],[3094,"Server compatibility"],[3140,"Server compatibility"],[3232,"Server compatibility"],[4377,"Server exits with an io_uring error in a container"],[3783,"Server OTLP export is unavailable in this revision: `grpc` prevents startup, and `http` does not deliver logs or traces. Leave `enabled = false`."],[559,"Server to client. 256 bytes, followed by `size - 256` bytes of body."],[593,"Server to client. 256 bytes, never a body. An eviction has no per-request correlation. Close the affected link. A stale-client eviction can occur while the logical session is still live, so try binding the retained session before replacing it. A terminal bind refusal requires a new registration."],[2906,"Server-assigned ids are **0-based**: the first stream, topic, partition, or consumer group gets id 0. The root user has id 0 as well."],[2411,"Server-side storage of **consumer offsets**"],[90,"Server-to-server"],[638,"Server-to-server: prepare/ack replication, view changes, and state transfer."],[3137,"Session retry protection and producer idempotence solve different problems. A client can retain an uncertain request and resolve it using its original identity. Ending that session can remove receipt access. An idempotent producer adds batch identity at the partition level, but does not turn an uncertain application operation into a confirmed success."],[2934,"Session-control codes (login, logout, and the like) are rejected with an invalid-command error, and the operation is TCP-only: HTTP clients throw `FeatureUnavailableException`."],[557,"Session-qualified retry protection is retained by the metadata and partition state machines. A full partition table rejects new writers instead of evicting live protection. Ordered retirement releases ended sessions. Receipt recovery is bounded by the session lifetime and retained state. It is not an unlimited exactly-once guarantee."],[83,"Set `command` to the absolute path of the built `iggy-mcp` executable. This Claude Desktop example uses the broker and development credentials from the getting started guide:"],[4278,"Set `encryption.key` to the new key and add the old key to `encryption.previous_keys` on every node. Restart nodes with the updated configuration. Retain the old key during this phase."],[1599,"Set `field` to the envelope key that holds the row data. See the transforms documentation and your source connector's documentation for details on the envelope shape."],[4181,"Set `IGGY_ENCRYPTION_KEY` to the standard base64 encoding of 32 random bytes before starting this configuration. Enabling encryption does not change existing unencrypted streams. Follow Stream options to create keyed streams and rotate the server key without re-encrypting messages."],[3133,"Set `IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS=true` on the Kafka gateway to enable its idempotent producer path. The bridge must also be enabled. The default is false. Without this switch, a Kafka producer can obtain an ID but its retries do not receive the batch deduplication guarantee."],[4361,"Set `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` before first boot. The server no longer generates a root password. Use `--with-default-root-credentials` only for development."],[4408,"Set `IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all` when CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding fits the deployment. Inspect the host topology and allowed CPU set before changing production placement. See Linux tuning."],[2897,"Set `IggyClientConfigurator.MessageEncryptor` to encrypt message payloads and user headers client-side. They are encrypted on send and decrypted on poll. Routing information and message metadata remain visible to the server. The built-in `AesMessageEncryptor` uses AES-GCM (confidentiality + authenticity) and takes a 16-, 24-, or 32-byte key for AES-128/192/256:"],[1519,"Set `include_metadata = false` to send the payload without wrapping (useful when the downstream service expects bare JSON, e.g. Slack webhooks)."],[3754,"Set `path` before any TOML table header. `[runtime] path = \"runtime\"` and `[logging] path = \"logs\"` are relative to it. Data uses the fixed layout `{path}/streams/{stream_id}/topics/{topic_id}/partitions/{partition_id}`. Stream, topic, and partition path overrides are rejected."],[31,"Set `telemetry.enabled = true` to export to a running collector. For HTTP export, set `transport = \"http\"` and use complete signal URLs: `http://localhost:4318/v1/logs` for logs and `http://localhost:4318/v1/traces` for traces. The MCP server does not append those paths."],[1227,"Set active sink version: `PUT {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/active`"],[1228,"Set active source version: `PUT {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/active`"],[4032,"Set allocator variables in the launching environment or the service's `Environment=` entries before process startup. The server can allocate before loading its `.env` file. Verbose allocator output is useful for checking the experiment, then turn it off for timed runs."],[1350,"Set either `credentials_path` or `credentials_json`, never both. Without either, the connector uses Application Default Credentials. The identity needs table schema read and row append permissions, `bigquery.tables.get` and `bigquery.tables.updateData`."],[1180,"Set the broker credentials and connector configuration directory before starting the runtime. The embedded default has an empty connector directory and cannot start unchanged. For a complete setup, follow the quick start."],[1761,"Set the pool limit with `max_connections`. It does not report pool usage"],[2249,"Sets fields to static or computed values, optionally guarded by a condition:"],[2160,"Setting `max_count = 0` stops production immediately (the connector produces zero messages). To generate messages without a limit, leave the key out."],[1674,"Setting `wait_for_tasks = false` returns after submission, before Meilisearch confirms indexing. Later task failures are neither observed nor retried by the connector. Index-creation tasks are still awaited during startup. Closing the connector does not wait for outstanding document tasks."],[4306,"Setting options"],[2619,"Setting up the project"],[448,"Several commands take an optional partition id encoded as 5 fixed bytes:"],[3650,"Several sections validate relationships between keys (QUIC windows, sharding shutdown budgets, metadata journal sizing, partition transfer floors). A violation aborts boot with an error naming the keys. The constraints are listed with their sections below."],[2484,"Shard 0 has a special role: it binds **every listener** - the replica plane and all client transports (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTP). Connections are then spread across shards at accept time:"],[2469,"Shard roles and connection distribution"],[3517,"Share an existing client"],[425,"Shared encodings"],[635,"Shared encodings: primitives the payloads are built from."],[2508,"Shared metadata (streams, topics, consumer groups) uses a **left-right** concurrent data structure. Shard 0 holds the single write handle and is the only shard that commits metadata mutations, while every shard holds a read handle. This provides:"],[764,"Shell completions"],[2650,"Short definitions of the terms used across the Apache Iggy docs, with links to where each one is explained."],[743,"Show info about the current connection: client ID, user ID, client address as seen by the server, protocol. Supported on TCP, QUIC and WebSocket. HTTP returns `FeatureUnavailable`."],[744,"Show server statistics: streams, topics, partitions, messages, plus host OS details. `-o, --output <table|list|json|toml>` selects the format (default: `table`)."],[1070,"Sink"],[1872,"Sink"],[1878,"Sink connectors are responsible for writing data from Iggy streams to external systems or destinations. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources and destinations, enabling data flow between Iggy and external systems."],[1267,"Sink stream entries use `max_wait` and `min_count`. The default wait is one second and the minimum count is one. `batch_length` limits returned messages. A wait must be positive, and `min_count` must be from one through the batch length. The server can cap a requested wait."],[1118,"Sinks are responsible for consuming the messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s) and sending them further to the specified destination. For example, the Quickwit sink connector is responsible for sending the messages to the Quickwit indexer."],[1297,"Sinks can keep in-memory state, but the runtime does not persist it. Their consumer-group offsets are tracked by Iggy. See the **Observability page** for checkpoint storage and the **sink guide** for offset handling."],[4017,"Size file-descriptor limits for connections, partitions, open segment files, and runtime overhead. `LimitNOFILE=65536` is an example starting budget, not an Iggy requirement. Increase it if measurements justify more. A systemd service can use the following drop-in:"],[2694,"Size limits"],[1870,"Size your rotation thresholds accordingly: smaller files bound the loss window, larger files upload more efficiently."],[690,"snapshot"],[2862,"Snapshots"],[4042,"Socket-buffer requirements depend on bandwidth and round-trip time. For example, 10 Gbit/s across 10 ms has a bandwidth-delay product of about 12.5 MB. If the connection is window-limited, inspect `net.core.rmem_max`, `net.core.wmem_max`, `net.ipv4.tcp_rmem`, and `net.ipv4.tcp_wmem`. Larger ceilings permit larger buffers, but do not force the application to use them or guarantee higher throughput."],[3030,"Some operations are **TCP-only** and throw `FeatureUnavailableException` on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, `GetMeAsync`, `DeleteSegmentsAsync`, and `SendBinaryRequestAsync`."],[1071,"Source"],[2164,"Source"],[1556,"Source Compatibility"],[2170,"Source connectors are responsible for ingesting data from external sources into Apache Iggy. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources, such as databases, message queues, or file systems."],[1295,"Source connectors can persist their position between restarts. Each batch of `ProducedMessages` may carry an optional `ConnectorState`. After processing and sending the batch to Iggy, the runtime saves that state through the configured file or HTTP backend before acknowledging the batch. The last saved state is passed to `new()` on the next startup. `ConnectorState` wraps raw bytes and provides `serialize`/`deserialize` helpers backed by MessagePack."],[1199,"Source plugins supply optional checkpoint bytes. The default `file` backend writes them to `{state.path}/source_{key}.state` using a temporary file, file synchronization and atomic rename. On Unix, it also synchronizes the parent directory. The runtime stores these bytes unchanged. The SDK provides MessagePack serialization helpers."],[1121,"Sources produce messages to an Iggy stream and topic. Configure one `[[streams]]` entry per source instance: the runtime currently retains only the last configured producer. For example, the Random source connector generates random messages that are then sent to the configured stream and topic."],[1597,"Sources that wrap row data in an envelope need a transform when the table schema describes the inner row. Otherwise, the Arrow JSON reader maps envelope keys to table columns, producing nulls or schema errors."],[1296,"Sources with cursors or destructive operations must stage changes during `poll()` and apply them on `SourceBatchResult::Ack`. `Nack` discards staged progress so the batch can be polled again. The SDK allows one batch in flight and stops polling if `on_batch_result()` returns an error. Its default no-op is suitable only for sources without staged work."],[893,"Spanning multiple hosts"],[4474,"Standalone mode"],[3248,"Start a development server using the example setup, with `iggy`/`iggy` credentials, then run the producer before the consumer. Bootstrap credentials do not replace recovered credentials."],[1859,"Start a local MinIO server:"],[1942,"Start a local SurrealDB instance with the credentials used below. This example stores SurrealDB data in memory:"],[27,"Start an Iggy 0.10.0 or edge broker using the getting started guide. From the matching `apache/iggy` checkout, run:"],[4489,"Start an Iggy server with HTTP enabled, as shown in Getting started, then run the Web UI as a separate container:"],[910,"Start each replica in a separate terminal. Use a different data path for every process:"],[4441,"Start it"],[1197,"Start it from the repository root:"],[1113,"Start the connector runtime `cargo run --bin iggy-connectors --release` in the terminal configured in step 3. The Quickwit sink indexes the produced records in the `events` index. At the same time, you should see the new messages being added to the `example_stream` stream and `example_topic` topic by the Random source connector - you can start the Iggy Web UI to browse the data. The messages will have applied the basic fields transformations."],[3547,"Start the development server with `iggy`/`iggy` credentials. Run these calls from an async Swift context:"],[2315,"Start the Iggy server from the release source with development credentials. Then run the gateway from the same repository root:"],[1110,"Start the Iggy server in a separate terminal with credentials matching the sample connector configuration:"],[2041,"Start the runtime as described in the source guide. After a polling cycle, read the produced records:"],[1985,"Start the runtime as described in the source guide. After the configured 30-second polling delay, read the produced message:"],[2677,"Start the server"],[4412,"Start the server"],[2197,"Start with the imports, then define the internal state and the public source connector along with its configuration."],[1903,"Start with the imports, then define the state, connector and configuration."],[2618,"Starting the Iggy server"],[2590,"Starting the server"],[3075,"Starting the server"],[3213,"Starting the server"],[1858,"Startup writes an empty object at the bucket-root key `.iggy-sink-probe`, then attempts to delete it. The probe ignores `prefix`, needs `PutObject` on that key, and rejects non-success HTTP statuses. `ListBucket` is not required. Failed probe cleanup does not prevent startup. Reserve this key: an existing object at that name is overwritten, and deleted if the credentials permit cleanup."],[165,"StartViewChange, DoViewChange and StartView establish a new view. DoViewChange includes an untrusted-log flag so a replica without proven history cannot vote as an authoritative empty log. RequestStartView probes for the current view after recovery."],[103,"StartViewChangeHeader"],[105,"StartViewHeader"],[1277,"State"],[1978,"State"],[2033,"State and Failures"],[1132,"State files"],[1166,"State storage"],[298,"State transfer handles larger gaps. RequestStateTransfer obtains a manifest, then RequestStateChunk and StateChunk transfer artifacts. The receiver checks each complete artifact against its manifest length and checksum before using it. The manifest has magic `ISM1`, a u32 entry count, a u8 entry length and a trailing XxHash3-64 checksum. Entries contain kind, frontier, length and checksum."],[112,"StateChunkHeader"],[110,"StateTransferTargetHeader"],[1552,"Static Routing"],[689,"stats"],[1018,"Status"],[1923,"Stdout Sink"],[3466,"Stop all senders, then call `producer.shutdown().await` to drain background queues and wait for writes and error callbacks. Dropping the producer without shutdown can lose buffered messages. Shutdown does not turn failed writes into successful ones. The default error callback logs and drops failed sends."],[4188,"Storage Engine"],[2477,"Storage structure"],[1712,"Store messages from Iggy topics in a PostgreSQL table, with optional table creation and configurable payload storage."],[4369,"Store offsets after processing, or select an explicit automatic commit policy. Without stored offsets, a restarted consumer can read retained messages again. Committing on read can advance the offset before application processing. See Deferred polling."],[1680,"Stored Documents"],[1936,"Stored Shape"],[3560,"Storing and comparing results"],[671,"stream"],[2540,"Stream"],[2913,"Stream and topic purge are removed. `DeleteTopicAsync` deletes the topic and its data. There is no on-demand flush command. Select both durability policies when creating the topic."],[3513,"Stream Builder"],[4256,"Stream names, per-stream encryption keys and server key rotation."],[4255,"Stream Options"],[2087,"Stream rows from PostgreSQL into Iggy topics by incremental table polling or change data capture over logical replication."],[4169,"Stream-scoped `poll_messages` on stream 1 lets the user poll from **all topics** in stream 1, but not from other streams."],[4206,"Stream, topic, and partition ids are numeric and **0-based**. Each partition directory holds pairs of `.log` and `.index` files. The filename is the segment's start offset, zero-padded to 20 digits. Recovery can create an empty active segment at a reserved offset beyond the last stored message. Next to the segments, every partition keeps its own superblock pair (replica identity and consensus state for that partition) and an `offsets/` tree for consumer offset storage. Multi-replica partitions also keep a prepare WAL when either topic durability policy is `persisted`."],[1855,"Stream/topic substitutions preserve ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`, replacing other characters with `_`. Distinct names can therefore produce the same key. Keep stream/topic separation in the template, use names that remain distinct after replacement, and avoid sharing the same output paths across independent producers. A repeated object key overwrites the current object, subject to bucket versioning. The offset range alone does not provide global deduplication. Extensions are `.jsonl`, `.json` and `.bin` for the three formats."],[14,"Streams"],[310,"Streams"],[2843,"Streams"],[2905,"Streams, topics, users, and consumer groups are referenced by an `Identifier`, which can be either numeric or a name:"],[4500,"String decoder (UTF-8 text)"],[443,"String: `kind = 2`, `length = 1..255`, value is UTF-8 bytes."],[2510,"Strongly consistent writes serialized through shard 0"],[3869,"Sub-sections:"],[697,"Subcommands have single-letter aliases too: `c` (create), `d` (delete), `g` (get), `l` (list), `u` (update). So `iggy s l` is `iggy stream list`."],[949,"Success therefore does not prove that the operation reached stable storage on any replica. Replication protects against failures while sufficient peers retain the history and can form the quorums needed for recovery and progress. For example, a healthy three-replica group can continue after one replica fails."],[589,"Success: `count = 0`, and the typed response payload (if any) follows the 4 count bytes."],[2066,"Successful polls return a versioned MessagePack checkpoint, including empty polls. After forwarding messages, the runtime saves it and sends `Ack`. Only then does the plugin apply its candidate cursor and row count. `Nack` discards that candidate so the next poll can fetch the records again. A failure after forwarding but before acknowledgement can produce duplicates."],[831,"Superblock failure handling"],[4364,"Supply a valid `logging.level`. An invalid filter stops startup. Log files rotate by size, with retention and total-size cleanup."],[2404,"Support for **multiple transport protocols** (QUIC, TCP, WebSocket, HTTP)"],[2403,"Support for multiple streams, topics and partitions"],[1285,"Supported Features"],[2383,"Supported languages SDK"],[2308,"Supported operations"],[3015,"Supported protocols"],[1185,"Supported scalar fields and indexed list entries can be overridden by environment variables using the `IGGY_CONNECTORS_<SECTION>_<KEY>` convention (nested keys joined by underscores), e.g. `IGGY_CONNECTORS_IGGY_USERNAME` or `IGGY_CONNECTORS_HTTP_ADDRESS`."],[1116,"Supported scalar fields and indexed list entries can be overridden with the `IGGY_CONNECTORS_` prefix, followed by section and key names joined by underscores, e.g. `IGGY_CONNECTORS_IGGY_USERNAME`. Header and URL-template maps are configured in TOML. Local connector settings use a per-connector prefix, described in the runtime configuration guide."],[1065,"Supported Schemas"],[1932,"SurrealDB Sink"],[1971,"SurrealQL `INSERT IGNORE` preserves existing records with the same ID, including their old payloads. With the SurrealDB 3.1.4 instance above, `IGNORE` also silently skips unique-index conflicts and field-assertion failures while accepting valid records from the same chunk. These skips return a successful statement and are included in processed counters. This protects repeated writes of the same identities. It does not ensure that a failed batch will be delivered. There is no end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[4004,"Swapping and writeback"],[2784,"Swift accepts the TCP option names, but checks and then ignores pending mutation limits because it has no retained mutation pool. Its reconnect interval is a full pause between endpoint rounds. Node.js accepts `reestablish_after` for format compatibility without applying a Node equivalent. Do not assume one SDK's parser and timing behavior across all languages."],[3554,"Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution or mutation lanes. It does track uncertain committing reads. `fetchProgressUncertain` carries an operation ID, and affected progress calls fail with `partitionLaneBlocked` until `abandonOperation` releases it. Abandonment does not undo a committed offset. A `ReadError` can stop a partition until `seek` selects a new position."],[3537,"Swift SDK"],[309,"System"],[2859,"System operations"],[3975,"System requirements"],[25,"Systemd integration"],[1593,"Table name: `users`"],[1717,"Table Schema"],[400,"Targets have unique partition IDs and use offset or next strategies. The request applies its count, minimum count and byte limit across all targets. Each target also has a byte limit no greater than the request limit. A fetch requires the one-second reply margin even when its wait is zero."],[4080,"TCP"],[2904,"TCP clients publish connection state changes, useful for reacting to reconnects. HTTP subscription methods are no-ops:"],[2925,"TCP groups reads by primary. HTTP uses one poll per partition and supports standalone consumers only. A group reader joins and follows its assignment. `ReadException` reports the partition and any continuation that skips an undecodable record. A stopped partition needs an explicit seek before reading resumes."],[3716,"TCP is the highest-throughput transport. These two keys, plus the TLS block below, are the entire section."],[2711,"TCP options"],[4095,"TCP provides the highest performance as it works directly with binary data without the overhead of HTTP or TLS handshakes (unless TLS is enabled). The full set of keys:"],[3256,"TCP retains uncertain mutation identities. Keep the client and use `resolveOperation` or `abandonOperation` when an exception reports a retained operation. An uncertain committing poll blocks progress for its partition until abandonment. HTTP supports standalone consumers and deferred reads, but not consumer group membership."],[3031,"TCP uses the current binary protocol and routes partition requests to their primaries. The client discovers the cluster roster and follows metadata leadership separately. Balanced and message-key partitioning are resolved on the client."],[2735,"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials during `connect()`. HTTP requires an explicit login, as described below."],[3507,"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials on `connect()`. HTTP requires an explicit login and supports ordinary consumers only."],[4284,"Testing with Iggy"],[1638,"Text carriage returns and newlines become the literal sequences `\\r` and `\\n` in the stored string. Use base64 if those original bytes must round-trip. Tabs in measurement names or tag values are rejected. Quoted text fields allow tabs."],[1476,"Text payloads are wrapped as `{ \"text\": \"...\", \"data_type\": \"text\" }`."],[2560,"Thanks to having multiple partitions, we can achieve the horizontal scalability between many independent consumers, since each consumer can read the messages from the different partitions. This can be achieved by using more advanced concepts such as consumer groups."],[80,"The `[permissions]` section provides a first layer of access control, checked before any request is forwarded to the Iggy server. This lets you restrict the MCP server to read-only operations, for example:"],[1155,"The `[telemetry]` section configures OTLP export of logs and traces. Set `enabled = true` to export to a running collector:"],[4108,"The `[websocket]` section exposes the frame-tuning knobs (`read_buffer_size`, `write_buffer_size`, `max_write_buffer_size`, `max_message_size`, `max_frame_size`, `accept_unmasked_frames`). See Configuration. TLS for WebSocket has its own `[websocket.tls]` section."],[3455,"The `background` mode buffers messages and sends them from shard workers, trading latency for throughput and backpressure control. `BackgroundConfig::builder()` exposes:"],[1509,"The `batch_mode` option controls how messages from one poll cycle are delivered to the endpoint."],[124,"The `command` byte is at offset 60. This is the complete registry, including client commands:"],[4245,"The `compression_algorithm` topic option accepts `none` (default) and `gzip`, but it is a **placeholder today**: the value is persisted and reported back, but no message compression is applied. Payload encryption still applies when enabled. To compress today, do it client-side and tag messages via user headers - see the message headers examples in the Iggy repo."],[480,"The `compression_algorithm` value used by topic options:"],[2250,"The `condition` values are `always` (the default when omitted - sets the field whether or not it exists), `key_exists` (update only when the field is already present), and `key_not_exists` (add only when the field is absent)."],[2236,"The `enabled` key is required. A transform section without it fails to load, and a transform with `enabled = false` is skipped."],[789,"The `IGGY_USERNAME` and `IGGY_PASSWORD` environment variables (both must be set)."],[3906,"The `iggy` CLI is on `PATH` in the official image. With the compose example above (container `iggy`, password `Secret123`):"],[3449,"The `IggyClient` internally wraps its transport in an `IggyRwLock`, but the client itself does not implement `Clone`. Share an `Arc<IggyClient>` across tasks. Cloning the `Arc` keeps the same client and connection. Typically, it's a good idea to create a separate connection for producing and consuming, but it's not a requirement."],[2493,"The `IggyNamespace` packs stream_id (20 bits), topic_id (12 bits), and partition_id (20 bits) into a single `u64` for efficient hashing and routing. This gives maximums of 1,048,576 streams, 4096 topics per stream, and 1,000,000 partitions per topic."],[1627,"The `include_checksum`, `include_origin_timestamp`, `include_stream_tag`, `include_topic_tag`, and `include_partition_tag` flags also default to `true`."],[1958,"The `include_headers`, `include_checksum`, and `include_origin_timestamp` flags also default to `true` and work independently of `include_metadata`. Retry and timeout options are `query_timeout` (`30s` per HTTP request), `max_retries` (`3` total write attempts, with `0` raised to `1`), `retry_delay` (`100ms`), and `max_retry_delay` (`5s`). Invalid duration strings log a warning and fall back to `1s`. Zero durations are accepted. The maximum delay is raised to the base delay if configured smaller. `verbose_logging` defaults to `false` and raises per-poll submission logs from debug to info. See the upstream surrealdb_sink README for the full table."],[528,"The `operation` byte tells the server which state-machine operation the request carries. Replicated operations are identified by `operation` alone. Non-replicated operations (reads, ping) all use `operation = 2` and carry their concrete u32 command code in header bytes 196..200."],[1190,"The `path` field of a connector configuration accepts both `plugin.so` and `plugin` - the OS-specific extension (`.so` / `.dylib` / `.dll`) is appended when missing. Absolute paths are checked at the literal location. Relative paths are searched in order:"],[1745,"The `payload_format` option determines the type of the `payload` column and how the message payload is stored:"],[2146,"The `pg_replicate` backend (based on Supabase's ETL framework) is **not implemented**: selecting `cdc_backend = \"pg_replicate\"` fails at startup unless the `cdc_pg_replicate` build feature is enabled, and the backend returns an error even when it is."],[1298,"The `retry` module ships helpers for connectors talking to flaky external systems: a `CircuitBreaker`, an `HttpRetryMiddleware` for `reqwest`-based clients, and a `build_retry_client` factory that wraps a reqwest client with the retry middleware (the circuit breaker is wired separately)."],[3651,"The `RUST_LOG` environment variable takes precedence over `logging.level`. An invalid effective filter stops startup."],[1321,"The `schema_registry_url` field is reserved and currently not implemented. The SDK never contacts a schema registry, and schemas are loaded only from `schema_path` or `descriptor_set`."],[3747,"The `self_signed` flag doesn't mean the same thing everywhere:"],[504,"The `size` field at offset 48 is the total frame length in bytes, header included. A body, when present, immediately follows the 256 header bytes and has length `size - 256`. Readers can decode `size` before typing the header. That offset is a protocol constant."],[35,"The `token` value can be either a literal PAT or a `file:` reference such as `token = \"file:/run/secrets/iggy_pat\"`, in which case the token is read from the given file and surrounding whitespace is removed (`~/` is expanded to the home directory). A non-empty token takes precedence over the username and password."],[4210,"The 256-byte batch header carries `partition_id`, `base_offset`, `base_timestamp`, `origin_timestamp`, `batch_length`, `batch_checksum` (XxHash3-64), and `message_count`. An optional producer section carries a producer ID, epoch and sequence. Remaining reserved bytes must be zero. Each 48-byte frame header carries `checksum` (XxHash3-64), `id` (u128), `offset_delta` (u32), `timestamp_delta` (u32), and the two lengths. A message's absolute offset is `base_offset + offset_delta`. Its server timestamp is the flat `base_timestamp`, while `timestamp_delta` resolves producer time against `origin_timestamp`. All fields are **little-endian**, and records are stored contiguously with no padding."],[994,"The 32-byte minimum is enforced whenever the cluster is enabled, even with `auth.enabled = false`: a configured non-empty secret shorter than 32 bytes fails boot."],[355,"The 40-byte prefix is followed by a stream of batch records: each record's header carries the stamped `base_offset` and `base_timestamp`, and each frame's deltas resolve against them. A record may be a server-sliced view of a larger stored batch, so the first polled offset is `base_offset + offset_delta` of the first frame, not necessarily `base_offset` itself. `current_offset` is the partition's newest offset at poll time. The server rebuilds records when decrypting encrypted payloads."],[668,"The 64-byte `IggyMessageHeader` (checksum, id, offset, timestamp, origin_timestamp, lengths, reserved) that earlier protocol generations put in front of every message **no longer exists on the wire or on disk**. It survives only as an in-memory model."],[2,"The AI-related components of Apache Iggy: the MCP server that lets LLM applications work with a running Iggy server."],[1427,"The Apache Doris sink connector consumes JSON messages from Iggy streams and writes them to a pre-created Doris table via Doris's Stream Load HTTP API. Batches are loaded under deterministic labels so that retries deduplicate while Doris retains those labels."],[2865,"The API fragments below reuse a connected, authenticated `client` (see Creating a client) and the imports in the configuration example. Use existing streams and topics for read, update, delete, publish, and poll operations. The create examples show how to prepare them."],[2357,"The append-only log"],[2554,"The append-only log is the core concept of Iggy. It's a simple data structure, which is optimized for the append-only operations. It's a sequence of records, that are being appended to the end of the log. The records are **immutable**, so that they can't be changed once they are written to the log. Records within a partition follow the order admitted by that partition's primary. There is no global ordering across partitions."],[3013,"The async-first C# SDK for .NET applications over TCP and HTTP, from installation to creating a client."],[4427,"The automatic policy counts a message after its handler succeeds. A handler exception ends the run and leaves that message uncounted. A later run can read it again. Await consumer shutdown to finish pending progress and leave the group."],[2240,"The available computed values are:"],[640,"The batch layout messages travel and rest in, from the 256-byte batch header down to individual message frames."],[1347,"The BigQuery sink writes Arrow record batches through the Storage Write API `_default` stream. The target table must already exist. Startup reads its schema and rejects unsupported column types before consuming messages."],[1095,"The BigQuery sink writes through the Storage Write API. The MQTT source supports deferred broker acknowledgements."],[900,"The binaries are:"],[1507,"The body size limit defaults to 10 MiB and applies after serialization: per message in `individual`/`raw`, or to the whole batch in `ndjson`/`json_array`. Setting `max_payload_size_bytes = 0` disables it. Oversized bodies fail without being sent. Optional startup health checks use the same URL, retry policy and success codes, with `HEAD` as the default method."],[2314,"The bridge is the gateway's connection to Iggy. It is disabled by default. Without it, data and topic APIs do not store data. Producer ID allocation and in-memory group coordination alone do not prove that storage is enabled."],[2143,"The builtin backend creates (or reuses) a logical replication slot using the `test_decoding` output plugin. The default slot name is `iggy_slot`. No publication is created or required, since `test_decoding` ignores publications entirely."],[2798,"The C++ SDK supports the same connection string format as the Rust SDK:"],[2786,"The C++ SDK, a CXX bridge over the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source with Bazel."],[2706,"The canonical reference for Iggy connection strings, covering every scheme, credential form and option."],[4305,"The catalog"],[479,"The catalog is discoverable with DescribeOptions, code 13, using scope 1 for topics, 2 for streams and 3 for users. All three catalogs have entries. Stream keys are create-only and secret. Stream and topic names and user identity/status can be updated. Topic retention fields can be reset, while compression and storage policies are fixed at creation. See Topic options and Stream options."],[306,"The catalog of command codes, their operation bytes, and which commands are replicated."],[1642,"The circuit breaker counts at most one failure per consumed batch, based on its first error. A permanent HTTP error does not increment the counter. A fully successful batch resets it. While the breaker is open, incoming batches fail without a write. After the cool-down window, writes resume and the failure counter resets."],[773,"The CLI talks to the server over one of four transports, selected with `--transport`. Accepted values are `tcp`, `quic`, `http` and `ws` (**not** `websocket`). The default is `tcp`."],[1365,"The ClickHouse sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and inserts them into ClickHouse tables over the HTTP interface. It supports three insert formats: `json_each_row` (default), `row_binary`, and `string` passthrough for pre-formatted CSV/TSV/JSON payloads."],[1053,"The client API is the same in single-node and cluster mode. This example uses the `iggy` / `iggy` credentials explicitly provisioned in Getting started:"],[3336,"The client constructor accepts a few options:"],[423,"The client creates and protects the random binding secret for its session. The server stores a verifier. Session zero can resolve a lost registration reply using this retained secret. Binding supports connections to different partition primaries while preserving one session identity."],[3119,"The client exposes `CreateConsumerGroup`, `JoinConsumerGroup`, `LeaveConsumerGroup`, `GetConsumerGroups` and `SyncConsumerGroup`. A one-shot `PollMessages` reads an explicit partition. Use `reader.NewPartitionReader` or `reader.NewIggyConsumer` with `iggcon.NewGroupConsumer` to read the full assignment."],[3327,"The client resolves the target partition and routes requests to its primary. A primary can differ from the metadata leader."],[420,"The client stores `session` and echoes it in every subsequent request header. Logout is command code **39** (`LOGOUT_USER`), `operation = 3` (`Logout`), empty body."],[4505,"The Clients, Logs and terminal routes are placeholders hidden from navigation. The Server settings page displays information, but saving settings is disabled. Editing and deleting existing users, changing their permissions and bulk user actions are also placeholders. See the roadmap for what is planned."],[1008,"the cluster PSK: when HTTP is enabled, cluster auth is enabled, and no `http.jwt` secrets are configured, the signing key is derived from `shared_secret`."],[3935,"The committed batches are flushed even below the ordinary flush thresholds. **Setting `durability=persisted` is sufficient. Setting `messages_required_to_save=1` is not required.** A single-replica partition does not use the cluster's prepare WAL."],[2629,"The completed sample can be found in the repository. We will go through the implementation step by step so you get a clear understanding of how it all fits together."],[3965,"The concurrency cap applies per shard, so total in-flight callouts can reach the shard count multiplied by this value. There is no per-client rate limit in the callout. Apply one at the service or network boundary if required."],[4252,"The config loader rejects the old `[system]` table and `IGGY_SYSTEM_*` environment mappings. The full mapping is in Relocated configuration keys."],[32,"The configuration file must be in the `toml` format. By default, the server looks for `core/ai/mcp/config.toml` relative to its working directory. Set `IGGY_MCP_CONFIG_PATH` to use another path. Embedded defaults are loaded first, then the file if it exists, then environment overrides."],[1182,"The configuration file path defaults to `core/connectors/runtime/config.toml` and can be overridden by the `IGGY_CONNECTORS_CONFIG_PATH` environment variable."],[3506,"The connection string is the simplest way to configure the client. It supports all four transport protocols:"],[1177,"The connector are implemented as Rust libraries, and these are loaded dynamically during the runtime initialization process."],[1997,"The connector does not use scroll, `search_after` or a document-ID tiebreaker. If a timestamp group spans multiple batches, advancing past that timestamp skips the remaining tied documents. Late arrivals and updates at or below the watermark are also skipped. `scroll_timeout` is accepted but unused. Without a usable timestamp, repeated polls can produce duplicates. The default `@timestamp` sort still requires a sortable field in the index mapping."],[1523,"The connector doesn't expect the metadata envelope on input. It adds it on the way out. Producers must supply payloads accepted by the configured stream decoder and the receiving endpoint."],[1928,"The connector emits info-level tracing events through the runtime to stdout. The runtime selects text or JSON with `logging.format` and filters events with `RUST_LOG` (default `INFO`). It logs batch metadata on every invocation. `print_payload` additionally logs each message's offset and decoded payload. The invocation counter is in memory and resets when the plugin is recreated."],[1994,"The connector file remains TOML. `plugin_config_format = \"json\"` only selects the default format of the HTTP API's plugin-config response. It does not change how the local file is parsed. The upstream config example also uses TOML with a structured `[plugin_config]` table."],[1451,"The connector preserves the `Authorization` header across Doris FE-to-BE 307/308 redirects, validating each target first. When `fe_url` uses HTTPS, an HTTP redirect target is refused unless `allow_insecure_redirect = true`. `Location` must be absolute, and at most five redirects are followed per attempt. A nonempty `allowed_redirect_hosts` list restricts destinations to the listed hosts or `host:port` endpoints. An unset or empty list does not restrict hosts."],[1707,"The connector retries transient insert failures up to `max_retries` total attempts, with linear waits of `retry_delay`, twice that delay, and so on. An invalid delay silently falls back to `1s`. Driver-level retryable writes may add retries independently, according to the URI and MongoDB deployment. Startup has no connector-level retry loop."],[1133,"The connector runtime exposes its health through logs, Prometheus metrics, a stats endpoint, OpenTelemetry export, and per-source state files."],[1077,"The connector runtime operates in two directions - **source** (ingest) and **sink** (egress):"],[1532,"The connector runtime uses consumer auto-commit before `consume()` completes. A plugin error is logged and counted, the failed batch adds no processed messages, and polling continues without replaying that batch. Earlier messages or requests from the same batch may already have succeeded. A crash or exhausted retry budget can lose messages. A retry after an ambiguous response can deliver them again. There is no end-to-end at-least-once or exactly-once guarantee."],[1060,"The connector runtime: dynamically loaded source and sink plugins, how data flows through them, and what ships today."],[2161,"The connector stages its count for each batch and commits it only after the runtime acknowledges delivery and saves the checkpoint. Restoring that checkpoint enforces `max_count` across restarts. Missing or undecodable state starts the count at zero. After reaching the limit, polling continues but returns no messages or new checkpoint."],[2057,"The connector substitutes its RFC3339 cursor into the query. Both versions require ascending timestamps and a stable, unique order within each timestamp group. Include all necessary tag or key columns after the timestamp in the sort. The examples use `host` because their data model has one point per host and timestamp."],[2699,"The consumer decodes the envelope, reads `message_type`, then decodes the inner payload as that type. This works with any client, but every message is decoded twice, and the inner payload is a JSON string inside the envelope. See the message-envelope example."],[2647,"The consumer prints the messages and stores its progress. Running it again waits and exits if no new messages exist. Running the producer again appends ten more messages. A processing failure before checkpoint storage can cause replay on the next run, so application side effects must tolerate retries."],[3113,"The consumer reads partition `0` independently and keeps its offset only in memory. Restarting reads from offset `0` again, or the earliest retained message if older messages were removed."],[3250,"The consumer reads partition `0` independently and stops on an empty poll. Its cursor starts at offset `0` or the earliest retained message each time it runs. `autoCommit` is disabled, so this sample does not store its progress on the server."],[2701,"The consumer reads the header and decodes the payload once:"],[3002,"The consumer reads through a `PartitionReader` and yields one message at a time. A standalone consumer requires `WithPartitions`. A group consumer follows the server assignment."],[2642,"The consumer starts after its stored offset. It waits up to five seconds for messages and processes the returned batch before storing its checkpoint. An empty reply ends this example."],[3523,"The consumer uses a group by default. This example creates the group, reads messages and commits each checkpoint after printing its payload. Add `futures-util` to use `StreamExt`."],[2632,"The container needs a host kernel of 5.19 or newer, because the server uses `io_uring`. See System requirements if it fails to start. The capabilities, seccomp setting, and memlock limit form a permissive development setup. Production deployments can use narrower syscall permissions and a finite memory budget. See Docker & Helm for the details. `IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS` is needed because the server binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default, which a published port cannot reach. `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` is needed because that wildcard leaves the server with no address to give clients, and it refuses to start rather than publish one nobody can dial. Here the port is published to the host, so `localhost` is that address. Setting the root credentials explicitly means the username and password used later in this guide will work."],[2351,"The coordinator log uses 16 partitions. The owner of partition zero is the controller for topic management. Other gateways return `NOT_CONTROLLER`, and the Kafka admin client follows it. After a gateway fails, another gateway takes over its assigned groups once the Iggy session expires and ownership moves."],[4061,"The core metrics are below. The gauges are read fresh on every scrape."],[925,"The cross-SDK BDD suites use a standalone server for basic features and add the two-node cluster from `bdd/docker-compose.cluster.yml` for `leader_redirection` or `all`:"],[3106,"The current source requires Go 1.25 or newer. Run these commands from your application module:"],[4254,"The current storage format is incompatible with 0.9 data directories. Boot checks it before metadata replay. Preserve old files for rollback and use a fresh directory for 0.10. See Upgrading."],[902,"The default `core/server/config.toml` has clustering disabled, so a plain run starts a single node:"],[2140,"The default `payload_format = \"json\"` reads a selected column as BYTEA but reports JSON to the runtime, so those bytes must contain valid JSON. Without a selected column, the connector always emits a JSON envelope. If the selected column is absent, it falls back to the envelope while retaining the selected format's schema. Use a column present in every configured table. Type mismatches reject the poll. See the upstream README for sink round-trip recipes."],[3482,"The default automatic policy stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds, after processing. `CommitAfter::Delivery` counts a yielded message immediately. `CommitAfter::Marked` counts checkpoints that the application marks. A handler failure leaves its message uncounted when using processing-based commits."],[1210,"The default configuration provider reads connector configurations from local files. Each connector (source or sink) is configured in its own separate file within the directory specified by `connectors.config_dir`. An empty `config_dir` is a fatal startup error. A missing directory is created automatically with a warning, and no connectors are loaded from it."],[2067,"The default file backend stores `source_influxdb.state` under the runtime's state path (default `local_state`). The runtime also supports an HTTP state backend. V2 restores versioned state or its legacy unversioned state. V3 requires versioned V3 state. Corrupt checkpoints, invalid saved timestamps and mismatched versions prevent startup. To reset file-backed progress, stop the runtime and remove the source's checkpoint, then restart with the desired `initial_offset`."],[87,"The default HTTP address is `127.0.0.1:8082`, which inside a container is unreachable from the outside, so `IGGY_MCP_HTTP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8082` is required for the published port to work."],[2131,"The default query selects rows with `tracking_column > last_offset`, orders that column ascending, and applies `batch_size` separately to each table. Use a non-null, unique tracking value that increases in the database's sort order. A timestamp with ties can skip rows at a batch boundary, and inserts or updates at or below the watermark are not revisited. Polling does not capture deletions."],[3304,"The default-root flag sets the `iggy`/`iggy` credentials used by the examples. `--fresh` deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables override the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials."],[1399,"The Delta Lake sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and writes them to Delta Lake tables on the local filesystem, AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, or Google Cloud Storage. Each successful nonempty batch is flushed and appended in one Delta transaction."],[2214,"The destination `schema` selects the `StreamEncoder`. A decode, transform or encoding error rejects the entire source batch, leaves its checkpoint uncommitted and produces `Nack`. The runtime logs and counts the error. An intentional filter result (`Ok(None)`) may drop selected messages without rejecting the batch."],[3451,"The direct producer splits each `send()` call into requests of at most 1000 messages. For sequential calls, the 5 ms linger setting delays a call until at least 5 ms after the previous successful send. It does not buffer messages between calls or delay individual chunks within a call. Choose `background` to buffer sends on worker tasks. `balanced` routes requests round-robin across partitions. By default, `init()` creates a missing stream and topic, requiring the corresponding permissions."],[2317,"The Docker Compose example starts a local Iggy server and gateway. Review its image selection and development credentials before running it. The gateway image is `apache/iggy-kafka-gateway`."],[1075,"The Docker image for this release is `apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0`."],[1181,"The Docker image for this release is `apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0`."],[29,"The Docker image for this release is `apache/iggy-mcp:0.6.0`."],[2450,"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via `docker pull apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0`."],[2452,"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via `docker pull apache/iggy-mcp:0.6.0`."],[2537,"The domain model behind Iggy, and how an append-only streaming log differs from a message broker."],[1460,"The Elasticsearch sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and indexes them into an Elasticsearch index using the bulk API."],[1979,"The Elasticsearch source connector polls documents from an Elasticsearch index and produces them to Iggy streams. With a timestamp field configured, it filters searches using the last acknowledged timestamp. This is a timestamp watermark, with the limitations described below."],[1332,"The encoder follows the same pattern:"],[2900,"The encryptor applies to **every message on the connection** - topics mixing encrypted and plaintext messages are not supported."],[2734,"The environment takes precedence over the flag. Stored or recovered root credentials are not replaced by bootstrap settings. `--fresh` deletes this replica's local data. In a cluster it can recover committed state, including credentials, from another replica. Use this reset only for disposable development data. A fresh standalone server requires credentials. Provision root credentials on every replica for a new cluster."],[3365,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and bootstrap the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials for a new data directory. Existing stored credentials are not replaced. For the source command, ensure `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` are unset or match the sample. Environment values take precedence over the flag. `--fresh` removes the local replica state, so use it only for disposable development data."],[3390,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and bootstrap the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced, and environment credentials override the source command's default-credentials flag. Use `--fresh` only with disposable local replica data."],[3406,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and bootstrap the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced. Environment credentials override the default-credentials flag, so unset them or set them to match the sample. `--fresh` deletes local replica state. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."],[3325,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to `127.0.0.1` inside the container by default) and set the root credentials the samples log in with. Without `--with-default-root-credentials` (or the `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` / `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` variables), a fresh server refuses to start. For source runs, `--fresh` deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables take precedence over the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace credentials already stored in recovered data."],[4338,"The example needs a cluster with at least three nodes. The metadata group still spans the full cluster, independent of each topic's factor."],[1565,"The example requires a REST catalog at `http://localhost:8181` and S3-compatible storage at `http://localhost:9000`, configured with the credentials below. Create the storage bucket, namespace and target table before starting the connector. The sink does not create them."],[2105,"The example uses an existing PostgreSQL database named `iggy`, with an `iggy` user and password. Run this SQL in that database:"],[3418,"The examples are cargo example targets of the `iggy_examples` crate. Run them from the **repository root** with `cargo run --example <name>` so relative certificate paths resolve. Run each producer before its consumer. Some examples create fixed names and require fresh data when repeated. The getting-started consumer also expects stream and topic IDs `0`, so run that pair first on a fresh server. Partition IDs are **0-based**, but balanced producers and consumer groups can use multiple partitions."],[2696,"The examples are in Rust and come from the examples in the Apache Iggy repository."],[3500,"The examples below log in with `iggy`/`iggy`. For a new local development instance, with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides, use:"],[3171,"The examples below use the `iggy`/`iggy` root credentials. For a new local development instance, with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides, use:"],[3035,"The examples use `iggy` / `iggy`. For a new server data directory, start the matching source server from the Iggy repository root:"],[2733,"The examples use `iggy`/`iggy`. For a new local development instance, with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides, use:"],[3892,"The examples use a permissive syscall profile and unlimited locked memory for development. Production deployments can use a custom seccomp profile and a finite locked-memory budget that permit the server's required operations. See Linux tuning."],[2818,"The examples/csharp directory in the Iggy repository contains complete, runnable projects:"],[3349,"The experimental PHP SDK, a native extension wrapping the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source."],[911,"The exported settings must be present in all three terminals."],[3358,"The extension is experimental. It's defined as the `apache/iggy-php` composer package (`php-ext` type). To use it today, build it from the repository source."],[3377,"The extension owns a lazy global Tokio runtime. Don't call `pcntl_fork()` after the first Iggy call: the child inherits file descriptors but not the runtime's worker threads."],[2273,"The extension point is the SDK (`core/connectors/sdk/src/transforms/`):"],[4487,"The feature alone does not generate these assets. A server binary built without them returns 404 at `/ui`."],[2801,"The FFI bridge exposes command-level operations for streams, topics, partitions, and segments. Message sending and polling with user headers. Consumer groups and consumer offsets. Stats, client info, and cluster metadata. Snapshots. User create/get/update/delete, permission updates and password changes. Topic option discovery (`describe_options`). And raw binary requests (`send_binary_request`)."],[2238,"The field-level transforms (`add_fields`, `delete_fields`, `filter_fields`, `update_fields`, `unwrap_envelope`) operate on JSON objects only. Other JSON values and other payload formats pass through them unchanged."],[2135,"The file state backend stores `source_postgres-source.state` under the runtime's state path (default `local_state`). Its MessagePack state contains per-table offsets, a last-poll time and a processed-row count. The HTTP state backend is also available. Missing or undecodable plugin state starts fresh. File access errors prevent startup. Stop the runtime before resetting its checkpoint. `initial_offset` is used only where saved offsets are absent."],[926,"The first argument selects the SDK (`rust`, `python`, `php`, `go`, `go-race`, `node`, `csharp`, `java`, `cpp`, `swift`, or `all`) and the optional second argument selects one feature (`basic_messaging`, `leader_redirection`, `raw_command`, `session_contract`, `stream_crud`, `stream_options`, `resource_options_patch`, `metadata_changes`, `deferred_polling`, or `all`). `leader_redirection` is the clustered feature. Coverage depends on the selected SDK and feature. Use `--help` for the runner's current selections."],[488,"The fixed 256-byte header every binary-transport message rides in, and the request, reply and eviction shapes it carries."],[346,"The fixed fields are the shape of the operation: which stream, how many partitions, what name. Every topic setting (`compression_algorithm`, `message_expiry`, `max_topic_size`, `segment_size`, `durability`, `consumer_offset_durability`, `messages_required_to_save`, `size_of_messages_required_to_save`, `preallocate_segments`, `partition_resize_policy`, `replication_factor`) rides the options block. See Topic options. `partitions_count` is an argument, not a setting: it is consumed at admission and never persisted as an option."],[4219,"The flush thresholds and completion policies are **per-topic creation options**:"],[1084,"The following built-in transforms are available:"],[4424,"The following output illustrates the message shape. Customer choices, offsets and partition scheduling vary."],[1309,"The format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults. Every non-optional key shown above must be present, or the configuration fails to deserialize (`schema_path`, `message_type`, `field_mappings`, and `descriptor_set` are optional)."],[4344,"The former `enforce_fsync` option is rejected. Use `durability` for messages and `consumer_offset_durability` for explicit offset changes. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous, and HTTP `ack=none` confirms dispatch only, regardless of these policies."],[3932,"The former `enforce_fsync` topic option is rejected. Use `durability` for messages and `consumer_offset_durability` for offsets. The old server-wide `consumer_offset_enforce_fsync` setting has also been removed."],[4076,"The four transports Iggy serves simultaneously, TCP, QUIC, WebSocket and HTTP, and what each is best suited to."],[3131,"The fragment assumes a connected `IggyClient` in an async function. Initialization obtains the producer ID. The producer selects each chunk's partition and sends chunks for that partition in sequence. The default producer does not enable this mode."],[3003,"The fragment assumes a connected client. Manual commits are the default. Commit the checkpoint after processing. `DisposeAsync` calls `ShutdownAsync` to close the reader and leave the group."],[2958,"The fragments below reuse an authenticated `client` and the imports in the first example. The named streams/topics must already exist unless the snippet creates them. Both abstractions can be built from an existing connected client via `IggyPublisherBuilder.Create(...)` / `IggyConsumerBuilder.Create(...)`, or equivalently via the `client.CreatePublisherBuilder(...)` / `client.CreateConsumerBuilder(...)` extension methods from `Apache.Iggy.Extensions`."],[4211,"The full byte-level tables live on the Message batches page. Absent at-rest encryption, what lands on disk is exactly what the producer sent, with the server stamping `partition_id` at admission and `base_offset` / `base_timestamp` (plus a `batch_checksum` recompute) at journal append."],[2348,"The gateway configuration reference also lists timeouts, TLS reload, client-certificate mapping, authentication capacity and producer session expiry."],[3134,"The gateway does not support transactions. Idempotent appends do not make application processing and offset commits one atomic operation. See Kafka gateway."],[2322,"The gateway supports Produce, Fetch, ListOffsets, Metadata, topic creation and deletion, partition additions, and topic retention changes. Consumer group APIs support membership, rebalances, static membership, offset commits, group inspection and offset deletion. It also supports ApiVersions, InitProducerId and DescribeCluster."],[2805,"The generated bridge passes `ErrorInfo` by reference to mutations, login, polls and recovery calls. Use a separate value for each concurrent call. `GetMetadataChanges` follows the metadata log, and `send_binary_request_to_replica` targets a node for supported non-replicated commands."],[2825,"The getting-started producer sends 50 messages to partition 0. Its consumer reads five nonempty batches and then exits. Envelope and header examples also use five batches. NewSdk uses four partitions and its group consumer runs until stopped."],[4148,"The global permissions and the operations they unlock:"],[3079,"The Go SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples require Go 1.25 or newer and log in as `iggy`/`iggy`. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides:"],[3093,"The Go SDK, a blocking client speaking the VSR wire protocol over TCP, and which servers it pairs with."],[1272,"The guarantee has limits. A partition retains five batches per producer, at most 4096 producers, and expires producers idle for 24 hours. A changed batch count, a changed partition, expired producer state, or more than five unsaved batches can cause duplicates after recovery. The plugin must return new checkpoint state often enough to preserve its replay position."],[304,"The header definitions and command registry define the layouts. The replica transport implements preamble admission and authentication."],[499,"The header shape is identified by the `command` byte at offset 60. Values a client sends or receives:"],[3505,"The high-level API is built on top of the low-level one, so you can always drop down when needed."],[1074,"The highly performant and modular runtime for statically typed, yet dynamically loaded connectors. Ingest the data from the external sources and push it further to the Iggy streams, or fetch the data from the Iggy streams and push it further to the external sources. Create your own Rust plugins by simply implementing either the `Source` or `Sink` trait and build custom pipelines for the data processing."],[2507,"The historical migration benchmarks compared v0.5.0 with v0.7.0 at approximately 1,000 MB/s per node. The chart shows selected latency reductions for 8, 16 and 32 producers, each using its own stream. The 8-producer case also reported higher P95 and P99 latency. These are not measurements of 0.10.0:"],[2720,"The host and port are **always required**. Use a hostname, IPv4 address or bracketed IPv6 address such as `[::1]:8090`. Options are appended as `key=value` pairs separated by `&`. Scheme names and option keys are case-sensitive."],[3994,"The HTTP API endpoints can be found in server.http file, which can be used with REST Client extension for VS Code."],[4102,"The HTTP API is built on axum and provides a standard REST interface. It includes JWT authentication, CORS configuration, and optional TLS."],[4182,"The HTTP API uses JWT (JSON Web Tokens) for session management. Tokens are signed with HS256 by default and have configurable expiry, clock skew tolerance, and audience/issuer validation:"],[1215,"The HTTP configuration provider allows the runtime to fetch connector configurations from a remote HTTP/REST API. The provider fetches active configurations at startup and handles configuration operations requested through the runtime API. It does not periodically poll for remote changes."],[86,"The HTTP endpoint uses the configured Iggy account for every MCP client. This example publishes it only on the host loopback interface."],[1237,"The HTTP provider expects the remote API to implement these endpoints and return connector configuration data in the same format as used by the local provider."],[2778,"The HTTP scheme constructs a plain `http://` API URL. Use the Rust HTTP builder's `with_api_url()` for HTTPS. HTTP parses the credential field but does not log in with it. In Rust, call `login_user()` or `login_with_personal_access_token()` explicitly before protected requests."],[4104,"The HTTP server also hosts:"],[1202,"The HTTP server must implement this checkpoint contract:"],[1494,"The HTTP Sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and delivers them to any HTTP endpoint: webhooks, REST APIs, serverless functions, or SaaS integrations. This is the transport-level, generic HTTP connector. It's distinct from sinks such as Elasticsearch or Quickwit, which use HTTP internally to talk to one specific system. You bring the URL, headers, and batching strategy, and the sink handles transport, retries, and metadata wrapping."],[1524,"The HTTP sink supports authentication via custom headers under `[plugin_config.headers]`. Custom headers are sent with data requests and health checks, except `Content-Type`: the sink ignores a configured value and sets it from the batch mode for data requests."],[2010,"The HTTP source accepts webhook POST bodies and produces raw messages to one configured stream and topic. Instances that use the same listener address share the listener. A separate admin address serves management, health details and metrics."],[1558,"The Iceberg Sink Connector allows you to consume messages from Iggy topics and store them in Iceberg tables."],[2793,"The Iggy C++ SDK wraps the Rust SDK through a CXX FFI bridge, so it can use all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket) via connection strings. The source code is available at github.com/apache/iggy/tree/master/foreign/cpp."],[765,"The Iggy CLI is a command-line interface for managing an Iggy server: streams, topics, partitions, messages, users, tokens and more. It lives in the core repository under the `core/cli` directory. The crate is named `iggy-cli`, the installed binary `iggy`."],[3104,"The Iggy Go SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Go applications. It is a blocking client that speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol over TCP, with or without TLS. The package is available on pkg.go.dev and the source code lives in foreign/go."],[1584,"The Iggy Iceberg Connector will skip messages in the following cases:"],[3242,"The Iggy Java SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Java applications. It provides a blocking client and an async client (based on `CompletableFuture`) over TCP, plus a blocking HTTP client. The artifact is published to Maven Central and the source code lives in foreign/java."],[3322,"The Iggy Node.js SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Node.js and TypeScript applications. It communicates with the Iggy server over TCP or TLS using the binary protocol. The package is available on npm and the source code can be found on GitHub."],[3357,"The Iggy PHP SDK is a native PHP extension built in Rust with ext-php-rs. It wraps the Rust SDK and exposes a synchronous `Iggy\\Client`: each call drives the extension's internal Tokio runtime and blocks the calling PHP thread until the operation completes. The source code is available on GitHub."],[3401,"The Iggy Python SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Python application. It is built as a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, which means it supports TCP, QUIC, HTTP, and WebSocket transports via connection strings. The package is available on PyPI and the source code can be found on GitHub."],[3022,"The Iggy SDK for C# is a modern, async-first client library for interacting with an Iggy message streaming server from your .NET applications. It supports TCP and HTTP transports. The package is available on NuGet and the source code lives on GitHub."],[4483,"The Iggy server must allow cross-origin requests from the Web UI origin. CORS is enabled for all origins by default (`[http.cors]` in the server config). If you restricted `allowed_origins`, add the Web UI origin."],[2328,"The Iggy topic's durability policy controls storage guarantees. The development stack uses `replicated`, so an acknowledgement does not prove stable-storage synchronization. Use `persisted` when acknowledged messages must survive loss of volatile copies. A named Docker volume alone does not strengthen an acknowledgement."],[4470,"The Iggy Web UI dashboard, for monitoring server health and browsing streams, topics, messages and users."],[3889,"The image bakes in no address overrides, so the server binds the loopback defaults (`127.0.0.1`) and is **unreachable from outside the container** even with published ports. Set `IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090` (and the equivalent for every other transport you expose) alongside the `-p` flags."],[2207,"The implementation also uses `humantime`, `rand`, `simd-json`, and `uuid`. The reference crate uses `rmp-serde` directly in its tests. The production state helpers come from the SDK."],[1024,"The implementation includes:"],[3956,"The implementation references are the topic policy definitions, partition completion paths, and HTTP response handling."],[485,"The incarnation identifies the partition history. The owner generation identifies group ownership and is zero for ordinary consumers. The metadata operation establishes the minimum metadata needed to interpret the context. Poll replies, continuations and checkpoints preserve these fields."],[1609,"The InfluxDB sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and writes them to InfluxDB as line-protocol points. It supports both InfluxDB V2 (OSS 2.x / Cloud) and InfluxDB V3 (Core / Enterprise), selected with the `version` option."],[2034,"The InfluxDB source connector polls InfluxDB on an interval and produces the resulting rows as messages into an Iggy stream. It supports both InfluxDB V2 (OSS 2.x / Cloud, Flux queries) and InfluxDB V3 (Core / Enterprise, SQL queries), selected with the `version` option. An acknowledged timestamp cursor and row offset are stored in connector state. Restarts resume from that checkpoint, subject to the ordering and late-arrival limits below."],[2267,"The input payload variant must match `source_format`. The default `flat_buffer` stream decoder already returns JSON metadata, so it does not supply `Payload::FlatBuffer` to a following reverse transform."],[2442,"The interactive CLI is implemented under `core/cli`, to provide the best developer experience. This is a great addition to the Web UI, especially for developers who prefer using the console tools."],[3231,"The Java SDK, with blocking and CompletableFuture clients over TCP, and which servers they pair with."],[1154,"The JSON status values are `starting`, `running`, `stopping`, `stopped`, and `error`. A connector whose plugin fails to load (for example, a missing library) is reported with `error` without blocking the remaining connectors. Retrieve its `last_error` from `GET /sources/{key}` or `GET /sinks/{key}`. `/stats` does not include the failure message."],[2313,"The Kafka gateway 0.2.0 is a separate service named `iggy-gateway-kafka`. It accepts Kafka wire requests and uses the Rust SDK to store and read data in Iggy. Kafka clients connect to the gateway port, 9093 by default. Native Iggy clients continue to use Iggy listeners."],[2292,"The Kafka gateway supports Kafka producers, consumers and selected admin APIs. Run it separately with the Iggy bridge enabled. It supports a defined protocol subset and does not support transactions."],[421,"The legacy login commands **38** (`LOGIN_USER`) and **44** (`LOGIN_WITH_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN`) are refused: the server answers them with an `Eviction` frame, reason `15` (`MalformedLogin`). Before authentication, a connection can ping or use the registration and binding flows. Protected commands require an authenticated session."],[1333,"The loaded schema is used for JSON-to-protobuf conversion. This example maps `user_id` and `full_name` to the schema's field names:"],[1294,"The macros generate the C FFI symbols loaded by the runtime: `iggy_sink_open`, `iggy_sink_consume`, `iggy_sink_close`, and `iggy_sink_version` for sinks, and `iggy_source_open`, `iggy_source_handle_v2`, `iggy_source_batch_result`, `iggy_source_close`, and `iggy_source_version` for sources. Configurations cross FFI as JSON, message batches and metadata use postcard, and source checkpoint bytes are opaque. The SDK creates one Tokio runtime per loaded plugin library, shared by that library's connector instances."],[1034,"The main components are:"],[36,"The MCP server exposes these 41 tools:"],[1652,"The Meilisearch sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and indexes them as documents in a Meilisearch index through the official Rust SDK. It can create the index on startup and waits for Meilisearch indexing tasks to complete by default, so task failures surface as connector errors."],[1587,"The message does not contain the field specified in the `dynamic_route_field` configuration option, or is not a JSON object."],[2558,"The messages are not being stored in the topic directly, but rather in the **partitions**, which are assigned to the topic. The topic can have one or more partitions assigned, that could help achieve higher parallelism and throughput. The topic can also have a **retention policy**. Expiry removes whole sealed segments after their newest message has expired, subject to the stored consumer-offset barrier. It does not delete each message immediately at its expiry time. See topic options for expiry and size-limit behavior. Topics also support maximum size limits and per-topic storage options (`segment_size`, `durability`, `consumer_offset_durability`, and flush thresholds) set at creation, plus a `compression_algorithm` option (a placeholder today: no compression is applied yet). Both durability policies independently default to `replicated`."],[3266,"The metadata change log records public results of committed metadata operations. It includes resource changes, permissions, sessions and consumer group membership. Reading it requires root or the global `manage_servers` permission."],[163,"The metadata group orders resources, users and sessions. Every partition has its own group for messages and offsets. The `group` field identifies the target. The metadata group uses `1 << 63`, while partition groups use packed stream, topic and partition IDs."],[555,"The metadata plane orders resource and session changes. Each partition plane orders messages and offset changes independently. Partition requests retain their incarnation, ownership generation and metadata requirement across retries. Refreshing a route does not authorize a request against a replacement history."],[3709,"The metrics route **requires authentication**: a missing or invalid bearer credential is rejected with 401. Any authenticated user may scrape. There's no extra RBAC rule. Scrapers present a JWT or a personal access token as the bearer:"],[30,"The minimal viable configuration requires at least the Iggy credentials, used to create the TCP connection to the running Iggy server that the MCP server talks to. You can choose between HTTP (the default) and STDIO transports (e.g. for the local usage with tools such as Claude Desktop choose `stdio`)."],[2451,"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The **Iggy MCP Server** is an implementation of the MCP protocol for message streaming infrastructure. It exposes 40+ tools covering streams, topics, partitions, messages, consumer groups, users, and more. It supports both HTTP and stdio transports, making it compatible with tools like Claude Desktop and other MCP clients."],[26,"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The Apache Iggy MCP Server is an implementation of the MCP protocol for the message streaming infrastructure. It is built using the `rmcp` crate and supports both **stdio** and **HTTP** transports."],[1683,"The MongoDB sink connector writes messages from Iggy streams to a MongoDB database. Each message is inserted as a document into the configured collection."],[2075,"The MQTT source subscribes to an MQTT broker and sends payloads to one Iggy stream and topic. It supports MQTT 3.1.1 and MQTT 5, TLS, optional client certificates and per-filter QoS. MQTT 5 is the default."],[2395,"The name is an abbreviation for the Italian Greyhound - small yet extremely fast dogs, the best in their class. See the lovely Fabio & Cookie ❤️"],[4402,"The name is missing from the message because of a known bug where error details are lost between the server and the client (#3735)."],[4067,"The names have no `iggy_` prefix, so watch for clashes if Prometheus scrapes other exporters with similar names."],[3312,"The Node.js and TypeScript SDK, from installation to keeping client and server versions in step."],[873,"The node's roster address. Replica-to-replica consensus traffic and follower-to-primary HTTP forwarding dial it, so it must be a **literal IPv4 or IPv6 address**. Hostnames and unspecified addresses (`0.0.0.0` or `::`) are rejected when the server resolves the roster at startup."],[3971,"The normal-status metadata primary performs callouts. Other nodes and view changes can produce retryable errors. Timeouts, network failures, overload, HTTP 429 and HTTP 5xx map to retryable failures. Other unsuccessful HTTP responses and invalid decisions terminate the login."],[770,"The official `apache/iggy` image ships the CLI alongside the server, installed as `/usr/local/bin/iggy`. The image entrypoint is the server, so override it to run the CLI:"],[2453,"The official Apache Iggy images can be found on Docker Hub. These docs target server 0.10.0. Use `docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0`, or `docker pull apache/iggy:edge` for a development image. SDK and tooling versions are independent of the server version."],[3139,"The official Iggy client SDKs, their packages and registries, and the transports each one supports."],[2462,"The official releases follow the regular semver (`0.5.0`) or have a `latest` tag applied (`apache/iggy:latest`)."],[303,"The offset artifact also retains partition history, retry state and producer state. These internal readability windows do not provide migration from a 0.9 data directory. Use the upgrade procedure."],[3649,"The old `[system]` table and its environment mappings are rejected. Removed placeholders such as `archive_expired` and `recreate_missing_state` must be removed, not set to `false`."],[3447,"The option keys differ per transport (for example QUIC uses `reconnection_max_retries` and `reconnection_reestablish_after` where TCP uses `reconnection_retries` and `reestablish_after`). Unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."],[3184,"The option keys differ per transport and unknown keys are rejected as **hard errors**. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables, defaults, and default ports."],[2001,"The optional `[plugin_config.state]` enables a separate JSON snapshot loaded during `open()` and saved during `close()`. Its loaded values can override the runtime checkpoint. It defaults to `./connector_states/elasticsearch_source_<numeric_id>.json`. `state_id` and `storage_config.base_path` customize that location. Only file storage is implemented. Selecting `elasticsearch`, `redis` or an unknown storage type warns and falls back to `./connector_states`, ignoring the supplied backend location."],[1351,"The optional `emulator_endpoint` and `emulator_grpc_endpoint` must be supplied together. They disable credentials and use plaintext connections. Use them only for local test services."],[2237,"The order of the sections in the file doesn't determine the execution order, so don't rely on one transform seeing the output of another."],[2721,"The parser does not percent-decode credentials or option values. Reserved separators such as `:` and `@` cannot be escaped inside a username or password. Use a PAT for credentials that contain them. Duplicate option keys use the last value. Boolean options enable only for the literal `true`. Use `false` to disable them."],[2559,"The partition has its own unique ID and belongs to the topic. The partition is responsible for storing the records. The records are being distributed between the partitions, therefore the partition acts as a simple database, which is optimized for the append-only operations. The partition is identified by its unique ID, which is an integer. Stream, topic and partition IDs are assigned **starting from 0**. Deleted stream and topic IDs can be reused. New partitions use IDs above the highest remaining partition ID, so deleting the highest partitions can also allow their IDs to be reused. The partition ID is unique per topic, thus the same partition ID can be used in multiple topics."],[2951,"The per-call `IIggyClient` API is explicit but verbose - you manage partitioning, batching, retries, and offsets yourself. The high-level `IggyPublisher` and `IggyConsumer` wrap that surface with fluent builders that handle:"],[4118,"The per-shard verifier evicts stale group-member connections. A link eviction does not itself end the logical session. The metadata liveness process expires its lease and rebalances membership. The session timeout is 1.2 times `heartbeat.interval`, 36 seconds by default, with periodic checks around that threshold."],[2136,"The plugin's retries cover transient polling `SELECT` failures, including connection I/O, pool timeouts, serialization failures and deadlocks. Connection setup, CDC fetches and delete/mark statements do not use that retry loop. A poll error is logged and polling continues without incrementing the runtime's forwarding-error counter. Repeated delivery or checkpoint failures instead receive `Nack`. The SDK stops after five consecutive Nacks."],[3948,"The policy applies to the completed server operation, not merely to enqueueing a request in a producer or receiving an early dispatch response."],[939,"The policy changes what a replica must retain before its acknowledgement can count toward commit. It does not replace quorum commit with a primary-only disk write."],[3328,"The poll response does not wait for the auto-commit to finish. The sample processes messages after polling, so auto-commit does not confirm that the application handled each message."],[3776,"The pool has 28 buckets with buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB."],[1721,"The PostgreSQL sink connector consumes messages from Iggy topics and stores them in a PostgreSQL table, with optional automatic table creation and configurable payload storage."],[2095,"The PostgreSQL source connector fetches data from PostgreSQL databases and streams it to Iggy topics. It supports incremental table polling and Change Data Capture (CDC) via logical replication."],[122,"The preamble selects a shard and is not authentication. ReplicaHello repeats the peer and link identity and supplies the link count. The acceptor rejects a mismatch. With authentication enabled, placement fields participate in the handshake MAC. Shard counts can differ between nodes."],[395,"The primary value is a configured replica ID, not a roster position. Retain the incarnation and metadata operation when constructing a request. Group ownership adds its own generation."],[426,"The primitives command payloads are built from: names, identifiers, consumers and the other shared encodings."],[4413,"The producer"],[2596,"The producer and consumer below match the getting-started examples in the repository."],[2639,"The producer connects, creates missing resources and sends ten messages to partition zero. Resource creation returns errors for existing names, which this example handles separately from other failures."],[3081,"The producer creates `sample-stream` and `sample-topic` (if missing) and sends 5 batches of 10 messages to partition `0`. The consumer polls them from offset `0` and exits after 5 batches. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are **0-based**. These repository examples use numeric stream and topic IDs `0`, so create no other streams or topics before running the producer on the fresh server. Run the producer first."],[3535,"The producer creates `sample-stream` and `sample-topic` if needed and sends five batches of ten messages. The consumer reads from the first message, stores no offset and exits after five batches. Running it again replays retained messages."],[4435,"The producer creates the stream and topic only if they are missing, so it can be run any number of times. A restarted service resumes from the group's stored offset. To start from nothing, stop the container, and `--rm` removes it along with its data."],[3450,"The producer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to send messages to the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke `client.producer()` to get the `IggyProducerBuilder` allowing you to configure the producer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"],[2795,"The project builds with the Bazel version pinned in `.bazelversion` (`BUILD.bazel` / `MODULE.bazel`). Its Rust bridge uses the Cargo and Rust toolchain provisioned by `rules_rust`, with a separate target directory in the Bazel output tree. Use a compatible Java runtime for Bazel. The pinned Bazel 9.2.0 requires Java 21 or newer."],[4458,"The Prometheus targets page also shows the `iggy` job as down."],[410,"The protocol version is a semver packed into one u32, 10 bits per component (each must be below 1024):"],[3288,"The Python implementation example shows follow and rebuild loops. The other SDK source guides show their equivalent typed queries."],[3394,"The Python SDK, a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, and how to install it."],[3071,"The query uses `wait_us`, `min_count`, `max_bytes` and `request_timeout_us` alongside the normal consumer, partition, strategy and count fields. A positive wait needs at least one second between the wait and request timeout. Configure a proxy timeout long enough to receive the reply."],[3952,"The query value `ack=replicated` selects the awaited path even when the topic uses `persisted`. It does not override the topic policy."],[2746,"The QUIC reconnection keys **differ from TCP**: `reconnection_max_retries` (not `reconnection_retries`) and `reconnection_reestablish_after` (not `reestablish_after`). Using the TCP names in a QUIC string is a hard error."],[1769,"The Quickwit connector allows you to send data to the Quickwit API using HTTP. At startup it checks readiness, creates a missing index, and appends messages as newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON). Each poll can become multiple requests, each limited to 8 MiB including document newlines."],[1803,"The RabbitMQ sink publishes messages to an AMQP 0.9.1 exchange. It declares the configured exchange, enables publisher confirmations and uses mandatory publishing. Configure a queue and binding that match the routing key before sending messages."],[2154,"The random source connector generates random messages and sends them to Iggy streams. This is useful for testing, benchmarking, and verifying that your connector pipeline is working correctly without needing an external data source."],[3034,"The reader and high-level consumer follow group assignments. A one-shot poll names one partition. They default to manual checkpoint commits. See Deferred polling and the Guide."],[3120,"The reader joins, refreshes assignments and leaves on `Close`. Its default wait is five seconds. `Commit` stores a delivered checkpoint after processing. `iggcon.AutoCommitPolicy(iggcon.DefaultAutoCommit())` selects automatic commits after processing. See Deferred polling."],[3529,"The reader reserves up to 16 MiB and 16000 messages for buffered and in-flight replies. If the poll count is larger, that count is the default message reservation. Configure `prefetch_bytes` and `prefetch_messages` to bound read-ahead independently of each fetch."],[3970,"The reason is optional. An allowed username must resolve to the provisioned account at admission. The server rejects malformed decisions and refuses redirects from the callout endpoint."],[1813,"The Redshift sink serializes batches to Parquet, stages them in S3 and loads an existing Redshift table with COPY. Configure both the Redshift connection and S3 access before starting the runtime."],[2196,"The reference crate is core/connectors/sources/random_source. Use its `Cargo.toml` from the matching checkout, including `[lib] crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"lib\"]` and its workspace dependency features. The Rust blocks in this sample implementation section combine into `src/lib.rs`. The trait above is an API reference and is not part of that file."],[1905,"The reference manifest supplies these dependencies:"],[2200,"The reference manifest supplies these dependencies:"],[3979,"The releases are published to GitHub and can be found here. The official Docker images can be found here, use `docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0`, or `apache/iggy:latest` for the newest stable release."],[2926,"The rental API returns payload slices backed by a pooled buffer. Dispose each rental and copy any bytes that must outlive it. Do not retain a payload or raw-header reference after disposal. The high-level consumer exposes `ReceiveRentedAsync` for this path."],[3572,"The repo's driver script builds, starts the server, runs a send and a poll benchmark, and shuts the server down. **Use it only in a disposable checkout on an isolated host: it deletes `local_data` and signals matching `iggy-server` and `iggy-bench` processes by name.**"],[3904,"The repository root carries its own `Dockerfile` and `docker-compose.yml` for building from source (a multi-stage build: Rust compilation including the Web UI, then a minimal runtime image):"],[3545,"The repository root contains the Swift Package Manager manifest and exports the `Iggy` product. The package dependency below names the 0.10.0 release tag:"],[3916,"The repository-root `Dockerfile` builds a local image with wildcard transport binds and `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost`. The release image uses `core/server/Dockerfile` and its `/app` data path. Keep the advertised address explicit in your deployment so reconnects work from the client network. Do not infer the release image's defaults from the local development Dockerfile."],[4030,"The requested count may not be allocated on a fragmented host. Boot-time reservation is more reliable. Reserved HugeTLB memory is unavailable for ordinary allocations or page cache, even while unused. Check NUMA placement and the actual pool, not just the requested count. See HugeTLB administration."],[354,"The response body:"],[923,"The response lists the cluster name, nodes, client endpoints, and current roles. `cluster m` is a shorthand for `cluster metadata`."],[401,"The response starts with a u32 section count, followed by a table of 56-byte section headers:"],[397,"The response uses the same 40-byte prefix as PollMessages. Waiting requests need a positive count, `min_count` from 1 through count and a byte budget of at least 344. A positive wait requires at least one second of reply margin. Waits cannot exceed 600 seconds, and request timeouts cannot exceed 630 seconds. The server can shorten waits through its configuration."],[4139,"The root user **cannot be deleted**, and its permissions are fixed: it always holds every permission."],[4147,"The root user has full access to everything and **bypasses all permission checks**. Other users can be assigned specific permissions using the CLI, HTTP API, SDK, or Web UI."],[919,"The roster `ip` is the address other replicas dial. It's **not the bind interface** for the client transports: `tcp`, `quic`, `http`, and `websocket` bind whatever their own `address` settings say, and the roster supplies only the port. The defaults listen on loopback, so a cluster spread across hosts needs each transport's `address` set to `0.0.0.0` or the routable interface. The server warns at startup when a bind cannot serve the advertised `ip`."],[120,"The roster supplies each node's `tcp_replica` port and literal IP address. Lower replica IDs dial higher IDs. Each pair can use several links, which distribute traffic across shards. The default frame limit is 64 MiB through `message_bus.max_message_size`."],[1201,"The runtime appends `source_{key}` as a URL path segment, preserving any query string. URL query values are redacted from runtime configuration logs and transport errors. Static request headers are passed to the state server. Configure this map in TOML. The other state settings support environment overrides such as `IGGY_CONNECTORS_STATE_STORAGE` and `IGGY_CONNECTORS_STATE_HTTP_URL`."],[1522,"The runtime applies stream decoding and configured transforms before the sink formats its requests. To forward opaque message bytes, use `schema = \"raw\"` with `batch_mode = \"raw\"` and no transforms. See the SDK format limits when selecting other schemas."],[1969,"The runtime auto-commits while polling, logs/counts plugin callback failures, and continues without replaying the failed batch. The sink retries transaction-conflict errors, connection/timeouts, and HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503 and 504. Delays use capped exponential backoff with jitter. `Retry-After` is not used. Connection errors trigger reconnection and repeat sign-in/health/optional DDL. A failed reconnect stops that chunk, and retries plus startup requests can exceed `query_timeout` in total."],[1709,"The runtime auto-commits while polling, records plugin errors and continues without replaying the failed batch. Earlier or later chunks may already be stored, and a timeout can leave the write outcome uncertain. Duplicate tolerance and retries do not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[1867,"The runtime auto-commits while polling. It logs/counts a failed plugin callback and continues without replaying that batch. A successful callback can mean only that messages were buffered, so runtime processed counts do not establish delivery to S3. A crash loses unflushed buffers. There is no write-ahead log or dead-letter queue."],[1808,"The runtime commits Iggy offsets on read and does not replay a failed callback after its retry budget. Publisher confirmations do not provide an end-to-end exactly-once guarantee. See the source reference."],[1357,"The runtime commits offsets on read and does not replay a failed sink callback. A successful processed count does not prove that every row reached BigQuery. Preserve Iggy metadata when downstream deduplication or reconciliation is required. See the source reference for type mappings and row-error handling."],[1827,"The runtime commits offsets on read. A COPY failure that outlasts plugin retries is logged and is not replayed by the runtime. A retry after an uncertain load can duplicate rows. See the source reference."],[1208,"The runtime forwards a source batch to Iggy, saves its optional checkpoint, then acknowledges the batch to the plugin. A failed send or checkpoint save produces a negative acknowledgement. Remote checkpoint durability depends on the state server. Connector lifecycle operations do not delete remote state."],[2212,"The runtime forwards the encoded payload, optional message ID and headers. Although `ProducedMessage` also accepts `checksum`, `timestamp` and `origin_timestamp`, the current forwarding path does not copy those three fields into the Iggy message."],[2277,"The runtime loads transforms generically by `TransformType`, so no runtime changes are needed."],[1916,"The runtime logs and counts decoding or transform failures, drops the affected messages and passes the remaining batch to the plugin. A plugin `consume()` error is counted as a failed batch. The runtime uses auto-commit when polling and does not automatically retry that failed batch. Any destination retry must be implemented by the plugin."],[1134,"The runtime logs via the tracing crate. Merge the runtime configuration snippets on this page into the minimal runtime configuration. The output format is configured in the `[logging]` section:"],[1196,"The runtime opens two Iggy TCP clients, one for producers and one for consumers. Set credentials matching the broker and a connector configuration provider. Omitted settings use the embedded defaults, including file-based source state storage. Save this example as `connectors.toml` in the repository root and replace `path/to/connectors` with your connector configuration directory."],[1643,"The runtime records a plugin error and continues polling with consumer auto-commit. Failed chunks and batches skipped by the circuit breaker are not queued for replay, so retries and the circuit breaker do not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee. Other chunks from the same batch may already have reached InfluxDB."],[1269,"The runtime selects `CommitPolicy::OnRead`. Offsets can advance before a plugin finishes its callback. A failed sink callback is recorded and does not cause the runtime to replay that batch. Deferred reads change waiting and batching, not this delivery guarantee."],[1209,"The runtime supports two types of configuration providers for managing connector configurations:"],[1176,"The runtime uses a shared Tokio runtime for its connector-management and forwarding tasks. Each loaded plugin library also has an SDK Tokio runtime shared by its instances. Additionally, it has built-in support for logging via tracing crate."],[1388,"The runtime uses consumer auto-commit and does not replay a failed sink batch. See sink guide. End-to-end at-least-once delivery is therefore not guaranteed. Plugin retries resend the same batch without an `insert_deduplication_token`, so a lost acknowledgement can also produce duplicate rows."],[1447,"The runtime uses consumer auto-commit before the sink finishes. After a chunk fails, the plugin still attempts later chunks and returns the first error. The runtime logs and counts that error, adds no processed messages for the failed batch, and continues polling without replaying it. A crash or exhausted retry budget can therefore lose data. A non-JSON payload aborts the remaining chunks immediately."],[3067,"The Rust automatic policy defaults to every 10,000 messages or five seconds, whichever happens first, after processing. It also stores progress on close, shutdown and partition revocation. Processing counts when the application requests the next item, a handler succeeds, or the reader closes. Marked progress supports applications that finish work concurrently. Do not close a reader as if processing succeeded after a failed handler."],[454,"The Rust binary SDK resolves balanced and key routing client-side and sends `kind = 2`. The binary server requires explicit partition routing. The HTTP API can resolve the other strategies."],[3130,"The Rust high-level producer supports opt-in idempotence on binary transports:"],[2298,"The Rust producer and Kafka gateway support opt-in idempotent batches. This suppresses retries within bounded producer history. It does not make external side effects atomic with offset commits."],[3414,"The Rust SDK examples in the core repository, and how to start a server to run them against."],[4341,"The Rust SDK exposes the same call as `describe_options`. The scope is `topic`, `stream`, or `user`."],[3185,"The Rust SDK exposes these operations through traits on `IggyClient`. Availability and method names vary across foreign SDKs and transports:"],[3158,"The Rust SDK is always the first to receive new features."],[3497,"The Rust SDK is the primary and most feature-complete client for Iggy. It is available on crates.io and the source code is part of the core repository."],[1051,"The Rust SDK needs no feature flags. Depend on the published crate:"],[3207,"The Rust SDK resolves these strategies before sending a binary request. The HTTP API can resolve the strategy on the server. Binary clients route to the selected partition primary."],[3492,"The Rust SDK, the primary and most complete Iggy client, and where its source lives."],[2574,"The Rust SDK's `IggyMessageHeader::to_bytes()` representation is 64 bytes, with little-endian fields. The diagram and table describe that SDK representation, not Rust struct memory layout. On the wire and on disk, messages travel inside batch records with a compact 48-byte per-message frame header. See the Binary Protocol section for the exact encodings."],[3183,"The Rust snippets assume an async Tokio context. HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Connection-string credentials are applied automatically only by the binary transports."],[1837,"The S3 sink connector writes messages from Iggy streams to Amazon S3 and S3-compatible object stores (MinIO, Cloudflare R2, DigitalOcean Spaces, Backblaze B2). Messages are buffered in memory and uploaded as files, rotated by size or message count, under configurable path templates with offset ranges in the object names."],[630,"The same 256-byte header layout carries two traffic planes. The pages below cover both:"],[3228,"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Return to `examples/java` for the client commands. Their CA path is relative to that directory."],[3090,"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Run the producer before the consumer with the TLS flags (paths relative to `examples/go`):"],[3364,"The sample below targets Iggy 0.10.0 on `127.0.0.1:8090`. Start the server:"],[1368,"The sample expects a local ClickHouse HTTP endpoint with user `default` and an empty password. Create a test table with matching columns:"],[3324,"The samples below expect an Iggy server on `127.0.0.1:8090`. See Getting started for the server prerequisites:"],[3405,"The samples below target Iggy 0.10.0 on `127.0.0.1:8090`:"],[2003,"The saved counters include fetched documents, successful polls (including empty polls), empty polls, serialized payload bytes, errors and the last error text. The average successful-poll duration includes the configured polling delay. Document-ID, scroll-ID and offset fields are retained in state but do not drive polling. These plugin counters are separate from the runtime's sent/error metrics."],[2723,"The scheme picks the transport. Each transport has its own default server port:"],[3105,"The SDK and the server must speak the same wire protocol. Tagged releases up to `v0.8.0` predate VSR (and the current API) and pair only with servers of the same era. The current SDK speaks VSR only. This page targets server `0.10.0`. Use a compatible SDK release or edge version. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."],[3972,"The SDK can replay a login. The service must accept repeated verification of the same credentials safely. One-time passwords that become invalid after the first verification do not survive this replay model. A failed external service never grants access by default."],[1301,"The SDK includes support for Protocol Buffers (protobuf) format with both encoding and decoding capabilities. Protocol Buffers provide efficient serialization and are particularly useful for high-performance data streaming scenarios."],[3023,"The SDK is built around the `IIggyClient` interface, which aggregates every feature (publishing, consuming, stream/topic management, users, offsets, consumer groups, and system operations). For the low-level per-call API and the full configuration reference, see the Guide. For the ergonomic, batteries-included producer/consumer abstractions, see the High-level SDK."],[2211,"The SDK permits only one batch in flight. Returning an error from `on_batch_result()` stops polling. Returning an error from `poll()` logs the error and continues the polling loop. A source that tracks a cursor or performs destructive upstream work must implement the callback instead of relying on its default no-op."],[3502,"The SDK provides two layers. The snippets below assume an async Tokio context with a `Result` return type."],[3544,"The SDK requires Swift 6.0 or later. Supported platforms are macOS 13, iOS 16, tvOS 16, watchOS 9, visionOS 1 and Linux. The runnable examples require Swift 6.1 or later."],[812,"The SDK retains this context with a request. A route refresh does not replace the history of an already uncertain operation. `HistoryUnavailable` requires an application decision about the new history. Readers expose a seek operation to choose a new start."],[3027,"The SDK supports two transport protocols:"],[3024,"The SDK targets .NET 8 and .NET 10 (`net8.0` and `net10.0`). Version `0.10.0` matches the server 0.10.0 protocol and includes both topic durability options. Version `0.8.0` uses the older TCP framing. When building from source, use the server and SDK from the same checkout."],[991,"The secret must be **at least 32 bytes** of CSPRNG output and **byte-identical on every node**."],[3998,"The seeder logs in as `iggy`/`iggy` by default. Pass `--username` and `--password` to use other credentials."],[2564,"The segment, being a part of the partition, is the actual **physical layer** which stores the records in the binary format in a form of the files. Each segment has a soft size limit (1 GiB unless the topic sets its own `segment_size`). A segment can exceed it by one whole batch before it is sealed and a new segment is created. The segment name is based on the start offset of the first record in the segment and is unique per partition."],[4189,"The segmented append-only log, and how streams, topics, partitions and segments map onto files on disk."],[3307,"The separate producer and consumer scripts do not consistently share resources: the envelope and headers consumers create new empty topics, and the tenant producer deletes its streams before exiting. The getting-started and basic producers spread batches over five partitions, while their consumers poll partition 0. Use the self-contained stream-builder command above for a complete send-and-consume walkthrough. The sink-data producer deletes its selected topic and stream when it finishes, including resources that already existed."],[3658,"The server configuration no longer has a `[system]` wrapper. Its runtime tables live at the root, and the data directory is the root `path` key. Old `IGGY_SYSTEM_*` overrides are rejected. Retention, durability, and segment layout are topic creation options, not server-wide defaults."],[4072,"The server doesn't export metrics over OTLP. To bring them into an OpenTelemetry pipeline, scrape `/metrics` with the collector's Prometheus receiver, using the same bearer token. Server export of logs and traces over OTLP isn't available yet. See `[telemetry]`."],[4465,"The server exports about forty metrics, including `http_requests_total`, `streams`, `topics`, `partitions`, `segments`, `messages`, `users` and `clients`. To list them all:"],[4056,"The server exports Prometheus metrics on its HTTP API at `/metrics`. Both the HTTP API and the metrics endpoint are on by default. See `[http.metrics]` to change the path or turn it off."],[4451,"The server flags are explained on Docker & Helm, and metrics are on by default. Grafana is on port 3001 because the server already uses 3000."],[2511,"The server is built on the **Viewstamped Replication (VSR)** consensus protocol. Metadata operations are committed through the VSR write-ahead log **even on a single node**, which is the default deployment. Multi-node clustering is enabled via the `[cluster]` configuration (a node roster plus `--replica-id` to pick the local entry). See clustering for the details and maturity caveats."],[3589,"The server is thread-per-core and shared-nothing, built on `io_uring` on Linux (via `compio`), with shard and CPU pinning configurable under `[sharding]`. Throughput and latency depend heavily on hardware, transport, and payload shape (`messages-per-batch * message-size`). Run the benchmarks on your own hardware, or browse current, dated results on the benchmarking platform."],[3634,"The server reads a single TOML file. A copy of `core/server/config.toml` is embedded into the binary and serves as the default configuration, so the server boots with working defaults even without any file on disk. The embedded file is commented throughout and is the authoritative reference for the exact version you're running."],[4431,"The server rebalances the group. The following example shows `svc-b` reading partition 2 while `svc-a` reads 0 and 1. Rebalances and retries can replay messages."],[4094,"The server runs one thread and runtime per selected shard (thread-per-core, shared-nothing), with CPU selection and optional pinning controlled by `[sharding]`. All listeners (every client transport) bind on shard 0 only. Shard 0's coordinator hands accepted plaintext TCP and WebSocket connections to peer shards round-robin by transferring the file descriptor at accept time. From then on the connection lives entirely on its shard. QUIC, TCP with TLS, WebSocket with TLS, and HTTP terminate on shard 0, because their per-connection state cannot move between threads. Sockets **never migrate** after accept: an operation that touches a partition owned by another shard rides the internal message bus instead."],[2695,"The server stores each message payload as bytes without interpreting it, so you choose the format: plain text, JSON, Protobuf or anything else. This page shows how to send more than one kind of message to the same topic, so the consumer knows how to decode each one."],[3981,"The server uses `io_uring` on Linux, which sets a minimum kernel version."],[901,"The server uses Linux `io_uring`. Use a recent Linux kernel and a sufficient locked-memory limit."],[3597,"The server's TOML configuration, how a file is merged over the embedded defaults, and what each section controls."],[4414,"The service"],[4436,"The service is the same consumer shape as the high-level consumer example in the repository, with a JSON handler and signal handling added."],[3772,"The service maps a caller to an existing active, non-root account. Local usernames never use the callout. See External authentication for the JSON contract and failure behavior."],[3968,"The service must return a successful HTTP status and a JSON decision:"],[3072,"The sharding configuration bounds parked requests, per-session requests, disk reads and completion delivery. HTTP requests without a binary session count against their user. Capacity exhaustion can return a retryable error. Keep retries within a bounded application deadline."],[2494,"The sharding system supports multiple allocation modes via the `cpu_allocation` config:"],[4014,"The shell's limits are not necessarily the service's limits. For a running process, inspect `/proc/PID/limits`, `/proc/PID/status`, and its cgroup limits. Replace `PID` with that process's ID. Record the VM type, vCPU topology, memory limit, disk type and provisioned throughput, filesystem, and network bandwidth with the result."],[3920,"The single-node write path"],[1275,"The Sink and Source traits, the connector macros, and the decoders and encoders used to read and write Iggy streams."],[1796,"The sink calls `POST /api/v1/<index_id>/ingest?commit=auto`. HTTP success means submission for indexing, not search visibility or acceptance of every document. The sink checks the status and does not inspect per-document rejection counts in the response. See the Quickwit ingest API."],[1788,"The sink does not add Iggy IDs, offsets, timestamps or headers. It serializes the payload after runtime decoding and transforms:"],[1879,"The sink is represented by the single `Sink` trait, which defines the basic interface for all sink connectors. It provides methods for initializing the sink, writing data to external destination, and closing the sink."],[1601,"The sink logs each skipped message with its offset. It does not expire table snapshots or rewrite manifests. Run table maintenance through the catalog or an engine that supports it, with retention appropriate for your time-travel requirements."],[1807,"The sink pipelines publishes and waits for confirmations. Transient failures retry from the first unconfirmed message. An in-flight tail can already be delivered, so retries can duplicate it. An unroutable mandatory publish fails the batch and forces a new connection before the next poll."],[1868,"The sink retries HTTP 408, 429, 5xx and client errors. Other HTTP statuses stop the upload immediately. Its exponential backoff includes jitter and a 60-second cap, without handling `Retry-After`. The S3 library also retries transport errors once internally and uses a 60-second request timeout, so `max_attempts` counts outer `PutObject` calls rather than individual network requests or a total time budget. The startup probe does not use the sink's upload retry loop."],[1268,"The sink starts processing when the read is ready, its byte budget is reached or the wait ends. Replace the old `poll_interval` field. A sink that still supplies it fails to start, even when `max_wait` is also present."],[1758,"The sink uses plain `INSERT`, with no conflict-ignore or upsert clause. Reusing an ID already in the table rejects the entire chunk, including any new IDs alongside it. There is no transaction covering the complete poll. The runtime auto-commits while polling and does not replay failed chunks. A connection failure can leave the write outcome uncertain. These retries do not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[3742,"The size knobs are optional. Leaving one unset keeps the `compio-ws` (tungstenite) default shown in parentheses. A malformed size string fails config load."],[2796,"The snippet below mirrors the SDK's own end-to-end tests in `foreign/cpp/tests/e2e`. Start a local server on `127.0.0.1:8090` with the `iggy`/`iggy` credentials first. The stream name must not already exist."],[3442,"The snippets assume an async function with a `Result` return type and the `iggy`, Tokio and `futures-util` dependencies. Use the Rust SDK setup and a development server with the sample credentials."],[3109,"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are **0-based**: the first partition of a topic is partition `0`."],[3247,"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are **0-based**: the first partition of a topic is partition `0`."],[2171,"The source is represented by the single `Source` trait, which defines the basic interface for all source connectors. It provides methods for initializing the source, reading data from it, and closing the source."],[2025,"The source retains a negatively acknowledged batch and retries it with bounded backoff. There is no fixed NACK stop limit by default. Set `max_consecutive_nacks` only when accepted webhooks can be replayed externally after a restart. A stopped source can leave a buffer that no task drains."],[2084,"The source returns no durable connector checkpoint. Recovery after restart depends on the broker retaining the MQTT session. MQTT packet IDs are temporary and cannot serve as durable deduplication keys. The source does not provide an end-to-end exactly-once guarantee, including when MQTT uses QoS 2."],[1308,"The source sends 100 records, then continues polling without new messages. Stdout logs message offsets and the serialized JSON envelope bytes, containing `type_url` and base64 `value`. The sink's `raw` schema also determines how the plugin receives those transformed bytes."],[2449,"The source tree contains 17 sinks and 6 sources. Sinks include PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, Apache Iceberg, Delta Lake, Quickwit and S3. Sources include PostgreSQL, Elasticsearch, InfluxDB, HTTP, MQTT and random data generation. See the connectors documentation for the full catalog."],[1303,"The source's `schema = \"proto\"` selects the default protobuf encoder, which wraps each JSON record in a `google.protobuf.StringValue` inside `google.protobuf.Any`. The sink reads raw bytes and applies `proto_convert` to expose the Any envelope as JSON. This does not unpack a custom protobuf message schema."],[4440,"The stack"],[1927,"The stdout sink connector prints messages from Iggy streams to the standard output. This is useful for debugging, development, and verifying that your connector pipeline is working correctly."],[965,"The storage mechanisms are implemented in the partition persistence worker and prepare journal. The crash-recovery tests exercise acknowledged persisted messages and offsets below ordinary flush thresholds, on both a singleton and a three-replica cluster."],[3519,"The stream builder creates an `IggyProducer`, an `IggyConsumer`, or both from configuration objects. It uses the same APIs described in High-level SDK. Stream and topic names identify the resources. The 0.10 API does not take stream or topic IDs in these configuration objects."],[341,"The stream catalog accepts a create-only, write-only `encryption_key`. An omitted key uses the server creation default. See Stream options."],[2138,"The stream config should use `schema = \"json\"`. Flat-schema sinks such as Iceberg need the `unwrap_envelope` transform to extract the `data` field. See the transforms documentation."],[4267,"The stream key travels in the create request. Use a transport with TLS. The server never returns the data key or its wrapped bytes. Reads report `encryption_key_source` as `stream`, `server` or `none`, and fingerprints for encrypted streams."],[733,"The stream permission format is `STREAM_ID[:STREAM_PERMISSIONS][#TOPIC_ID[:TOPIC_PERMISSIONS]]`:"],[3530,"The stream-builder examples cover producer, consumer and combined configurations. The source consumer configuration defines each builder field."],[1470,"The stream-level `batch_length` governs batching: each consume cycle submits its supported messages in one bulk request. The connector performs no retries of its own. Request, HTTP-status, and response-decoding failures return an error. The runtime logs and counts it, adds no processed messages for that batch, and continues polling. Consumer auto-commit occurs before indexing completes, and the failed poll is not replayed."],[1795,"The strict mapping above accepts only mapped fields and requires `timestamp`. To use wrapper documents, map their fields or use `mode: dynamic`, and remove `timestamp_field` and its dependent `retention` section unless every document supplies a timestamp. The `add_fields` transform enriches JSON objects. It does not add timestamps to raw/text wrappers created later by this sink."],[1939,"The SurrealDB sink connector writes messages from Iggy streams into SurrealDB over the HTTP API. Each poll is split into chunks of at most `batch_size` messages, with one SurrealQL bulk `INSERT IGNORE ... RETURN NONE` for each chunk containing valid records. Every record gets a deterministic record id derived from stream, topic, partition, offset, and message id, so replaying the same identities leaves existing records untouched. Messages are not buffered across polls."],[3533,"The Swift examples use the SDK from the same checkout. Start the server from the repository root with development credentials and a separate data directory:"],[3543,"The Swift SDK 0.2.0 provides an async TCP client with optional TLS, resource management, producers, partition readers and consumers. It routes partition requests to their primaries and reconnects with heartbeat support."],[1585,"The table declared in the message field cannot be loaded, including missing tables and catalog lookup failures."],[1586,"The table name has no namespace or contains an empty name component."],[1416,"The table schema is captured once at startup and not refreshed, so restart the connector after changing it."],[2719,"The tables below describe the Rust SDK and its Python, C++ and PHP bindings. Native Go, Node.js, C# and Swift clients also accept TCP connection strings. Java supports a smaller subset, described below."],[3677,"The tables below list every section with its shipped defaults. In cluster mode, every enabled transport takes its **port** from this node's `cluster.nodes` `ports` entry. The transport's own `address` picks only the bind interface."],[1430,"The target database and table must be pre-created. The connector never issues DDL. `database` and `table` must match `[A-Za-z0-9_]+` or startup fails. Streams must use `schema = \"json\"`. Startup validates the configuration but does not check the table or connection. These are first used when a batch arrives."],[3253,"The TCP builder also accepts TLS and resilience options. Start the TLS development server and run this fragment with `examples/java` as the working directory:"],[3257,"The TCP builders accept `fromConnectionString`. Only `iggy://` and `iggy+tcp://`, username/password credentials, `tls` and `tls_ca_file` are supported. PAT connection strings and additional options are rejected. The builder also exposes shutdown quiet-period configuration for applications that need prompt close."],[2880,"The TCP client pings the server on its own every `HeartbeatInterval` while connected, so an idle session survives the server's heartbeat verification and consumer-group memberships and assignments stay fresh. The ping goes through the regular request path: a lost session is repaired by the same reconnect and auto-login any other request would trigger. Heartbeat failures are logged and the loop keeps going until the client is disposed."],[3308,"The TCP/TLS pair needs a TLS-enabled source server. Run this command from the repository root, then the two `test:tcp-tls:*` scripts from `examples/node`, where they load `../../core/certs/iggy_ca_cert.pem`:"],[4060,"The token can read every metric the server exports, so store it like any other secret: in a Kubernetes Secret or your secrets manager, not in a file you commit. With the Helm chart, set it through `server.serviceMonitor.authorization`. See Docker & Helm."],[4059,"The token is printed once. The Prometheus scrape config is in `[http.metrics]`."],[2557,"The topic is also the logical concept, which is a part of the stream. The topic is identified by its ID, which is unique within its stream. You could think of topic as an entity being responsible for storing the specific type of the records. For example, you could have a topic for the user events, and another topic for the order events, etc."],[364,"The trailing `ack` byte is mandatory on both write commands. A payload without it fails to decode. Ack levels:"],[3584,"The transport subcommands are `tcp`, `quic`, `http`, and `websocket` (alias `ws`). Consumer-group workloads (`bcg`, `bpcg`, and `e2ecg`) require a binary transport: HTTP consumer-group joining returns `FeatureUnavailable`, even though the benchmark CLI accepts that combination. Each transport subcommand takes `--server-address` for a non-default server, for example:"],[1310,"The two `[[streams]]` shapes differ: a source produces to a single `topic` and can tune batching via `batch_length` and `linger_time`, while a sink consumes from a list of `topics` and can additionally set `batch_length`, `max_wait`, `min_count`, and `consumer_group`."],[1891,"The two flags at the end are optional and default to `false`: `verbose` switches the connector's per-batch logging to info level, and `benchmark` emits per-batch timing events for performance measurement."],[2802,"The typed `IggyBlockingClient` also provides resource management, readers, consumers, offsets, user administration and client inspection. Personal access token creation is exposed by the bridge. Use `iggy.hpp` for typed `StreamCreateOptions`, `TopicCreateOptions`, update patches and `PollOptions`."],[325,"The u32 command codes. Replicated commands are identified on the wire by their `operation` byte (see Operation discriminants). The code column below is the protocol-level registry and, for non-replicated commands, the value carried in header bytes 196..200."],[2037,"The V2 example requires an existing `telemetry` bucket in `iggy_org`, a token with read access, and `cpu` points with a numeric `usage` field and a `host` tag. Save this entry in the runtime's connector directory and start the runtime from the checkout root."],[953,"The WAL also retains predecessors across message and offset operations. Consequently:"],[4481,"The Web UI is a purely client-side application: every call to the Iggy HTTP API is issued by your browser, not by the Web UI container or server. Two things follow from this:"],[4485,"The Web UI is compiled directly into the Iggy server binary via the `iggy-web` cargo feature, which is enabled by default (official binaries and Docker images include it). To serve it at the `/ui` endpoint, flip the `web_ui` flag (disabled by default):"],[4491,"The Web UI lives in the `web/` folder of the repository. Use a Node.js version supported by the frontend toolchain (`^20.19.0 || ^22.13.0 || >=24`, for example Node.js 22.13 or later in the 22.x series) and `npm` (`pnpm` and `yarn` are not part of the supported workflow):"],[2444,"The Web UI provides a comprehensive dashboard for the Iggy server, built with SvelteKit and TypeScript. It can run in two modes:"],[4493,"The Web UI provides the following pages and functionality:"],[3402,"The wheel bundles the Rust SDK, whose binary transports speak the current Iggy wire protocol without falling back to older formats. Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build the SDK and server from the same source checkout. The published wheel can lag source API changes."],[3890,"The wildcard says nothing about where clients reach the container, so the server refuses to start until `IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS` supplies that address. Use `localhost` when the ports are published to the host, the compose service name when the clients are containers on the same network, and the external hostname or load balancer name when they are further away. The value reaches clients through cluster metadata, where it is the endpoint they reconnect through."],[3888,"The working directory is `/app` and the `iggy-server` and `iggy` binaries are on `PATH` (`/usr/local/bin`). The default data directory `local_data` therefore resolves to `/app/local_data` - **mount your volume there**, or set `IGGY_PATH` and mount that path instead."],[2646,"Then run the consumer:"],[4025,"There are three distinct controls:"],[3001,"There is **no built-in deserializer counterpart** - consumers implement `IDeserializer<T>` themselves (see Typed consumer)."],[3702,"There is **no default secret**. When both secrets are empty and cluster authentication supplies no key, each server start mints a random signing key, so issued tokens **do not survive a restart** and are valid only on the node that issued them. If you configure a secret, set it through the environment (`IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET` / `IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET`), never commit it, and use a long random value."],[4217,"There is no index caching configuration: the in-memory index cache is an internal per-segment structure the server manages itself."],[4355,"There is no migration from a 0.9.x data directory. Keep a backup and export messages that you need with the old server and compatible clients. Start 0.10.0 with a separate, empty data directory. Import the retained data through a compatible client. Do not point the new server at the old directory."],[798,"There's no `--context` flag: the active context is persistent state, switched with `iggy context use <name>`. Flags passed on the command line override the corresponding values from the active context. Credential fields merge independently: supplying `--username` does not clear a context token, which still has higher authentication priority."],[3309,"These are repository development certificates."],[1311,"These are SDK configuration fields, not Random or Stdout `plugin_config` keys. The runtime's `schema = \"proto\"` uses the default encoder or decoder."],[28,"These are the development credentials configured in that guide. Use your broker's credentials or a PAT when connecting to an existing installation."],[3059,"These are the shared poll defaults. High-level consumers can select a positive wait by default. The SDK derives a request timeout from the wait plus 10 seconds. The wire protocol permits waits up to 600 seconds, but the server caps them at `sharding.deferred_poll_max_wait`, 30 seconds by default."],[3931,"These are topic creation options, not `config.toml` settings or server environment overrides. `GetTopic` reports the effective values. Both policies are create-only. Resource updates cannot change them on an existing topic."],[3427,"These certificates are for local testing. Then run:"],[2360,"These docs describe Apache Iggy 0.10.0. The upgrade guide covers the coordinated cutover and fresh data directory. The 0.9 docs remain available."],[3323,"These docs target server **0.10.0** and Node SDK **0.11.0**. The Node package 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. The current SDK speaks only the VSR wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats."],[767,"These docs target server 0.10.0 and CLI 0.15.0, prepared for the same release. The CLI has its own version number, so use the version that matches your server. Install it from crates.io with:"],[3389,"These examples target Iggy 0.10.0. Start the server:"],[3217,"These examples target server **0.10.0** and speak the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples log in as `iggy`/`iggy`. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` or `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` overrides:"],[3303,"These examples target server **0.10.0**. See Getting started for prerequisites:"],[4479,"These instructions target Iggy server 0.10.0. Use a matching source checkout for local builds."],[2081,"These plugin limits are separate from the runtime producer's `batch_length` and `linger_time`. Configure only one destination `[[streams]]` entry per source instance. There is no per-message destination routing."],[3887,"These properties of the published image matter for deployment:"],[3039,"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the equivalent low-level, per-call flow, see the Guide."],[2812,"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the low-level, per-call equivalents, see the Guide."],[4291,"These settings apply whichever language your tests are in:"],[867,"These settings live under `[cluster.coordinator]` and apply when the server has more than one shard:"],[4138,"These variables initialize the root account only when it is first created. Supplied values are still validated on later starts, but they do not replace the stored credentials. Use the password-change API or `iggy user password` with the current password to change an existing account. The `--fresh` flag deletes all data. It is not needed for a password change."],[2899,"Things to know:"],[768,"This builds and installs the `iggy` binary. With cargo-binstall, you can download a prebuilt binary instead. It builds from source if there isn't one for your platform:"],[3520,"This complete example uses a local server with the development credentials. The producer creates missing resources during initialization. Existing topics keep their configuration."],[977,"This configuration requires at least three cluster nodes. A topic can override it at creation. Increasing the number of partitions does not change the factor."],[3243,"This documentation targets server **0.10.0** and SDK **0.10.0**. The TCP transport speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. The Java artifact `0.8.0` uses the older protocol. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."],[2513,"This example shows a fresh partition with the default paths. The stream, topic and partition directories are named after their numeric IDs, **assigned from 0**. The `metadata/journal.wal` file is the VSR write-ahead log for metadata operations (stream/topic/user creation, etc.). Its index has 1024 slots by default (`journal_slots` under `[metadata]`). Before they run out, the server checkpoints the metadata into `metadata/snapshot.bin`, records the checkpoint in a superblock under `metadata/`, and drops the checkpointed operations from the log. Root initialization is recorded through metadata replication, and startup checks that journal or checkpoint state proves initialization. The `runtime/current_config.toml` file is rewritten at every boot with the configuration the server booted with and the addresses it actually bound. Secrets (the JWT secrets, the encryption key and the cluster shared secret) are left out. The `state/log` file is empty. The server creates it at boot and does not otherwise use it. Segment files are named by their 20-digit start offset. Recovery can create an empty active segment at a reserved offset beyond the last stored message. Partition superblocks record the recovery frontiers. A superblock is written to two files in turn, `superblock.a` and `superblock.b`. A fresh partition has only `superblock.a`. `superblock.b` appears at the next superblock write, for example after a later persistence operation. The `.index` file is created automatically and speeds up searches by keeping track of the offsets and timestamps of the records. The `offsets/` directory holds the server-side consumer and consumer group offsets."],[4297,"This example uses `testcontainers` 0.27 and the `iggy` crate 0.12, which goes with server 0.10.0."],[4301,"This example uses `testcontainers` with `apache-iggy` 0.11.0, the Node.js SDK that goes with server 0.10.0, and the built-in `node:test` runner. `testcontainers` needs Node.js 22.19 or newer."],[4299,"This example uses `testcontainers`, `pytest` and `pytest-asyncio` with `apache-iggy` 0.10.0:"],[1302,"This example uses the Random source and Stdout sink from the matching checkout. Start a server using the **getting-started guide**, then build the plugins, runtime, and CLI from the repository root:"],[3571,"This flag affects only the first creation of the root user. Existing credentials remain unchanged, and environment variables take precedence. For another server, pass its credentials with `iggy-bench -u USER -p PASSWORD` before the benchmark kind."],[3551,"This fragment uses an existing connected client. Commit after processing. Automatic commits use `.auto(.default)`, which counts processing and stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. `.onRead` stores with the read. Close readers and shut down consumers to finish offset work and leave groups."],[2864,"This guide covers client configuration and the full `IIggyClient` API surface - the low-level, per-call operations. For the ergonomic producer/consumer abstractions built on top of these, see the High-level SDK."],[2631,"This guide targets server **0.10.0**. Use its official Docker image:"],[2393,"This is **not yet another extension** running on top of existing infrastructure, such as Kafka or SQL database."],[2702,"This is the better choice for Protobuf or any other binary format, because the payload doesn't have to fit inside JSON. See the message-type example."],[3330,"This loop waits for messages until it is stopped. It stores checkpoints after printing. The group has its own offsets, separate from a standalone consumer."],[3851,"This node's own client-facing identity. The address is read only while `cluster.enabled = false`: in cluster mode the roster answers the same question per node, so the address itself is ignored and the server says so at startup. The key is still validated in every mode, so a malformed value refuses boot even with `cluster.enabled = true`."],[766,"This page covers installing the CLI, connecting to a server, authenticating, and managing connection contexts. The full command tree is documented in the command reference."],[2480,"This page covers the internals of the Iggy server: how work is scheduled across CPU cores, how requests are routed, and how data ends up on disk. If you're looking for the domain model (streams, topics, partitions, consumer groups, message format), see concepts first."],[4383,"This page covers the problems people most often hit the first time they run Apache Iggy. Each entry shows what you see, what causes it, and how to fix it."],[4057,"This page covers the server's own metrics. The connectors runtime has a separate endpoint with its own metric names. See Connectors observability."],[2680,"This page gets a server running and a message sent and received, in one file. To build separate producer and consumer applications step by step, see Getting started. For other languages, see the SDK section."],[1366,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream clickhouse_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1400,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream delta_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1610,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream influxdb_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1653,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream meilisearch_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1838,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream s3_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1940,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream surrealdb_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[2096,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including payload extraction recipes, custom query parameters, and sink round-trip examples, is the upstream postgres_source README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1722,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including SQL query recipes for each payload format, is the upstream postgres_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1495,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including the full configuration table, deployment patterns, and performance analysis, is the upstream http_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[2035,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including the full cursor semantics and stuck-timestamp handling, is the upstream influxdb_source README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[1428,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including the label scheme, redirect security model, and operational guidance, is the upstream doris_sink README in the `apache/iggy` repository."],[694,"This page lists every top-level `iggy` command. Examples assume you're already authenticated, for example via `iggy login`. See installation and authentication. Run `iggy <command> --help` for the full flag list of any command."],[2794,"This SDK isn't published to any package registry. Consume it from the monorepo and build it from source with Bazel."],[628,"This section describes **binary protocol version 0.12.0**. The protocol version is the `major.minor.patch` of the `iggy_binary_protocol` crate, ignoring prerelease tags, and it's exchanged and checked during login (see Connection lifecycle). The crate itself is the source of truth: request and response types document their payload layouts, and the server dispatch defines which operations are supported."],[1899,"This sink keeps an in-memory invocation counter. The runtime does not persist sink plugin state. Iggy consumer-group offsets are managed separately."],[3942,"This table describes acknowledged messages on one node, with no later retention or deletion:"],[2625,"This tutorial uses the **low-level Rust SDK** to show how things work under the hood - creating streams, topics, sending and polling messages step by step. This is great for understanding the fundamentals."],[2595,"This tutorial uses the Python SDK's low-level API (`IggyClient`) directly, so you can see each step. It needs Python 3.11 or newer. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer. MacOS has no kernel requirement. See System requirements."],[2467,"Thread per core (shared nothing) + io_uring"],[1049,"Three replicas tolerate one unavailable replica. Five tolerate two. Larger groups use a capped replication quorum and a growing view-change quorum, so the usual `2f + 1` rule does not apply to every group size. See Cluster durability for the quorum rules. Use **at least three replicas** for one-node fault tolerance. A two-node cluster is useful for development, but it **cannot make progress** after either node fails."],[4,"Through the MCP server, an MCP client can check server health and statistics, create and manage streams, topics and partitions, send and poll messages, manage consumer groups and offsets, and administer users and personal access tokens. It connects to Iggy over TCP with its own credentials, and supports **HTTP** (the default) and **stdio** transports, with stdio suited to clients running on the same machine."],[1537,"Throughput is dominated by batch-mode choice: the runtime calls `consume()` sequentially within each topic task, so a full N-message batch uses N requests in `individual`/`raw`, or one in `ndjson`/`json_array`, before retries or skipped messages. Connection pooling and HTTP keep-alive are enabled by default. `max_connections` limits idle connections retained per host, not concurrent requests. Topic tasks can issue requests concurrently. Each topic awaits its own batch before continuing. Tune pooling and `batch_length` for the receiving service."],[1207,"Timeouts, connection failures, `425`, `429` and `5xx` responses are retried. `max_attempts` counts retries after the first request, so the default 4 allows up to 5 requests. Integer-seconds `Retry-After` values are honored up to `max_backoff`. Other save failures, including version conflicts or missing ETags, latch the provider: further saves fail until the connector restarts. An HTTP state-load failure while an enabled source starts aborts runtime startup."],[2072,"TLS"],[3077,"TLS"],[3099,"TLS"],[4084,"TLS"],[3617,"TLS across transports"],[759,"TLS and encryption"],[1001,"TLS requires `cluster.auth.enabled = true`, and the server **refuses to start** otherwise. The reason: replica TLS carries no client certificates, so it authenticates the acceptor only. The PSK handshake authenticates the peer. TLS supplies confidentiality. Without the PSK requirement, any TLS-capable host could register as a replica."],[3376,"TLS uses the Rust SDK connection-string format, for example `iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port?tls=true&tls_domain=localhost&tls_ca_file=/path/to/ca.pem`."],[4468,"To add a panel, edit `grafana/dashboards/iggy.json` and Grafana picks up the change within a few seconds. Watch the denied requests panel: if it starts to climb, the server is too busy to take more requests. The server's metrics settings are under `[http.metrics]` in the configuration reference."],[769,"To build it from source instead, use the same checkout as your server and run this from its root:"],[4384,"To collect diagnostic data for a bug report instead, see the `snapshot` command in CLI commands."],[2271,"To consume raw Avro records in a sink, configure `schema = \"avro\"` and `avro_schema_json` or `avro_schema_path` in its `[[streams]]` entry. The runtime decodes the datum into JSON before running transforms. A following `avro_convert` with `source_format = \"avro\"` would therefore receive the wrong payload variant."],[1125,"To find out more about the transforms, please refer to the **Transforms documentation**. For the stream decoders and encoders, see the **SDK documentation**."],[4388,"To make the client fail with an error instead of retrying forever, set `reconnection_retries=0` on the connection string, or call `.with_reconnection_max_retries(Some(0))` on the Rust client builder. Turning on logging in your application also shows each reconnect attempt."],[3107,"To pin the release that matches server 0.10.0:"],[3969,"To reject the login:"],[2584,"To see how the server schedules these concepts across CPU cores and stores them on disk, head over to architecture."],[3996,"To seed the example data, start a development server with known credentials from the root of the repository:"],[2917,"To send many payloads without a `byte[]` allocation per message, build them into a single pooled buffer with `RentedMessageBatchBuilder` - see Publishing with rented batches."],[3335,"To start again with an empty server, stop the container and run the `docker run` command again. `--rm` removes the container and its data."],[2613,"To start again with an empty server, stop the container, which `--rm` removes, and run `docker run` again."],[4466,"To stop everything:"],[3481,"To store progress after successful processing, use:"],[1200,"To use an HTTP state server, replace the `[state]` section above and add:"],[3025,"To use the source SDK with these examples, run from the Iggy checkout root:"],[1910,"tokio"],[2205,"tokio"],[1214,"Top-level `plugin_config` fields can be overridden per connector via environment variables using the `IGGY_CONNECTORS_{SINK|SOURCE}_{KEY}_PLUGIN_CONFIG_<FIELD>` convention, e.g. `IGGY_CONNECTORS_SINK_QUICKWIT_PLUGIN_CONFIG_URL`."],[1897,"Top-level fields of `plugin_config` can be overridden (or injected) the same way with the `IGGY_CONNECTORS_SINK_[KEY]_PLUGIN_CONFIG_[FIELD]` pattern. This lets you supply credentials without writing them into the TOML file:"],[2190,"Top-level fields of `plugin_config` can be overridden (or injected) the same way with the `IGGY_CONNECTORS_SOURCE_[KEY]_PLUGIN_CONFIG_[FIELD]` pattern. This lets you supply credentials without writing them into the TOML file:"],[827,"Top-level settings"],[672,"topic"],[2541,"Topic"],[2321,"Topic auto-creation is disabled by default. Enable `IGGY_KAFKA_AUTO_CREATE_TOPICS_ENABLED` only when Metadata requests are allowed to create missing topics. `IGGY_KAFKA_AUTO_CREATE_TOPICS_PARTITIONS` defaults to one and cannot exceed 1000."],[2323,"Topic configuration changes support `retention.ms`. The gateway is not a complete Kafka broker implementation. Unsupported API keys and most unsupported versions close the connection. ApiVersions has a discovery error response for unsupported versions. Use the protocol coverage for exact API version ranges."],[2845,"Topic options"],[4303,"Topic Options"],[4504,"Topic updates use the HTTP set/reset contract. Omitted fields keep their current values. See Topic options for mutable fields and creation-only policies. Some settings require the CLI or HTTP API."],[4055,"Topic usage and replica progress"],[4170,"Topic-scoped `poll_messages` on topic 5 of stream 1 lets the user poll **only** from that topic."],[15,"Topics"],[311,"Topics"],[2844,"Topics"],[1911,"tracing"],[2206,"tracing"],[2500,"Traditional async runtimes like tokio use `epoll` which is **readiness-based** - you ask the kernel \"is this file descriptor ready?\" and then perform the I/O yourself. Regular files cannot be registered with epoll. Tokio runs file I/O on a blocking thread pool that grows on demand (up to 512 threads by default, configurable). This does not scale well."],[1276,"Traits"],[2327,"Transactions are unsupported. Transactional producer initialization and Produce requests are refused. Do not configure `transactional.id`."],[1284,"Transform Options"],[1073,"Transformations"],[1064,"Transforms"],[1279,"Transforms"],[1364,"Transforms"],[1398,"Transforms"],[1426,"Transforms"],[1608,"Transforms"],[1651,"Transforms"],[1682,"Transforms"],[1836,"Transforms"],[1938,"Transforms"],[2220,"Transforms"],[2234,"Transforms are configured per connector in the `transforms` section of the connector configuration file. Each transform type is a table keyed by its name, so a connector can have at most one instance of each type:"],[1590,"Transforms are declared at the top level of the connector file, next to `[plugin_config]`, and every `[transforms.*]` section requires `enabled = true`. See the transforms documentation for details."],[1675,"Transforms can be applied before indexing into Meilisearch. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[1390,"Transforms can be applied before inserting into ClickHouse. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[1453,"Transforms can be applied before loading into Doris. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[1418,"Transforms can be applied before writing to Delta Lake. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[1644,"Transforms can be applied before writing to InfluxDB. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[1871,"Transforms can be applied before writing to S3. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[1972,"Transforms can be applied before writing to SurrealDB. See the transforms documentation for the available types and their configuration."],[2232,"Transforms mutate, filter, or convert messages as they flow through the connector runtime. They run inside the runtime process, not inside the plugins. For source connectors they're applied after the plugin's messages are decoded and before producing to Iggy. For sink connectors, after consuming from Iggy and before handing the messages to the plugin."],[1526,"Transient failures allow up to `max_retries` additional attempts after the initial request: the default `3` means at most four attempts. Retry delays use full jitter from zero up to `min(retry_delay * retry_backoff_multiplier^n, max_retry_delay)`, where `n` starts at zero for the first retry. With the defaults, the three delay caps are 1, 2 and 4 seconds."],[1757,"Transient insert failures are retried within the chunk. `max_retries` counts total attempts, and waits grow linearly as `retry_delay`, twice that delay, and so on. Retried SQLSTATEs are `40001`, `40P01`, `57P01`, `57P02`, `57P03`, `08000`, `08003` and `08006`. SQLx I/O and pool-acquisition timeouts are also retried. Other errors stop that chunk's retry loop. Startup has no plugin retry loop."],[1673,"Transient submission and task-status errors are retried within their budgets. A failed indexing task is returned as an error without resubmitting it. A task timeout does not cancel the remote task, so it may finish later. The runtime records plugin errors and continues polling with consumer auto-commit. It does not replay failed batches. This does not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee, and retries or manual replay after uncertain outcomes can repeat writes."],[4007,"Transparent huge pages"],[92,"Transport"],[4130,"Transport encryption (TLS)"],[424,"Transport loss alone does not end a retained session. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation do. A password or PAT change does not revoke an already established session. Once the original session ends, unresolved operations cannot recover their old receipts through a new login."],[4077,"Transport protocols"],[2708,"Transport selection"],[3559,"Transports"],[4046,"Trial a change with a runtime setting first. Keep the previous value for rollback. Persist accepted sysctl values in a dedicated `/etc/sysctl.d/` file and accepted service limits or allocator variables in that service's systemd drop-in. THP sysfs writes require a boot-time mechanism of their own. Recheck effective values after reboot and process restart."],[4373,"Troubleshooting"],[4031,"Try the allocator option on a mimalloc-enabled server, with access to the pool configured for its service account:"],[3833,"Tunables for the internal bus that ships consensus traffic between replicas and client traffic between shards."],[3812,"Tunables for the metadata consensus plane (shard 0's VSR replica: users, streams, topics, sessions)."],[1273,"Turning the mode off preserves plugin state in the ordinary format after the next save. Before rolling back to an older runtime, disable it and wait for that save. Older runtimes do not understand the producer record. See Idempotent producers."],[1003,"Two certificate modes exist:"],[2729,"Two forms are accepted:"],[4235,"Two independent retention policies exist, both **per-topic options configurable at creation or update** (see Topic options). The segment cleaner enforces them:"],[4161,"Two kinds of implication apply on top of the table:"],[1135,"Two per-connector flags, both disabled by default, add more detail. Set them at the top level of an existing connector configuration file, before any TOML section headers:"],[2235,"Two rules apply to every transform:"],[3645,"Two variables live outside the config schema: `IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME` and `IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD` set the root credentials, always as a pair. They initialize the root user **only at first creation**. On an existing data directory the stored root user is recovered unchanged, but supplied environment credentials must still pass validation."],[1396,"Type Coercion"],[2950,"Typed consumer"],[2810,"Typed messages"],[2945,"Typed publisher"],[461,"Typed values used by user headers and option values. The kind byte:"],[3114,"Uncompressed payloads and user headers alias the reply buffer, so retaining their slices pins the whole reply. Copy those bytes to retain only the message you need. S2 decompression allocates a separate payload buffer."],[478,"Unknown keys are rejected at the wire edge, never silently skipped."],[2015,"Unknown plugin keys are ignored. Inspect the effective authentication posture in startup logs after changing configuration. The runtime control API exposes raw plugin configuration, so access to it also exposes configured secrets."],[3278,"Unknown record kinds retain their codes and raw bodies. Stop without advancing the cursor when the application cannot apply a record. Skipping it can leave a mirror inconsistent. Key material is never exposed in stream records. Their public options contain only the key source and fingerprints."],[3648,"Unknown top-level TOML fields and unknown server `IGGY_` environment names **reject startup**. Some nested tables can still ignore unknown fields, so compare the effective configuration with the intended settings."],[3448,"Unless you need client-side encryption, a custom `Partitioner`, or other settings outside the connection-string options, you should be good to go with the connection string. On the other hand, you can always make use of `IggyClientBuilder::from_connection_string()` to extend the options on top of the provided connection string."],[2272,"Unlike the field-level transforms, the three format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults: every key shown in their examples (except the ones listed as optional) must be present, or the connector configuration fails to load. The `source_format` and `target_format` keys accept any schema value: `json`, `raw`, `text`, `proto`, `flat_buffer`, `avro`. The FlatBuffers transform additionally restricts the pair: only JSON to/from FlatBuffers, FlatBuffers to Text or Raw, and identity conversions are accepted. Any other combination fails the configuration load."],[4018,"Unlimited locked memory is an option for a dedicated service with a planned memory budget. A finite limit is also valid if it covers the runtime's locked-memory requirements. Neither setting creates RAM or overrides a container's memory ceiling."],[2227,"unwrap_envelope"],[2225,"update_fields"],[392,"UpdateResource is code 16, operation 150:"],[752,"Updates preserve omitted fields. Compression and storage options remain fixed at creation. `topic get` displays partition details and explicit and derived options. See Topic options."],[4332,"Updates use UpdateResource with a set/reset patch. SDKs retain resource-specific update methods, but these methods send the shared command. Only `name`, `message_expiry` and `max_topic_size` can change on a topic."],[4349,"Upgrade clients and tools"],[3136,"Upgrade every reader of the topic to a compatible 0.10 client before writing producer-aware batches. The batch format contains additional producer fields. The older SDKs cannot safely decode it."],[4346,"Upgrading from 0.9 to 0.10"],[2715,"Usage"],[1289,"Usage Notes"],[3004,"Use `Consumer.Group(\"my-group\")` and `WithConsumerGroup(\"my-group\", createIfNotExists: true)`. Do not supply standalone partitions. Set `WithPollOptions(new PollOptions { Commit = CommitPolicy.Auto() })` for automatic commits after processing."],[963,"Use `durability=persisted` when acknowledged messages must be recoverable after volatile copies are lost. Select `consumer_offset_durability=persisted` independently when explicit offset stores and deletes need the same guarantee. Ordinary flush thresholds can remain at their defaults."],[3069,"Use `GET /streams/{stream_id}/topics/{topic_id}/messages/deferred` for a deferred read without offset commits. A read that sets `auto_commit=true` must use `POST` on that path. The server rejects a committing `GET`."],[1450,"Use `https://` for `fe_url` in production: credentials travel as HTTP Basic auth."],[962,"Use `replicated` when the workload accepts the risk of losing acknowledged operations after correlated failures in exchange for avoiding a required storage barrier at completion. Isolate replicas across failure domains. See Deployment."],[1535,"Use `schema = \"json\"` and publish JSON in the shape Slack expects, for example `{\"text\":\"hello\"}`. `include_metadata = false` skips the envelope, but JSON is still reserialized. The original bytes and whitespace are not preserved."],[1596,"Use `schema = \"json\"` on the stream. Each JSON object represents a table row. Nested objects and arrays are supported when they match the table schema. Unknown fields are ignored. Missing nullable fields become null. Missing required fields or incompatible values fail the table write."],[3007,"Use `WithPollOptions` for count, wait, minimum count, byte budget and commit policy. Use `WithPartitions` for standalone partition selection, and `WithPollingStrategy` for the initial position. The previous `WithBatchSize`, `WithPollingInterval`, `WithPartitionId` and `WithAutoCommitMode` consumer methods are replaced."],[1404,"Use a `file:///...` URI for an absolute local path. Bare filesystem paths are not accepted."],[918,"use a different root `path` for each process on the same host"],[2078,"Use a stable `client_id`, `clean_start=false` and a nonzero MQTT 5 session expiry to resume broker sessions. With `include_metadata=true`, the source places MQTT 5 properties in a JSON envelope. Without it, payloads remain raw bytes."],[1759,"Use an appropriate `batch_size` for your workload (larger batches give better throughput)"],[3499,"Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build both from the same checkout. The Getting Started guide shows the source dependency setup."],[2688,"Use another language: SDK section."],[820,"Use checkpoint commits after processing. A checkpoint retains the partition history and owner that delivered the data. Committing only a numeric offset loses that context."],[2352,"Use consistent coordinator-log configuration across gateways. With it disabled, the gateway with the lowest node ID coordinates all groups, and groups wait while it is down. See the multi-gateway deployment reference for permissions and takeover timing."],[2648,"Use deferred polling to tune wait and batch limits. Use the high-level SDK for producers and group consumers. The examples cover headers, serialization, TLS and stream builders."],[3208,"Use deferred polling to wait for messages. A partition reader groups partitions by primary, and a consumer hands messages to the application one at a time. `PollOptions` defines the count, wait, byte budget and commit policy. Manual commits are the shared default."],[1479,"Use HTTPS when credentials must be protected in transit. The connector uses the Elasticsearch client's certificate verification. This configuration does not expose a switch to disable it."],[3244,"Use Java 17 or newer. The SDK is published to Maven Central."],[3548,"Use new resource names when running this creation example again. `disconnect()` closes the current connection and permits a later `connect()`. `shutdown()` is final."],[4406,"Use Rust SDK 0.12.0 or Python SDK 0.10.0 with this server. See Server compatibility for the other languages. Also make sure that credentials match the stored account, not just the current bootstrap environment."],[4045,"Use storage with measured sustained bandwidth and latency, and record cloud volume and instance limits. Keep free space for segments, WAL, and recovery. Compare warm-cache and cold-cache runs explicitly. Do not periodically drop caches or disable storage flushes to improve a benchmark number. Storage, retention, and the selected durability policy must match the workload being evaluated."],[3538,"Use Swift concurrency to produce, read and manage Iggy resources over TCP with optional TLS."],[4012,"Use the benchmarking guide to compare throughput, p50, p99 and p99.9 latency, memory use, and errors under the same workload. Keep the server version, transport, topic durability, CPU allocation, and storage identical between runs."],[3229,"Use the blocking client for scripts, CLI tools, integration tests, and anywhere sequential code is easier to reason about. Use the async client for high throughput, reactive applications, and composing non-blocking requests with `CompletableFuture`. The async client runs I/O on Netty event loop threads: never block them with `.join()`, `.get()`, `Thread.sleep()`, or blocking I/O inside `thenApply`/`thenAccept`. Offload blocking work with `thenApplyAsync(fn, executor)`. The examples/java README covers these async patterns in detail."],[2036,"Use the broker credentials and main runtime configuration from the source guide. Build the plugin from the matching 0.10.0 checkout root:"],[2104,"Use the broker credentials and main runtime configuration from the source guide. Build the plugin from the matching 0.10.0 checkout root:"],[1980,"Use the broker credentials and main runtime configuration from the source guide. From the matching 0.10.0 Iggy checkout root, build the plugin:"],[1770,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 Iggy checkout root, build the plugin:"],[1839,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 Iggy checkout root, build the plugin:"],[1941,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 Iggy checkout root, build the plugin:"],[1367,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1496,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1564,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1611,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1654,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1684,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1729,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1929,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. Save the configuration below in that runtime's connector directory. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1429,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. Save the configuration below in the runtime's connector directory and replace the Doris password with your own. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1461,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. Save the configuration below in the runtime's connector directory and replace the Elasticsearch credentials with your own. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[1401,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the sink guide. Save the configuration below in the runtime's connector directory. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[2155,"Use the broker credentials and runtime setup from the source guide. Save the configuration below in that runtime's connector directory. From the matching 0.10.0 repository root, build the plugin:"],[4462,"Use the CLI to create a stream and topic, then send 60 messages to it:"],[2907,"Use the credentials configured when the server was initialized. These examples use the `iggy` / `iggy` setup from the introduction:"],[1805,"Use the guest credentials only with a local broker. Exchange type accepts `direct`, `topic`, `fanout` or `headers`. Exchange durability must match an existing exchange. Delivery mode accepts `persistent` or `non_persistent`. Durable delivery also depends on the broker and queue configuration."],[2813,"Use the installation and server setup first. The snippets share their named resources: run a producer before its corresponding consumer."],[3590,"Use the Linux tuning guide to check process limits, swap, huge pages, CPU/NUMA placement, writeback, and network capacity before measuring. Record the exact host settings and change one variable at a time. Use a separate load generator, or disjoint CPU sets when it shares a host with the server."],[1893,"Use the matching 0.10.0 checkout and broker from the quick start. Its broker must use the credentials below. Create the `connectors` directory, then save these two configuration files there and in the repository root as labeled."],[2185,"Use the matching 0.10.0 checkout and broker from the quick start. Its broker must use the credentials below. Create the `connectors` directory, then save these two configuration files there and in the repository root as labeled."],[2687,"Use the producer and consumer builders with batching and offset management: High-level SDK."],[1902,"Use the reference crate's Cargo.toml from the matching checkout, including `[lib] crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"lib\"]` and its workspace dependency features. The Rust blocks through the `Sink` implementation below combine into `src/lib.rs`. The trait above is a separate API reference."],[1804,"Use the sink runtime setup and build `iggy_connector_rabbitmq_sink`. Its plugin configuration is:"],[1814,"Use the sink runtime setup and build `iggy_connector_redshift_sink`. The plugin requires `connection_string`, `target_table`, `aws_iam_role`, `s3_bucket`, `s3_prefix` and `aws_region`. Supply S3 credentials through the supported credential configuration for your deployment."],[2316,"Use these credentials only for local development. In a deployment, provision a separate bridge account with the required permissions. A transient connection failure retries at startup for up to 60 seconds. Bad credentials or missing required permissions stop startup. The gateway does not silently switch back to storage-free responses."],[678,"user"],[1806,"User headers are preserved. With metadata enabled, the sink also adds `iggy_stream`, `iggy_topic`, `iggy_partition_id` and `iggy_offset`. String headers become AMQP LongString and raw headers become ByteArray."],[434,"User headers block"],[2839,"User login"],[2850,"User-defined headers"],[4124,"Username and password"],[317,"Users"],[20,"Users & Access Tokens"],[4136,"Users authenticate with a username and password, for example via the Rust SDK's `login_user()`. Passwords are hashed using **Argon2id** (a memory-hard hashing algorithm). A fresh server requires explicit root credentials. It does not generate or log a root password. Set both environment variables:"],[3806,"Valid `cpu_allocation` syntaxes:"],[3601,"Validation at boot"],[829,"Validation rules"],[624,"Value 0 is reserved and never sent."],[554,"Value 0 is reserved and rejected. The 64..127 range is reserved for server-internal operations  and every value in it is refused from clients."],[4326,"Value forms are forgiving: byte sizes accept a raw number of bytes or a string like `\"128 MiB\"`, expiry accepts microseconds or a humantime string like `\"7 days\"`, booleans accept `true`/`false`. Create admission re-parses and re-encodes what you send, so a string `segment_size=128MiB` is stored as the number it names."],[529,"Values a client may send:"],[4037,"Verify actual usage in `/proc/PID/smaps`: `AnonHugePages` reports PMD-sized anonymous THP, while `Private_Hugetlb` and `Shared_Hugetlb` identify explicit huge-page mappings. A successful pool reservation alone does not prove Iggy used it."],[2390,"Versioning"],[1026,"view changes and deterministic primary selection"],[1012,"Viewstamped Replication"],[1050,"VSR framing is the *only binary* wire protocol, used by TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket connections. HTTP uses the REST API. Every SDK (Rust, Go, Java, C#, C++, Node.js, Python, PHP, Swift) speaks it, and every BDD suite runs against the clustered-capable `iggy-server`. Binary clients built before the VSR migration **cannot talk to current servers**."],[1054,"VSR started as a parallel `server-ng` implementation behind a `vsr` build feature. That phase is over. The VSR server moved into the mainline `core/server` crate and the `iggy-server` binary, the feature flag is gone, and the legacy server and its wire format were deleted. What this page describes is the only server implementation."],[1045,"VSR uses three main flows:"],[3045,"Wait for messages, read several partitions and choose when to store consumer offsets."],[1772,"Wait for readiness, then save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory. `index` is a YAML string following the Quickwit index configuration format. Use a format version supported by your Quickwit server. The sink sends this YAML when creating a missing index. It does not update or compare an existing index's mapping."],[4295,"Wait for the `server client listeners started` log line before connecting. The server logs `server running; waiting on shard threads` earlier, before the port is open, and connecting on that line fails some of the time."],[1731,"Wait for the readiness command to report accepting connections. Save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory. Replace the URI for your deployment. Startup opens the SQLx pool and runs `SELECT 1`. It creates the table only when requested."],[1982,"Wait until the health response reports `timed_out: false`, then create the source index and a document:"],[958,"WAL history is reclaimed only after the materialized segment and offset state needed to replace it has been synchronized. The server's `[partition] wal_bytes_max` setting, default `256 MiB`, bounds retained and queued/in-flight prepare bytes per partition. The accounting includes message bodies even when the WAL stores only references to them. Capacity pressure causes checkpointing and backpressure. It does not downgrade a persisted operation. Temporary rewrites need additional disk space."],[2463,"We also publish edge/dev/nightly releases (e.g. `0.6.0-edge.1` or `apache/iggy:edge`), for both SDKs and the Docker images. These are typically compatible with the latest changes but are not guaranteed to be stable and, as the name suggests, are not recommended for production use."],[1904,"We can invoke the expected macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink within the runtime."],[2199,"We can invoke the expected macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the source within the runtime."],[2628,"We will build a **producer** (publishing messages to a stream) and a **consumer** (reading those messages back). In a real system, a service can be both at the same time - publishing its own events while consuming events from other services."],[3565,"We've also built the **benchmarking platform** where you can browse published benchmarks and compare results. This is the another open-source project available here."],[2385,"Web UI"],[1489,"Webhook (Slack)"],[4083,"WebSocket"],[2713,"WebSocket options"],[4107,"WebSocket provides bidirectional streaming over HTTP upgrade. Iggy uses the `compio-ws` crate to connect tungstenite framing to compio's completion-based I/O. The default WebSocket read buffer is 128 KiB and the default maximum message size is 64 MiB. These are separate settings."],[2775,"WebSocket TLS does not validate the server certificate unless `tls_validate_certificate=true`. With validation enabled, `tls_ca_file` replaces the built-in Mozilla roots. Size limits cannot be removed through connection strings. Use the WebSocket configuration object for an unlimited message or frame size."],[2353,"Welcome"],[3918,"What a completed write guarantees, how to select topic durability, and what survives a single-node crash."],[929,"What an acknowledgement means"],[2381,"What Apache Iggy is: a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, with QUIC, TCP, WebSocket and HTTP transports."],[1873,"What sink connectors do, and the Sink trait every one of them implements."],[2165,"What source connectors do, and the Source trait every one of them implements."],[3922,"What survives a failure"],[4286,"What the container needs"],[3974,"What the Iggy server does, and where its releases and Docker images are published."],[4374,"What the most common first-run failures look like, what causes them, and how to fix them."],[4445,"What the server exports"],[4053,"What to alert on"],[2617,"What we will build"],[2525,"What works differently"],[4052,"What's exported"],[1239,"When `api_key` is set, every request must carry it in the `api-key` header - except `GET /` and `GET /health`, which stay public. An empty key disables authentication. Configuration endpoints return plugin credentials and accept configuration changes, so set an API key before exposing this API to other hosts. Enable TLS or use a trusted TLS proxy for remote access."],[4180,"When `encryption.enabled` is true, the server generates a key for each new stream. A client can also supply a stream key at creation. The server wraps that key with a key derived from `encryption.key`. Reads expose fingerprints, never keys."],[1515,"When `include_metadata = true` (default), the JSON-mode payload is wrapped:"],[4336,"When `replication_factor` is absent, the server resolves `cluster.default_replication_factor` and stores the result with the topic. The default `all` uses every cluster node. A later server configuration change does not change existing topics."],[1407,"When `storage_backend_type` selects a backend, the connector validates these credential options:"],[1996,"When `timestamp_field` is set, the query is also filtered to values strictly greater than the last acknowledged timestamp. Only top-level strings that parse as RFC3339 update the watermark. Numeric timestamps, date-only strings and nested paths do not. Without a `timestamp_field` there is no incremental cursor and each poll returns the first `batch_size` matching documents."],[4434,"When a partition moves, the server stops the old owner polling it before handing it to the new one, and waits up to 30 seconds by default (see How does consumer group rebalancing work?)."],[4229,"When a prepare WAL exists, recovery reconciles it with materialized segment and offset state. Unrecoverable partition data is fenced and repaired from peers when available. It must not be replaced by an empty healthy partition. A singleton has no peer copy to fetch."],[2520,"When a segment reaches or passes the topic's segment size (default 1 GiB), it is **sealed** and a new segment is created. The batch that crosses the size is written whole, so a sealed segment can exceed it by up to one batch."],[361,"When an offset is stored, the response body is 20 bytes. When none is stored, a successful reply has an empty body."],[4486,"When building from source, generate the static assets before compiling the server. Run from the repository root:"],[904,"When clustering is disabled, `--replica-id 0` is still accepted. Any other id is rejected because it would have to match a `cluster.nodes` entry."],[2296,"When consumers join or leave a consumer group, the server triggers cooperative partition rebalancing. Partitions are redistributed among active members. During rebalancing, a pending revocation phase fences new polls from the previous owner. Its configurable timeout defaults to 30s, after which the server can force a transfer. This coordinates polling ownership. It does not prove that application processing has finished."],[2149,"When decommissioning a CDC connector, stop it and drop its replication slot to stop WAL retention. With PostgreSQL's default `max_slot_wal_keep_size = -1`, an unused slot can retain unbounded WAL. See the upstream README for details."],[951,"When either policy is `persisted`, each multi-replica partition uses a bounded on-disk prepare WAL. Message prepares contain headers and segment references. Their payloads live in segment files retained by hard links until the WAL history can be reclaimed. Other operations are stored inline. A prepare requiring persistence cannot release its `PrepareOk` until its message bodies, WAL history, and durable frontier are recoverable."],[3781,"When enabled, the per-shard verifier evicts stale group-member connections after the heartbeat threshold. Evicting a link does not itself end its logical session. The metadata liveness process expires the session lease and reassigns group membership. The session timeout is 1.2 times `heartbeat.interval`, or 36 seconds by default. Periodic checks mean expiry is not an exact wall-clock deadline."],[1011,"When HTTP TLS is enabled, forwarding uses HTTPS and pins the destination certificate to the forwarding node's own HTTP leaf certificate. All nodes therefore need the same HTTP certificate for forwarding. Distinct per-node HTTP certificates fail verification. Replica TLS is configured separately and does not encrypt the HTTP forwarding hop."],[1120,"When implementing `Sink`, make sure to use the `sink_connector!` macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink with the runtime. Each sink should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID via expected `new()` method."],[3197,"When polling messages, you can choose from several strategies (Rust method names are shown):"],[3203,"When sending messages, you can control partition routing (Rust method names are shown):"],[1669,"When the configured primary key is absent from a document, the connector injects a stable value derived from the stream, topic, partition, offset, and message ID, avoiding Meilisearch primary-key inference failures. An existing key is preserved, including an invalid or null value that Meilisearch may reject, unless a reserved metadata field below overwrites it. Use unique values that meet Meilisearch's document ID rules. Reusing a key with `replace` replaces the whole document, removing omitted fields. `update` keeps fields omitted from the new document. Both actions can insert a new document."],[976,"When the request omits it, `cluster.default_replication_factor` applies. Its default is `all`. The metadata group always uses the full cluster roster. A topic with fewer copies does not reduce the metadata quorum requirement."],[3587,"When the server address uses `localhost` or `127.0.0.1`, the saved command includes non-secret environment settings from the server configuration catalog. Credentials and unknown `IGGY_` variables are omitted."],[2780,"When you need settings a connection string cannot express (client-side encryption, a custom `Partitioner`, and similar), start from `IggyClientBuilder::from_connection_string()` and extend the builder on top."],[804,"Where requests are served"],[3444,"Which can be used to create the client like this:"],[3923,"Which responses prove completion"],[1915,"While the schema of messages (that will be consumed from the Iggy stream), cannot be controlled by the sink connector itself, the built-in configuration allows to decide what's the expected format of the messages (the particular `StreamDecoder` will be used)."],[2556,"While we could put an equal sign between the log and the stream, they are not the same, at least in a case of Iggy streaming server. The stream is a logical concept, and you might think of it as a **namespace**. For example, you could have a single stream for the whole system, or multiple streams e.g. representing the different environments, such as `dev`, `staging` and `production`. The stream is identified by its unique ID. The stream can have zero or more topics assigned, which results in the records being published to the specific topics that belong to the particular stream."],[3877,"Why these capabilities?"],[1017,"Wire protocol and SDKs"],[1826,"With `archive=false`, a successfully loaded staging file is deleted. With it enabled, the file is moved under the archive prefix. A staged file alone does not prove that COPY completed."],[1751,"With `auto_create_table = true` and all three metadata flags enabled, the connector creates the following structure if the table is absent. The `payload` column type follows `payload_format`:"],[1582,"With `dynamic_routing = false`, every batch is copied to every successfully loaded table in `tables`. Invalid names and tables that cannot be loaded are skipped at startup. Startup fails if no table can be loaded. The writer uses each table's schema and default partition spec captured at startup, so restart the connector after changing them."],[1705,"With `include_metadata = true`, documents contain `iggy_offset`, `iggy_timestamp`, `iggy_stream`, `iggy_topic` and `iggy_partition_id`. Offsets above the signed 64-bit range use `iggy_offset_str` instead. Partition IDs use a BSON 32-bit integer when possible and a 64-bit integer otherwise. `iggy_checksum` is a 64-bit integer when possible and a decimal string otherwise. Message and origin timestamps become BSON datetimes, truncating microseconds to milliseconds. Checksum and origin timestamp flags are independent of `include_metadata`. `_id` and `payload` are always present."],[1629,"With `include_metadata = true`, stream, topic and partition are tags by default. Disabling an individual `include_*_tag` flag writes that value as an `iggy_stream`, `iggy_topic`, or `iggy_partition` field instead."],[1670,"With `include_metadata = true`, stream, topic, partition, offset, checksum, message ID, timestamps and available headers are written as reserved `iggy_*` fields after payload parsing, overwriting same-named payload fields. The exception is a supplied `iggy_id` when it is the configured primary key. With a different primary key, `iggy_id` holds the generated Iggy identity. Avoid other reserved metadata fields as your primary key because metadata overwrites them."],[2142,"With `mode = \"cdc\"` the connector reads changes from a logical replication slot instead of polling tables:"],[3934,"With `persisted`, successful message completion follows this path:"],[4225,"With `replicated`, completion does not wait for an additional stable-storage barrier. With `persisted`, a single-replica partition flushes and synchronizes committed segment state before success. A multi-replica partition instead gates the required prepare acknowledgements on recoverable WAL history, so persisted success can precede an ordinary segment flush. `messages_required_to_save=1` is not required."],[3933,"With `replicated`, the server commits and applies the operation locally. Message batches can remain in the in-memory partition journal after success. Ordinary segment writes still happen when a flush trigger fires, and a write that triggers a flush can wait for that I/O, but success does not require stable-storage synchronization."],[4117,"With `self_signed = false`, `cert_file` and `key_file` must both exist. For production, provide proper certificate files everywhere. Ephemeral certificates **change on every start**, so a trust configuration pinned to one generated certificate does not survive a restart."],[2745,"With `tls=true`, TCP always validates the server certificate. Without `tls_ca_file`, it uses the built-in Mozilla roots. An explicit CA file can trust a private CA. The example above runs from the repository root against a TLS-enabled listener using the test certificate and key in `core/certs/iggy_cert.pem` and `core/certs/iggy_key.pem`. Those certificates are for local testing. See Security for TLS configuration."],[4231,"With `validate_checksum = true` (the default), every batch a disk poll reads is re-hashed and compared against its stored checksum. A mismatch stops the disk walk and logs an error on the server. The consumer receives any valid prefix already read, or an ordinary empty poll if none was read. The protocol does not report the checksum failure to the consumer. Repeated polls can therefore wait indefinitely at damaged data. Setting it to `false` skips the re-hash and serves whatever decodes, which can hand a consumer bytes provably not the ones written. Only disable it with a corruption guard elsewhere in the stack."],[4269,"With a server encryption key configured, create a private key file and pass it to the CLI:"],[3573,"With a server running, invoke the benchmark kind and transport of your choice. Workloads that produce create their benchmark streams and topics, deleting pre-existing `bench-stream-{n}` streams unless `--reuse-streams` is set. Consumer-only workloads (`pc` and `bcg`) require those streams and enough messages to exist already. Run the corresponding producer workload first, with matching stream, partition, and message counts."],[2902,"With an encryptor configured, polling with `autoCommit: true` throws `InvalidOperationException` by default: the server commits the offset **before the client decrypts**, so a decryption failure would silently skip the whole batch. Poll with `autoCommit: false` and store offsets after processing, or opt in with `AllowAutoCommitWithEncryptor = true`. `IggyConsumer` separately rejects wire auto-commit with encryption. Use `AfterReceive` or `Disabled` there."],[3000,"With background sending enabled, typed values are serialized at flush time. Keep them immutable or pass a snapshot until `WaitUntilAllSendsAsync` completes."],[948,"With both policies set to `replicated`, partition prepares stay off the disk prepare-WAL path. Replicas retain the prepares in memory, and committed messages reach segment files through ordinary flush scheduling."],[842,"With clustering enabled, the server validates the section at boot and **refuses to start** on an invalid value, printing the rule that failed. The repair chunk limit and superblock failure timeout are validated even when clustering is disabled."],[2318,"With Kafka command-line tools installed, use the gateway as the bootstrap server:"],[1865,"With metadata enabled, each wrapper also contains `offset`, a UTC timestamp at second precision, `stream`, `topic` and `partition_id`. Message IDs, checksums and origin timestamps are not included. Headers are a separate optional object: string values remain strings, raw values become base64, booleans become booleans and supported numeric types become JSON numbers. Other values use strings."],[1349,"With metadata enabled, the table needs `iggy_stream` and `iggy_topic` as STRING, `iggy_partition_id` and `iggy_offset` as INT64, `iggy_timestamp` as TIMESTAMP and `iggy_id` as STRING. `include_headers=true` also needs `iggy_headers` as JSON or STRING."],[2141,"With no selected column, `include_metadata = false` currently retains the envelope and adds an extra `data` level. Whole-row BYTEA fields are base64 strings. Keep database timestamps in the payload when needed. The runtime does not copy the plugin's timestamp fields into broker message metadata."],[3391,"With Python 3.11 or newer and Rust/Cargo available, run from `examples/python`. `uv` uses the local SDK path in `pyproject.toml`. The pip command below names that path explicitly:"],[1960,"With root, namespace or database authentication, startup calls `/signin` and SQL requests use Basic authentication with the configured scope. Namespace/database users require pre-existing resources and `auto_define_table = false`. With `auth_scope = \"none\"`, credentials are ignored. The server must permit those unauthenticated operations. Automatic DDL uses `IF NOT EXISTS` and does not migrate existing definitions. The optional `<table>_iggy_offset_idx` index is not a uniqueness constraint. `define_indexes = true` with metadata disabled rejects startup. Without automatic DDL it only logs a warning and skips index creation."],[2867,"With TCP auto-login configured, the client logs in once connected. Successful manual username/password or PAT login is also remembered for reconnects. HTTP `ConnectAsync` does no network work and does not auto-login. Call `LoginUserAsync` or `LoginWithPersonalAccessTokenAsync` explicitly."],[2350,"With the bridge and broker list configured, the shared coordinator log is enabled by default. It stores group generations, offset commits and deletions in Iggy. The bridge account needs `manage_servers` for metadata changes. Explicitly enabling the log with insufficient permissions stops startup. An implicitly enabled log can fall back with a warning, so inspect startup logs."],[1750,"With the default `bytea` format, valid UTF-8 JSON payloads can still be queried by converting the bytes: `convert_from(payload, 'UTF8')::jsonb->>'user_id'`. With `json`, the native operators apply directly: `payload->>'user_id'`."],[1157,"With the default `file` state backend, source plugins can supply checkpoints (see the SDK documentation) for the directory configured in the `[state]` section. An HTTP state backend is also available:"],[1521,"With the envelope enabled, this object becomes the `payload` field shown above. JSON values are reserialized and text payloads become JSON strings."],[1880,"With the local configuration provider, each sink connector has a TOML file in the directory specified by the main runtime config. The runtime also supports an HTTP configuration provider."],[2172,"With the local configuration provider, each source connector has a TOML file in the directory specified by the main runtime config. The runtime also supports an HTTP configuration provider."],[1111,"With the server running, create the example streams and topics using the CLI from this checkout. An existing server must have these credentials, or you must adjust the commands and connector configuration to match it."],[4461,"Within a few seconds the Prometheus targets page shows the `iggy` job as up."],[2325,"Without gateway idempotence, delivery is at-least-once. A retry after an uncertain write can append records again. Set `IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS=true` after every Iggy node, gateway and reader uses a compatible release. Idempotent batches then retain their producer ID, epoch and sequence through the gateway."],[658,"Without the producer-present flag, every producer field is zero. With it, the partition can deduplicate producer retries. Sequence numbers wrap after the signed 32-bit maximum. See Idempotent producers."],[3381,"Working examples are available in the examples/php directory. They read `IGGY_CONNECTION_STRING` for a binary transport with credentials. Otherwise they connect over TCP using `IGGY_HOST` and `IGGY_PORT`, then log in with the literal `IGGY_USERNAME` and `IGGY_PASSWORD` values:"],[3385,"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. CI runs the source examples against a source-built server, including a separate TLS pass:"],[3412,"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. See Examples for the list and how to run them."],[3383,"Working Python examples from the core repository, covering connection strings, message headers and TLS."],[2698,"Wrap each message in an envelope that names its type, and send the envelope as JSON:"],[1342,"Write Iggy messages to an existing BigQuery table through the Storage Write API."],[1392,"Write messages from Iggy streams into Delta Lake tables on local disk, S3, Azure Blob Storage or Google Cloud Storage."],[1933,"Write messages from Iggy streams into SurrealDB over the HTTP API, bulk inserts split by the configured batch size."],[1829,"Write messages from Iggy streams to Amazon S3 and S3-compatible object stores such as MinIO, R2, Spaces and B2."],[1603,"Write messages from Iggy streams to InfluxDB as line-protocol points, on both InfluxDB V2 and V3."],[2479,"Write pipeline"],[4194,"Write pipeline"],[4501,"XML decoder"],[2302,"Yes. Replication based on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) is built into the server. Multi-node clustering is configured via the `[cluster]` section (single-node mode is the default, `enabled = false`). It covers consensus, view changes, node authentication, and TLS between nodes, and is maturing toward production readiness. See the Clustering documentation for the protocol and Deploy for setting up a cluster."],[2555,"You address the log by **offset**, the position of the record in the log. Offsets start from 0 and increase within a partition. They are not guaranteed to be contiguous: after recovery, the server can skip reserved offsets to avoid reusing them. When the client reads the records, it specifies the offset to start from and the maximum number of records it wants. Starting from the beginning, or from any earlier offset, is how you replay the messages."],[2454,"You can also find the images for all the different tooling such as Connectors, MCP Server etc. here."],[1710,"You can apply transforms before writing to MongoDB. Every `[transforms.*]` section requires `enabled = true`. See the transforms documentation for details."],[3886,"You can easily run the Iggy server with Docker - the official images can be found here, use `docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0`, or `apache/iggy:latest` for the newest stable release."],[2456,"You can find the development `Dockerfile` and `docker-compose.yml` in the root of the repository. Published server images use `core/server/Dockerfile`. To build and start the server, run: `docker compose up`."],[1331,"You can load or reload schemas programmatically:"],[1115,"You can provide the following envs during the runtime startup:"],[4418,"You need Docker, Python 3.11 or later, and the Python SDK in a virtual environment. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer, but macOS has no kernel requirement (see System requirements)."],[2901,"You own the encryptor and must dispose it once the client is done with it."],[4417,"You will build a small order-processing service. A producer writes order events to a topic, keyed by customer. Two copies of the service share the topic through a consumer group. You stop one and watch the other take over, then start it again and see it resume from stored progress."],[4447,"You will run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana, and watch the broker on a dashboard as you send it messages."],[2549,"You've probably used RabbitMQ or Kafka already. They look similar at the first glance, and you can achieve the similar results with both (e.g. publishing and consuming the events by the different applications built on top of microservices architecture), but they work differently underneath."],[4467,"Your files stay in the directory, so `docker compose up -d` starts it all again. The server starts empty each time, so create a new token and send some messages before you open the dashboard."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":27,"2":28,"3":29,"4":30,"5":31,"6":32,"7":33,"8":34,"9":35,"10":36,"11":37,"12":38,"13":39,"14":40,"15":41,"16":42,"17":43,"18":44,"19":45,"20":46,"21":47,"22":48,"23":49,"24":50,"25":51,"26":52,"27":53,"28":54,"29":55,"30":56,"31":57,"32":58,"33":59,"34":60,"35":61,"36":62,"37":63,"38":64,"39":65,"40":66,"41":67,"42":68,"43":69,"44":70,"45":71,"46":72,"47":73,"48":74,"49":75,"50":76,"51":77,"52":78,"53":79,"54":80,"55":81,"56":82,"57":83,"58":84,"59":85,"60":86,"61":87,"62":88,"63":89,"64":90,"65":91,"66":92,"67":93,"68":94,"69":95,"70":96,"71":97,"72":98,"73":99,"74":100,"75":101,"76":102,"77":103,"78":104,"79":105,"80":106,"81":107,"82":108,"83":109,"84":110,"85":111,"86":